window.I18N_DATA = {"lang":"en","recettes":{"baghrir":{"title":"Baghrir, thousand-hole pancakes","darija":"baghrir (also beghrir)","tagline":"Light, spongy semolina pancakes cooked on one side only: their thousand little holes soak up the hot butter and honey syrup.","region":"Morocco; also Algeria and Tunisia (Wikipedia)","occasion":"Breakfast, an afternoon snack with tea, Ramadan iftar","img_alt":"A baghrir on a tea towel, its surface riddled with tiny holes","serving":"Hot or warm, holey side dipped in the hot butter and honey syrup, then laid holes facing up (Cooking with Alia; Lalla Moulati; Taste of Maroc), with mint tea (Choumicha; Lalla Moulati, who also mentions coffee). For breakfast or as an afternoon snack (Yabiladi; Lalla Moulati; Taste of Maroc), and at the Ramadan iftar (Morocco World News 2022; Wikipedia). Other toppings: jam (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2022), amlou, or fresh cheese with olive oil (Morocco World News 2019). Storage, according to Taste of Maroc (the only source): plain baghrir keep for 2 days at room temperature, wrapped in cling film; those dipped in syrup go in the fridge; plain, they freeze for 2 to 3 months, with a square of cling film between each baghrir.","ing.0.group":"Batter","ing.semoule.name":"fine durum wheat semolina","ing.semoule.note":"this is what gives the yellow colour and the texture","ing.farine.name":"plain white flour (soft wheat)","ing.levure-boulangere.name":"dried yeast","ing.levure-chimique.name":"baking powder","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.sucre.name":"sugar","ing.sucre.note":"optional: half the recipes consulted leave it out","ing.eau.name":"lukewarm water","ing.eau.note":"at most: add it in two stages, you may not need it all","ing.1.group":"Butter and honey syrup","ing.beurre.name":"Butter","ing.beurre.pl":"Butter","ing.beurre.note":"the same amount as the honey","ing.miel.name":"Honey","ing.miel.pl":"Honey","ing.miel.note":"the same amount as the butter","equipment.0":"A blender (or a large mixing bowl and an electric whisk)","equipment.1":"Kitchen scales and a measuring jug","equipment.2":"A large bowl and a clean tea towel or cling film","equipment.3":"A non-stick frying pan in good condition, preferably small (a crêpe pan), kept for baghrir only if possible","equipment.4":"A small ladle","equipment.5":"A spatula","equipment.6":"Clean tea towels to lay the baghrir flat on","equipment.7":"A soft cloth or kitchen paper to wipe the pan","equipment.8":"A small saucepan for the butter and honey syrup","steps.0.title":"Put the dry ingredients in the blender","steps.0.text":"Put {{semoule}} fine semolina, {{farine}} flour, {{levure-boulangere}} dried yeast, {{levure-chimique}} baking powder, {{sel}} salt and, if using, {{sucre}} sugar into the blender jug.","steps.0.tip":"No blender? Put everything in a large mixing bowl and use an electric whisk (My Moroccan Food).","steps.1.title":"Blend with two-thirds of the water","steps.1.text":"Add about two-thirds of the {{eau}} of lukewarm water: just a little warmer than the room, not hot. Blend for 3 minutes, with short pauses, until the batter is very smooth, with no lumps.","steps.1.timer.label":"Blending","steps.1.timer.done":"Is the batter smooth? Now add the rest of the water, a little at a time.","steps.2.title":"Adjust the consistency","steps.2.text":"Pour in the rest of the water a little at a time, blending for a few seconds after each addition. Stop when the batter has the consistency of pouring cream: slightly thicker than crêpe batter, much runnier than American-style pancake batter. You may not need all the water.","steps.2.tip":"The consistency makes the holes: if it is too thick, the batter won't bubble (Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food).","steps.2.img.alt":"Smooth, runny baghrir batter in its bowl","steps.2.img.caption":"A smooth, runny batter","steps.3.title":"Leave to rest somewhere warm","steps.3.text":"Pour the batter into a large bowl, cover it with a clean tea towel or cling film and leave it to rest for 15 minutes somewhere warm. It is ready when its surface is covered in small bubbles and it looks light and frothy.","steps.3.timer.label":"Resting the batter","steps.3.timer.done":"Is the surface covered in small bubbles? Heat the pan.","steps.4.title":"Heat the pan without greasing it","steps.4.text":"Heat a non-stick frying pan in good condition over medium heat, with no fat. Wait until it is properly hot before the first ladleful: in a pan that isn't hot enough, few holes form.","steps.4.tip":"If you can, keep a pan just for baghrir: in a scratched pan, they stick (Lalla Moulati; Taste of Maroc).","steps.5.title":"Pour a small ladleful into the centre","steps.5.text":"Stir the batter with the ladle, then pour a small ladleful of batter into the centre of the pan. Don't swirl the pan as you would for a crêpe: the batter spreads out on its own into a round about 12 cm across.","steps.6.title":"Cook on one side only","steps.6.text":"Let it cook without ever turning the baghrir over: bubbles rise, burst and leave little holes all over the surface. It is cooked in 1 to 2 minutes, when there are no wet patches left: touched with a fingertip, it feels spongy and the batter doesn't stick. The underside shouldn't brown too much: if it does, turn the heat down a little.","steps.6.tip":"If you turn it over, you lose the holes (My Moroccan Food).","steps.7.title":"Fix it if there are no holes","steps.7.text":"No holes in the first baghrir? If the batter is too thick, add a little lukewarm water, a spoonful at a time, stir and wait a few minutes. If it hasn't rested long enough, leave it for another 5 to 10 minutes before trying again.","steps.8.title":"Lay flat on a tea towel","steps.8.text":"Lift the baghrir out with a spatula and lay it flat on a clean tea towel. Don't stack them while they are hot: they would stick together. Leave them to cool in a single layer before stacking them.","steps.8.tip":"To stack them while still hot, slip a sheet of kitchen paper between each baghrir (My Moroccan Food).","steps.9.title":"Wipe the pan and carry on","steps.9.text":"Wipe the pan with a soft cloth or kitchen paper, then repeat until all the batter is used up, stirring it before each ladleful. If the underside colours too quickly, turn the heat down.","steps.10.title":"Make the butter and honey syrup","steps.10.text":"Put equal amounts of butter and honey in a small saucepan. Melt over low heat, stirring, until the butter has completely melted and the syrup is very hot, then turn off the heat.","steps.10.tip":"You can add a few drops of orange blossom water to the syrup (My Moroccan Food).","steps.11.title":"Dip and serve","steps.11.text":"Dip the holey side of each baghrir in the hot syrup for a few seconds, then place it on the serving dish, holes facing up. Serve straight away, with mint tea.","steps.11.img.alt":"Baghrir soaked in butter and honey, its little holes glistening with syrup","steps.11.img.caption":"The holes soak up the syrup","secrets.0.text":"Use good-quality fine durum wheat semolina: this is what gives the yellow colour and the texture. In Morocco, a shop-bought baghrir that is too bright a yellow sometimes betrays a poor batter coloured with turmeric or an additive.","secrets.0.source":"Choumicha (fine durum wheat semolina, and “good-quality semolina and flour”); Taste of Maroc","secrets.1.text":"The right mix: about three-quarters semolina to one quarter flour. You can also use semolina only.","secrets.1.source":"Choumicha (450 g semolina to 150 g flour); Cooking with Alia (300 g to 100 g); Taste of Maroc (2 volumes to 1, or all semolina); Choumicha and Lalla Moulati (all-semolina versions)","secrets.2.text":"Two raising agents: the yeast makes the bubbles, the baking powder speeds things up. In the past, the batter had to rest for a long time; baking powder shortened the wait, hence the so-called “instant” recipes.","secrets.2.source":"Taste of Maroc; Bladi.net, Choumicha, Cooking with Alia, My Moroccan Food and Morocco World News use both raising agents","secrets.3.text":"It all comes down to consistency: like pouring cream, slightly thicker than crêpe batter, much runnier than American-style pancake batter. Neither too thick nor too thin.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia; Morocco World News 2019 (“precision is key”)","secrets.4.text":"Add the water in two stages: first two-thirds for blending, then the rest a little at a time until the consistency is right.","secrets.4.source":"Cooking with Alia (500 ml then the rest, out of 750 ml); Morocco World News 2019 (600 ml then the rest, out of 800 ml)","secrets.5.text":"Stick to the amount of yeast and the resting time. If the first baghrir has no holes, leave the batter to rest for another 5 to 10 minutes, or thin it with a little lukewarm water if it is thick.","secrets.5.source":"Choumicha (tip “Pour réussir les crêpes au mille trous”); My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc","secrets.6.text":"Don't let the batter over-ferment: resting it too long thickens it. If the holes stay small, shorten the rest next time and add a little water.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (reply to readers); Lalla Moulati (“don't let it ferment too much”, “10 minutes, no more, no less”)","secrets.7.text":"A non-stick frying pan in good condition, kept for baghrir only: most Moroccans insist on it, according to Taste of Maroc. And in a scratched pan, baghrir stick, warns Lalla Moulati.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc; Lalla Moulati","secrets.8.text":"No need to grease the pan: just wipe it between baghrir, with a soft cloth or a barely oiled piece of kitchen paper to remove any residue.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha (“wipe the pan”, “with a soft cloth”)","secrets.9.text":"Start with a properly hot pan, otherwise few holes form.","secrets.9.source":"My Moroccan Food; Choumicha (preheated pan)","secrets.10.text":"Medium heat. Choumicha even recommends medium to high heat, turned down towards the end of cooking each pancake; Taste of Maroc adjusts the heat so that the underside doesn't brown too much.","secrets.10.source":"Choumicha (recipes and tip); Taste of Maroc; Lalla Moulati (high heat until the holes appear, then turned down)","secrets.11.text":"Never turn a baghrir over: it cooks on one side only, and you would lose the holes.","secrets.11.source":"My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia; Wikipedia","secrets.12.text":"Don't stack them hot: leave them to cool flat, in a single layer, on a clean tea towel.","secrets.12.source":"My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia; Morocco World News 2022","secrets.13.text":"Dip the holey side in the hot syrup for a few seconds: the holes soak up the syrup.","secrets.13.source":"Cooking with Alia; Lalla Moulati; My Moroccan Food","mistakes.0":"Batter that is too thick: the bubbles don't burst and there are no holes (My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.1":"A pan that isn't hot enough at the start: few holes (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.2":"Turning the baghrir over: it loses its holes (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.3":"Stacking the baghrir while still hot: they stick together (My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia; Morocco World News 2022).","mistakes.4":"Keeping the heat too high right to the end: the underside browns too much (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha).","mistakes.5":"Using a scratched pan: the baghrir stick (Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.6":"Measuring by eye: weigh and measure your ingredients, as the consistency of the batter depends on it (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2019).","mistakes.7":"Giving the butter and honey syrup to a baby under one year old: no honey before the age of 1, because of the risk of infant botulism (NHS, the British health service).","variants.0":"All semolina: 375 g fine durum wheat semolina, 50 cl lukewarm water, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp yeast and 1 sachet of baking powder, blended for 3 minutes, rested for 15 minutes (Choumicha). Lalla Moulati also makes them with white vinegar: 4 glasses of fine semolina, 1 tbsp yeast, 2 sachets of baking powder, 1 tbsp white vinegar, 4 to 5 glasses of lukewarm water, rested for 10 minutes.","variants.1":"With vinegar, Choumicha's version: 450 g fine semolina, 150 g flour, 2 sachets of baking powder (7 g each), 1 tsp yeast, 1 tsp salt, 1 tbsp sugar and 75 cl water blended together, then 1 tbsp vinegar added at the end; rested for 15 minutes (Choumicha).","variants.2":"The old way, with a long rest: 200 g flour, 200 g semolina and ½ tsp salt; 1 tbsp yeast and 1 tsp baking powder dissolved in a little lukewarm water; ¾ l lukewarm water in all; covered rest of 1 h to 1 h 30 min (Bladi.net). With fresh yeast alone, you wait until the batter doubles in volume (Lalla Moulati).","variants.3":"With milk and egg yolk: 250 g semolina, 250 ml milk, 1 egg yolk, 1 tbsp yeast, ½ tsp salt and lukewarm water, rested for an hour (Yabiladi). Taste of Maroc also mentions versions with eggs and milk.","variants.4":"Khringo: in some regions the baghrir is briefly turned over, and it is then called khringo (Taste of Maroc; Wikipedia gives the name kh'ringu in Morocco and Algeria).","variants.5":"Choumicha's cream syrup: 100 g butter, 200 g honey and 3 tbsp crème fraîche melted over low heat, whisked together with a fork and served hot in a sauce boat (Choumicha).","variants.6":"Scented syrup: a few drops of orange blossom water in the butter and honey syrup (My Moroccan Food).","variants.7":"Margarine syrup: Lalla Moulati melts “vegetable butter” with the honey (Lalla Moulati).","variants.8":"Toppings: jam (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2022), amlou, fresh cheese and olive oil (Morocco World News 2019), chocolate (Morocco World News 2022)."},"batbout":{"title":"Batbout, Moroccan pan-cooked breads","darija":"batbout (also mkhamer, toghrift or matlou')","tagline":"Soft little round breads, cooked in a dry frying pan, that puff up as they cook and form a pocket for filling.","region":"Morocco","occasion":"Breakfast, meals, sandwiches; stuffed mini batbout at the Ramadan iftar","img_alt":"Two puffed-up batbouts cooking on a cast-iron griddle in Morocco","serving":"Warm or cold (My Moroccan Food). Split open and filled like a sandwich: chicken, tuna, cheese, salad or cooked vegetables (Morocco World News; My Moroccan Food), with halal meat. With grilled meats such as kefta or boulfaf (Taste of Maroc), with a tagine (Morocco World News) or with Moroccan salads (MarocMama). At breakfast, for mopping up eggs (MarocMama), with butter, honey, jam or fresh cheese (Taste of Maroc), or plain with a drizzle of olive oil and honey (Morocco World News). For strict halal, choose a cheese made with a microbial coagulant, without animal rennet. During Ramadan, stuffed mini batbout are almost always on the iftar table (MarocMama; Morocco World News; Taste of Maroc). Leftovers keep for a few days in the fridge in a sealed bag, then reheat for a few minutes in a frying pan or in the oven (MarocMama). They can be frozen once completely cooled (Taste of Maroc; MarocMama); MarocMama mentions 3 to 4 months in a tightly sealed bag.","ing.0.group":"Dough","ing.farine.name":"white flour (plain flour or strong bread flour)","ing.farine.note":"plus a little for the work surface","ing.semoule.name":"fine durum wheat semolina (fino), or durum wheat flour","ing.semoule.note":"this is what gives batbout its texture and flavour","ing.levure.name":"dried yeast","ing.levure.note":"a little less if the weather is very hot","ing.sucre.name":"sugar","ing.sucre.note":"optional: Cooking with Alia and Bladi.net leave it out","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.huile.name":"oil (sunflower or olive)","ing.huile.note":"optional: many Moroccans leave it out","ing.eau.name":"lukewarm water","ing.eau.note":"approximately: pour it in gradually, you may need a little less","ing.1.group":"For rolling out","ing.semoule-etalage.name":"fine semolina","ing.semoule-etalage.note":"for the work surface; or a little flour","equipment.0":"A large mixing bowl and a small bowl","equipment.1":"Kitchen scales and a measuring jug","equipment.2":"A rolling pin","equipment.3":"Two large clean, dry cotton tea towels (or a cotton sheet)","equipment.4":"A large heavy-based frying pan (cast iron) or a non-stick one, or a griddle","equipment.5":"A wire rack, or a basket lined with a tea towel","equipment.6":"Optional: a glass or a round cutter","steps.0.title":"Activate the yeast","steps.0.text":"In a small bowl, put {{levure}} dried yeast, a little of the sugar and a little lukewarm water, taken from the {{eau}} of water. Stir and leave for 5 minutes, until the mixture is frothy.","steps.0.tip":"Taste of Maroc, My Moroccan Food and MarocMama all let the yeast froth with a little of the sugar before making the dough.","steps.0.timer.label":"Yeast","steps.0.timer.done":"Is the mixture frothy? Make the dough. If not, wait another 5 minutes.","steps.1.title":"Mix the dough","steps.1.text":"In a large mixing bowl, mix {{farine}} flour, {{semoule}} fine semolina, the rest of the {{sucre}} of sugar and {{sel}} salt. Add {{huile}} oil, then the yeast. Pour in the rest of the water gradually, mixing by hand, until you have a soft dough. Stop adding water if it becomes sticky.","steps.1.tip":"My Moroccan Food pours in the water gradually so that the dough doesn't become too sticky. If it does, add a little flour.","steps.2.title":"Knead for 10 minutes","steps.2.text":"Knead the dough for 10 minutes on a lightly floured work surface, or in a stand mixer with the dough hook. It should be smooth, soft and elastic, and not sticky. If it sticks, add a little flour. If it is firm, add a little water, a spoonful at a time.","steps.2.timer.label":"Kneading","steps.2.timer.done":"Smooth, soft and elastic? Shape the balls. If not, knead a little longer.","steps.2.img.alt":"Hands kneading a dough","steps.2.img.caption":"Kneading the dough","steps.3.title":"Shape the balls","steps.3.text":"Divide the dough into smooth plum-sized balls, about 70 g each, one per batbout. Place them on a lightly floured surface, at least 2.5 cm apart. Cover them with a tea towel and leave to rest for 10 minutes.","steps.3.timer.label":"Resting the balls","steps.3.timer.done":"The balls have rested: roll them out.","steps.4.title":"Roll out thinly","steps.4.text":"Sprinkle your work surface with a little {{semoule-etalage}}. Roll out each ball with the rolling pin into a thin disc, about 3 mm thick. Don't make it any thicker, or the batbout may not puff up.","steps.4.tip":"You can also roll out all the dough and cut out rounds with a glass or a cutter, as Morocco World News and Cooking with Alia do. Gather up the trimmings, knead them, leave them to rest for 10 to 15 minutes and roll them out again (Taste of Maroc).","steps.5.title":"Leave to rise for 1 hour","steps.5.text":"Place the discs on a clean, dry tea towel, leaving space between them: they grow as they rise. Cover them with another tea towel. Leave to rise for 1 hour, until they are light and puffy. In a cold kitchen, it takes a little longer.","steps.5.timer.label":"Discs rising","steps.5.timer.done":"Are the discs light and puffy? Heat the frying pan. If not, leave them to rise a little longer.","steps.6.title":"Heat the frying pan dry","steps.6.text":"Heat a large heavy-based frying pan (cast iron) or a non-stick one over medium heat, with no fat, for several minutes. It must be very hot before you add the first batbout: this is what helps the pocket form.","steps.6.tip":"Christine Benlafquih (Taste of Maroc) lets her griddle heat for a good 5 minutes before she starts.","steps.7.title":"Place without squashing","steps.7.text":"Pick up a disc gently, without stretching it or pressing on it, and place it in the hot frying pan. Cook the batbout in several batches, depending on the size of your frying pan. If you handle them roughly, they deflate or split, and the pocket doesn't form.","steps.7.tip":"To move a disc without misshaping it, MarocMama slides a very thin chopping board underneath it.","steps.8.title":"Turn several times","steps.8.text":"Turn the batbout over as soon as the underside has set, after a few seconds. Then turn it several more times, until it puffs up and is golden on both sides: this takes a few minutes in all. Never press on it. Slightly uneven colouring is normal.","steps.8.tip":"If it browns too quickly, turn the heat down a little (Taste of Maroc). Careful: when you turn a puffed-up batbout, scalding steam can escape from a split (Taste of Maroc).","steps.9.title":"Leave to cool","steps.9.text":"Place the cooked batbout on a wire rack, or in a basket lined with a tea towel. You can stack them while still hot. Cook the remaining discs in the same way.","steps.10.title":"Open and fill","steps.10.text":"Serve the batbout warm or cold. To fill them, make a slit in the edge with a knife and open up the pocket. Fill it with whatever you like: chicken, tuna, cheese, salad or cooked vegetables.","steps.10.tip":"For a halal meal, use halal meat. For strict halal, choose a cheese made with a microbial coagulant, without animal rennet.","steps.11.title":"Or serve them plain","steps.11.text":"Batbout also goes well with a tagine, Moroccan salads or grilled meats such as kefta. At breakfast, eat it with butter, honey, jam or fresh cheese, or with a drizzle of olive oil and honey.","steps.12.title":"Store the leftovers","steps.12.text":"Keep the batbout for a few days in the fridge, in a sealed bag. To keep them longer, freeze them once completely cooled. Reheat them for a few minutes in a frying pan or in the oven, until they are soft again.","secrets.0.text":"Fine semolina (fino) or durum wheat flour is essential: without it, batbout has neither its texture nor its flavour.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih); every recipe consulted includes it, except Morocco World News (MarocMama gives a choice between it and wheat flour)","secrets.1.text":"You can use more semolina: Taste of Maroc advises not using more than half white flour, otherwise the crumb can become sticky.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.2.text":"A little oil in the dough prevents a dry bread, which holds up badly when filled. Many Moroccans leave it out.","secrets.2.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.3.text":"Sugar helps batbout brown and rise, and it helps the pocket form. It also makes the crumb more tender.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih's reply to a reader, 25 August 2021)","secrets.4.text":"The pocket forms when the discs have risen well and are placed in a frying pan that is already hot.","secrets.4.source":"MarocMama (Amanda Mouttaki); Taste of Maroc (frying pan heated for several minutes)","secrets.5.text":"Roll out thinly: a batbout that is too thick may not puff up. If it hasn't puffed up, open it gently with a knife to make the pocket.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.6.text":"Handle the risen discs gently: if they deflate or split, the air escapes and the pocket doesn't form.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.7.text":"Turn early and often: Christine Benlafquih turns the batbout as soon as the dough has set, after 10 to 15 seconds, before bubbles appear, then several more times.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food, MarocMama, Morocco World News and Cooking with Alia also turn it several times","secrets.8.text":"Never press on the batbout while it cooks: it would no longer puff up and would be hard to open and fill.","secrets.8.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou)","secrets.9.text":"If it doesn't puff up, turn the heat down a little and let it cook a little longer on one side. Also check that the discs have risen enough.","secrets.9.source":"MarocMama (Amanda Mouttaki's reply to a reader, 19 August 2019)","secrets.10.text":"In hot weather, the discs rise too quickly and their surface dries out, then splits: use a little less yeast. In cold weather, leave them to rise for longer.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.11.text":"Let the discs rise on a large tea towel or cotton sheet, folded back over the top to cover them.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc; Bladi.net (one cloth underneath, another on top)","mistakes.0":"Rolling the discs out too thick: the batbout may not puff up (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.1":"Putting the discs in a frying pan that isn't hot yet: the pocket forms in a pan that is already hot (MarocMama; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.2":"Skipping the rise of the discs: they must be left to rest once shaped (MarocMama).","mistakes.3":"Handling the risen discs roughly: they deflate or split (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.4":"Pressing on the batbout while it cooks (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.5":"Pouring in all the water at once: the dough becomes too sticky (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.6":"Turning a puffed-up batbout without care: the scalding steam escaping from a split can burn you (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.7":"Freezing the batbout while still warm: wait until they are completely cold (Taste of Maroc; MarocMama).","variants.0":"Other names: mkhamer, toghrift or matlou', depending on the region and the family. For some, mkhamer is a batbout cooked in an oiled frying pan; for others, a thick batbout without a pocket, served with butter and honey (Taste of Maroc). Choumicha calls it “batbot” or “pain metlouâ” (titles of her recipes).","variants.1":"With wholemeal flour: Taste of Maroc mixes white flour, fine semolina and wholemeal flour (375 g, 384 g and 120 g for 20 batbout); Morocco World News uses half white flour, half “wheat flour”.","variants.2":"All semolina, as large thick flatbreads: “semouline”, yeast, salt and water, the dough kneaded for about twenty minutes, four balls sprinkled with medium semolina and flattened by hand to 1.5 cm, a rise of about 1 hour, then cooked one at a time on a hot cast-iron dish (Bladi.net).","variants.3":"Ramadan mini batbout: small breads about 5 cm across, filled and served as bite-sized snacks at the iftar table (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News; MarocMama). Lalla Moulati publishes many filled versions.","variants.4":"Stuffed with chicken: small pieces of chicken sautéed with onion and peppers in three colours, served with a yoghurt, salt and lemon sauce (Morocco World News); or a quick tagine-style filling with chicken, preserved lemon, green olives and spring onions (My Moroccan Food).","variants.5":"Fried, then coated: instead of cooking it dry, fry the batbout in oil, then coat it in honey and butter (MarocMama).","variants.6":"With milk powder: one Lalla Moulati recipe adds 1 tbsp of milk powder per 600 g of flour and semolina.","variants.7":"Large stuffed batbouta in the frying pan: half the dough rolled out in a lightly oiled frying pan, a filling of thinly sliced vegetables (pepper, courgette, onion), cheese and spices in the centre, the other half on top, the edges well sealed, then cooked on both sides over low heat (Lalla Moulati). For strict halal, use a cheese made with a microbial coagulant, without animal rennet."},"bid-maticha":{"title":"Moroccan eggs in tomato","darija":"Bid w maticha","tagline":"Tomatoes cooked down in olive oil with garlic and cumin, then eggs cooked on top under a lid: a Moroccan dish everyone knows how to make, to mop up with bread. For lack of a recipe with quantities for the plain version, the amounts are reconstructed from Choumicha and from kefta tagines with eggs.","region":"Morocco: a dish everyone knows how to make (Lalla Moulati, 2018), common among students, single people and people in a hurry, and one Moroccans are very attached to (Hespress, 2012).","occasion":"A quick dinner, when you don't know what to make (Hespress, 2012); it is also eaten at breakfast (Lalla Moulati, 2018). It is also the fallback meal of students in a hurry (Hespress, 2012).","img_alt":"Bid w maticha: eggs cooked in a tomato sauce, served in an earthenware dish with bread","serving":"Piping hot, straight away, in the frying pan or the tagine set in the middle of the table: everyone helps themselves with bread (My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc). Choumicha suggests mint tea alongside, as do Cooking With Alia and Taste of Maroc with their kefta tagines.","ing.0.group":"Tomatoes and spices","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe medium tomato","ing.tomates.pl":"very ripe medium tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"about 125 g each","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust to taste","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.paprika.name":"paprika","ing.paprika.note":"optional","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.1.group":"Eggs and serving","ing.oeufs.name":"egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"eggs","ing.oeufs.note":"one per person","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.khobz.name":"bread (khobz)","ing.khobz.note":"for mopping up the sauce","equipment.0":"Large frying pan with a lid (or a tagine; if you are not cooking on gas, set it on a heat diffuser)","equipment.1":"Chopping board and knife","equipment.2":"Wooden spoon","steps.0.title":"Wash and slice the tomatoes","steps.0.text":"Rinse {{tomates:full}} under running water, then slice them into rounds.","steps.1.title":"Chop the garlic","steps.1.text":"Peel and finely chop {{ail:full}}.","steps.2.title":"Chop the herbs","steps.2.text":"Rinse the parsley and coriander under running water, dry them, then chop them finely until you have {{persil}} parsley and {{coriandre}} coriander. Set them aside for the end.","steps.2.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.2.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.3.title":"Fill the pan","steps.3.text":"Pour {{huile}} olive oil into a large frying pan. Spread out the tomato slices, then add the garlic, {{sel}} salt, {{cumin}} cumin, {{paprika}} paprika and {{poivre}} black pepper.","steps.3.tip":"Choumicha cooks her tomatoes and eggs in a tagine, over low heat. If you are not cooking on gas, slip a heat diffuser under the tagine (Taste of Maroc).","steps.4.title":"Bring to the boil","steps.4.text":"Put the pan over medium heat. As soon as it boils, cover and turn the heat down.","steps.5.title":"Let the tomatoes soften","steps.5.text":"Cook, covered, over low heat for 10 to 15 minutes, stirring from time to time, until the tomatoes are meltingly soft.","steps.5.tip":"Want a thicker sauce? Crush the tomatoes with the back of a spoon and cook for longer: My Moroccan Food allows about 25 minutes (Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food).","steps.6.title":"Taste and adjust","steps.6.text":"Taste the sauce and add a little salt if needed.","steps.7.title":"Crack in the eggs","steps.7.text":"With a spoon, make a small well in the sauce for each egg. Gently crack an egg into each well, without breaking the yolk ({{oeufs:full}} in all).","steps.8.title":"Cover and leave to set","steps.8.text":"Cover and cook over low heat for about 5 minutes, until the whites are set.","steps.8.tip":"The FDA advises cooking eggs until both the yolk and the white are firm. For young children, pregnant women, and elderly or vulnerable people, never serve lightly cooked eggs: keep the lid on until the yolk is firm (Anses; FDA).","steps.8.timer.label":"Eggs, covered","steps.8.timer.done":"Are the whites set? For a firm yolk, leave it covered a little longer.","steps.9.title":"Add the herbs","steps.9.text":"Scatter over {{persil}} chopped parsley and {{coriandre}} chopped coriander.","steps.10.title":"Serve straight away","steps.10.text":"Serve immediately, piping hot, in the pan, with {{khobz}} for mopping up the sauce.","steps.10.tip":"Choumicha serves her tomato and egg tagine with mint tea, as Cooking With Alia and Taste of Maroc do with their kefta tagines.","secrets.0.text":"In Morocco, this is the dish everyone knows how to cook. It is made for breakfast as well as for dinner.","secrets.0.source":"Lalla Moulati, article of 29 December 2018","secrets.1.text":"When they don't know what to make for dinner, many Moroccan women turn to bid w maticha. Moroccan TV series show how attached people are to it.","secrets.1.source":"Hespress, Fatima Zahra Zaim, 16 October 2012","secrets.2.text":"Students used to call it “BM”: a quick meal, cooked between classes, with hardly any measuring.","secrets.2.source":"Hespress, Latifa Baqa, 22 November 2012","secrets.3.text":"Use very ripe tomatoes.","secrets.3.source":"My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc; Cooking With Alia","secrets.4.text":"The cooking time for the tomatoes varies a lot with their ripeness, their juice and the pan: watch the sauce rather than the clock.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.5.text":"Grated tomatoes, skins discarded, cook a little faster than chopped tomatoes.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking With Alia (grated tomatoes)","secrets.6.text":"Make a small well for each egg and crack the egg in gently, without breaking the yolk.","secrets.6.source":"My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc","secrets.7.text":"With the lid on, allow 4 to 5 minutes for a set white and a still-runny yolk.","secrets.7.source":"My Moroccan Food (4 to 5 min); Cooking With Alia (5 min); Choumicha (3 to 4 min, until the white sets)","secrets.8.text":"Beldi (farm) eggs, if you can find them.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.9.text":"Serve piping hot, in the middle of the table: everyone dips into the dish with pieces of bread, with mint tea alongside.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Cooking With Alia","secrets.10.text":"In Morocco, chakchouka is often made with green pepper. Here there are no peppers: just tomatoes, garlic, spices and eggs.","secrets.10.source":"Wikipedia FR (Chakchouka, Morocco section: with green pepper); Cooking With Alia (chekchouka with green pepper); Choumicha (tomato and egg tagine, no peppers)","secrets.11.text":"Don't wash eggs before storing them: washing helps germs get into the egg. Always keep them at the same temperature.","secrets.11.source":"Anses, Salmonella spp. fact sheet (June 2021)","secrets.12.text":"Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours, in shallow containers. Eat them within 3 to 4 days, thoroughly reheated (74 °C).","secrets.12.source":"Anses (2 hours); FDA (3 to 4 days, 165 °F, i.e. 74 °C)","mistakes.0":"Cracking the eggs into tomatoes that are still raw: wait until they are soft (Choumicha; My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.1":"Breaking the yolks when cracking the eggs (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.2":"Serving a lightly cooked egg to a young child, a pregnant woman, or an elderly or vulnerable person (Anses; FDA).","mistakes.3":"Leaving leftovers out of the fridge for more than 2 hours (Anses; FDA).","mistakes.4":"Washing eggs before storing them (Anses).","variants.0":"Scrambled eggs: pour the beaten eggs over the tomatoes and stir until they are cooked (Lalla Moulati, 2014; Wikipedia FR, Chakchouka).","variants.1":"With onion: first fry a chopped onion in the oil, then add the tomatoes (Lalla Moulati, 2014; Wikipedia FR, which also mentions garlic instead of onion).","variants.2":"With khlii (preserved dried meat): 5 minutes before the eggs, add some khlii, fat removed and crumbled, along with the cumin (Choumicha: 180 g khlii for 4 large tomatoes and 6 eggs, serving 6 people). Use khlii made from halal meat.","variants.3":"Spicier: a pinch of cayenne pepper (My Moroccan Food) or chillies (Taste of Maroc).","variants.4":"Simmered sauce: grated tomatoes, skins discarded, cooked for 20 to 25 minutes over medium heat, stirring, until the sauce is thick (Cooking With Alia). The dish then takes more than 30 minutes.","variants.5":"With kefta meatballs, it becomes kefta tagine with eggs (My Moroccan Food; Cooking With Alia; Taste of Maroc); with green peppers, chakchouka (Wikipedia FR). These are two other recipes in the app.","variants.6":"In the oven, Lalla Moulati's way (2018): hollowed-out tomatoes filled with egg white mixed with onion, parsley and spices, then with the yolk and the rest of the mixture, and baked in the oven or cooked over embers."},"bissara":{"title":"Bissara, dried fava bean soup","darija":"Bissara (bayssar in the north)","tagline":"The fava bean purée-soup of the north: very simple in the pot, irresistible in the bowl with olive oil, cumin and chilli.","region":"All over Morocco, but a speciality of the north: Tangier, Tetouan, Chefchaouen and the Jbala country, where it is also called “bayssar”","occasion":"A winter dish, for breakfast, lunch or dinner; a very popular street food, served from early morning; sometimes at the Ramadan iftar","img_alt":"Bowls of bissara topped with olive oil and cumin, with olives and khobz","serving":"Piping hot, in a bowl for the soup or on a deep plate for the purée to scoop up with bread. Drizzle with olive oil and sprinkle with cumin and paprika; leave hot chilli, cumin, olive oil and lemon on the table; serve with khobz or barley bread (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Hespress; Sky News Arabia; Bladi.net). In the north, it comes with olives and onion, sometimes fried fish (Hespress 2011 and 2014; Taste of Maroc). It is eaten at breakfast, lunch or dinner (Hespress; My Moroccan Food; Taste of Maroc), especially when it is cold (Hespress; Le360; My Moroccan Food).","notes.0.title":"Fava beans and favism (G6PD deficiency)","notes.0.text":"In people with G6PD deficiency, fava beans can cause the destruction of red blood cells (haemolytic anaemia, known as favism), which is sometimes serious: do not serve this recipe to anyone affected (MedlinePlus, US National Library of Medicine; WHO).","ing.0.group":"Bissara","ing.feves.name":"dried fava beans, split and skinned","ing.feves.note":"without the skin. Favism: read the box above.","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"peeled, not cut","ing.eau.name":"cold water","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end","ing.eau-chaude.name":"hot water","ing.eau-chaude.note":"during cooking and for the texture, if needed","ing.1.group":"To serve","ing.huile-service.name":"extra virgin olive oil","ing.huile-service.note":"a generous drizzle per bowl","ing.cumin-service.name":"ground cumin","ing.paprika-service.name":"paprika","ing.piment.name":"hot chilli powder","ing.piment.note":"optional, on the table","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.note":"in wedges, optional","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"for mopping up","equipment.0":"Large heavy-based pot","equipment.1":"Spoon or small skimmer, to remove the foam","equipment.2":"Stick blender (or a food mill, or a fine sieve)","equipment.3":"Wooden spoon","equipment.4":"Ladle","equipment.5":"Bowls or deep plates","steps.0.title":"Sort and wash the beans","steps.0.text":"Spread {{feves}} split fava beans out on a tray. Remove any small stones and bits of brown skin. Wash them in water, then drain them.","steps.0.tip":"Split, skinned fava beans don't need soaking. If you soak them overnight, as Taste of Maroc does, the last bits of skin come off more easily.","steps.1.title":"Peel the garlic","steps.1.text":"Peel {{ail:full}}. Leave the garlic whole; don't cut it.","steps.1.tip":"Choumicha adds the garlic in its skin, then passes the purée through a fine sieve; Nargisse Benkabbou (My Moroccan Food) also cooks it in its skin, then peels it before crushing it. As you are blending here, peel it first.","steps.2.title":"Put the beans on to cook","steps.2.text":"Put the beans, the garlic and {{eau}} cold water in a large heavy-based pot. Bring to the boil over medium heat.","steps.3.title":"Skim off the foam","steps.3.text":"As soon as it boils, turn the heat down. With a spoon, skim off the white foam that rises to the surface, until there is hardly any left.","steps.3.tip":"Choumicha skims for 10 to 15 minutes.","steps.4.title":"Simmer for an hour","steps.4.text":"Half-cover the pot so that it doesn't boil over. Cook over low heat for 1 hour, until the beans are meltingly soft. Stir from time to time, scraping the bottom. If the level drops too much, add a little {{eau-chaude}}.","steps.4.timer.label":"Cooking the beans","steps.4.timer.done":"Crush a bean with the back of a spoon: it should crush with no effort.","steps.5.title":"Check that the beans are cooked","steps.5.text":"Crush a bean against the side of the pot with the back of a spoon: it should give way without any resistance. If it is still firm, add a little hot water and keep cooking, keeping an eye on it, until it is meltingly soft.","steps.6.title":"Blend to a smooth purée","steps.6.text":"Take the pot off the heat. Blend the beans and garlic with their cooking water using the stick blender, until you have a smooth, creamy purée.","steps.6.tip":"No blender? Pass them through a food mill or a fine sieve, as people used to do.","steps.7.title":"Season and add the oil","steps.7.text":"Put the pot back over low heat. Add {{cumin}} cumin, {{paprika}} paprika, {{sel}} salt and {{huile}} olive oil. Simmer gently for 5 minutes, stirring with a wooden spoon, until the oil is well blended into the purée.","steps.7.timer.label":"Seasoning","steps.7.timer.done":"The purée should be even and creamy. Now adjust the texture.","steps.8.title":"Adjust the texture","steps.8.text":"For a soup, add hot water a little at a time, stirring. For a purée to scoop up with bread, keep it thicker. Taste and adjust the salt, without overdoing it: the condiments will add flavour at the table.","steps.9.title":"Serve piping hot","steps.9.text":"Pour the piping hot bissara into bowls or deep plates. Drizzle each bowl generously with {{huile-service}}, then sprinkle with {{cumin-service}} and {{paprika-service}}.","steps.10.title":"Put the condiments on the table","steps.10.text":"Put {{piment}}, olive oil, cumin and {{citron}} wedges on the table: everyone spices up their own bowl to taste. Serve with {{khobz}} or barley bread.","secrets.0.text":"Use dried fava beans that are split and skinned, that is, without their skins.","secrets.0.source":"Choumicha (“split dried fava beans (without the skin)”); Bladi.net; Taste of Maroc; Hespress (2011)","secrets.1.text":"With split beans, soaking is not essential: many Moroccan recipes cook them straight after washing. If you soak them overnight, the last bits of brown skin come off easily.","secrets.1.source":"Without soaking: Choumicha, Lalla Moulati, Chhiwat, Morocco World News, Cooking with Alia (no. 858). With soaking: Taste of Maroc (which mentions the bits of skin), My Moroccan Food, Cooking with Alia (no. 132)","secrets.2.text":"At the start of cooking, skim off the white foam that rises to the surface.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha; Cooking with Alia; Hespress (2011)","secrets.3.text":"Half-cover the pot, or leave a small gap: fully covered, it boils over.","secrets.3.source":"Choumicha; Cooking with Alia (no. 132); Taste of Maroc also cooks it half-covered","secrets.4.text":"The beans must be meltingly soft before blending: a bean should crush with no effort. If not, add hot water and cook for longer.","secrets.4.source":"Choumicha (“until the beans become meltingly soft”); Morocco World News","secrets.5.text":"Add the spices and olive oil after blending, over low heat, and stir until the purée is smooth and creamy.","secrets.5.source":"Choumicha; Hespress (2011); Cooking with Alia","secrets.6.text":"In the pot, bissara stays simple. Its flavour comes mostly from what goes into the bowl: good olive oil, cumin, paprika and chilli.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha; Lalla Moulati (2020)","secrets.7.text":"Choose a good olive oil and, if you can, freshly ground cumin.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.8.text":"The water decides: thinner, bissara is eaten from a bowl with a spoon, as on the street; thicker, it is scooped up with bread.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia (no. 132); Bladi.net (“it must stay runny”)","secrets.9.text":"In the past, the beans were left to cook for hours, then passed through a sieve or a food mill. A blender is quicker and gives a smoother purée.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha still passes her purée through a fine sieve","secrets.10.text":"In the north, it is eaten with a few olives and pieces of onion. Lalla Moulati adds green chilli and fried chilli.","secrets.10.source":"Hespress (2014, a restaurant in Tangier); Lalla Moulati (2014); My Moroccan Food (chopped onion)","secrets.11.text":"To reheat, put the bissara over medium-low heat and stir often: it burns easily. Bring it to a good bubbling boil before serving.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc (medium-low heat, stir often); USDA (soups should be reheated to a rolling boil)","secrets.12.text":"Put leftovers in the fridge within 2 hours, in small closed containers so that they cool quickly, and eat them within 3 days.","secrets.12.source":"ANSES (2 hours, 3 days, small portions); USDA (2 hours, 3 to 4 days, small containers)","mistakes.0":"Using fresh or frozen fava beans: bissara is made with dried fava beans (Taste of Maroc; Hespress).","mistakes.1":"Covering the pot completely: the water boils over (Choumicha; Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.2":"Leaving it to cook without stirring or keeping an eye on the water: the purée sticks to the bottom (Morocco World News; Hespress; Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.3":"Blending before the beans are meltingly soft (Choumicha; Morocco World News).","mistakes.4":"Oversalting in the pot: the condiments add flavour at the table (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.5":"Reheating over high heat without stirring: the bissara burns (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.6":"Leaving leftovers at room temperature for more than 2 hours (ANSES; USDA).","variants.0":"Split pea bissara, or “bissara d'jelbana” (Hespress, 2011). Choumicha soaks the split peas for an hour, then cooks them with onion, garlic, parsley, coriander, ginger, turmeric and pepper. Taste of Maroc doesn't soak them and adds onion.","variants.1":"A mix of fava beans and split peas: My Moroccan Food; Cooking with Alia (no. 858: half and half, with chilli powder in the purée).","variants.2":"With vegetables: tomato, red pumpkin and carrot; in the north and the Rif, turnip and cabbage instead. A restaurant owner in Larache leaves them out, to please everyone (Hespress, 2011).","variants.3":"Argan oil instead of olive oil, in the Souss and the Atlas (Hespress, 2011; Sky News Arabia, 2021).","variants.4":"Garlic pounded with the salt in a mortar, added to the cooked beans just before blending (Lalla Moulati, 2014).","variants.5":"Garlic oil to serve: gently heat olive oil with grated garlic and a pinch of salt, without burning the garlic, then pour it over the bissara (Morocco World News).","variants.6":"Beans dry-toasted for a few minutes in a hot frying pan before cooking, and a tomato blended into the purée (Lalla Moulati, 2018).","variants.7":"Fava bean cream served with a salad of tomatoes, grilled pepper, onion and green olives, dressed with lemon and cumin (Choumicha)."},"cake-marocain":{"title":"Moroccan milk cake, a teatime meskouta","darija":"meskouta; also called lkik or kika","tagline":"The simple home-made cake: well-whisked eggs, oil, milk and lemon zest, to cut into slices for afternoon tea.","region":"Morocco; according to Wikipedia (in Arabic), the “economical” meskouta is very widely eaten there","occasion":"Afternoon snack with tea, breakfast, Ramadan iftar","img_alt":"Slices of Moroccan cake (meskouta) on a plate","serving":"In slices, with mint tea (Morocco World News; English Wikipedia, which also mentions coffee), as an afternoon snack or at breakfast (Taste of Maroc; English Wikipedia), and also at the Ramadan iftar (Taste of Maroc). It is very good plain. To decorate it: icing sugar (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha), or jam and desiccated coconut or flaked almonds (Morocco World News; Taste of Maroc), or a little honey and coconut (Lalla Moulati). Choumicha counts this cake as serving 10 people. Well covered, it keeps for a few days at room temperature and for several months in the freezer (Taste of Maroc, the only source).","ing.0.group":"Batter","ing.oeufs.name":"large egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"large eggs","ing.sucre.name":"caster sugar","ing.sucre-vanille.name":"sachet of vanilla sugar","ing.sucre-vanille.pl":"sachets of vanilla sugar","ing.sucre-vanille.note":"alcohol-free: it replaces vanilla extract","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.huile.name":"neutral oil (sunflower or vegetable oil)","ing.lait.name":"milk","ing.farine.name":"plain flour","ing.levure-chimique.name":"baking powder","ing.levure-chimique.note":"3 sachets of 7 g per cake, i.e. 21 g (the weight shown is rounded to the nearest 5 g)","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.pl":"lemons","ing.citron.note":"for the grated zest and the juice","ing.1.group":"For the tin","ing.beurre-moule.name":"A little butter","ing.beurre-moule.pl":"A little butter","ing.farine-moule.name":"A little flour","ing.farine-moule.pl":"A little flour","equipment.0":"An oven","equipment.1":"A 28 cm loaf tin (or a ring tin, kugelhopf type)","equipment.2":"An electric hand mixer or a whisk","equipment.3":"A large mixing bowl and a bowl","equipment.4":"A sieve","equipment.5":"A spatula","equipment.6":"A fine grater and a citrus juicer","equipment.7":"A wooden skewer","equipment.8":"A wire rack","steps.0.title":"Preheat the oven","steps.0.text":"Preheat the oven to 180 °C.","steps.1.title":"Butter and flour the tin","steps.1.text":"Butter the loaf tin, then dust it with flour.","steps.2.title":"Prepare the lemon","steps.2.text":"Wash {{citron:full}}, finely grate the zest, then squeeze the juice. Always grate the zest before squeezing the fruit.","steps.3.title":"Sift the flour and baking powder","steps.3.text":"Mix {{farine}} flour with {{levure-chimique}} baking powder, then sift them together.","steps.4.title":"Whisk the eggs and sugar","steps.4.text":"In a large mixing bowl, whisk {{oeufs:full}} with {{sucre}} sugar, {{sucre-vanille:full}} and {{sel}} salt until the mixture turns pale and doubles in volume.","steps.4.tip":"Take your time: Choumicha whisks until the mixture doubles in volume, Taste of Maroc until it is pale and thick.","steps.5.title":"Drizzle in the oil and milk","steps.5.text":"Whisking all the time, pour in {{huile}} oil in a thin stream, then {{lait}} milk. The mixture should stay light and frothy.","steps.6.title":"Fold in the flour","steps.6.text":"Add the flour and baking powder mixture a little at a time, stirring gently with a spatula, just until no flour is visible.","steps.7.title":"Add the lemon","steps.7.text":"Add the lemon zest and juice, and mix just enough to spread them through.","steps.8.title":"Pour into the tin","steps.8.text":"Pour the batter into the buttered and floured tin.","steps.9.title":"Bake for 45 minutes","steps.9.text":"Put it in the oven and bake for about 45 minutes at 180 °C. Don't open the oven during the first half hour.","steps.9.tip":"In a ring tin, Taste of Maroc allows about 40 minutes for her meskouta: adjust the time to your tin and do the skewer test.","steps.9.timer.label":"Baking the cake","steps.9.timer.done":"Insert a skewer into the centre: if it comes out dry, the cake is done.","steps.10.title":"Check that it is done","steps.10.text":"The cake is done when its edges come away from the tin and a skewer inserted into the centre comes out dry. If not, give it a few more minutes.","steps.11.title":"Cool slightly, then turn out","steps.11.text":"Take the cake out and leave it to cool for about 10 minutes in the tin. Turn it out carefully, then leave it to cool on a wire rack.","steps.12.title":"Decorate and serve","steps.12.text":"Serve it in slices, with mint tea. You can dust it with icing sugar, or spread it with jam and sprinkle it with desiccated coconut or flaked almonds.","secrets.0.text":"Whisk the eggs and sugar for a long time, until the mixture turns pale and doubles in volume.","secrets.0.source":"Choumicha (two economical cake recipes); Taste of Maroc (“pale and thick”)","secrets.1.text":"Pour the oil in a thin stream while whisking constantly: the mixture should stay light and airy.","secrets.1.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc","secrets.2.text":"Sift the flour with the baking powder, then fold it in gently, a little at a time, with a spatula.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha; Cooking with Alia; Taste of Maroc (“stir lightly”)","secrets.3.text":"As much oil as liquid, as here (15 cl of each in Choumicha's recipe): this is also the ratio used by Taste of Maroc, Morocco World News and two of Lalla Moulati's cakes. Other recipes use less oil than liquid (Cooking with Alia; Lalla Moulati) or more (Choumicha, chocolate chip cake).","secrets.3.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News; Lalla Moulati","secrets.4.text":"Wash and zest the citrus fruit before squeezing it.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha (grated zest, then juice)","secrets.5.text":"No vanilla extract containing alcohol: a sachet of vanilla sugar does the same job, and it is the traditional measure.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc (1 sachet of vanilla sugar in the traditional measures); Morocco World News (8 g vanilla sugar); Choumicha (1 sachet in the chocolate chip cake)","secrets.6.text":"Butter and flour the tin, and bake in an oven fully preheated to 180 °C.","secrets.6.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News; Cooking with Alia (180 °C)","secrets.7.text":"Don't open the oven before the cake has baked for half an hour.","secrets.7.source":"Morocco World News (Layla Dahamou)","secrets.8.text":"Leave to cool slightly before turning out, then finish cooling on a wire rack.","secrets.8.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc (7 to 10 minutes); Morocco World News","secrets.9.text":"The most common tin for meskouta is the ring tin (kugelhopf type); some cooks prefer a loaf tin: in that case, adjust the baking time to your tin.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News (tube tin or round tin); Choumicha (28 cm loaf tin)","secrets.10.text":"The Moroccan way is to measure with a tea glass and a bowl, or with the yoghurt pot.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News; Cooking with Alia","secrets.11.text":"For an even lighter cake, some cooks whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks separately; Taste of Maroc considers this unnecessary.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia (whisked egg whites)","secrets.12.text":"Don't overdo the baking powder: Taste of Maroc reduced her amount after reading, on other sites, worries about a bitter taste or a centre that rises too fast and then sinks; she herself never had this problem.","secrets.12.source":"Taste of Maroc","mistakes.0":"Stopping whisking before the egg and sugar mixture has turned pale and doubled in volume (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.1":"Pouring the oil in all at once instead of in a thin stream (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.2":"Working the batter hard once the flour is in: mix gently (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.3":"Putting the cake into an oven that hasn't been preheated (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.4":"Forgetting to butter and flour the tin (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News).","mistakes.5":"Opening the oven during the first half hour (Morocco World News).","mistakes.6":"Turning the cake out while it is still piping hot: let it cool slightly (Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News).","mistakes.7":"Using vanilla extract that contains alcohol: vanilla sugar replaces it (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News).","variants.0":"Yoghurt meskouta (Morocco World News, Layla Dahamou): 4 eggs; 1 pot of vanilla or banana yoghurt (110 g), used as the measure; 1½ pots of sugar; 1 pot of oil; 3 pots of flour; 16 g baking powder (2 sachets); 1 sachet of vanilla sugar (8 g). Ring tin or round tin, buttered and floured; 45 minutes at most, in a moderate oven (no temperature given), without opening before 30 minutes.","variants.1":"Yoghurt meskouta with whisked egg whites (Cooking with Alia): 3 eggs, separated; 1 pot of flavoured yoghurt (114 g); 1½ pots of sugar; ½ pot of oil; 2½ pots of flour; 1 tsp baking powder; vanilla (use vanilla sugar); 1 pinch of salt; ground ginger, optional. 20 cm tin, buttered and lined with baking paper; 180 °C, about 50 minutes.","variants.2":"Orange meskouta (Taste of Maroc): ½ cup fresh orange juice and 2 tsp zest; 4 large eggs; 1½ cups sugar; ½ cup oil; 2 cups flour; 3 tsp baking powder; ½ tsp salt; vanilla (1 sachet of vanilla sugar in the traditional version). Ring tin, buttered and floured; 180 °C, about 40 minutes.","variants.3":"Traditional measures for orange meskouta (Taste of Maroc): 1 tea glass of fresh orange juice, the zest of 1 or 2 oranges, 4 large eggs, 1 level bowl of sugar, 1 tea glass of oil, 1 heaped bowl of flour, 2 sachets of baking powder (7 g each), 1 pinch of salt, 1 sachet of vanilla sugar. For the tea glass, Taste of Maroc gives “about 6 to 8 ounces of liquid (or 1/3 to 1/2 cup)”, i.e. 177 to 237 ml or 79 to 118 ml (calculated): these two values don't match, and only the second fits her recipe in cups (½ cup of juice and ½ cup of oil where the traditional measures use 1 tea glass). The bowl holds about 1½ cups (355 ml, calculated).","variants.4":"Measured by the glass, marbled (Lalla Moulati): 3 eggs, 1 glass of sugar, 1 glass of oil, 1 glass of milk, just under 3 glasses of flour, 3 sachets of baking powder, vanilla and a few drops of vinegar; part of the batter is mixed with 1 spoonful of cocoa and poured into the middle; buttered and floured tin; topped with honey and coconut straight out of the oven.","variants.5":"With chocolate chips (Choumicha): 4 eggs, 180 g sugar, 1 sachet of vanilla sugar, ½ tsp salt, 18 cl oil, 12 cl milk, 350 g flour, 14 g baking powder and 80 g chocolate chips; buttered and floured loaf tin; 180 °C, 35 to 40 minutes; icing sugar.","variants.6":"Orange cake with butter (Choumicha): 160 g butter, 200 g sugar, ½ tsp salt, 4 eggs, the zest and juice of 3 oranges, 2 tbsp orange blossom water, 240 g flour and 1 sachet (7 g) of baking powder, poured over orange slices candied in sugar and orange blossom water; 180 °C, 40 minutes. Choumicha recommends adding ½ tsp cinnamon.","variants.7":"Small “7 spoons” cake (Lalla Moulati): 3 eggs, 7 spoonfuls of sugar, 7 of oil, 7 of milk and 7 of flour, a little salt, 1 sachet of vanilla and 16 g baking powder.","variants.8":"With only 2 eggs (Lalla Moulati): 2 eggs, 1 glass of lukewarm milk, ½ glass of oil, 2 glasses of flour, 1 glass of sugar, 16 g baking powder and vanilla sugar."},"chakchouka":{"title":"Moroccan-style chakchouka","darija":"Chakchouka","tagline":"Roasted green peppers and tomatoes simmered with garlic, cumin and paprika, with eggs cooked on top. It is eaten piping hot, in the pan, with bread.","region":"The whole Maghreb. In Morocco, the name refers to the pan-cooked mix of green peppers and tomatoes with garlic, eaten on its own as a cooked salad or hot with eggs (Wikipedia FR, Morocco section; Yabiladi forum).","occasion":"Breakfast, dinner or a simple meal (quick with raw peppers); Rachida also says it is very popular during Ramadan. Without eggs, it is served as a cooked salad.","img_alt":"Eggs cooked in a cast-iron pan on peppers and tomatoes, with parsley","serving":"Serve the chakchouka with eggs piping hot, in the pan, as soon as it is cooked, with bread for mopping up (Lalla Moulati 2019; Rachida). It is eaten at breakfast (Lalla Moulati 2019; Rachida) or as a main course (Rachida). Without eggs, serve it warm or cold, as a cooked salad, like a taktouka (Wikipedia FR; chef Brahim Sakroune in Femmes du Maroc).","ing.0.group":"Vegetables","ing.poivrons.name":"green pepper","ing.poivrons.pl":"green peppers","ing.poivrons.note":"to be roasted whole","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"to be grated","ing.oignon.name":"onion","ing.oignon.pl":"onions","ing.oignon.note":"to be chopped","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"to be chopped","ing.1.group":"Cooking and spices","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust to taste","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.2.group":"Eggs and serving","ing.oeufs.name":"egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"eggs","ing.oeufs.note":"one per person","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"or coriander","ing.huile-service.name":"olive oil","ing.huile-service.note":"a drizzle, to serve","ing.khobz.name":"bread (khobz)","ing.khobz.note":"for mopping up","equipment.0":"Gas hob (otherwise, an oven with a grill setting and a baking tray)","equipment.1":"Clean plastic bag (a freezer bag, for example)","equipment.2":"Large deep frying pan with a lid","equipment.3":"Grater","equipment.4":"Chopping board and knife","equipment.5":"Wooden spoon","steps.0.title":"Roast the peppers","steps.0.text":"Roast {{poivrons:full}} directly over the gas flame, turning often, until the skin is blackened on all sides. If you don't have gas, use the oven grill, on a baking tray, turning regularly.","steps.0.tip":"Rachida also roasts them in the oven, on the grill setting, at 200 °C.","steps.1.title":"Seal them in a bag","steps.1.text":"Slip the still-hot peppers into a plastic bag, close it and wait 15 minutes. The steam loosens the skin.","steps.1.timer.label":"Peppers in the bag","steps.1.timer.done":"The peppers have cooled a little: you can peel them.","steps.2.title":"Peel and cut the peppers","steps.2.text":"Pull off the charred skin with your fingers, without rinsing the peppers under the tap. Remove the stalk and seeds, then cut the flesh into small dice.","steps.2.tip":"Rinse your hands rather than the peppers: that way they keep their smoky flavour.","steps.3.title":"Prepare the tomatoes, onion, garlic and parsley","steps.3.text":"Cut {{tomates}} tomatoes in half and grate the flesh; throw away the skin. Chop {{oignon:full}} and {{ail:full}}. Wash and chop the parsley.","steps.3.tip":"You can also peel and deseed the tomatoes and cut them into small dice, as Rachida does.","steps.3.img.alt":"Grated tomato on a grater","steps.3.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.4.title":"Soften the onion","steps.4.text":"In a large deep frying pan, heat {{huile}} olive oil over low heat. Add the onion and stir until it turns translucent.","steps.5.title":"Add the garlic and peppers","steps.5.text":"Add the garlic and stir for a few seconds, without letting it brown. Add the diced peppers and stir for 1 minute.","steps.6.title":"Season","steps.6.text":"Add {{sel}} salt, {{paprika}} paprika, {{cumin}} cumin and {{poivre}} of black pepper. Mix well.","steps.7.title":"Simmer the tomatoes","steps.7.text":"Add the grated tomatoes and mix. Simmer over low heat, stirring often so that nothing sticks, until you have a thick, jammy mixture with almost no liquid. Taste and adjust the salt.","steps.8.title":"Crack in the eggs","steps.8.text":"Make a small well in the sauce for each egg. Crack {{oeufs:full}} into the sauce, one in each well, as for fried eggs.","steps.9.title":"Cover and leave to set","steps.9.text":"Cover the pan and cook over low heat for about 5 minutes, until the whites are fully set. For a well-cooked yolk, leave it 2 to 3 minutes longer, until it is firm.","steps.9.tip":"The FDA advises cooking eggs until both the yolk and the white are firm. Always do so for children, elderly people, pregnant women and vulnerable people: they are the most at risk of a serious salmonella infection (FDA).","steps.9.timer.label":"Eggs, covered","steps.9.timer.done":"Are the whites fully set? For a firm yolk, allow another 2 to 3 minutes.","steps.10.title":"Serve straight away","steps.10.text":"Take off the heat. Scatter over {{persil}} chopped parsley and drizzle with a little {{huile-service}}. Serve immediately in the pan, with {{khobz}} for mopping up.","secrets.0.text":"In Morocco, roasted peppers are preferred, for their smoky flavour. In a hurry? Dice them raw and soften them with the onion: that works too.","secrets.0.source":"Rachida (Gâteau et cuisine Rachida); Yabiladi forum (2006) for both methods; Lalla Moulati (2019) for raw peppers","secrets.1.text":"Seal the roasted peppers in a closed bag while they are still hot: the steam loosens the skin, which then comes off very easily.","secrets.1.source":"Rachida (15 min); Cooking With Alia (15 to 20 min); Lalla Moulati (taktouka, 2014 and 2019); MarocMama (her mother-in-law's method)","secrets.2.text":"Don't rinse the roasted peppers under the tap: peel them dry to keep the smoky flavour, and rinse your hands instead.","secrets.2.source":"Chef Brahim Sakroune in Femmes du Maroc (2023); Lalla Moulati (taktouka, 2014 and 2019)","secrets.3.text":"Cut the tomatoes in half and grate the flesh, throwing away the skin: it's quick, and the sauce has no bits of skin.","secrets.3.source":"Lalla Moulati (grated tomatoes, 2019); Cooking With Alia (taktouka: flesh grated, skin discarded)","secrets.4.text":"The garlic must not brown: a few seconds in the oil are enough to flavour it.","secrets.4.source":"Rachida","secrets.5.text":"Before the eggs go in, the sauce must cook down to a thick, jammy mixture. Stir often so that it doesn't stick.","secrets.5.source":"Rachida; Lalla Moulati (2019: let the mixture thicken)","secrets.6.text":"Crack each egg into a small well in the sauce: it cooks on the surface, like a fried egg.","secrets.6.source":"Rachida; Wikipedia FR (Morocco section: fried eggs cooked on the surface)","secrets.7.text":"The lid cooks the top of the eggs: about 5 minutes for a still-runny yolk, a little longer for a firm yolk.","secrets.7.source":"MarocMama (5 min covered, runny yolk); Rachida (cover for a well-cooked yolk); Lalla Moulati (2019: about 8 min covered)","secrets.8.text":"Serve in the pan, piping hot, and eat it with your hands and bread, the Moroccan way.","secrets.8.source":"Rachida; Lalla Moulati (2019: serve straight away)","secrets.9.text":"Don't confuse it with taktouka: it is the same pepper and tomato pairing, but as a cooked salad, without eggs, served warm or cold. For My Moroccan Food, they are two different dishes.","secrets.9.source":"My Moroccan Food (2016); Cooking With Alia; Choumicha (Tektouka marqa); Lalla Moulati (taktouka, 2014 and 2019); MarocMama; Rachida","secrets.10.text":"The everyday Moroccan dish of eggs and tomato has another name: “bid w maticha” (eggs and tomatoes).","secrets.10.source":"Wikipedia EN (Shakshouka); Lalla Moulati (2018: “البيض ومطيشة”, which “everyone knows how to cook”); Hespress (2012: many Moroccan women turn to it for dinner); Al-Quds al-Arabi (2023: for Moroccans, the simplest, best-known and cheapest meal)","secrets.11.text":"Tomatoes not sweet enough? My Moroccan Food adds half a teaspoon of sugar to her taktouka, and leaves it out when the tomatoes are nice and sweet, as they are in Morocco.","secrets.11.source":"My Moroccan Food (taktouka, 2016)","secrets.12.text":"Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours, in a shallow container, to be eaten within 3 days (the FDA allows up to 3 to 4 days). Reheat them to 74 °C (165 °F).","secrets.12.source":"FDA, “What You Need to Know About Egg Safety”; Anses, “Eviter les toxi-infections alimentaires en confinement : les bonnes pratiques” (8 April 2020)","mistakes.0":"Rinsing the roasted peppers under the tap: they lose their smoky flavour (chef Brahim Sakroune, Femmes du Maroc; Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.1":"Cracking the eggs into a sauce that is still runny: wait until it is thick (Rachida; Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.2":"Letting the sauce stick: stir often, over low heat (Rachida).","mistakes.3":"Serving a runny yolk to a child, an elderly person, a pregnant woman or a vulnerable person: cook the yolk and the white until they are firm (FDA).","mistakes.4":"Leaving leftovers out of the fridge for more than 2 hours (FDA).","variants.0":"Without eggs, as a cooked salad: stop once the mixture is thick and jammy, and serve warm or cold. In Morocco, some people then also call it taktouka (Yabiladi forum, 2006: “chekchouka or taktouka”; “cooked salad” according to Wikipedia FR; “warm chakchouka salad” by chef Brahim Sakroune, Femmes du Maroc, 2023).","variants.1":"Raw peppers, quicker: cut them into small dice and soften them with the onion until tender, then add the garlic and spices (Lalla Moulati, 2019; Yabiladi forum, 2006).","variants.2":"Scrambled eggs: pour the beaten eggs over the sauce and stir until they are cooked (Lalla Moulati, 2014, with just onion and tomatoes; Rachida also mentions lightly scrambled eggs).","variants.3":"With tomato purée: Lalla Moulati (2019) uses 2 tbsp for 400 g grated tomatoes.","variants.4":"Mixed peppers: red, green and yellow together (Rachida; chef Brahim Sakroune). Choumicha uses one green pepper and one red one in her tektouka.","variants.5":"Spicier: a chilli, piment d'Espelette or harissa (Rachida); a pinch of cayenne pepper (My Moroccan Food, taktouka).","variants.6":"Without onion: just peppers, tomatoes, garlic and spices, the way several Yabiladi forum users (2006; others add onion) and chef Brahim Sakroune (2023) make it; Rachida also suggests trying it without onion."},"couscous-sept-legumes":{"title":"Seven-vegetable couscous","darija":"Kesksou b sbe3 khdari","tagline":"The Friday lunchtime couscous: light grains, rolled by hand and steamed three times over a fragrant broth with seven vegetables.","region":"Casablanca (“bidaoui” couscous); the Friday couscous all over Morocco","occasion":"Friday lunch, after the main Friday prayer, with the family around a shared dish","serving":"Set the gsaa in the middle of the table: everyone eats together from the dish, with a spoon or by hand, rolling little balls, so have spoons ready for those who aren't used to it. Serve the rest of the broth in bowls and, if you like, a hot sauce on the side. To drink, the traditional lben (fermented milk).","ing.0.group":"Meat and broth","ing.viande.name":"halal lamb or beef (shoulder, neck or shank), on the bone, in large pieces","ing.huile.name":"olive oil or neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.oignon.name":"large onion (about 200 g)","ing.oignon.pl":"large onions (about 200 g each)","ing.tomates-rapees.name":"ripe tomato","ing.tomates-rapees.pl":"ripe tomatoes","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"taste and adjust at the end of cooking","ing.safran.name":"saffron threads","ing.safran.note":"optional","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.pois-chiches.name":"dried chickpeas","ing.pois-chiches.note":"to be soaked the day before","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.1.group":"The seven vegetables","ing.chou.name":"round cabbage (white or green)","ing.petits-oignons.name":"small onion","ing.petits-oignons.pl":"small onions","ing.tomates-quartiers.name":"small tomato","ing.tomates-quartiers.pl":"small tomatoes","ing.carottes.name":"carrots","ing.navets.name":"medium turnips","ing.courge.name":"red squash (pumpkin)","ing.courgettes.name":"small courgettes","ing.piments.name":"green chilli","ing.piments.pl":"green chillies","ing.piments.note":"optional","ing.2.group":"Steamed couscous","ing.semoule.name":"medium-grain durum wheat couscous","ing.semoule.note":"not instant couscous","ing.huile-graine.name":"olive oil or neutral oil","ing.eau-1.name":"cold water (1st rolling)","ing.eau-2.name":"water (2nd rolling)","ing.sel-graine.name":"fine salt","ing.eau-3.name":"water at most (3rd rolling)","ing.eau-3.note":"only add as much as you need","ing.3.group":"Finishing","ing.beurre.name":"softened butter","ing.bouillon-graine.name":"broth taken from the pot","ing.smen.name":"smen (aged salted butter)","ing.smen.note":"optional","equipment.0":"Couscoussier (keskas), the couscous steamer: pot and steamer basket","equipment.1":"Gsaa (a large, wide, deep dish) or a very large mixing bowl","equipment.2":"Cling film (or aluminium foil) to seal the join","equipment.3":"Kitchen string for the bunch of herbs","equipment.4":"Small saucepan for the chillies","equipment.5":"Clean cloth (optional, for the chickpeas)","equipment.6":"Ladle","steps.0.title":"The day before: soak the chickpeas","steps.0.text":"The evening before, put {{pois-chiches}} dried chickpeas to soak in plenty of water. The next day, drain them. If you like, tie them up in a clean cloth, like a little bundle, before putting them in the pot.","steps.1.title":"Prepare the broth base","steps.1.text":"Roughly chop {{oignon:full}}. Grate {{tomates-rapees:full}} and throw away the skin. Tie {{persil}} of parsley and {{coriandre}} of coriander together into a bouquet.","steps.1.img.alt":"Grated tomato on a grater","steps.1.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.2.title":"Prepare the seven vegetables","steps.2.text":"Peel {{carottes}} carrots and {{navets}} turnips; cut the turnips in half and split the large carrots lengthways. Cut {{chou}} cabbage into wedges through the core, and {{courge}} squash into large chunks. Just trim the ends of {{courgettes}} courgettes. Peel, deseed and quarter {{tomates-quartiers:full}}, and cut {{petits-oignons:full}} in half.","steps.3.title":"Brown the meat","steps.3.text":"In the pot of the couscoussier, put {{viande}} meat, {{huile}} oil, the chopped onion and the grated tomatoes. Add {{gingembre}} ground ginger, {{curcuma}} turmeric, {{poivre}} pepper, {{sel}} salt and, if you like, {{safran}} of saffron. Cook uncovered over medium-high heat for 10 to 15 min, stirring often, until you have a thick, rich sauce.","steps.3.timer.label":"Meat and spices","steps.3.timer.done":"Is the sauce thick? Add the chickpeas, the bunch of herbs and the water.","steps.4.title":"Make the broth","steps.4.text":"Add the drained chickpeas (or their bundle), the bunch of herbs and {{eau}} water. Bring to the boil, cover and cook for 30 min over medium heat. Meanwhile, prepare the couscous.","steps.4.timer.label":"Broth, from when it boils","steps.4.timer.done":"The broth is ready: put the couscous on for the first steaming.","steps.5.title":"Roll the grains by hand (first rolling)","steps.5.text":"Tip {{semoule}} couscous into the gsaa and drizzle with {{huile-graine}} oil. Rub the grains between your palms for 1 to 2 min to coat them well. Sprinkle over {{eau-1}} cold water, then rub again until it is absorbed, breaking up any small lumps.","steps.6.title":"Steam the grains (first steaming)","steps.6.text":"Lightly oil the basket and lay a long, folded strip of cling film over the rim of the pot before setting the basket on it: the steam must only pass through the grains. Tip the couscous into the basket without packing it down, and don't cover it. Keep the broth boiling, over medium heat. Start the timer when the steam comes through the grains.","steps.6.timer.label":"1st steaming","steps.6.timer.done":"Tip the couscous out into the gsaa for the second rolling.","steps.6.img.alt":"Couscous grains in the top of a couscoussier","steps.6.img.caption":"The grains steaming","steps.7.title":"Roll the grains again (second rolling)","steps.7.text":"Tip the couscous out into the gsaa, let it cool a little and break it up. Sprinkle with half of the {{eau-2}} of water and rub, then sprinkle over {{sel-graine}} fine salt. Pour in the rest of the water and rub until there are no lumps left.","steps.8.title":"Add the cabbage, then steam again","steps.8.text":"Put the cabbage, small onions and tomato quarters into the pot. Put the couscous back in the basket, without packing it down or covering it. Start the timer when the steam comes through the grains.","steps.8.timer.label":"2nd steaming","steps.8.timer.done":"Tip out the couscous and drop the carrots and turnips into the broth.","steps.9.title":"Add the carrots and turnips, then roll a third time","steps.9.text":"Tip the couscous out into the gsaa. Drop the carrots and turnips into the broth, cover and cook over medium heat. Meanwhile, moisten the couscous little by little with no more than {{eau-3}} water, rubbing after each addition: use only as much as you need for al dente grains. Taste them and adjust the salt.","steps.9.timer.label":"Carrots and turnips","steps.9.timer.done":"Add the squash and courgettes to the pot.","steps.10.title":"Add the squash and courgettes, then season","steps.10.text":"Add the squash and courgettes to the pot; if the broth no longer covers the vegetables, pour in a little hot water. Taste: the broth should be full of flavour, slightly salty and peppery. Cook {{piments:full}} separately, in a small saucepan with a little broth, until tender.","steps.11.title":"Steam a third time","steps.11.text":"Put half the couscous in the basket, without packing it down, and set the basket back on the pot. As soon as the steam comes through, add the rest. When the steam comes through all the grains again, start the timer: at the end, the grains should be light and tender with a slight bite, and the vegetables tender when pierced with the tip of a knife.","steps.11.timer.label":"3rd steaming","steps.11.timer.done":"Taste the grains: light, tender, with a slight bite? They are ready.","steps.12.title":"Check the meat","steps.12.text":"The meat should be very tender. If not, take out the vegetables that are already cooked, cover and cook the meat for longer, then put the vegetables back to warm through. Remove the bunch of herbs and, if you like, stir {{smen}} smen into the broth.","steps.13.title":"Work butter and broth into the grains","steps.13.text":"Tip the couscous out into the gsaa and work in {{beurre}} softened butter, rolling the grains between your hands. Sprinkle with {{bouillon-graine}} broth and rub again to break up any lumps.","steps.14.title":"Arrange and serve","steps.14.text":"Shape the couscous into a flattened dome and make a well in the centre. Put the meat in the well, the cabbage and squash on top, and arrange the other vegetables all around. Scatter over the chickpeas, add the chillies, spoon over a few ladlefuls of broth and serve the rest piping hot in bowls.","steps.14.img.alt":"A hand rolling a ball of couscous over the dish","steps.14.img.caption":"By hand, you roll little balls","secrets.0.text":"Start by cooking the meat, onion, tomatoes, oil and spices uncovered over medium-high heat for 10 to 15 min, until you have a thick sauce, before pouring in the water.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe","secrets.1.text":"Keep the meat in large pieces (on the bone at Taste of Maroc): according to Nargisse Benkabbou, small pieces shrink during the hours of cooking and get lost in the broth.","secrets.1.source":"My Moroccan Food, Nargisse Benkabbou; Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe","secrets.2.text":"Parsley and coriander cook in the broth tied in a bunch; Nargisse Benkabbou removes hers (coriander only) before adding the first vegetables, Cooking with Alia before serving.","secrets.2.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe; Couscous Dari; Cooking with Alia; My Moroccan Food, Nargisse Benkabbou","secrets.3.text":"Dari's tip: the chickpeas cook in the pot tied up in a cloth bundle.","secrets.3.source":"Couscous Dari","secrets.4.text":"Oil first, then water: rub the oiled grains between your palms, then moisten them little by little, breaking up every lump.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc, steaming guide; Royal Mansour, chef Karim Benbaba","secrets.5.text":"An airtight seal: all the steam must pass through the grains. Folded cling film or foil today; in the old days, a strip of cloth coated with a thin paste of flour and water.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc, steaming guide; Morocco's Ministry of Culture, couscous inventory sheet; Bladi.net","secrets.6.text":"Never pack the grains down in the basket and don't cover them: most Moroccans leave them uncovered.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc, steaming guide","secrets.7.text":"Steaming time is counted from the moment the steam comes through the grains, not before.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc, steaming guide and bidaoui couscous recipe; Bladi.net","secrets.8.text":"The salt for the grains goes in at the second rolling, between two sprinklings of water.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc, steaming guide","secrets.9.text":"At the last rolling, moisten little by little and stop in time: the finished grains should be light and tender, with a slight bite.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc, steaming guide","secrets.10.text":"The last steaming is done in two stages: half the grains first, the rest once the steam comes through.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe","secrets.11.text":"The vegetables go in according to how firm they are, so that they are all ready together: squash and courgettes last, because once cooked they become very fragile.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe; Couscous Dari; My Moroccan Food, Nargisse Benkabbou","secrets.12.text":"The vegetables cook submerged: if they stay on the surface of the broth, they cook unevenly.","secrets.12.source":"My Moroccan Food, Nargisse Benkabbou","secrets.13.text":"The broth should be full of flavour, slightly salty and peppery.","secrets.13.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe","secrets.14.text":"Finishing: butter in the grains and smen in the broth; Dari and Nargisse Benkabbou put the smen straight onto the grains.","secrets.14.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe; Couscous Dari; My Moroccan Food, Nargisse Benkabbou","secrets.15.text":"Moisten the grains with broth before arranging the dish, and serve the rest separately, piping hot.","secrets.15.source":"Taste of Maroc, bidaoui couscous recipe; Royal Mansour, chef Karim Benbaba; Bladi.net","mistakes.0":"Preparing the grains with boiling water like instant couscous: in Morocco, they are steamed, several times over.","mistakes.1":"Packing the couscous down in the basket or putting a lid on it.","mistakes.2":"Letting steam escape between the pot and the basket.","mistakes.3":"Letting the bottom of the basket touch the broth.","mistakes.4":"Starting the timer before the steam comes through the grains.","mistakes.5":"Drowning the grains at the last rolling: too much liquid makes them heavy and soggy.","mistakes.6":"Skipping the lump-breaking step between steamings.","mistakes.7":"Putting all the vegetables in at the same time: squash and courgettes, very fragile once cooked, fall apart.","mistakes.8":"Cutting the meat into small pieces: they shrink and get lost in the broth.","mistakes.9":"Leaving vegetables sticking out of the broth: they cook unevenly.","variants.0":"Tfaya (a variation, not the basic recipe): onions, preferably red, and raisins soaked for 15 min, simmered covered over medium-low heat for 30 min to 1 h with butter, honey, cinnamon, pepper, ginger, turmeric, saffron, a pinch of salt and a little water, until the juices become a thick syrup; a little orange blossom water at the end if you like. Spoon it over the couscous or serve it separately (Taste of Maroc; a honey and cinnamon version at My Moroccan Food).","variants.1":"Grains with milk: replace the water of the third rolling with milk for richer couscous (Taste of Maroc).","variants.2":"Seasonal vegetables: fava beans with the cabbage, sweet potato with the squash (Taste of Maroc); aubergine at Royal Mansour, Dari and Le Petit Journal, which also mentions celery and fava beans in summer.","variants.3":"Smen on the hot grains instead of in the broth (Couscous Dari; My Moroccan Food).","variants.4":"A second bunch of parsley and coriander added with the tender vegetables (Couscous Dari).","variants.5":"Gueddid (dried meat) instead of fresh meat (Taste of Maroc).","variants.6":"Without meat: a little more oil makes a richer broth (Taste of Maroc).","variants.7":"Only two steamings: some Moroccans do it this way (Taste of Maroc), and Choumicha steams the grains twice for 20 min in her recipe for Gulf News.","variants.8":"Luxury hotel version by chef Karim Benbaba (Royal Mansour): each vegetable cooked separately in a strained vegetable stock, then arranged in alternating colours."},"dchicha":{"title":"Dchicha, barley and tomato soup","darija":"Dchicha (h'ssoua hamra)","tagline":"Cracked barley simmered with tomatoes, onion and parsley: a velvety, filling soup for breakfast, dinner or iftar.","region":"In many Moroccan homes; dchicha is also part of the iftar in the southern provinces","occasion":"Breakfast or a light dinner; at the Ramadan iftar, sometimes instead of harira","img_alt":"Barley and tomato soup in the pot","serving":"Piping hot, straight away, in bowls, with a drizzle of olive oil if you like (Choumicha; MWN Lifestyle). For breakfast or a light dinner (Taste of Maroc), and at the Ramadan iftar, sometimes instead of harira (Lalla Moulati; MWN Lifestyle; Choumicha lists it among her Ramadan recipes).","ing.0.group":"Soup","ing.orge.name":"barley dchicha (cracked barley)","ing.orge.note":"coarse or medium","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.oignon.name":"medium onion","ing.oignon.pl":"medium onions","ing.oignon.note":"to be grated","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe tomato","ing.tomates.pl":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"to be grated, without the skin","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"or half parsley, half coriander","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end of cooking","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.concentre.name":"tomato purée","ing.concentre.note":"optional, for colour","ing.eau-chaude.name":"hot water","ing.eau-chaude.note":"to thin the soup, if needed","ing.1.group":"To serve","ing.huile-service.name":"olive oil","ing.huile-service.note":"a drizzle, optional","equipment.0":"Large pot (at least 3 litres)","equipment.1":"Large mixing bowl and fine sieve, for washing the barley","equipment.2":"Grater","equipment.3":"Chopping board and knife","equipment.4":"Wooden spoon","equipment.5":"Ladle","steps.0.title":"Wash the barley","steps.0.text":"Put {{orge}} dchicha in a large bowl of water and swirl it with your hand. Drain in a fine sieve. Repeat until the water stays clear, then leave to drain.","steps.0.tip":"Coarse dchicha keeps its grains firm to the bite; medium gives a softer soup and cooks faster (Taste of Maroc).","steps.1.title":"Grate the onion","steps.1.text":"Peel and finely grate {{oignon:full}}.","steps.2.title":"Grate the tomatoes","steps.2.text":"Grate {{tomates:full}}, flesh side against the grater, and throw away the skin.","steps.2.img.alt":"Grated tomato on a grater","steps.2.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.3.title":"Chop the herbs","steps.3.text":"Finely chop some flat-leaf parsley: you need {{persil}} once chopped. You can use half parsley, half coriander.","steps.3.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.3.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.4.title":"Soften the onion","steps.4.text":"Heat {{huile}} olive oil in a large pot over medium heat. Add the grated onion and stir until it turns translucent.","steps.5.title":"Add the tomatoes, herbs, spices and water","steps.5.text":"Add the grated tomatoes, the parsley, {{curcuma}} turmeric, {{sel}} salt and {{poivre}} pepper. Pour in {{eau}} water and bring to the boil over high heat.","steps.6.title":"Add the barley","steps.6.text":"As soon as it boils, pour in the drained barley, stirring. If using, add {{concentre}} tomato purée. Turn the heat down.","steps.7.title":"Simmer, stirring","steps.7.text":"Cook over low heat for 30 minutes, stirring from time to time so that the barley doesn't stick to the bottom. If the soup gets too thick, add a little {{eau-chaude}}.","steps.7.tip":"Don't let it boil too hard: the soup can boil over.","steps.7.timer.label":"Cooking the barley","steps.7.timer.done":"Taste a few grains: they should be tender.","steps.8.title":"Check that it is cooked","steps.8.text":"Taste a few grains: they should be tender, and the soup velvety, barely thick. If not, keep cooking, stirring, and taste again: coarse dchicha takes longer to cook. Adjust the salt.","steps.9.title":"Serve straight away","steps.9.text":"Serve the soup immediately, piping hot, with a drizzle of {{huile-service}} if you like. If it stands, the barley keeps swelling and the soup thickens.","secrets.0.text":"Wash the dchicha in several changes of water, until the water is no longer cloudy, then drain it.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha; Lalla Moulati","secrets.1.text":"Coarse cracked barley, “dchicha”, keeps its grains firm; medium (belboula) gives a softer soup. The coarse kind takes longer to cook and needs a little more water.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha uses medium barley semolina","secrets.2.text":"Grate the onion and the tomatoes, and throw away the tomato skins.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia","secrets.3.text":"Add the barley when the broth is boiling.","secrets.3.source":"Choumicha (h'ssoua hamra and prawn h'ssoua); Lalla Moulati (2016) first boils the water with the aromatics","secrets.4.text":"Stir from time to time and add more water if the level drops: barley sticks to the bottom and can burn.","secrets.4.source":"Lalla Moulati (2016); Choumicha; Cooking with Alia; Taste of Maroc","secrets.5.text":"Don't let it boil too hard: the soup can boil over.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha cooks it over low heat","secrets.6.text":"Tomato purée is optional, but it gives a lovely colour and more tomato flavour.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha and Cooking with Alia use it","secrets.7.text":"For the herbs: parsley alone, or parsley and coriander.","secrets.7.source":"Parsley: Taste of Maroc, Choumicha; parsley and coriander: Choumicha (prawn h'ssoua), Cooking with Alia","secrets.8.text":"Serve the soup as soon as it is ready: if it waits, the barley swells and the soup thickens.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha (“serve the h'ssoua straight away”)","secrets.9.text":"A drizzle of olive oil on each bowl, just before serving.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha (barley soups with thyme); MWN Lifestyle (dchicha)","secrets.10.text":"To reheat, thin with a little water, adjust the seasoning and bring to the boil, stirring.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc; USDA (soups should be reheated to a rolling boil)","secrets.11.text":"Put leftovers in the fridge within 2 hours, in small closed containers, and eat them within 3 days.","secrets.11.source":"ANSES (2 hours, 3 days, small portions); USDA (2 hours, 3 to 4 days)","mistakes.0":"Not washing the barley (Taste of Maroc, Choumicha and Lalla Moulati always wash it).","mistakes.1":"Leaving it to cook without stirring: the barley sticks and burns on the bottom (Lalla Moulati; Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.2":"Cooking over too high a heat: the soup boils over (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.3":"Letting the soup stand before serving: it becomes too thick (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha).","mistakes.4":"Reheating without adding more water (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.5":"Leaving leftovers at room temperature for more than 2 hours (ANSES; USDA).","variants.0":"With milk, h'ssoua belboula with milk (“white hessoua” at Cooking with Alia): the barley first cooks in water with a little olive oil, salt, pepper and cumin, stirring; the milk goes in at the end and the soup cooks a little longer. Lalla Moulati (2014): 250 g barley, 1.5 l water, 2 tbsp oil, 30 min cooking with thyme added halfway through, then 1/4 l milk and 10 min. Cooking with Alia: milk added after 15 min, then 10 min, with dried thyme crumbled on top. Taste of Maroc: 30 to 40 min, then milk, butter and evaporated milk, with cumin when serving.","variants.1":"With a little halal meat: brown some lamb or beef, cut into small dice, with the onion, then let the broth cook for 15 to 20 minutes before adding the barley (Taste of Maroc: 85 g for 4). Choumicha uses 250 g lamb breast for 6, browned for 5 minutes.","variants.2":"With prawns: Choumicha makes a stock with the heads and shells, fries the prawns for 1 minute in the oil and sets them aside, adds bay leaf, harissa, parsley and coriander, then puts the prawns back into the hot soup just before serving. Leave them in until their flesh is firm, pearly and opaque (FDA: 63 °C in the centre). Keep the prawns in the coldest part of the fridge until you cook them, as ANSES recommends for meat and fish.","variants.3":"With herbs, no tomato: the water boils for 10 minutes with thyme, fliou (pennyroyal), celery, garlic in its skin, ginger and turmeric, then the dchicha goes in (Lalla Moulati, 2016). Choumicha makes a barley soup with thyme and garlic, with nigella seeds and cress seeds, or with a little millet.","variants.4":"With vegetables: turnip, carrots, courgettes, cabbage and tomatoes, at iftar instead of harira (Lalla Moulati, 2020).","variants.5":"Spicier: Taste of Maroc adds 1 tsp ground ginger for 4; Lalla Moulati also puts some in her herb dchicha.","variants.6":"Wheat tchicha: in Ouarzazate, tchicha is a wheat soup (Hespress, 2021)."},"double-cheeseburger-smash":{"title":"Double smash cheeseburger","tagline":"Balls of beef smashed very thin in a scorching-hot pan: a well-browned crust, melted cheddar, a soft bun. The real American recipe, in a halal version.","region":"United States","occasion":"A quick meal, with friends","serving":"Straight away, as soon as the burger is assembled.","notes.0.title":"The golden rule","notes.0.text":"Beef, salt, pepper. No egg, no breadcrumbs, no onion or spices in the meat: otherwise it's kefta, not a burger.","notes.1.title":"At the (halal) butcher's","notes.1.items.0":"Ask for blade or chuck, minced in front of you on the same day.","notes.1.items.1":"Plain: no onion, no parsley, no spices.","notes.1.items.2":"About 20% fat. If the cut is too lean, ask the butcher to add some beef fat (chehma): it's this fat that makes the burger juicy.","notes.1.items.3":"No bacon: a classic cheeseburger doesn't need it.","notes.1.items.4":"If you want to be strict: cheese labelled halal or “microbial coagulant” (no animal rennet), and bread with no alcohol in the list of ingredients.","notes.2.title":"Assembly, from bottom to top","notes.2.items.0":"Bottom bun: 1 tbsp sauce on the toasted side.","notes.2.items.1":"1 folded lettuce leaf.","notes.2.items.2":"1 slice of tomato, 5 mm thick.","notes.2.items.3":"The first patty with its cheddar, then the second.","notes.2.items.4":"2 thin onion slices.","notes.2.items.5":"4 gherkin slices.","notes.2.items.6":"Top bun: 1 tbsp sauce on the toasted side.","ing.0.group":"Meat","ing.boeuf.name":"minced beef, about 20% fat","ing.boeuf.note":"2 balls of 80 g per burger","ing.1.group":"Buns and cheese","ing.pains.name":"soft sesame burger bun (or brioche bun)","ing.pains.pl":"soft sesame burger buns (or brioche buns)","ing.cheddar.name":"slice of burger cheddar (the kind that melts well)","ing.cheddar.pl":"slices of burger cheddar (the kind that melts well)","ing.2.group":"Vegetables","ing.oignon.name":"white onion","ing.oignon.note":"2 thin slices per burger","ing.salade.name":"iceberg lettuce, or romaine","ing.salade.note":"1 leaf per burger","ing.tomate.name":"round tomato, ripe but nice and firm","ing.tomate.pl":"round tomatoes, ripe but nice and firm","ing.tomate.note":"1 slice per burger, 2 good slices per tomato","ing.cornichons.name":"jar of gherkins","ing.cornichons.note":"for the sauce and the topping","ing.3.group":"Sauce","ing.mayo.name":"mayonnaise","ing.ketchup.name":"ketchup","ing.moutarde.name":"mustard","ing.corn-haches.name":"very finely chopped gherkins","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.4.group":"Everything else","ing.beurre.name":"butter, for the buns","ing.huile.name":"A drizzle of neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.sel-poivre.name":"Fine salt and freshly ground black pepper","equipment.0":"A heavy cast-iron or stainless steel frying pan, or a plancha. No non-stick: it isn't made for high heat.","equipment.1":"A wide, stiff metal spatula.","equipment.2":"Baking paper, cut into squares of about 20 cm.","equipment.3":"Kitchen scales.","equipment.4":"A chopping board and a sharp knife.","equipment.5":"A bowl, a tablespoon and a teaspoon.","equipment.6":"Kitchen paper.","steps.0.title":"Make the sauce","steps.0.text.0":"Chop some gherkins very finely, into 2 to 3 mm dice, until you have {{corn-haches}}.","steps.0.text.1":"Put {{mayo}} mayonnaise, {{ketchup}} ketchup, {{moutarde}} mustard, the chopped gherkins and {{paprika}} sweet paprika in a bowl.","steps.0.text.2":"Mix until the colour is even, with no white or red streaks.","steps.0.text.3":"Cover and put in the fridge: it rests while you prepare everything else.","steps.0.tip.l":"Good to know","steps.0.tip.t":"All spoon measures are level. Quick version: ketchup and mustard, the classic pair.","steps.1.title":"Slice the onion","steps.1.text.0":"Peel the white onion and cut 2 very thin slices (2 mm) per burger from the middle of the onion, where they are widest.","steps.1.text.1":"Separate each slice into rings.","steps.1.text.2":"Too strong for you? Leave the rings in a bowl of cold water for 10 min, then dry them well in kitchen paper.","steps.1.text.3":"Keep the rest of the onion for another recipe.","steps.1.timer.label":"Soaking the onion","steps.1.timer.done":"Drain the onion and dry it well.","steps.2.title":"Prepare the lettuce","steps.2.text.0":"Pull off 1 iceberg (or romaine) leaf per burger.","steps.2.text.1":"Wash the leaves, then dry them thoroughly with kitchen paper: wet lettuce makes the bun soggy.","steps.2.text.2":"Fold each leaf to the size of the bun and keep them cool.","steps.3.title":"Slice the tomato","steps.3.text.0":"Take {{tomate:full}}.","steps.3.text.1":"Cut 1 slice about 5 mm thick per burger from the middle of the tomato, where the slices are widest (2 good slices per tomato).","steps.3.text.2":"Keep the rest for a salad.","steps.4.title":"Slice the gherkins","steps.4.text.0":"Cut 4 thin slices lengthways per burger, which is 1 to 2 gherkins depending on their size.","steps.4.text.1":"You can also cut them into rounds.","steps.4.text.2":"Lay them on kitchen paper.","steps.5.title":"Shape the beef balls","steps.5.text.0":"Weigh out 80 g balls, 2 per burger, which makes {{boeuf}} in all. Without scales, divide the meat into equal portions.","steps.5.text.1":"Roll them without packing them tight, just enough for them to hold together.","steps.5.text.2":"No salt, no spices.","steps.5.text.3":"Put them on a plate and keep them in the fridge until it's time to cook.","steps.5.text.4":"Wash your hands.","steps.6.title":"Set up the cooking station","steps.6.text.0":"Next to the pan: the fine salt, the pepper, the spatula and squares of baking paper.","steps.6.text.1":"Take the cheddar slices out of their wrapping ({{cheddar}}).","steps.6.text.2":"Split the buns in half.","steps.6.text.3":"Keep the sauce, lettuce, tomato, onion and gherkins within reach.","steps.6.tip.l":"Why?","steps.6.tip.t":"A batch takes only 3 to 4 minutes: everything must be ready before you turn on the heat.","steps.7.title":"Toast the buns","steps.7.dur":"1 min","steps.7.text.0":"Pan over medium heat.","steps.7.text.1":"Spread {{beurre}} butter on the cut sides (5 g per bun).","steps.7.text.2":"Place the halves cut side down in the pan for about 1 min, until golden.","steps.7.text.3":"Set them aside, toasted side up.","steps.7.text.4":"Wipe the pan with well-folded kitchen paper, as it is hot: over high heat, the butter would burn.","steps.7.timer.label":"Buns","steps.7.timer.done":"Cut sides golden? Take the buns out.","steps.8.title":"Dress the buns","steps.8.text.0":"On each bottom bun: 1 tbsp sauce spread right to the edges, 1 lettuce leaf, then 1 tomato slice.","steps.8.text.1":"On each top bun: 1 tbsp sauce.","steps.8.text.2":"The patties will go straight on top as they come out of the pan.","steps.8.tip.l":"Good to know","steps.8.tip.t":"There will be a little sauce left over; that's normal.","steps.9.title":"Heat the pan","steps.9.dur":"3 to 4 min","steps.9.text.0":"High heat, empty pan, for 3 to 4 min.","steps.9.text.1":"Turn on the extractor fan or open the window: it's going to smoke.","steps.9.text.2":"Just before adding the meat, add a drizzle of neutral oil.","steps.9.text.3":"It's ready when the oil shimmers and smokes slightly.","steps.9.tip.l":"Careful","steps.9.tip.t":"No non-stick pan: it isn't made for high heat.","steps.9.timer.label":"Preheating","steps.9.timer.done":"The pan is very hot: add the oil.","steps.10.title":"Smash","steps.10.text.0":"Take the balls you are about to cook out of the fridge.","steps.10.text.1":"Put 1 or 2 balls in the pan, well spaced out. Once smashed, each patty is 13 to 14 cm across: they mustn't touch. On a plancha, you can do more.","steps.10.text.2":"Put a square of baking paper on the ball and smash it hard with the spatula, straight away. Press on the spatula with your other hand, or use the bottom of a saucepan.","steps.10.text.3":"Aim for about 0.5 cm thick, a little wider than the bun: the patty shrinks as it cooks.","steps.10.text.4":"Remove the paper. After that, never press again.","steps.10.tip.l":"Why only once?","steps.10.tip.t":"If you press later, the juices run out of the patty.","steps.11.title":"Season with salt and pepper","steps.11.text.0":"Straight after smashing: a good pinch of fine salt, dropped from about twenty centimetres up so that it covers right to the edges.","steps.11.text.1":"Then 1 or 2 turns of the pepper mill.","steps.11.text.2":"One side is enough: the patty is very thin.","steps.11.text.3":"If you cook in several batches, season each patty as you smash it.","steps.11.tip.l":"Why not before?","steps.11.tip.t":"Salt mixed into raw meat dissolves some of the proteins, which then stick together: the patty becomes dense and springy, like a sausage.","steps.12.title":"Cook the first side","steps.12.dur":"2 min","steps.12.text.0":"Don't touch anything for about 2 min.","steps.12.text.1":"It's ready to flip when the edges are dark brown and crisp, and the top is turning grey around the edge.","steps.12.timer.label":"First side","steps.12.timer.done":"Edges well browned? Flip the patties.","steps.13.title":"Flip and add the cheddar","steps.13.dur":"30 s to 1 min","steps.13.text.0":"Slide the spatula under the patty, scraping the bottom of the pan hard: the crust must stay stuck to the patty.","steps.13.text.1":"Flip it.","steps.13.text.2":"Like it saltier? Add a small pinch of salt on this side.","steps.13.text.3":"Immediately put 1 slice of cheddar on each patty.","steps.13.text.4":"Leave for 30 s to 1 min, until the cheese melts. At this thickness, the patty is cooked through.","steps.13.timer.label":"Cheese","steps.13.timer.done":"Has the cheese melted? Take the patties out.","steps.14.title":"Build the burger","steps.14.text.0":"Place the first cheese-topped patty on a dressed bottom bun, then the second one on top.","steps.14.text.1":"Add 2 onion slices, then 4 gherkin slices.","steps.14.text.2":"Close with the top bun, sauce side down.","steps.14.text.3":"Eat straight away.","steps.15.title":"Cook the next batch","steps.15.text.0":"If there are balls left: scrape the pan with the spatula to remove any stuck-on bits, otherwise they burn.","steps.15.text.1":"Add another drizzle of oil and wait until it smokes slightly.","steps.15.text.2":"Start again from the “Smash” step, until all the patties are cooked.","secrets.0.text":"Smash only once, straight after putting the ball in the pan: after that, the juices escape.","secrets.1.text":"Salt only the top, just after smashing, never the raw meat in advance.","secrets.2.text":"A very hot cast-iron pan, no non-stick: the crust is what gives the flavour.","secrets.3.text":"Scrape hard under the patty when you flip it, to keep all the crust.","secrets.4.text":"The beef fat (about 20%) makes the burger juicy: ask your butcher for it.","mistakes.0":"Salting the meat in advance: it turns springy, like a sausage.","mistakes.1":"Pressing the patty again: smash only once, at the start, or the juices escape.","mistakes.2":"Putting too many patties in at once: the pan cools down and the meat boils instead of browning.","mistakes.3":"A pan that isn't hot enough: no crust.","mistakes.4":"Lettuce that isn't properly dried: the bun goes soggy.","mistakes.5":"Waiting before eating: a smash burger is eaten straight away."},"escalopes-poulet-panees":{"title":"Breaded chicken escalopes","tagline":"Nice thin chicken escalopes, dipped in flour, egg and breadcrumbs, then browned in a frying pan: crisp outside, tender inside.","region":"France and Central Europe. Breading “à l'anglaise” (flour, egg, breadcrumbs) is a classic technique of French cooking (Escoffier, Chef Simon); the Viennese escalope (Wiener Schnitzel, Vienna) is its cousin, originally made with veal. Breaded chicken is also eaten in Egypt (“frakh pané”) and in Israel (Wikipedia).","occasion":"A quick dinner for tired evenings. Children love breaded escalopes (Migusto, Betty Bossi).","img_alt":"Golden breaded chicken escalopes, with broccoli and potatoes","serving":"Straight away, with lemon, in wedges (Migusto, Diana Henry) or in slices (Vienna Tourist Board). On the side: a green salad, a potato or cucumber salad, or parsley potatoes, as in Vienna; they are also served with rice or fries (Vienna Tourist Board, Migusto, Wikipedia). Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours (Anses, FSA), to be eaten within 2 days (FSA; Anses says less than 3 days for a cooked dish), reheated only once, until piping hot all the way through (FSA, Anses).","ing.0.group":"Escalopes","ing.escalopes.name":"halal chicken escalope","ing.escalopes.pl":"halal chicken escalopes","ing.escalopes.note":"one per person, about 100 to 150 g each; or chicken breasts sliced in half horizontally","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.1.group":"Coating","ing.farine.name":"plain flour","ing.farine.note":"ordinary white flour","ing.oeufs.name":"egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"eggs","ing.chapelure.name":"breadcrumbs","ing.chapelure.note":"very fine; or dried stale bread, finely blitzed","ing.2.group":"Cooking and serving","ing.huile.name":"neutral oil","ing.huile.note":"sunflower, rapeseed or groundnut: an oil that can take the heat","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.pl":"lemons","ing.citron.note":"in wedges, to serve","equipment.0":"A chopping board kept for raw chicken only","equipment.1":"A rolling pin or a meat mallet","equipment.2":"Greaseproof paper (or cling film)","equipment.3":"Three deep plates","equipment.4":"A large frying pan","equipment.5":"Tongs or a spatula","equipment.6":"Kitchen paper and an ovenproof dish","equipment.7":"A cooking thermometer (optional)","steps.0.title":"Set out the three plates","steps.0.text":"Put {{farine}} flour in the first deep plate, with a little {{sel}} and {{poivre}}. Crack {{oeufs:full}} into the second, add a little salt and just beat with a fork. Tip {{chapelure}} breadcrumbs into the third.","steps.0.tip":"Don't overbeat the eggs: beaten for too long, they become too runny (Wiener Schnitzel recipe from the Vienna Tourist Board).","steps.1.title":"Flatten the escalopes","steps.1.text":"Flatten {{escalopes:full}}: put the meat between two sheets of greaseproof paper and beat it with the rolling pin, without tearing it, to about 5 mm thick, nice and even.","steps.1.tip":"Got whole breasts? First slice them in half horizontally, with your hand flat on top, before flattening them (Chef Simon).","steps.2.title":"Dry and season","steps.2.text":"Pat the meat dry with kitchen paper, then season it with salt and pepper on both sides.","steps.2.tip":"Don't wash raw chicken: the water splashes its bacteria onto your hands, the worktop and the utensils, and only cooking kills them (FSA, the UK food safety agency).","steps.3.title":"Coat them in flour","steps.3.text":"Coat an escalope in the flour on both sides. Shake it to remove the excess.","steps.4.title":"Then dip in the egg","steps.4.text":"Dip it in the beaten egg on both sides. Let the excess drip off over the plate.","steps.5.title":"Then coat in the breadcrumbs","steps.5.text":"Lay it in the breadcrumbs, turn it over and press lightly so that the crumbs stick well. Put it on a dish, then coat any other escalopes the same way. Then wash your hands with soap.","steps.5.tip":"Coat just before cooking: if it waits, the coating absorbs moisture and comes away during cooking (Betty Bossi, Migusto).","steps.6.title":"Heat the oil","steps.6.text":"Pour {{huile}} oil into the large frying pan (a small pan if you are cooking only one escalope, so that the oil comes high enough) and heat it over medium-high heat, to about 170 °C. Without a thermometer, dip a wooden skewer or a wooden handle into the oil: when small bubbles form all around it, it is ready. From now on, don't leave the pan.","steps.6.tip":"If the oil smokes, it is too hot: turn off the heat and let it cool a little (UK government fire safety advice).","steps.7.title":"Brown the first side","steps.7.text":"Carefully lay the escalopes in the hot oil, two at a time at most. Let them brown for about 4 minutes, over medium heat, without touching them.","steps.7.tip":"Meanwhile, spoon a little hot oil over the top: the coating will be crisper (Betty Bossi; Migusto tilts the pan for the same reason).","steps.7.timer.label":"First side","steps.7.timer.done":"Turn the escalopes over.","steps.8.title":"Turn them over","steps.8.text":"Turn the escalopes over with the tongs and let the other side brown for about 4 minutes. The coating should be golden, not brown.","steps.8.timer.label":"Second side","steps.8.timer.done":"Check that they are cooked.","steps.9.title":"Check that they are cooked","steps.9.text":"Cut the thickest one through the middle: the meat should be white, with no trace of pink, and the juices clear. With a thermometer, the centre should reach at least 75 °C. If not, turn the heat down and cook for a few more minutes.","steps.9.tip":"Anses recommends a core temperature of 70 °C for poultry; the FSA, 75 °C for 30 seconds (or 70 °C for 2 minutes).","steps.10.title":"Drain and keep warm","steps.10.text":"Put the cooked escalopes on kitchen paper, then in a clean dish in a low oven (60 to 80 °C) while you cook any remaining ones. Don't use the dish that held the raw chicken.","steps.11.title":"Cook the rest","steps.11.text":"If there are escalopes left, cook them the same way, two at a time at most, and check that they are cooked too.","steps.12.title":"Turn off the heat","steps.12.text":"Turn off the heat as soon as the last escalope is out. Let the oil cool completely in the pan before moving it, straining it or throwing it away.","steps.12.tip":"Throw away any leftover flour, egg and breadcrumbs: they have touched the raw chicken (the FSA points out that its bacteria spread to whatever it touches).","steps.13.title":"Serve straight away","steps.13.text":"Serve the escalopes immediately, piping hot, with {{citron:full}} cut into wedges.","steps.13.tip":"A breaded poultry escalope won't wait, or it toughens: serve it at once, tender and piping hot (Escoffier).","secrets.0.text":"Flatten the escalopes to an even thickness, about 5 mm: they cook quickly and evenly.","secrets.0.source":"Betty Bossi (butcher's escalopes are about 5 mm thick), Ben Tish (Great British Chefs, 0.5 cm), Chef Simon, Migusto, Diana Henry (BBC Good Food)","secrets.1.text":"Score the surface of the meat very lightly before flattening it: it is easier to beat out and shrinks less as it cooks.","secrets.1.source":"Chef Simon (escalope breaded “à l'anglaise”; cutting and flattening a poultry escalope)","secrets.2.text":"The order flour, egg, breadcrumbs has a reason: the flour dries the surface so that the egg clings, and the egg glues on the breadcrumbs.","secrets.2.source":"Betty Bossi (“Paner”); Chef Simon (glossary entry “Paner”: breading “à l'anglaise”)","secrets.3.text":"Shake off the excess flour and let the egg drip off: the coating stays thin and even, and doesn't come away.","secrets.3.source":"Migusto (“Paner une escalope”), Betty Bossi, Chef Simon, Ben Tish (Great British Chefs)","secrets.4.text":"The oil must be properly hot before the escalopes go in: if it is too cold, the coating soaks up the oil and doesn't crisp.","secrets.4.source":"Betty Bossi (“Paner”); Migusto (“La friture”)","secrets.5.text":"No more than two escalopes at a time: overloaded, the pan cools down and the coating goes soft.","secrets.5.source":"Betty Bossi (“Frire”), Great British Chefs (GBC Kitchen), Journal des Femmes (the editorial team cooks the escalopes two at a time)","secrets.6.text":"Baste the top with the hot oil as they cook, or gently tilt the pan: the coating puffs up and crisps.","secrets.6.source":"Betty Bossi (“Paner”); Migusto (Viennese escalopes); Vienna Tourist Board (Sacher recipe)","secrets.7.text":"Drain the escalopes on kitchen paper as soon as they come out of the pan.","secrets.7.source":"Vienna Tourist Board, Migusto, Great British Chefs, Cyril Lignac (quoted by the Journal des Femmes)","secrets.8.text":"For a buttery flavour, use clarified butter: ordinary butter starts to smoke at around 130 to 150 °C, clarified butter only at around 250 °C.","secrets.8.source":"Chef Simon (glossary entry “Beurre clarifié”); Escoffier and Cyril Lignac cook their breaded escalopes in clarified butter","secrets.9.text":"Use very fine breadcrumbs, as the Viennese recipe requires; homemade breadcrumbs are made from dried stale bread, blitzed and then sieved.","secrets.9.source":"Vienna Tourist Board (breadcrumbs “very finely grated”); Larousse and Chef Simon (definition of breadcrumbs)","secrets.10.text":"Boneless chicken thighs, flattened the same way, make juicier escalopes than breast.","secrets.10.source":"Diana Henry (BBC Good Food)","mistakes.0":"Washing raw chicken: it splashes bacteria everywhere, and only cooking kills them (FSA).","mistakes.1":"Defrosting chicken on the worktop: defrost it in the fridge or in the microwave (Anses).","mistakes.2":"Coating long in advance: the coating goes soft and comes away (Betty Bossi).","mistakes.3":"Putting the escalopes into oil that isn't hot enough: they soak up the oil (Betty Bossi, Migusto).","mistakes.4":"Overfilling the pan: the oil cools down and the coating goes soft (Betty Bossi, Great British Chefs).","mistakes.5":"Leaving the pan of oil unattended (BPA, UK government).","mistakes.6":"Throwing water on burning oil: never. Turn off the heat if you can do so safely; the BPA advises smothering the flames with a lid or a fire blanket, the UK government advises not tackling the fire yourself. If a fire starts, get out, stay out and call the emergency services (UK government).","mistakes.7":"Letting the coating go brown: aim for a golden yellow colour, as the FSA advises for fried starchy foods, including bread (FSA, Betty Bossi).","mistakes.8":"Serving without checking that the centre is properly cooked (Anses, FSA).","mistakes.9":"Putting the cooked escalopes back in the dish that held the raw chicken (Anses).","variants.0":"Milanese style: mix grated parmesan into the breadcrumbs (Chef Simon: one third parmesan; the Journal des Femmes editorial team adds it with parsley). If you want to be strict: cheese made with microbial coagulant, no animal rennet.","variants.1":"With panko, Japanese breadcrumbs (Great British Chefs, Ben Tish; Betty Bossi suggests it too).","variants.2":"Double coating: dip the already coated escalope in the egg again, then in the breadcrumbs, for a thicker crust (Betty Bossi, Ben Tish).","variants.3":"Lighter, Cyril Lignac's way: just frothy egg whites, no flour, and breadcrumbs mixed with crushed peanuts and a little tandoori spice; the coated escalopes rest for 30 minutes in the fridge before cooking (Journal des Femmes, 2024).","variants.4":"Israeli style: breadcrumbs spiced up with paprika or sesame seeds, fried in vegetable oil (Wikipedia).","variants.5":"Holstein: a fried egg on each escalope (Chef Simon, who adds anchovy fillets).","variants.6":"With boneless chicken thighs, which are juicier, and brown butter with capers (Diana Henry, BBC Good Food).","variants.7":"With clarified butter instead of oil, like Escoffier, Chef Simon and Cyril Lignac."},"frites":{"title":"Homemade fries","darija":"btata mqliya, or “frit”","tagline":"Crisp outside, soft inside: homemade fries cooked twice, as in Belgian chip shops.","region":"Belgium and northern France: the two countries dispute its origin (Wikipedia). In Belgium, chip shop culture (the “fritkot”) has been recognised as intangible cultural heritage by the Flemish Community (2014), the Wallonia-Brussels Federation (2016), then the German-speaking Community and the Brussels-Capital Region (2017) (Navefri-Unafri, the national union of chip shop owners).","occasion":"An everyday side dish: steak, chicken, fish, meatballs. In Belgium and France, it's steak-frites and moules-frites (Wikipedia, VLAM); in Morocco, fries also go with dishes, for example laid on top of chicken in sauce (Bladi.net). Every November, Belgium celebrates fries: the Semaine de la Frite in Wallonia, the Week van de Friet in Flanders (Apaq-W, VLAM).","img_alt":"Golden homemade fries, cut into thick sticks, on a plate","serving":"Serve the fries immediately, piping hot and uncovered, batch after batch (Chef Simon, Meilleur du Chef). In Belgium, they are eaten from a paper cone or a tray, with mayonnaise, the Belgians' favourite sauce (Apaq-W, VLAM, Wikipedia); ketchup and mustard are classics too (Wikipedia). They go with steak, mussels or meatballs (Wikipedia, VLAM), and in Morocco, for example, with chicken in sauce (Bladi.net). Throw away any fries that are too brown (Anses), and keep them as a treat: Anses advises eating fatty and fried foods in moderation.","ing.0.group":"Fries","ing.pdt.name":"floury potatoes","ing.pdt.note":"such as Bintje or Agria","ing.huile.name":"vegetable frying oil (sunflower or groundnut)","ing.huile.note":"enough for the fries to swim in; 100% vegetable: no lard, and no beef fat that isn't certified halal; if anyone is allergic to peanuts, use sunflower oil (peanuts are an allergen, EU Regulation 1169/2011)","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.sel.note":"to taste","equipment.0":"An electric deep fryer with a thermostat: the safest option (London Fire Brigade, Quebec government)","equipment.1":"Failing that: a deep saucepan, a cooking thermometer and a skimmer (saucepan no more than a third full); the Quebec government advises against using a saucepan, and Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service recommends the oven instead","equipment.2":"A lid (or a baking tray) big enough to cover the fryer or the saucepan, always within reach","equipment.3":"A vegetable peeler and a large knife, or a chip cutter","equipment.4":"A chopping board","equipment.5":"A large bowl of cold water","equipment.6":"Clean, dry tea towels, and kitchen paper","equipment.7":"A large baking tray or dish for resting the fries","equipment.8":"A timer","steps.0.title":"Peel the potatoes","steps.0.text":"Wash {{pdt:full}} if they are muddy, then peel them with the peeler. Remove any blemishes and sprouts. As you go, put them in a large bowl of cold water.","steps.0.tip":"The cold water stops them going dark while they wait (Meilleur du Chef); Chef Simon also keeps them in cold water before cutting them.","steps.1.title":"Cut the sticks","steps.1.text":"Cut each potato into slices 1 cm thick, then each slice into sticks 1 cm square, nice and even. Put them back in the cold water.","steps.1.tip":"This is the “pont-neuf” cut, the classic fry (Escoffier, Chef Simon, Meilleur du Chef). Belgian chip shop owners compare it to a 1 cm-wide ruler (Navefri-Unafri). You can also use a chip cutter with a 1 cm grid (Wikipedia).","steps.2.title":"Rinse in cold water","steps.2.text":"Drain the sticks and rinse them well under cold running water, to remove the excess starch.","steps.2.tip":"If you have time, first leave them to soak in cold water, then rinse them in clean water: the European regulation on acrylamide (2017/2158) asks restaurants, where possible, to soak them preferably for 30 minutes to 2 hours in cold water (or a few minutes in hot water), then rinse them, to reduce their sugars; the Irish agency (FSAI) also advises home cooks to soak them, then drain them well.","steps.3.title":"Dry them thoroughly","steps.3.text":"Spread the fries out on a clean, dry tea towel and pat them well on all sides. Finish with kitchen paper if needed: they must be very dry.","steps.3.tip":"It's also a matter of safety: water makes the oil bubble up, and it can overflow (Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service, London Fire Brigade). It also lowers the temperature of the oil (Wikipedia).","steps.4.title":"Heat the oil to 140 °C","steps.4.text":"Fill the fryer with {{huile}} as its instructions say and set it to 140 °C. Without a fryer, use a deep saucepan: fill it no more than a third full and check the temperature with a cooking thermometer. Put a lid that fits within reach. From now on, don't take your eyes off the oil.","steps.4.tip":"If the oil catches fire: never use water, and don't move the fryer or pan. If you can do it safely, turn off the heat and put the lid on, then leave it to cool for at least 30 minutes without touching it. Otherwise, get out, close the door and call the fire brigade. Call them even if the fire seems to be out: it may have spread into the cooker hood duct (Quebec government, USFA, London Fire Brigade, Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service).","steps.5.title":"Fry them a first time","steps.5.text":"When the oil is at 140 °C, lower in a first batch, with the basket no more than half full. Cook for about 7 minutes. The fries should become soft without taking on any colour.","steps.5.tip":"This first bath “poaches” the fries and drives out their moisture (Navefri-Unafri). If they are already colouring, the oil is too hot.","steps.5.timer.label":"First frying","steps.5.timer.done":"Lift out the basket: the fries should be soft and still pale.","steps.5.img.alt":"Still-pale fries cooking in a basket lowered into the oil, on the hob","steps.5.img.caption":"The fries in the oil bath","steps.6.title":"Drain and carry on","steps.6.text":"Lift out the basket, shake it and spread the fries out on kitchen paper. Wait for the oil to come back to 140 °C, then cook the other batches the same way.","steps.6.tip":"An overfull basket makes the oil temperature drop: the fries stick together and turn greasy (Wikipedia, FSA).","steps.7.title":"Leave to rest","steps.7.text":"Leave the fries to cool completely in the open air, about 30 minutes. They can wait for up to 1 to 2 hours. If you leave the kitchen, turn off the fryer.","steps.7.tip":"During this rest, a thin crust forms (Semaine de la Frite). And the more the fries have cooled, the stronger the heat shock of the second bath: they sear quickly and turn crisp and golden (Navefri-Unafri).","steps.8.title":"Raise the oil to 175 °C","steps.8.text":"Just before serving, set the fryer to 175 °C, no higher; with a saucepan, watch the thermometer. If the oil smokes, it is too hot: turn off the heat and let it cool.","steps.8.tip":"Above 175 °C, the amount of acrylamide in fries can rise sharply (EFSA). It is also the ceiling set by Europe for restaurants (Regulation 2017/2158) and by the Food Safety Authority of Ireland (FSAI).","steps.9.title":"Fry them a second time","steps.9.text":"Lower in a batch, still with the basket no more than half full, and allow 2 minutes. The fries should be crisp and golden yellow. If not, give them a little longer, watching the colour, and take them out as soon as they are golden yellow.","steps.9.tip":"Aim for golden yellow, not brown: the browner a fry gets, the more acrylamide it contains, a substance that could increase the risk of cancer (FSA, EFSA, EU Regulation 2017/2158).","steps.9.timer.label":"Second frying","steps.9.timer.done":"Check the colour: golden yellow? Take them out straight away.","steps.10.title":"Shake and salt","steps.10.text":"Lift out the basket and shake it well over the oil. Tip the fries onto kitchen paper and salt them straight away with a little {{sel}}, while they are piping hot. Don't cover them.","steps.10.tip":"Shaking the basket knocks off the surface oil: the FSA talks of 20% less fat absorbed. In Belgian chip shops, they shake and tap the container to drain them (Wikipedia).","steps.11.title":"Cook the other batches","steps.11.text":"Let the oil come back up to 175 °C, then cook the other batches the same way. Serve each batch as soon as it is ready.","steps.12.title":"Turn off the fryer and let the oil cool","steps.12.text":"Turn off the fryer as soon as the last batch is out. Let the oil cool completely before moving or straining it.","steps.12.tip":"Strain the cooled oil, keep it in a closed container and change it often (Chef Simon: every 4 to 7 uses). Avoid frying fish in it: the oil holds on to flavours (Chef Simon).","steps.13.title":"Serve straight away","steps.13.text":"Serve the fries immediately, piping hot, with the sauce of your choice: mayonnaise, ketchup or mustard.","steps.13.tip":"Throw away any fries that are too brown or burnt: they contain the most acrylamide (Anses).","secrets.0.text":"Use a floury potato, such as Bintje: rich in starch, it gives golden, crisp fries.","secrets.0.source":"VLAM (Lekker van bij ons), Navefri-Unafri, Semaine de la Frite (Apaq-W), Frietmuseum in Bruges, Wikipedia","secrets.1.text":"Cut nice even sticks, 1 cm square: this is the “pont-neuf” fry, the “fundamental type of fried potatoes” according to Escoffier.","secrets.1.source":"Escoffier (Le Guide culinaire, 1903), Chef Simon, Meilleur du Chef, Navefri-Unafri, Wikipedia","secrets.2.text":"Rinse the fries in cold water to remove the excess starch, then dry them very well: it's one of the secrets of Belgian fresh-fry makers.","secrets.2.source":"Navefri-Unafri (“L'art des bonnes frites”), VLAM, Chef Simon, Meilleur du Chef","secrets.3.text":"Double frying: a first, gentle bath cooks the inside without colouring it, a second, hotter bath forms the golden, crisp crust.","secrets.3.source":"Navefri-Unafri, VLAM, Semaine de la Frite (Apaq-W), Chef Simon, Meilleur du Chef, Frietmuseum in Bruges","secrets.4.text":"Let the fries cool well between the two baths: a thin crust forms, and the heat shock of the second bath, stronger on well-cooled fries, sears them quickly: they turn crisp and golden.","secrets.4.source":"VLAM, Semaine de la Frite (Apaq-W), Navefri-Unafri","secrets.5.text":"Having guests? Do the first frying 1 to 2 hours ahead, and the second just before serving.","secrets.5.source":"Meilleur du Chef (1 to 2 hours), Navefri-Unafri (“a few hours” possible between the two baths)","secrets.6.text":"“A good fry must swim, then sing and, finally, jump”: lots of oil for a small amount of fries, and a basket never more than half full.","secrets.6.source":"Bernard Lefèvre, president of Unafri (Navefri-Unafri); EU Regulation 2017/2158 and FSA for the half-full basket","secrets.7.text":"Between batches, wait for the oil to come back to the right temperature: in oil that isn't hot enough, the fries soak up the fat.","secrets.7.source":"FSA (advice to chip shops in Northern Ireland), Navefri-Unafri, Wikipedia","secrets.8.text":"Shake the basket as soon as it comes out of the oil and drain on kitchen paper: fries keep absorbing oil as they cool.","secrets.8.source":"FSA (up to 20% less fat absorbed), Wikipedia, Navefri-Unafri","secrets.9.text":"Salt straight away, as they come out of the oil, while the fries are still piping hot: the salt dissolves and soaks in better. And don't cover them.","secrets.9.source":"Chef Simon, Semaine de la Frite (Apaq-W), Meilleur du Chef, Wikipedia","secrets.10.text":"Potatoes are harvested in September and October. At the end of the season, towards summer, they contain more sugar and brown faster: make the first bath a little shorter and a little cooler, and stop at golden yellow.","secrets.10.source":"Navefri-Unafri (“Frieten bakken is een kunst”)","secrets.11.text":"Store potatoes somewhere cool and dark, ideally between 8 and 10 °C. Too cold, they make sugar and brown quickly when fried.","secrets.11.source":"Apaq-W (8 to 10 °C), EFSA (below 8 °C, more sugars), FSAI (8 °C or slightly above)","secrets.12.text":"An electric fryer with a thermostat holds the right temperature on its own: more even fries, and a safer kitchen.","secrets.12.source":"Chef Simon (Sabine Simon), London Fire Brigade, Quebec government","secrets.13.text":"Use a neutral oil that can take the heat, such as sunflower or groundnut oil.","secrets.13.source":"INC (sunflower), Chef Simon (groundnut), Quebec government (smoke points: sunflower 210 °C, groundnut 220 °C)","mistakes.0":"Putting wet fries into the oil: it bubbles up and can overflow (Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service, London Fire Brigade).","mistakes.1":"Filling the basket to the brim: the oil cools, the fries stick together and turn greasy; fill it no more than half full (EU Regulation 2017/2158, FSA, Wikipedia).","mistakes.2":"Doing the second frying in oil that isn't hot enough: the fries absorb too much fat (Navefri-Unafri, Wikipedia).","mistakes.3":"Doing the first frying too hot: the sticks seal too quickly and then collapse, completely flat (Navefri-Unafri). Too cool, and the potato falls apart and spoils the oil (Chef Simon).","mistakes.4":"Going above 175 °C or frying until brown: that's when acrylamide shoots up (EFSA, FSA, FSAI, EU Regulation 2017/2158).","mistakes.5":"Letting oil that is smoking keep heating: turn off the heat and let it cool (London Fire Brigade, Quebec government, Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service).","mistakes.6":"Leaving hot oil unattended, even for a few seconds (Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service, Quebec government, USFA).","mistakes.7":"Filling a saucepan more than a third full of oil (London Fire Brigade, Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service).","mistakes.8":"Throwing water on an oil fire, or moving the fryer or pan while it is on fire (Quebec government, London Fire Brigade, Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service).","mistakes.9":"Storing potatoes meant for fries in the fridge: they get sweeter and brown faster when fried (VLAM, EFSA, FSAI). The UK's FSA has considered the fridge acceptable since spring 2022; if you have kept them there, watch the colour closely.","mistakes.10":"Keeping the same oil indefinitely: strain it after use and change it regularly (Chef Simon).","mistakes.11":"Covering the fries when they come out of the oil (Chef Simon: “whatever you do, don't cover them”).","variants.0":"Belgian fries: Bintje cut to 1 cm, rinsed and dried well; first bath at 140 °C for about 7 minutes, cooling, then a second bath at 170-175 °C until the crust is golden (Navefri-Unafri). The Belgian tradition is beef dripping (Frietmuseum in Bruges, Wikipedia), and some chip shops remain loyal to it (Navefri-Unafri): at home, use only beef dripping certified halal. It is a fat rich in saturated fat, about 50% compared with 10% for sunflower oil (FSA). Serve them in a paper cone, with mayonnaise (Apaq-W). At a chip shop in Belgium or northern France, ask what fat the fries are cooked in: some use beef dripping or a blend of beef and vegetable fat (Navefri-Unafri).","variants.1":"In the oven: preheat the oven to between 180 and 200 °C (190 °C at most for a fan oven). Toss the rinsed, well-dried fries with a drizzle of oil (Wikipedia: 2 tbsp for 1 kg potatoes), spread them on a baking tray and turn them halfway through. Stop at golden yellow (EU Regulation 2017/2158, FSAI). Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service actually recommends the oven, as it is safer than a pan of oil.","variants.2":"In an air fryer (Airfryer): the EU regulation mentions 180 to 220 °C for preheating an oven or an air fryer. Stop at golden yellow: according to tests by industry and consumer groups cited by EFSA, an air fryer can produce 30 to 40% more acrylamide than an oil fryer.","variants.3":"In a single frying: thick fries (at least 14 mm across), 5 to 6 minutes at 175 °C, until pale golden (FSA, advice to chip shops in Northern Ireland, which advises against double frying to limit fat). Escoffier cooked his Pont-Neuf potatoes this way, in a single bath, until they were “crisp on the outside, while staying soft on the inside”.","variants.4":"In Morocco: Bladi.net cooks chicken in sauce (onions, garlic, coriander, ginger, saffron) and lays the fries on top just before serving."},"gratin-dauphinois":{"title":"Gratin dauphinois","tagline":"Meltingly tender potatoes, slowly cooked in milk and cream, under a thin golden crust: the real gratin from the Dauphiné, with no cheese or eggs.","region":"Dauphiné, in south-eastern France (Isère). The dish is said to have been born in the Quatre Montagnes area, around Villard-de-Lans and Autrans (Wikipedia; Larousse gastronomique quoted by Lyon Capitale). First known mention: a dinner on 12 July 1788, given by the Duke of Clermont-Tonnerre, lieutenant general of the Dauphiné, for the municipal officers of Gap (Wikipedia, citing the archivist Auguste Prudhomme; Lyon Capitale; Meilleur du Chef). It has had its own brotherhood since 17 January 2019 (legratindauphinois.fr, Wikipedia).","occasion":"A side dish for a roast, meat or poultry, or a main course with a green salad (Wikipedia, Chef Simon, legratindauphinois.fr). A classic of festive meals (Jow).","img_alt":"Golden gratin dauphinois in its dish","serving":"Hot, in its baking dish, with roast meat or poultry, or as a main course with a green salad (Wikipedia, Chef Simon, legratindauphinois.fr, Jow). Wikipedia notes that it is also eaten reheated, or cold. Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours (Anses, FSA), to be eaten within 48 hours (FSA; Anses says less than 3 days for a cooked dish), cold or reheated only once until steaming hot in the centre (FSA). Anses advises reheating in a covered dish until steam escapes. Opened milk and cream: follow the “once opened” instructions on the label (Anses).","ing.0.group":"Potatoes and for the dish","ing.pdt.name":"firm-fleshed or creamy-textured potatoes","ing.pdt.note":"Charlotte, Monalisa or Amandine type; neither green nor sprouting","ing.ail-plat.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail-plat.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail-plat.note":"for rubbing the dish","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.beurre.note":"very soft, for the dish and the top","ing.1.group":"Milk and cream","ing.lait.name":"whole milk","ing.creme.name":"full-fat liquid cream","ing.creme.note":"at least 30% fat (crème fleurette)","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"for the milk","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.poivre.name":"pepper","ing.muscade.name":"nutmeg","ing.muscade.note":"optional, for grating","equipment.0":"A shallow gratin dish, preferably earthenware or stoneware (Lyon Capitale, Escoffier, Wikipedia)","equipment.1":"A mandoline or a good knife","equipment.2":"A vegetable peeler and a chopping board","equipment.3":"A saucepan, for the milk","equipment.4":"A pointed knife, to check that it is cooked","steps.0.title":"Preheat the oven","steps.0.text":"Preheat the oven to 160 °C.","steps.1.title":"Peel the potatoes","steps.1.text":"Peel {{pdt}} of potatoes. Remove any green parts and sprouts. Wash them quickly, keeping them whole.","steps.1.tip":"Throw away any potato that is very green, damaged or bitter: green parts and sprouts concentrate solanine, which can cause poisoning, and cooking does not destroy it (BfR, German agency; ACIA, Canadian agency).","steps.2.title":"Cut thin slices","steps.2.text":"Cut the potatoes into thin, even slices, about 3 mm thick, with the mandoline or a knife. Do not rinse the slices or put them in water: their starch will bind the gratin.","steps.2.tip":"With a mandoline, be very careful of your fingers (Meilleur du Chef, legratindauphinois.fr).","steps.3.title":"Prepare the dish","steps.3.text":"Cut {{ail-plat:full}} in half. Rub the bottom and sides of the dish well with the garlic. Then butter the dish generously with some of the butter ({{beurre}} in total).","steps.3.tip":"The garlic goes on before the butter; very soft butter spreads more easily (École de cuisine Alain Ducasse).","steps.4.title":"Bring the milk to the boil","steps.4.text":"Peel and chop {{ail:full}}. Put the chopped garlic in a saucepan with {{lait}} of whole milk, {{sel}}, {{poivre}} and a little grated {{muscade}}. Bring to the boil, then turn off the heat.","steps.5.title":"Build up the layers","steps.5.text":"Arrange an even layer of slices in the dish. Season with salt and pepper, add a little nutmeg and a few spoonfuls of cream. Repeat until all the potatoes are used up, using {{creme}} of full-fat liquid cream in total.","steps.6.title":"Pour in the hot milk","steps.6.text":"Pour in the hot milk, with the garlic, until it barely covers the potatoes: the top slices should still be slightly visible. If there is any milk left, keep it.","steps.7.title":"Add the butter","steps.7.text":"Dot the rest of the butter in small pieces over the top.","steps.8.title":"Bake for 1 h 30 min","steps.8.text":"Bake for 1 h 30 min. The gratin should cook gently. Check the top from time to time: it should turn golden without burning.","steps.8.tip":"If the potatoes have soaked up all the liquid before they are cooked, add a little of the milk you kept (Lyon Capitale).","steps.8.timer.label":"Gratin in the oven","steps.8.timer.done":"Push a knife into the centre: it should go in effortlessly.","steps.9.title":"Check that it is cooked","steps.9.text":"Push the tip of a knife into the centre: it should slide in very easily. The top should be golden. If not, cook it longer, up to 2 hours in total.","steps.10.title":"Serve in the dish","steps.10.text":"Leave to rest for a few minutes, then serve hot, in the baking dish.","steps.10.tip":"Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours, to be eaten within 2 days, cold or reheated only once until steaming hot in the centre (FSA, Anses).","secrets.0.text":"Never rinse the slices or leave them to soak: their starch binds the milk and cream, and the gratin holds together.","secrets.0.source":"legratindauphinois.fr; Chef Simon; Jow; Meilleur du Chef; Wikipedia (Jean-François Piège: slices “not washed, not wiped”)","secrets.1.text":"Thin slices, all the same thickness, about 3 mm. Too thin, and they turn to mush; too thick, and they stay undercooked.","secrets.1.source":"Jow; Alain Ducasse (3 mm); Wikipedia (3 mm); Lyon Capitale (“of equal thickness”)","secrets.2.text":"Rub the dish with garlic before buttering it.","secrets.2.source":"École de cuisine Alain Ducasse (“the garlic must go on before the butter”); Escoffier; Lyon Capitale; Alpes Isère","secrets.3.text":"Season each layer with salt and pepper, not just the top.","secrets.3.source":"Lyon Capitale; Alpes Isère; Académie du Goût; legratindauphinois.fr","secrets.4.text":"The liquid should just cover the potatoes: the top slices stay slightly visible.","secrets.4.source":"Lyon Capitale; legratindauphinois.fr (“until barely covered”)","secrets.5.text":"Whole milk and full-fat cream: this is the mix in most of today's recipes. Originally, it was made with whole milk from the farm.","secrets.5.source":"Lyon Capitale; legratindauphinois.fr; Meilleur du Chef; Alpes Isère; Wikipedia (citing Catherine Roig)","secrets.6.text":"Slow cooking is everything: a low oven, and patience.","secrets.6.source":"Alpes Isère (“very slow cooking”); legratindauphinois.fr (“Gratin dauphinois cooks slowly”); Hélène Darroze (160 °C, at least 1 h 30 min)","secrets.7.text":"An oven that is too hot burns the top before the potatoes in the top layer are cooked.","secrets.7.source":"Lyon Capitale; legratindauphinois.fr (keep an eye on the top)","secrets.8.text":"If the potatoes have soaked up all the liquid too early, add a little more milk.","secrets.8.source":"Lyon Capitale","secrets.9.text":"No need for cheese to get a golden top: it is the cream that browns on the surface.","secrets.9.source":"Chef Simon (“a surface glaze”); Jow; Lyon Capitale","secrets.10.text":"Choose a firm-fleshed or creamy-textured potato, which soaks up the milk and cream without falling apart.","secrets.10.source":"Meilleur du Chef; Chef Simon (Amandine, Charlotte, Belle de Fontenay, Roseval…); Jow (Monalisa, Charlotte, Amandine); Académie du Goût (Charlotte, Roseval); Hélène Darroze (Mona Lisa, Spunta, Agatha)","secrets.11.text":"Made the day before and reheated, it cuts more neatly and tastes even better.","secrets.11.source":"Meilleur du Chef; Wikilivres; Jow (“just as good reheated the next day”)","secrets.12.text":"An earthenware dish holds the heat: the gratin stays hot for a long time.","secrets.12.source":"Wikipedia; Lyon Capitale and Escoffier (earthenware dish)","mistakes.0":"Rinsing the slices or soaking them: the starch washes away and the gratin no longer holds together (legratindauphinois.fr, Jow, Meilleur du Chef).","mistakes.1":"Cutting slices of different thicknesses: some stay firm, others turn to mush (Lyon Capitale, Jow).","mistakes.2":"Setting the oven too hot: the top burns and the potatoes in the top layer do not cook (Lyon Capitale).","mistakes.3":"Adding cheese or eggs and calling it gratin dauphinois: with cheese, it is a different gratin (Wikipedia, Wikilivres, Lyon Capitale).","mistakes.4":"Cutting yourself on the mandoline (Meilleur du Chef, legratindauphinois.fr).","mistakes.5":"Using potatoes that are green, sprouting, damaged or bitter (BfR, ACIA).","mistakes.6":"Leaving the gratin at room temperature for more than 2 hours (Anses, FSA).","mistakes.7":"Reheating leftovers several times: reheat them only once, until steaming hot (FSA).","variants.0":"Escoffier's version (1903): 1 beaten egg, 3/4 litre of boiled milk and 125 g of grated Gruyère per kilo of potatoes, with Gruyère on top and 40 to 45 minutes in the oven (Escoffier); according to Lyon Capitale, Paul Bocuse uses the same ingredients and Joël Robuchon uses Gruyère without eggs. Choose a cheese made with microbial coagulant, without animal rennet, if you want to be strict.","variants.1":"With cream only, no milk: this is “the real” gratin according to the Larousse gastronomique (quoted by Lyon Capitale), and the way the Oustau de Baumanière makes it (Wikipedia).","variants.2":"With the potatoes pre-cooked in the milk: Alain Ducasse (40 minutes at a simmer with thyme and bay leaf, then 20 minutes at 220 °C), Chef Simon (30 minutes in a casserole, then 30 minutes at 190 °C and 15 minutes under the grill with the cream), Meilleur du Chef (8 minutes, then 15 to 20 minutes at 180 °C), Anne-Sophie Pic, according to a reproduction of her recipe (15 to 20 minutes, then about 25 minutes at 180 °C).","variants.3":"Hélène Darroze's way: fresh thyme and Espelette pepper, raw cream and whole milk, 160 °C for at least 1 h 30 min (Académie du Goût). Raw cream is a raw-milk product: Anses strongly advises against it for young children, pregnant women and people with weakened immune systems.","variants.4":"With thin slices of celeriac between the layers of potato (Wikilivres).","variants.5":"Very gentle, very long cooking at 120 °C, until fully cooked (Wikilivres).","variants.6":"With a few breadcrumbs (École de cuisine Alain Ducasse, ingredient list).","variants.7":"In small individual cocottes (Chef Simon).","variants.8":"Pommes de terre à la savoyarde: like gratin dauphinois, but with consommé instead of milk (Escoffier); use a certified halal stock."},"hachis-parmentier":{"title":"Hachis parmentier (French cottage pie)","tagline":"Thoroughly cooked minced beef with onion, under homemade mash browned in the oven: the great French family dish, without wine.","region":"France. A Parisian dish according to Wikipedia (citing the journalist Frédéric Zégierman), named in honour of Antoine-Augustin Parmentier (1737-1813), who popularised the potato in France. Its cousins: British cottage pie and Quebec's pâté chinois (Wikipedia).","occasion":"A comforting, economical family dish. It is also the classic French dish for using up leftover meat, such as pot-au-feu or roast (Wikipedia, Chef Simon).","img_alt":"Hachis parmentier with a golden top, already cut into: the mash over the minced meat","serving":"Piping hot, straight from the oven (Meilleur du Chef, Académie du Goût), as a one-dish meal. Inspirations Cuisine serves it with a green salad. Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours (Anses, FSA), to be eaten within 48 hours (FSA; Anses says less than 3 days for a cooked dish); otherwise, into the freezer (FSA). Reheat only once, in the oven or microwave, until steaming hot in the centre; a large dish reheats better in small portions (FSA). Anses advises reheating in a covered dish until steam escapes. Defrost in the fridge and eat within 24 hours (FSA; Anses says 3 days at most, without refreezing).","ing.0.group":"Homemade mash","ing.pdt.name":"floury potatoes","ing.pdt.note":"Bintje or Agria type; neither green nor sprouting","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"a large panful, salted","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.beurre.note":"in pieces","ing.lait.name":"milk","ing.muscade.name":"nutmeg","ing.muscade.note":"for grating","ing.1.group":"Meat","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.huile.note":"a drizzle; or neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.oignon.name":"medium onion","ing.oignon.pl":"medium onions","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.boeuf.name":"halal minced beef","ing.boeuf.note":"very fresh; keep it in the fridge until the last moment","ing.persil.name":"parsley","ing.persil.note":"optional","ing.2.group":"Seasoning and for the dish","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.poivre.name":"pepper","ing.beurre-plat.name":"butter","ing.beurre-plat.note":"a small knob, for the dish","ing.fromage.name":"grated cheese","ing.fromage.note":"optional: a small handful of Emmental, Comté or Gruyère; made with microbial coagulant, without animal rennet, if you want to be strict","equipment.0":"A large saucepan and a colander","equipment.1":"A vegetable peeler and a knife","equipment.2":"Two chopping boards: one for raw meat, one for everything else (Anses)","equipment.3":"A large frying pan or sauté pan, and a wooden spoon","equipment.4":"A food mill or a potato masher (not a blender)","equipment.5":"A small saucepan, for the milk","equipment.6":"A gratin dish","equipment.7":"A cooking thermometer (optional)","steps.0.title":"Peel the potatoes","steps.0.text":"Peel {{pdt}} of potatoes. Remove any green parts and sprouts. Wash them, then cut the largest ones in half or into quarters, so that all the pieces are the same size.","steps.0.tip":"Throw away any potato that is very green, damaged or bitter. Green parts and sprouts concentrate solanine, which can cause poisoning, and cooking does not destroy it (BfR, German agency; ACIA, Canadian agency). Peeling removes a good part of it (EFSA).","steps.1.title":"Cook the potatoes","steps.1.text":"Put the potatoes in a large saucepan and cover them with cold salted {{eau}}. Bring to the boil, then cook for about 30 minutes. They are done when the tip of a knife slides in without resistance.","steps.1.tip":"While the potatoes are cooking, prepare the meat.","steps.1.timer.label":"Potatoes","steps.1.timer.done":"Pierce a piece: the knife should go in without resistance.","steps.2.title":"Chop the onion and garlic","steps.2.text":"Peel and finely chop {{oignon:full}} and {{ail:full}}. Wash and chop {{persil}} of parsley.","steps.2.tip":"Cut the vegetables before you touch the raw meat, or use another board: Anses advises one board for raw meat and another for everything else.","steps.2.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.2.img.caption":"Chopping herbs","steps.3.title":"Soften the onion","steps.3.text":"In the large frying pan, heat a drizzle of {{huile}}. Add the onion and cook, stirring, until it turns translucent. Add the garlic just before the onion has finished cooking, and stir.","steps.4.title":"Cook the meat","steps.4.text":"Add {{boeuf}} of minced beef. Cook for about 10 to 15 minutes, stirring often and breaking up the meat well with the spoon. It is ready when it has browned all over, with no trace of pink, and is steaming hot. Wash your hands with soap after touching the raw meat.","steps.4.tip":"Minced meat must always be cooked right through: mincing spreads bacteria from the surface throughout the meat (FSA, British agency). Anses recommends a core temperature of 70 °C, especially for young children and elderly people.","steps.5.title":"Season the meat","steps.5.text":"Add {{sel}} and {{poivre}}, then the chopped parsley. Stir and take off the heat.","steps.5.tip":"If there is a lot of fat in the bottom of the pan, spoon it off (Chef Simon).","steps.6.title":"Turn on the oven","steps.6.text":"Preheat the oven to 180 °C. Grease the gratin dish with a small knob of {{beurre-plat}}.","steps.7.title":"Heat the milk","steps.7.text":"Heat {{lait}} of milk in a small saucepan until it is very hot.","steps.8.title":"Mash the potatoes","steps.8.text":"Drain the potatoes. Straight away, while they are still very hot, put them through the food mill or mash them with the potato masher. Do not use a blender: the mash would turn gluey.","steps.8.tip":"Throw away the cooking water: the German agency BfR advises against reusing it.","steps.9.title":"Finish the mash","steps.9.text":"Add {{beurre}} of butter in pieces and stir. Then pour in the hot milk a little at a time, stirring with a spatula, until the mash is soft but not runny. Season with salt and pepper and grate in a little {{muscade}}.","steps.9.tip":"The butter goes in while the potatoes are still very hot. The milk goes in a little at a time: poured in all at once, it makes the mash too runny (750g, Pascale Weeks).","steps.10.title":"Assemble the hachis","steps.10.text":"Spread the meat over the bottom of the dish. Cover it with all the mash and smooth the top with a spatula. If you like, sprinkle with a little {{fromage}}.","steps.10.tip":"Make ridges in the mash with a fork: it will brown better (Pradel).","steps.11.title":"Bake for 30 minutes","steps.11.text":"Bake for about 30 minutes, until the top is nicely golden. The centre of the dish must be piping hot and steaming.","steps.11.tip":"If the hachis was made ahead and comes out of the fridge, leave it in longer, until it is steaming hot in the centre (Chef Simon; FSA).","steps.11.timer.label":"Hachis in the oven","steps.11.timer.done":"Is the top golden? Take the dish out of the oven.","steps.12.title":"Serve straight away","steps.12.text":"Serve the hachis piping hot, straight from the oven.","steps.12.tip":"Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours, to be eaten within 2 days, reheated only once until steaming hot in the centre (FSA, Anses).","secrets.0.text":"For the mash, use floury potatoes, such as Bintje or Agria.","secrets.0.source":"Meilleur du Chef (Bintje); Alain Ducasse (Agria); Académie du Goût (floury potatoes); 750g, Pascale Weeks (floury potatoes)","secrets.1.text":"Mash the potatoes with a food mill or a potato masher, never in a blender: it releases too much starch and makes the mash gluey.","secrets.1.source":"Chef Simon (“gluey” mash); 750g, Pascale Weeks","secrets.2.text":"Add the butter while the potatoes are still very hot, then the hot milk.","secrets.2.source":"Meilleur du Chef; Escoffier (boiling milk); 750g, Pascale Weeks","secrets.3.text":"Pour in the milk a little at a time: the mash should stay soft without becoming runny.","secrets.3.source":"Chef Simon (“soft without being runny”); Escoffier (“little by little”); 750g, Pascale Weeks","secrets.4.text":"A little grated nutmeg in the mash is the classic flavouring.","secrets.4.source":"Meilleur du Chef; Chef Simon (mashed potato); Inspirations Cuisine; Pradel","secrets.5.text":"First soften the onion until translucent, and only then add the garlic.","secrets.5.source":"Meilleur du Chef (garlic added before the onion has finished cooking); Hervé Cuisine (garlic added after the onion, with the meat)","secrets.6.text":"Minced meat is always cooked right through: no trace of pink left, and steaming hot in the centre.","secrets.6.source":"FSA (British agency); Anses (70 °C core temperature for vulnerable groups)","secrets.7.text":"Remove the excess fat from the meat before putting it in the dish.","secrets.7.source":"Chef Simon (“having removed the fat if necessary”)","secrets.8.text":"Hachis parmentier is the ideal dish for leftovers: beef from a pot-au-feu or a roast, minced, instead of the raw meat.","secrets.8.source":"Wikipedia (Frédéric Zégierman); Chef Simon; Meilleur du Chef; Alain Ducasse","secrets.9.text":"Smooth the mash well with a spatula; ridges made with a fork help it brown.","secrets.9.source":"Meilleur du Chef, Académie du Goût and Chef Simon (smoothing); Pradel (ridges)","secrets.10.text":"You can make the hachis the day before and reheat it at mealtime. Put it in the fridge within 2 hours.","secrets.10.source":"Meilleur du Chef (answer from Chef Philippe, 6 February 2020); Anses and FSA (2 hours at most out of the fridge)","secrets.11.text":"The baking time depends on the ingredients: around thirty minutes, depending on whether they are still hot or cold.","secrets.11.source":"Chef Simon","secrets.12.text":"Serve straight from the oven.","secrets.12.source":"Meilleur du Chef; Académie du Goût","mistakes.0":"Putting raw minced meat straight under the mash: always cook it in the frying pan first, until no pink remains (FSA, Anses). One user-submitted recipe on 750g does this: do not follow it.","mistakes.1":"Blending the mash: it turns gluey (Chef Simon, 750g).","mistakes.2":"Pouring in all the milk at once: the mash becomes too runny (750g, Pascale Weeks).","mistakes.3":"Cutting the vegetables on the raw-meat board without washing it (Anses).","mistakes.4":"Forgetting to wash your hands with soap after touching the raw meat (Anses, FSA).","mistakes.5":"Keeping potatoes that are green, sprouting, damaged or bitter (BfR, ACIA).","mistakes.6":"Leaving the dish at room temperature for more than 2 hours (Anses, FSA).","mistakes.7":"Reheating leftovers several times: reheat them only once, until steaming hot (FSA).","mistakes.8":"Using sausage meat or a pork and veal mix, as some recipes suggest (750g, Chef Simon): here, only halal minced beef.","mistakes.9":"Adding a stock cube or a cheese without checking that they are halal.","variants.0":"The old-fashioned way, with leftover pot-au-feu (halal beef): the cooked meat, minced and mixed with softened onion, parsley and an egg (Meilleur du Chef; Wikipedia, citing Frédéric Zégierman). Alain Ducasse adds shallots and fresh breadcrumbs soaked in milk.","variants.1":"In three layers: mash, meat, then mash (Meilleur du Chef, Académie du Goût, Hervé Cuisine).","variants.2":"With carrots: cooked with the potatoes, in the mash (Académie du Goût), sautéed with the onion (Hervé Cuisine), or finely diced and added to the meat (Inspirations Cuisine).","variants.3":"With tomato: diced fresh tomatoes (Académie du Goût, Inspirations Cuisine) or tomato purée (Pradel), simmered with the meat.","variants.4":"More aromatic: thyme and four-spice mix (Hervé Cuisine), or herbes de Provence (Académie du Goût). Hervé Cuisine and Inspirations Cuisine add beef stock: use one that is certified halal.","variants.5":"Richer mash: an egg yolk (Hervé Cuisine), or a little crème fraîche (Académie du Goût, Chef Simon, Inspirations Cuisine).","variants.6":"With a browned topping of grated cheese (Chef Simon, Académie du Goût, Hervé Cuisine) or breadcrumbs (Meilleur du Chef; panko in Alain Ducasse's version).","variants.7":"With shallots as well as the onion (Chef Simon, Alain Ducasse).","variants.8":"In individual portions, in small cocottes (Hervé Cuisine).","variants.9":"Escoffier's “hachis Parmentier” (1903) was a different dish: potatoes baked in the oven, hollowed out, then stuffed with their flesh sautéed in butter and with diced meat. Today's layered dish is closer to his “hachis à la grand-mère”: minced meat bound with mash, in a buttered earthenware dish, under a thin layer of browned mash (Escoffier).","variants.10":"Elsewhere: cottage pie in the United Kingdom, and pâté chinois in Quebec, with a layer of sweetcorn (Wikipedia)."},"harcha":{"title":"Harcha, pan-cooked semolina flatbreads","darija":"harcha (also harsha)","tagline":"Shortbread-like semolina flatbreads, cooked in a dry frying pan, slightly crisp on the outside and soft on the inside, to be eaten warm with honey and butter.","region":"Morocco; also Algeria (origin disputed: the Middle Atlas according to the English Wikipedia, Algeria according to the French Wikipedia)","occasion":"Breakfast, afternoon snack with tea, Ramadan iftar","serving":"Warm, when it is slightly crisp on the outside and soft on the inside (Bladi.net; My Moroccan Food). The traditional topping in Morocco is a syrup of equal parts melted honey and butter, with a few drops of orange blossom water if you like (My Moroccan Food). You can also split it in two and fill it with cheese, jam or honey (Cooking with Alia), for example jben, the Moroccan fresh cheese (Morocco World News). With mint tea (Wikipedia; Amour de cuisine) or a glass of milk (Morocco World News), at breakfast, as an afternoon snack or for the Ramadan iftar (My Moroccan Food; Wikipedia; Hespress). It keeps for 1 to 2 days in a clean tea towel: warm it up for a few seconds in a frying pan (Amour de cuisine).","ing.0.group":"Dough","ing.semoule.name":"fine durum wheat semolina","ing.semoule.note":"this is what gives the shortbread-like texture: do not replace it","ing.sucre.name":"caster sugar","ing.sucre.note":"you can use less: other recipes use only 2 tbsp for 500 g semolina","ing.levure-chimique.name":"baking powder","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.beurre.name":"unsalted butter, very soft","ing.beurre.note":"at room temperature","ing.lait.name":"milk","ing.lait.note":"approximately: add it gradually, the amount depends on your semolina","ing.1.group":"For coating","ing.semoule-enrobage.name":"fine or medium semolina","ing.semoule-enrobage.note":"approximately","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.miel.name":"Honey","ing.miel.pl":"Honey","ing.miel.note":"in equal parts with the butter, for the syrup","ing.beurre-service.name":"Butter","ing.beurre-service.pl":"Butter","ing.beurre-service.note":"in equal parts with the honey","ing.fleur-oranger.name":"A few drops of orange blossom water","ing.fleur-oranger.pl":"A few drops of orange blossom water","ing.fleur-oranger.note":"optional, in the syrup","ing.jben.name":"Jben (Moroccan fresh cheese)","ing.jben.pl":"Jben (Moroccan fresh cheese)","ing.jben.note":"optional","ing.confiture.name":"Jam","ing.confiture.pl":"Jam","ing.confiture.note":"optional","equipment.0":"A large mixing bowl","equipment.1":"Kitchen scales and a measuring jug","equipment.2":"A clean tea towel or cling film to cover the dough","equipment.3":"A deep plate for the coating semolina","equipment.4":"A large heavy-based (cast-iron) or non-stick frying pan","equipment.5":"A flat spatula","equipment.6":"A small saucepan for the honey-butter syrup","equipment.7":"A very sharp knife","steps.0.title":"Mix the dry ingredients","steps.0.text":"In a large mixing bowl, mix {{semoule}} fine semolina, {{sucre}} sugar, {{levure-chimique}} baking powder and {{sel}} salt.","steps.1.title":"Rub in the butter","steps.1.text":"Add {{beurre}} very soft butter. Rub the mixture between your palms until all the semolina is moist and every grain is coated in butter: this is what makes the harcha shortbread-like and light.","steps.2.title":"Add the milk gradually","steps.2.text":"Pour in half of the {{lait}} of milk and mix. Add the rest gradually, just until you have a dough that holds together: depending on your semolina, you will need a little more or a little less. Do not knead, just bring it together: if the dough is a little sticky, that is normal.","steps.3.title":"Let the semolina swell","steps.3.text":"Cover the bowl and leave to rest for 20 minutes. The semolina soaks up the milk and swells: the dough becomes drier. If it crumbles, add a splash of milk.","steps.3.timer.label":"Dough resting","steps.3.timer.done":"The semolina has swelled. If the dough crumbles, add a splash of milk, then shape the flatbreads.","steps.4.title":"Shape the flatbreads","steps.4.text":"Divide the dough into equal balls, one per harcha. Flatten each ball by hand into a disc about 10 cm in diameter and 1 cm thick. No thicker: otherwise they will not cook evenly.","steps.5.title":"Roll in the semolina","steps.5.text":"Pour {{semoule-enrobage}} semolina into a deep plate. Lay each flatbread in it, then turn it over to coat both sides: this is what will give a crisp crust.","steps.6.title":"Heat the frying pan dry","steps.6.text":"Heat a large heavy-based (cast-iron) or non-stick frying pan over medium heat, without any fat. When it is hot, turn down to low or medium-low heat. This is very important: over too high a heat, the harcha burns on the outside and stays raw inside.","steps.7.title":"Cook the first side","steps.7.text":"Place a first batch of flatbreads in the hot pan. Cook for about 5 minutes without touching them, until the underside is nicely golden.","steps.7.timer.label":"First side","steps.7.timer.done":"Lift an edge with the spatula: if the underside is nicely golden, turn the flatbreads over. If not, leave them a little longer.","steps.8.title":"Turn over gently","steps.8.text":"Turn them over gently with a flat spatula, as they are crumbly, and cook the other side for about 5 minutes. They are ready when both sides are golden and they feel dry to the touch. If they brown too quickly, turn down the heat. Between batches, wipe out the pan so that fallen semolina does not burn or stick to the flatbreads, then cook the next ones in the same way.","steps.8.timer.label":"Second side","steps.8.timer.done":"Golden on both sides and dry to the touch? Take them out and move on to the next batch.","steps.9.title":"Make the honey-butter syrup","steps.9.text":"In a small saucepan, melt honey and butter together, in equal parts. Add a few drops of orange blossom water if you like. This is the traditional topping for harcha in Morocco.","steps.10.title":"Serve warm","steps.10.text":"Let the harchas cool a little, then split them in two with a very sharp knife if you want to fill them: when too hot, they crumble. Serve them warm, topped with honey-butter syrup or filled with jben or jam, with mint tea.","secrets.0.text":"Rub in first: rub the semolina and butter between your palms until every grain is coated in fat, before adding the milk. This is what gives the crumbly, light texture.","secrets.0.source":"Amour de cuisine (Soulef Zaoui); My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); Milk Street (fat added before the liquid limits gluten)","secrets.1.text":"Here the butter is soft, as with My Moroccan Food and Bladi.net: according to Milk Street, melted butter gives a slightly heavier, greasier texture. Choumicha and Amour de cuisine, however, use melted butter mixed with oil: it is a choice, not a rule.","secrets.1.source":"My Moroccan Food and Bladi.net (soft butter); Milk Street; Choumicha and Amour de cuisine (melted butter and oil)","secrets.2.text":"Add the milk a little at a time: the amount depends on your semolina. Stop as soon as the dough holds together.","secrets.2.source":"My Moroccan Food; Morocco World News (liquid added gradually)","secrets.3.text":"Do not knead, just bring the dough together: kneading activates the gluten and makes the harchas hard. A slightly sticky dough is normal.","secrets.3.source":"Amour de cuisine","secrets.4.text":"Do not skip the rest: the semolina needs 20 minutes to soak up the milk and swell. A well-rested dough gives soft harchas, not crumbly ones.","secrets.4.source":"My Moroccan Food (20 min); Amour de cuisine (20 to 30 min)","secrets.5.text":"After resting, the dough is drier; that is normal. If it crumbles, add a splash of milk.","secrets.5.source":"Cooking with Alia","secrets.6.text":"Coat both sides in semolina before cooking: this is what makes the crust crisp.","secrets.6.source":"My Moroccan Food; Paula Wolfert's recipe as described by Milk Street","secrets.7.text":"1 cm thick, no more, for even cooking. Thinner, the harcha is crisp; thicker, it is soft.","secrets.7.source":"My Moroccan Food, Bladi.net and Amour de cuisine (1 cm); Cooking with Alia (thin or thick)","secrets.8.text":"Cook it dry, with no fat in the pan.","secrets.8.source":"Bladi.net (“dry pan”); Julie Andrieu (“without fat”)","secrets.9.text":"Low to medium-low heat: preheat the pan over medium heat, then turn it down before putting in the flatbreads. Otherwise they burn on the outside and stay raw inside.","secrets.9.source":"Cooking with Alia; My Moroccan Food (medium-low heat); Bladi.net (low heat)","secrets.10.text":"Turn it over gently, with a flat spatula, when the underside is nicely golden: it is crumbly. The harcha is done when it is golden on both sides and dry to the touch.","secrets.10.source":"Amour de cuisine (turn gently); Choumicha (flat spatula); Cooking with Alia","secrets.11.text":"Let it cool a little before splitting it, with a very sharp knife: when too hot, it crumbles.","secrets.11.source":"Cooking with Alia","secrets.12.text":"The traditional topping is a syrup of equal parts melted honey and butter, with a few drops of orange blossom water if you like.","secrets.12.source":"My Moroccan Food","mistakes.0":"Pouring in all the milk at once: depending on the semolina, you need more or less (My Moroccan Food; Morocco World News).","mistakes.1":"Kneading the dough like bread: it activates the gluten and makes the harchas hard (Amour de cuisine).","mistakes.2":"Skipping the rest: the semolina does not have time to soak up the milk (My Moroccan Food; Amour de cuisine).","mistakes.3":"Making flatbreads thicker than 1 cm: they no longer cook evenly (Amour de cuisine).","mistakes.4":"Forgetting the semolina coating: it is what gives the crisp crust (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.5":"Cooking over high heat: the harcha burns on the outside and stays raw inside (Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.6":"Splitting it while still very hot, or with a knife that does not cut well: it crumbles (Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.7":"Replacing the semolina with something else: the recipe only works with semolina (Cooking with Alia).","variants.0":"Butter and oil: Amour de cuisine uses 100 ml melted butter and 50 ml oil for 500 g semolina; Julie Andrieu 40 g melted butter and 1 tbsp oil for 250 g; Morocco World News ½ cup of each for ½ kg.","variants.1":"With water and no raising agent, the simplest: 400 g fine semolina, 100 g softened butter, 50 g sugar, ½ tsp salt and ¾ glass of water; 1 cm flatbreads cooked over low heat in a dry pan, 5 to 10 min per side (Bladi.net).","variants.2":"Crunchier: half fine semolina, half medium semolina in the dough (Amour de cuisine).","variants.3":"One large harcha: a softer dough (20 cl milk for 250 g semolina) spread with a wet hand in the hot pan dusted with medium semolina, 1 cm thick at most, with medium semolina on top, 8 to 10 min per side (Julie Andrieu). Wikipedia mentions harchas as big as a lorry tyre; in Fez, Christopher Kimball ate one almost as wide as a tagine (Milk Street).","variants.4":"In the oven: a slightly runny dough made with oil and hot milk, spread in a cold buttered frying pan well dusted with semolina, topped with semolina and small pieces of butter, then put in the oven; no temperature or time given (Lalla Moulati, who attributes it to Choumicha).","variants.5":"Stuffed: a dough made with fresh yeast (butter and oil, milk and water, 30 min rest); each ball is hollowed out, filled with jben, black olives and thyme, closed up, flattened and then browned in a frying pan (Morocco World News).","variants.6":"Savoury with zaatar and olives: no sugar, with 3 tbsp zaatar, 2 tbsp chopped black olives and 1 tsp salt for 250 g semolina (My Moroccan Food). Nargisse Benkabbou also describes it as sweet or savoury, flavoured, or stuffed with dried fruit and nuts, meat or herbs.","variants.7":"With buttermilk or yoghurt: in the Rif, buttermilk or yoghurt thinned with water replaces the milk (Wikipedia); Milk Street makes its version with yoghurt.","variants.8":"With a pinch of yeast in addition to the baking powder (Amour de cuisine).","variants.9":"With crushed fennel seeds, cumin, coriander or dried herbs (Julie Andrieu).","variants.10":"In Khenifra, the iftar harcha stands out: it is made with medicinal plants (Hespress).","variants.11":"As a sandwich, filled with cheese or confit meat (Wikipedia)."},"harira":{"title":"Traditional Moroccan harira","darija":"Hrira","tagline":"The iftar soup, as silky as its name (harir, silk): tomatoes, lentils, chickpeas and fresh herbs, thickened with tadouira.","region":"All over Morocco; a mild version without caraway or allspice, like harira fassia (Fez)","occasion":"Ramadan iftar, served every day at the breaking of the fast; all year round as a starter or a light dinner, and at family gatherings","serving":"At the Ramadan iftar, harira is served piping hot as soon as the fast is broken, every day of the month. Everyone squeezes a lemon wedge into their bowl. Around it: dates (or dried figs), chebakia, hard-boiled eggs to dip in salt and cumin, and often msemen, briouates or homemade bread. Outside Ramadan, it is served as a starter, as a light dinner or at family gatherings.","ing.0.group":"Broth","ing.viande.name":"lamb or beef","ing.viande.note":"halal, cut into cubes of about 1 cm","ing.huile.name":"olive or sunflower oil","ing.smen.name":"smen (aged salted butter)","ing.smen.note":"optional, but it adds a distinctive flavour","ing.oignon.name":"large onion","ing.oignon.pl":"large onions","ing.celeri.name":"celery stick with its leaves","ing.celeri.pl":"celery sticks with their leaves","ing.celeri.note":"in Morocco, a fine-leaved celery similar to Chinese celery is used: a small bunch","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"small bunch","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.coriandre.note":"small bunch","ing.pois-chiches.name":"dried chickpeas","ing.pois-chiches.note":"soak the day before","ing.lentilles.name":"green or brown lentils","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.concentre.name":"tomato purée","ing.concentre.note":"heaped","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.gingembre.note":"not fresh ginger","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.cannelle.name":"ground cinnamon","ing.cannelle.note":"optional","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end of cooking","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.1.group":"Tadouira (thickener)","ing.farine.name":"plain white flour","ing.farine.note":"not wholemeal flour","ing.eau-tadouira.name":"water","ing.concentre-tadouira.name":"tomato purée","ing.concentre-tadouira.note":"heaped","ing.2.group":"Finishing and serving","ing.vermicelles.name":"fine vermicelli","ing.vermicelles.note":"or broken thin spaghetti","ing.coriandre-fin.name":"chopped coriander","ing.coriandre-fin.note":"taken from the bunch","ing.citron.name":"lemon, cut into wedges","ing.citron.pl":"lemons, cut into wedges","equipment.0":"Large heavy-based stockpot (about 6 l) or pressure cooker","equipment.1":"Saucepan for poaching the tomatoes","equipment.2":"Blender or food mill","equipment.3":"Grater","equipment.4":"Whisk and bowl","equipment.5":"Fine sieve","equipment.6":"Clean tea towel (for skinning the chickpeas)","equipment.7":"Ladle","steps.0.title":"The day before: soak the chickpeas","steps.0.text":"Rinse the chickpeas ({{pois-chiches}}) and cover them with plenty of water. Leave them to soak overnight.","steps.1.title":"Skin the chickpeas, pick over the lentils","steps.1.text":"Drain the chickpeas, then rub them vigorously in a tea towel to loosen their skins, and remove the skins. Pick over the lentils ({{lentilles}}) to remove any small stones, then rinse them.","steps.2.title":"Make the tadouira ahead","steps.2.text":"Whisk the flour ({{farine}}) with the water ({{eau-tadouira}}) until the mixture is perfectly smooth. Pass it through a fine sieve, cover and leave to rest until you are ready to thicken the soup, a few hours if you can.","steps.3.title":"Purée the tomatoes","steps.3.text":"Poach the tomatoes ({{tomates}}) for 5 minutes in a pan of simmering water, then peel them. Blend them to a smooth purée, or pass them through a food mill to remove the seeds as well.","steps.3.timer.label":"Poaching tomatoes","steps.3.timer.done":"Take out the tomatoes: the skin should come away easily.","steps.4.title":"Grate, chop and cut","steps.4.text":"Grate {{oignon:full}}. Very finely chop {{celeri:full}}, the parsley ({{persil}}) and the coriander ({{coriandre}}), removing the thick stalks, and set aside a little chopped coriander ({{coriandre-fin}}) for the end. Cut the meat ({{viande}}) into cubes of about 1 cm.","steps.4.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.4.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.5.title":"Sear the meat","steps.5.text":"In the large stockpot, heat the oil ({{huile}}) over medium heat. Fry the meat, stirring, until it changes colour.","steps.6.title":"Cook the aromatics and spices","steps.6.text":"Add the grated onion, chopped herbs, skinned chickpeas, lentils, puréed tomatoes and the tomato purée ({{concentre}}). Add the ginger ({{gingembre}}), turmeric ({{curcuma}}), pepper ({{poivre}}), cinnamon ({{cannelle}}), salt ({{sel}}) and smen ({{smen}}). Let it cook for 10 minutes over low heat, stirring from time to time.","steps.6.timer.label":"Aromatics over low heat","steps.6.timer.done":"Pour in the water and bring to the boil.","steps.7.title":"Add the water and cook","steps.7.text":"Pour in the water ({{eau}}) and bring to the boil over high heat. Cover and cook for 1 hour over medium heat, stirring from time to time, until the chickpeas are tender. In a pressure cooker, allow 20 to 30 minutes once it has come up to pressure.","steps.7.timer.label":"Cooking the broth","steps.7.timer.done":"Taste a chickpea: if it is tender, move on to the tadouira. If not, cook a little longer.","steps.8.title":"Thicken with the tadouira","steps.8.text":"Whisk the tomato purée ({{concentre-tadouira}}) into the tadouira. Turn up the heat so that the soup boils, then pour in the tadouira little by little, stirring constantly. Stop as soon as the harira is velvety and silky, without being thick: you may not need all of it. If it is too thick, add a little boiling water.","steps.9.title":"Add the vermicelli","steps.9.text":"As soon as the soup is boiling again, add the vermicelli ({{vermicelles}}), stirring. Simmer over medium heat, stirring regularly, and skim off the foam with a spoon. It is ready when the vermicelli is tender and the foam from the tadouira has gone.","steps.9.timer.label":"Vermicelli","steps.9.timer.done":"Vermicelli tender and no more foam? If not, give it another 2 or 3 minutes.","steps.10.title":"Taste and flavour","steps.10.text":"Taste and adjust the salt and pepper. Add the coriander you set aside and let it simmer gently for 5 minutes, then turn off the heat.","steps.10.timer.label":"Final simmer","steps.10.timer.done":"Turn off the heat: your harira is ready.","steps.11.title":"Serve piping hot","steps.11.text":"Serve the harira piping hot in bowls, with {{citron:full}}, to squeeze in at the last moment. Alongside, set out dates, chebakia and hard-boiled eggs to dip in a little salt and cumin.","steps.12.title":"Store and reheat the leftovers","steps.12.text":"As it cools, a skin forms: simply stir it back into the soup. Reheat over low or medium heat, stirring often so that the lentils do not catch on the bottom, and thin with a little boiling water if needed. It keeps for 3 days in the fridge, in an airtight container.","secrets.0.text":"Skin the chickpeas after soaking, rubbing them vigorously in a tea towel: all the Moroccan recipes consulted want them without their skins.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc; Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Royal Mansour (chef Karim Ben Baba); Choumicha (Gulf News); Morocco World News","secrets.1.text":"Ground ginger only: fresh ginger is not traditional in Moroccan slow-cooked dishes.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.2.text":"No ras el hanout: traditional harira does not contain any, not even in Choumicha's version.","secrets.2.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida","secrets.3.text":"Choose very ripe tomatoes: they give a lovely colour and a more intense flavour.","secrets.3.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Taste of Maroc","secrets.4.text":"In Morocco, harira is made with a fine-leaved celery, similar to Chinese celery; Choumicha keeps only the leaves.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha (Gulf News)","secrets.5.text":"Make the tadouira ahead: well whisked, passed through a fine sieve and rested for a few hours, it will not form lumps.","secrets.5.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Taste of Maroc","secrets.6.text":"Pour the tadouira little by little into soup that is boiling vigorously, stirring constantly.","secrets.6.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Taste of Maroc","secrets.7.text":"It all comes down to how much tadouira you add: stop when the harira is as silky as cream. It should be velvety, not thick.","secrets.7.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Taste of Maroc; Bladi.net","secrets.8.text":"After thickening, let it simmer until the foam produced by the tadouira has gone.","secrets.8.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Taste of Maroc","secrets.9.text":"A little fresh coriander at the end of cooking livens up the aroma; Choumicha also always adds a little celery and smen.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha (La Libre Belgique, 2014); Gâteau et cuisine Rachida","secrets.10.text":"Smen (aged salted butter) is optional, but it gives a distinctive flavour.","secrets.10.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida; Taste of Maroc","secrets.11.text":"Harira is even better the next day, once the flavours have melded.","secrets.11.source":"Choumicha (Gulf News)","secrets.12.text":"If you are planning on leftovers, use broken thin spaghetti: vermicelli swells in the soup.","secrets.12.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida","secrets.13.text":"To freeze it, do so before adding the tadouira and, if possible, before the vermicelli.","secrets.13.source":"Taste of Maroc; Gâteau et cuisine Rachida","mistakes.0":"Pouring in the tadouira all at once or without stirring: it forms lumps.","mistakes.1":"Thickening soup that is not boiling hard enough: it must come back to the boil quickly.","mistakes.2":"Adding all the tadouira without watching the texture: the harira should stay velvety, not thick.","mistakes.3":"Turning off the heat before the foam from the tadouira has gone.","mistakes.4":"Leaving the skins on the chickpeas.","mistakes.5":"Adding ras el hanout or fresh ginger.","mistakes.6":"Using wholemeal flour for the tadouira.","mistakes.7":"Reheating over high heat without stirring: the lentils stick and burn.","mistakes.8":"Freezing the harira once it has been thickened with the tadouira.","variants.0":"Rice instead of vermicelli: add it to the broth about 30 minutes before thickening (15 minutes in a pressure cooker) — Taste of Maroc.","variants.1":"Harira fassia (Fez): the least spiced, without caraway (karwiya) or allspice; this is the profile of this recipe — Gâteau et cuisine Rachida.","variants.2":"Harira oujdia (Oujda): made with a leaven kneaded the day before and plenty of parsley, coriander and celery (Aujourd'hui le Maroc, reported by Yabiladi); according to Rachida, strongly flavoured with caraway and allspice, and some families add dried fava beans to the chickpeas — Gâteau et cuisine Rachida.","variants.3":"Harira bidaouia (Casablanca): a beaten egg is most often added after the tadouira (Gâteau et cuisine Rachida); some families even thicken the harira with eggs instead of tadouira (Taste of Maroc).","variants.4":"Old-style fermented tadouira: made with a leaven by grandmothers, which made the harira easier to digest and slightly tangy (Gâteau et cuisine Rachida), or with yeast and 1 to 2 days of fermentation (Taste of Maroc).","variants.5":"Thickening with cornflour mixed with water: the Royal Mansour version, and a gluten-free option according to Taste of Maroc.","variants.6":"A little saffron in the broth: Royal Mansour, Morocco World News (2021), Le Courrier de l'Atlas.","variants.7":"A few soup bones added to the broth (optional) — Taste of Maroc.","variants.8":"Choumicha's version published by Gulf News: “harira” spices in equal parts (turmeric, cumin, ginger, cinnamon, black pepper) with a hint of cayenne, and the smen added right at the end.","variants.9":"Without meat: a vegetarian version exists, as the meat mainly adds flavour — Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News (2021)."},"kefta-poele":{"title":"Pan-fried kefta","darija":"Kefta","tagline":"Minced meat with herbs, cumin and paprika, shaped into small patties and browned in a frying pan: Moroccan kefta, ready in 30 minutes, with khobz and a fresh salad.","region":"Morocco. Kefta is an institution there: every family knows where to buy and eat the best, often on the outskirts of towns and in small towns, at butchers who grill it and serve it on the spot (My Moroccan Food). None of the sources read gives a precise region of origin.","occasion":"A quick weeknight dinner, for evenings when you are tired. In Morocco, people also eat grilled kefta at the butcher's, with very sweet mint tea (My Moroccan Food).","img_alt":"Cooked kefta balls on a plate, with a round loaf and small bowls of sauce and spices","serving":"Serve the kefta piping hot, straight from the pan (Cooking With Alia; Choumicha). In Morocco, it is eaten with bread (Choumicha; Cooking With Alia), mint tea (Choumicha; Cooking With Alia; My Moroccan Food) and a fresh salad: finely diced tomatoes and cucumber (Cooking With Alia; My Moroccan Food, for whom it is the most common salad alongside grilled meats), or tomatoes, onion and green pepper (Choumicha). Lalla Moulati also uses it in sandwiches with lettuce. Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours, and eat within 2 days; reheat them only once, until they are steaming hot in the centre (FSA). Your fridge should be at 4 °C in its coldest part (Anses).","ing.0.group":"The kefta","ing.viande.name":"halal minced meat (beef, lamb or a mix of both)","ing.viande.note":"not too lean: 15 to 20% fat","ing.oignon.name":"medium onion","ing.oignon.pl":"medium onions","ing.oignon.note":"to be grated, then squeezed to remove the juice","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.menthe.name":"large mint leaf","ing.menthe.pl":"large mint leaves","ing.menthe.note":"to be chopped","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.piment.name":"cayenne pepper","ing.piment.note":"optional","ing.1.group":"Cooking and serving","ing.huile.name":"oil","ing.huile.note":"a drizzle, for the pan","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"to serve","equipment.0":"A cast-iron griddle pan, or a large heavy frying pan","equipment.1":"A large mixing bowl","equipment.2":"A grater","equipment.3":"A chopping board and a knife for the herbs","equipment.4":"A plate for the raw kefta, and another, clean one for the cooked kefta","equipment.5":"A spatula","equipment.6":"A cooking thermometer, if you have one","steps.0.title":"Grate the onion","steps.0.text":"Peel {{oignon:full}}, then grate it into a bowl. Take the pulp in your hand and squeeze it over the sink to remove the juice.","steps.1.title":"Wash and chop the herbs","steps.1.text":"Wash the parsley, coriander and mint, then dry them well. Chop them finely: you need {{persil}} parsley, {{coriandre}} coriander and {{menthe:full}}.","steps.1.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.1.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.2.title":"Season the meat","steps.2.text":"Put {{viande}} minced meat in a large mixing bowl. Add the grated onion, the chopped herbs, {{paprika}} paprika, {{cumin}} cumin, {{sel}} salt, {{poivre}} pepper and, if you like, {{piment}} of cayenne pepper.","steps.3.title":"Mix by hand","steps.3.text":"Mix with your hands until the herbs and spices are evenly distributed through the meat.","steps.3.tip":"Got an extra 30 minutes? Cover the bowl and put it in the fridge: the herbs and spices will flavour the meat better (Cooking With Alia; Choumicha).","steps.4.title":"Shape the patties","steps.4.text":"Take a little meat, roll it into a ball, then flatten it between your palms into a small, thin patty, less than 1 cm thick. Make them all the same size: when thin, they cook quickly in the pan. You can also shape fingers, like small sausages.","steps.4.tip":"Wet your hands for shaping: the meat sticks less (Taste of Maroc, Christine Benlafquih and Nada Kiffa; Alia Al Kasimi, in her recipe published by Femmes du Maroc).","steps.5.title":"Wash your hands","steps.5.text":"Put the patties on a plate. Wash your hands with soap: you have just touched raw meat.","steps.6.title":"Heat the pan","steps.6.text":"Put the pan over medium heat with a drizzle of {{huile}}. Wait until it is very hot before adding the meat.","steps.7.title":"Brown both sides","steps.7.text":"Lay the patties in the hot pan. Let one side brown for a few minutes, then turn them over and brown the other side. If they do not all fit in the pan, cook them in batches.","steps.7.tip":"For kefta fingers on a griddle pan, Alia allows 3 to 4 minutes per side, over medium heat. Thicker patties take longer: she allows 8 minutes per side for her big turkey burgers (Cooking With Alia).","steps.8.title":"Check it is cooked through","steps.8.text":"Cut the biggest patty in half. There must be no pink left in the centre, and steam must come out of the middle. With a thermometer, the centre must reach at least 70 °C. Not cooked yet? Give it a few more minutes and check again.","steps.8.tip":"The FSA specifies: 70 °C in the centre for 2 minutes, or 75 °C for 30 seconds. Then take the kefta off the heat: overcooked, it becomes dry (Cooking With Alia).","steps.9.title":"Switch to a clean plate","steps.9.text":"Put the cooked kefta on a clean plate, never on the one that held the raw meat.","steps.10.title":"Serve straight away","steps.10.text":"Serve piping hot, with {{khobz}} and a fresh salad.","steps.10.tip":"Tomato and cucumber salad is the most common Moroccan salad alongside grilled meats (My Moroccan Food). Choumicha serves her kefta with a salad of tomatoes, onion and green pepper.","secrets.0.text":"Moroccan kefta is highly aromatic: plenty of fresh herbs (parsley, coriander, mint) with cumin and paprika.","secrets.0.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); Cooking With Alia, no. 664; Choumicha, lamb meatball skewers (no. 1120)","secrets.1.text":"Choose meat that is not too lean: 15 to 20% fat.","secrets.1.source":"My Moroccan Food; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen, Lebanese kafta (beef with 20% fat)","secrets.2.text":"Grate the onion, then squeeze it to remove its juice before mixing it into the meat.","secrets.2.source":"Cooking With Alia, Moroccan burger no. 182 (onion grated and squeezed); Mama's Lebanese Kitchen, kafta (onion blended then drained); grated onion also in My Moroccan Food, Cooking With Alia no. 664 and Choumicha no. 467","secrets.3.text":"If you have time, leave the seasoned kefta to rest for 30 minutes in the fridge, covered: the herbs and spices flavour the meat.","secrets.3.source":"Cooking With Alia, no. 664 and no. 453; Choumicha, kefta skewers and their Moroccan salad (no. 467)","secrets.4.text":"The same kefta can be shaped into fingers, patties or balls.","secrets.4.source":"Cooking With Alia, no. 453; Lalla Moulati (balls or oval pieces); Mama's Lebanese Kitchen (patties)","secrets.5.text":"Put the meat into a pan that is already very hot, with just a little fat.","secrets.5.source":"Cooking With Alia, no. 157 and no. 182; Lalla Moulati (hot pan with a little oil, cooked on both sides)","secrets.6.text":"On a griddle pan, Alia allows 3 to 4 minutes per side for kefta fingers, over medium heat.","secrets.6.source":"Cooking With Alia, no. 664","secrets.7.text":"Do not cook it longer than necessary: overcooked, kefta becomes dry.","secrets.7.source":"Cooking With Alia, no. 453 and no. 157; Femmes du Maroc (Alia Al Kasimi's recipe)","secrets.8.text":"A clean cooking thermometer lets you check that it is cooked: 70 °C in the centre. Wash the probe well after each use.","secrets.8.source":"FSA (United Kingdom), “Cooking your food” (70 °C for 2 minutes, probe to be washed); Anses, article on E. coli (2022)","secrets.9.text":"The kefta recipes from Choumicha, Cooking With Alia and My Moroccan Food contain neither egg nor breadcrumbs.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha nos. 467, 771, 943 and 1120; Cooking With Alia nos. 664 and 453; My Moroccan Food","secrets.10.text":"In Morocco, many butchers grill kefta on the spot and serve it with very sweet mint tea.","secrets.10.source":"My Moroccan Food; Choumicha (no. 943: with bread and mint tea)","mistakes.0":"Serving kefta that is still pink in the centre: minced meat must be cooked right through (70 °C), especially for young children, pregnant women, elderly people and people with weakened immune systems (Anses; FSA, according to which minced meat must be cooked to the centre and burgers must never be served pink at home).","mistakes.1":"Putting the cooked kefta back on the raw meat plate, or chopping the herbs on the board used for the meat (Anses).","mistakes.2":"Forgetting to wash your hands with soap after touching the raw meat (Anses).","mistakes.3":"Using meat that is too lean: My Moroccan Food asks for 15 to 20% fat.","mistakes.4":"Keeping all the juice from the grated onion (Cooking With Alia; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen).","mistakes.5":"Putting the meat into a pan that is still cold (Cooking With Alia; Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.6":"Overcooking the kefta: it dries out (Cooking With Alia).","mistakes.7":"Leaving leftovers at room temperature for more than 2 hours before putting them in the fridge (Anses; FSA).","mistakes.8":"Storing raw minced meat at the top of the fridge: put it at the bottom, in its packaging or a closed container, so that it cannot drip onto other food (FSA).","variants.0":"Lamb kefta (Cooking With Alia no. 453; Choumicha no. 1120; Bladi.net) or veal kefta (Choumicha no. 467; Nada Kiffa, Taste of Maroc, who allows beef, veal or lamb, alone or mixed).","variants.1":"With ginger and ground coriander: Cooking With Alia uses ½ tsp ginger and 1 tsp ground coriander for 500 g meat; Choumicha also adds them (no. 943).","variants.2":"With 3 tbsp olive oil mixed into the meat (Cooking With Alia, no. 664 and no. 453).","variants.3":"With a hint of cinnamon and some marjoram (Choumicha, no. 771 and no. 943; cinnamon also in Taste of Maroc, as an option, and in Lalla Moulati in 2014; dried marjoram also from Nada Kiffa, Taste of Maroc).","variants.4":"With garlic (Choumicha no. 943; Bladi.net).","variants.5":"Bladi.net's version: lamb, garlic, ras el hanout and an egg, shaped into balls on skewers, under the oven grill, 6 minutes per side.","variants.6":"Lalla Moulati's version: ground fennel (besbes), dried coriander, cumin, parsley, hot chilli (soudania) and small pieces of butter.","variants.7":"As skewers grilled over embers, the most classic way (Choumicha; Cooking With Alia; My Moroccan Food).","variants.8":"With a yoghurt, parsley, mint and lemon sauce (Cooking With Alia no. 453; Femmes du Maroc).","variants.9":"In a sandwich, with lettuce (Lalla Moulati).","variants.10":"Lebanese version (kafta): beef, onion, parsley and seven-spice mix; Hadia serves it with tabbouleh and hummus (Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine)."},"khobz":{"title":"Khobz, Moroccan homemade bread","darija":"khobz (khobz dyal dar)","tagline":"The everyday round bread, golden and crisp on the outside, soft inside, torn by hand to scoop up the tagine. Just flour, yeast, salt and lukewarm water.","region":"All over Morocco (bread is also called aghroum, aɣrum, in Tashelhit); similar breads in Algeria and Tunisia","occasion":"Every day, at almost every meal","img_alt":"Stacks of round khobz on a stall in Chefchaouen","serving":"Khobz goes with almost every meal. It is cut into wedges or torn, and it replaces the fork and spoon for scooping up salads, tagines, sauces and stews (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News); Nargisse Benkabbou points out that in Morocco tagine is eaten with bread (My Moroccan Food). The wedges can also be split open as sandwiches, filled with grilled meat (Taste of Maroc). At breakfast, it is eaten hot with a little olive oil and mint tea (Hespress). Leftover bread keeps wrapped in a tea towel, in a bread bin or a bag (MarocMama), but for no more than a day: beyond that, freeze it. Thaw it for a few hours at room temperature and reheat it for 5 to 8 min in the oven at 170 °C (Taste of Maroc). MarocMama also reheats it in a frying pan.","pairs_label":"With khobz","ing.0.group":"Dough","ing.farine.name":"plain white flour (ideally strong bread flour, high in gluten)","ing.farine.note":"plus a little for the work surface; in Morocco: “force” or “farine de blé”; you can replace some of it with fine semolina or wholemeal flour (see the variations)","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.levure.name":"dried yeast","ing.levure.note":"a little more than usual, for a single quick rise","ing.eau.name":"lukewarm water, not hot","ing.eau.note":"adjust to suit the flour; plus a little to moisten the loaves before baking","ing.1.group":"For the baking tray","ing.semoule.name":"Fine semolina, for dusting the baking tray","ing.semoule.note":"or baking paper, or a little oil","equipment.0":"A large mixing bowl, or a gsaa (the large traditional bowl)","equipment.1":"Kitchen scales and measuring spoons","equipment.2":"A large baking tray, or one per loaf if it cannot hold two 27 to 30 cm discs (and baking paper if you like)","equipment.3":"Two or three clean tea towels, plus a small blanket if it is cold","equipment.4":"A fork","equipment.5":"A clean water spray bottle, or a pastry brush","equipment.6":"A wire rack, or a basket lined with a tea towel","equipment.7":"A bread board, if you bake at the neighbourhood oven (ferran)","steps.0.title":"Prepare the baking tray","steps.0.text":"Dust a large baking tray, or one tray per loaf, with fine semolina: once flattened, each loaf will be 27 to 30 cm across. You can also line it with baking paper or oil it lightly.","steps.1.title":"Mix the dough","steps.1.text":"In a large mixing bowl, mix {{farine}} flour and {{sel}} salt. Make a well in the centre and put {{levure}} yeast in it. Pour a little of the {{eau}} of lukewarm water over it and stir with your fingertips to dissolve it. Add the rest of the water gradually, mixing by hand, until the dough comes together.","steps.2.title":"Knead for 10 minutes","steps.2.text":"Knead for 10 minutes, in the bowl or on the floured work surface. The dough should stay soft and supple, without sticking: if it sticks, add a little flour; if it becomes firm, sprinkle it with a little water as you knead. It is ready when it is smooth and elastic.","steps.2.timer.label":"Kneading","steps.2.timer.done":"Dough smooth, supple and elastic? Move on to the balls. If not, knead for up to 5 minutes more.","steps.2.img.alt":"Hands kneading dough","steps.2.img.caption":"Kneading the dough","steps.3.title":"Shape the balls","steps.3.text":"Divide the dough into equal balls, one per loaf, nice and smooth on top. Place them on the tray, well apart: flattened to 6 mm, each ball will make a disc 27 to 30 cm across. If two discs of this size do not fit side by side on your tray (it needs to be at least 55 cm long), put one ball per tray.","steps.4.title":"Leave to rest under a tea towel","steps.4.text":"Cover the balls with a clean tea towel and leave them to rest for 10 to 15 minutes.","steps.4.timer.label":"Resting the balls","steps.4.timer.done":"The balls have rested: flatten them.","steps.5.title":"Flatten into discs","steps.5.text":"Directly on the tray, flatten each ball with your palm, working from the centre out to the edges. Form a nicely round disc about 6 mm thick, even all over.","steps.6.title":"Leave to rise","steps.6.text":"Cover again with a tea towel and leave to rise for about 1 hour in a warm place, longer if the room is cool. If it is cold, add another layer, or a small blanket. While the loaves rise, preheat the oven to 220 °C: it must be very hot for bread.","steps.6.timer.label":"Loaves rising","steps.6.timer.done":"Press lightly with a fingertip: if the dough springs back into shape, it is ready. If not, wait a little longer.","steps.7.title":"Prick and moisten","steps.7.text":"When the oven is hot, prick each loaf in several places with a fork: this stops it puffing up. Then spray a little water over the top, or brush it with a wet brush: the crust will be crisper.","steps.8.title":"Bake for 20 minutes","steps.8.text":"Bake for about 20 minutes. If your oven browns more on one side, turn the tray during baking. The khobz is done when it is nicely golden and sounds hollow when you tap it.","steps.8.timer.label":"Baking the loaves","steps.8.timer.done":"Nicely golden, and it sounds hollow when you tap it? Take it out. If not, leave it a few more minutes, 25 minutes in total at most.","steps.9.title":"Or bake at the neighbourhood oven","steps.9.text":"To bake it at the ferran, the neighbourhood oven: from the ball stage, place the balls on a bread board dusted with semolina instead of the tray, flatten them and leave them to rise on it under a tea towel, then take the board to the oven. The oven keeper (terrah) bakes them over a wood fire, for about 20 minutes. He recognises each family's loaves from memory, by the order in which he arranged them, by the board and by the tea towel.","steps.9.img.alt":"Baker in front of the wood-fired oven, loaves laid on boards, in Marrakesh","steps.9.img.caption":"The neighbourhood oven, in Marrakesh","steps.10.title":"Leave to cool slightly","steps.10.text":"Take out the loaves and leave them to cool slightly on a wire rack, or in a basket lined with a tea towel.","steps.11.title":"Serve","steps.11.text":"Cut the khobz into wedges or tear it by hand: it replaces the fork for scooping up tagine, salads or zaalouk. At breakfast, serve it hot with a little olive oil and mint tea.","steps.12.title":"Store","steps.12.text":"Khobz does not stay fresh for more than a day. Whatever you will not eat the same day, freeze once it has cooled completely. To serve it, leave it to thaw for a few hours at room temperature, then reheat it for 5 to 8 minutes in the oven at 170 °C.","secrets.0.text":"The dough should be soft: supple and easy to knead, but not sticky. Too firm, and the bread comes out hard and dense. Always adjust the water or flour: every flour absorbs differently, and even the humidity of the air matters.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih), recipe and answers to readers","secrets.1.text":"Knead for 10 to 15 minutes, sprinkling the dough with a little water, until it becomes supple and pliable.","secrets.1.source":"Choumicha (semolina bread, wholemeal bread, sesame seed bread)","secrets.2.text":"No sugar or oil, as in Choumicha's semolina and wholemeal breads, which contain only flour, yeast, salt and water.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha","secrets.3.text":"The water should be lukewarm, not hot; cold water slows the yeast down.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc; MarocMama","secrets.4.text":"Not sure about your yeast? Mix it with a little lukewarm water and a pinch of sugar: it should foam within 10 minutes. If not, replace it.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.5.text":"A single rise is enough, once the loaves are flattened: a few minutes' rest as balls, then about 1 hour under a tea towel. In cold weather, cover with several layers or a small blanket, or let them rise in the oven with just the light on; in that case, take out the loaves, the tea towel and the blanket before preheating the oven.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc; MarocMama","secrets.6.text":"Prick the bread with a fork just before it goes into the oven, so that it does not puff up: a terrah in Casablanca does this as he puts each loaf in the oven.","secrets.6.source":"La Vie éco (traditional oven in Sidi Bernoussi, Casablanca); Taste of Maroc; MarocMama","secrets.7.text":"If you prefer to score it with a knife, keep it light: too deep, and the bread rises too much at that spot. A fork, a flatter loaf or a little less yeast avoid this problem.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.8.text":"Moisten the top just before baking, with a spray bottle or a brush: the crust will be crisper.","secrets.8.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc","secrets.9.text":"A very hot oven, around 220 °C, and about 20 minutes: at the ferran too, the oven must be hot for bread, and baking takes about 20 minutes.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc; La Vie éco","secrets.10.text":"Keep it flat: many Moroccans prefer a khobz no more than 1.5 cm high, and it rarely exceeds 3 cm.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.11.text":"Bread you will not eat the same day can be frozen: first let it cool completely, then reheat it for 5 to 8 minutes in the oven at 170 °C.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc; MarocMama (freezing)","secrets.12.text":"A festive meal or an iftar? Part-bake the loaves the day before until they are barely coloured, wrap them well, and finish baking just before serving.","secrets.12.source":"Taste of Maroc","mistakes.0":"Making the dough too firm: the bread comes out hard and dense (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.1":"Using hot water instead of lukewarm (Taste of Maroc), or cold water, which slows the yeast down (MarocMama).","mistakes.2":"Using self-raising flour: MarocMama advises against it.","mistakes.3":"Adding too much flour while kneading: the bread becomes dry (MarocMama).","mistakes.4":"Kneading too little, and the bread is dense; far too much, and it becomes hard (MarocMama).","mistakes.5":"Forgetting to prick the bread: it puffs up (La Vie éco).","mistakes.6":"Scoring too deeply: the bread rises too much at the cut (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.7":"Taking the bread out too early: it should be nicely golden and sound hollow when you tap it (Taste of Maroc; MarocMama).","mistakes.8":"Keeping it for several days at room temperature: it does not stay fresh for more than a day (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.9":"Throwing bread away: in Morocco, bread is not thrown away; leftovers are used in another dish or given away (Morocco World News).","variants.0":"Semolina khobz (Choumicha): 250 g superior-grade durum wheat flour and 500 g fine durum wheat semolina, 1 tsp salt, 20 g yeast, 50 cl lukewarm water. The dough rises until doubled and is knocked back to remove the air bubbles; the balls are rolled in 80 g fine semolina, flattened slightly and left to rise in a warm place; sprayed with water, they bake for 20 to 25 min at 220 °C.","variants.1":"Nargisse Benkabbou's small semolina breads (My Moroccan Food), her favourite Moroccan bread: 280 g flour, 300 g fine semolina, 250 ml water and 250 ml milk, lukewarm (pour in 300 ml first, then a spoonful at a time), 1 tbsp dried yeast, 2 tsp salt, 2 tsp sugar, 3 tbsp olive oil; 10 breads of 10 cm flattened to 2 cm, risen for 1 h, scored with a knife and dusted with semolina, 18 to 20 min at 200 °C.","variants.2":"Wholemeal khobz (Choumicha): 250 g soft wheat flour and 500 g wholemeal flour, 1 tsp salt, 20 g yeast, 40 cl lukewarm water; balls rolled in 80 g wholemeal flour, same method as the semolina bread. Taste of Maroc advises starting with half wholemeal flour, half white flour, and enough water for a soft dough. Wholemeal bread is called khobz zraa (Hespress; Taste of Maroc).","variants.3":"Sesame khobz (Choumicha): 750 g soft wheat flour, 1 tsp salt, 1 tbsp sugar, 20 g yeast, 40 cl lukewarm water; after the first rise, the balls are rolled in flour, flattened, brushed with water and covered with 80 g sesame seeds, then left to rise in a warm place and baked for 20 to 25 min at 220 °C. In Morocco, golden unhulled sesame is preferred; you can also glaze the risen bread with beaten egg just before baking, then sprinkle it with sesame (Taste of Maroc).","variants.4":"Aniseed khobz: along with cumin, aniseed is the most common flavouring in Moroccan bread (Morocco World News), and homemade khobz is sometimes flavoured with aniseed (Wikipedia). A mother from Beni Mellal adds to her flour “a little jeljlane (sesame) and nafaa” (Hespress). None of the sources read gives the amount for a khobz; as a rough guide only, Choumicha uses 1 tbsp aniseed for 500 g flour in her krichlat (brioches).","variants.5":"Festive khobz: enriched with egg and milk, and often flavoured with aniseed, sesame or cumin; in Morocco, it is mostly kept for festivals and special occasions (Taste of Maroc).","variants.6":"Mixed flours: replace part of the white flour, up to half, with very fine semolina (durum wheat finot), wholemeal flour or barley flour (Taste of Maroc). Barley and oat flours are usually mixed with other flours, otherwise the bread is too dense (Taste of Maroc). In the past, people ground their own wheat or barley with a millstone (Hespress).","variants.7":"Lighter crumb: first let the dough rise for an hour or more in an oiled bowl, before shaping (Taste of Maroc). This is also Choumicha's method: the dough doubles, is knocked back to remove the air bubbles, then the loaves rise a second time.","variants.8":"More flavour: less yeast and two long rises (Taste of Maroc). In the past, a homemade sourdough starter was used, and some Moroccan bakers still use one (Taste of Maroc); in Laayoune, a traditional wood-fired oven makes its bread with sourdough starter, several flours, salt and water (Le360).","variants.9":"Batbout: in her cooking classes, Christine Benlafquih sometimes keeps a little of this dough to show how to make batbout, small breads cooked on the hob (Taste of Maroc); Choumicha also publishes a recipe called “Batbot ou pain metlouâ”."},"lentilles":{"title":"Moroccan lentils with lamb and vegetables","darija":"Adess bel lham (l'3des)","tagline":"Lentils, a winter dish for Moroccan families, here simmered with lamb, carrots and potatoes in a tomato sauce with cumin and paprika, to mop up with bread.","region":"All over Morocco (Morocco World News 2019: a dish enjoyed throughout the country in winter)","occasion":"An everyday family dish, especially in winter; served as a main course, with bread (Morocco World News 2019 and 2020; Mamatkamal blog)","img_alt":"Lentils with potatoes and carrots in a decorated dish, photographed in Morocco","serving":"Piping hot, as a main course, with khobz to mop up the sauce, or with a spoon (Morocco World News 2019 and 2020; My Moroccan Food; Cooking With Alia). On the side: lemon wedges (Bladi; Mamatkamal blog; My Moroccan Food) and, for those who like it, harissa or hot chilli (My Moroccan Food; Mamatkamal blog).","ing.0.group":"Meat","ing.agneau.name":"boneless lamb (shoulder or neck)","ing.agneau.note":"halal; in pieces of about 3 cm, trimmed of excess fat","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.1.group":"Sauce","ing.oignon.name":"large onion","ing.oignon.pl":"large onions","ing.oignon.note":"to be grated","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"to be finely chopped","ing.tomates.name":"medium tomato, very ripe","ing.tomates.pl":"medium tomatoes, very ripe","ing.tomates.note":"flesh grated, skin discarded","ing.concentre.name":"tomato purée","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end of cooking","ing.cayenne.name":"a pinch of cayenne pepper","ing.cayenne.note":"optional","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau-bouillante.name":"a little boiling water","ing.eau-bouillante.note":"only if the sauce reduces too much","ing.2.group":"Lentils and vegetables","ing.lentilles.name":"green or brown lentils","ing.lentilles.note":"dried; not red lentils","ing.carottes.name":"medium carrot","ing.carottes.pl":"medium carrots","ing.pommes-de-terre.name":"small potato","ing.pommes-de-terre.pl":"small potatoes","ing.3.group":"To serve","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.pl":"lemons","ing.citron.note":"optional","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"to mop up the sauce","equipment.0":"Large casserole or heavy-based pot, with a lid","equipment.1":"Colander","equipment.2":"Grater","equipment.3":"One chopping board kept for raw meat, another for the vegetables and herbs","equipment.4":"Knife and vegetable peeler","equipment.5":"Bowl of cold water, for the potatoes","equipment.6":"Wooden spoon","steps.0.title":"Pick over and wash the lentils","steps.0.text":"Pick over {{lentilles}} lentils to remove any small stones. Wash them in a colander and leave them to drain.","steps.0.tip":"No need to soak them: the meat recipes from Choumicha and My Moroccan Food use the lentils dry. If you have soaked them, they will cook faster: keep an eye on them. Without soaking, Moroccan brown lentils can take much longer than 40 minutes (1 h 30 min to 2 h in a pot, according to the Mamatkamal blog): taste them before adding the potatoes.","steps.1.title":"Cut the lamb","steps.1.text":"On a chopping board kept for raw meat, trim the excess fat from {{agneau}} of lamb and cut it into pieces of about 3 cm.","steps.1.tip":"Keep another board for the vegetables and herbs (Anses).","steps.2.title":"Prepare the onion, tomatoes and garlic","steps.2.text":"Grate the onion ({{oignon:full}}). Cut the tomatoes in half ({{tomates:full}}), grate the flesh and discard the skin. Finely chop the garlic ({{ail:full}}).","steps.2.img.alt":"Tomato grated on a grater","steps.2.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.3.title":"Chop the herbs","steps.3.text":"Wash the parsley and coriander, dry them and chop them finely: you need {{persil}} parsley and {{coriandre}} coriander.","steps.3.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.3.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.4.title":"Peel the vegetables","steps.4.text":"Peel the carrots ({{carottes:full}}) and cut them into even pieces. Peel the potatoes ({{pommes-de-terre:full}}), cut them into large pieces and keep them in a bowl of cold water until it is time to cook them.","steps.4.tip":"Do not cut the potatoes too small: they would fall apart in the sauce (Cooking With Alia).","steps.5.title":"Brown the lamb","steps.5.text":"In the casserole, heat {{huile}} olive oil over medium heat. Brown the pieces of lamb for 5 to 10 minutes, turning them so that they colour on all sides.","steps.6.title":"Add the aromatics and spices","steps.6.text":"Add the grated onion, garlic, grated tomatoes and herbs. Add {{concentre}} tomato purée, {{paprika}} paprika, {{cumin}} cumin, {{poivre}} pepper, {{sel}} salt and, if you like, {{cayenne}}. Mix and let it cook for 5 minutes, stirring.","steps.6.timer.label":"Aromatics and spices","steps.6.timer.done":"Pour in the water and bring to the boil.","steps.7.title":"Simmer the meat","steps.7.text":"Pour in {{eau}} of water and bring to the boil. Cover, lower the heat and simmer for 45 minutes, until the meat starts to soften but is not yet meltingly tender.","steps.7.timer.label":"Lamb, half-cooked","steps.7.timer.done":"Is the meat starting to soften? Add the lentils. Still firm: give it up to 15 minutes more.","steps.8.title":"Add the lentils and carrots","steps.8.text":"Add the drained lentils and the carrots, then stir. The liquid should cover the lentils: if not, add {{eau-bouillante}}. Cover and simmer for 20 minutes over low heat, stirring from time to time, then taste the lentils: they should be starting to soften before you add the potatoes.","steps.8.timer.label":"Lentils and carrots","steps.8.timer.done":"Taste a few lentils: they should be starting to soften. If they are still hard (brown lentils can take much longer to cook), keep cooking, covered, tasting regularly, and only add the potatoes at that point.","steps.9.title":"Add the potatoes","steps.9.text":"Drain the potatoes and push them down into the sauce. Cover and cook for 20 minutes, until the potatoes, lentils and meat are tender. Stir gently from time to time and add a little boiling water if the sauce catches on the bottom.","steps.9.timer.label":"Potatoes","steps.9.timer.done":"Prick a potato: it should be tender. If not, cook for another 5 minutes.","steps.10.title":"Adjust the sauce and seasoning","steps.10.text":"The sauce should be velvety, neither dry nor too runny. If it is too runny, let it reduce for a few minutes without the lid. Taste and adjust the salt, pepper and cumin.","steps.11.title":"Serve piping hot","steps.11.text":"Serve piping hot, with {{khobz}} to mop up the sauce and lemon wedges ({{citron:full}}) to squeeze over the plate. Those who like it spicy add a little harissa.","steps.12.title":"Put away the leftovers","steps.12.text":"Do not leave the dish at room temperature for more than 2 hours: put the leftovers in the fridge in a closed container and eat them within 3 days. If there is more than a litre, divide them into small portions so that they cool quickly. Reheat, covered, until steam escapes, with a little hot water if it is too thick.","secrets.0.text":"First cook the lamb on its own in the sauce: lentils cook much faster than the meat. Add them when the pieces are starting to soften but are not yet meltingly tender.","secrets.0.source":"My Moroccan Food (meat “half-cooked”, about 1 hour, before the lentils); Bladi (meat 40 minutes before the lentils); Choumicha (dried meat 30 to 40 minutes before the lentils and carrots)","secrets.1.text":"The carrots cook with the lentils; the potatoes go in only 20 minutes before the end, otherwise they fall apart.","secrets.1.source":"Choumicha (carrots with the lentils; sweet potato and squash added for the last 20 to 25 minutes); My Moroccan Food and Cooking With Alia (potatoes cooked for 15 to 20 minutes in the sauce)","secrets.2.text":"Cut the potatoes into large pieces, never too small, and keep them in cold water until you cook them.","secrets.2.source":"Cooking With Alia (beef and potato tagine, no. 201); Bladi (vegetables “in large pieces”)","secrets.3.text":"Grate the onion and the tomatoes. For the tomatoes, grate the flesh and discard the skin.","secrets.3.source":"Morocco World News 2019 (grated onion and tomatoes); Cooking With Alia no. 462 (flesh grated, skin discarded); My Moroccan Food (grated tomatoes); Mamatkamal blog","secrets.4.text":"Always pick over the lentils before washing them: there are sometimes small stones left in them.","secrets.4.source":"Bladi; Mamatkamal blog","secrets.5.text":"Use green or brown lentils. Red lentils turn to mush in this dish.","secrets.5.source":"MarocMama (the recipe “does not work” with red lentils); green lentils in My Moroccan Food, brown in Cooking With Alia and Morocco World News","secrets.6.text":"There should always be some sauce left. If it reduces too much before the end, add a little boiling water, not cold water.","secrets.6.source":"Cooking With Alia no. 094; Choumicha (“a little boiling water if necessary”); Mamatkamal blog (hot water)","secrets.7.text":"At the end, the sauce should be velvety, not watery: if need be, reduce it for a few minutes uncovered.","secrets.7.source":"Mamatkamal blog (sauce “not too runny”); Choumicha (let the sauce reduce for a velvety jus)","secrets.8.text":"Taste at the end of cooking and adjust the salt, pepper and cumin.","secrets.8.source":"Cooking With Alia no. 094 and no. 462; My Moroccan Food (“salt, or more to taste”)","secrets.9.text":"Serve with lemon to squeeze over the plate.","secrets.9.source":"Bladi (lemon juice when serving); Mamatkamal blog (lemon at the end of cooking); My Moroccan Food (lemon wedges, meat-free version)","secrets.10.text":"Soaking is not essential: the versions with meat use the lentils dry. Soaked (1 hour to overnight), they cook faster.","secrets.10.source":"Choumicha and My Moroccan Food (no soaking); Morocco World News 2019 and 2020, Cooking With Alia no. 094 and no. 462 (with soaking)","secrets.11.text":"In Morocco, adess is eaten with bread: you scoop up the lentils with a piece of khobz, or eat them with a spoon.","secrets.11.source":"Morocco World News 2020; Morocco World News 2019; My Moroccan Food","secrets.12.text":"Cut the raw meat on a separate board, and do not reuse that board for the herbs or vegetables.","secrets.12.source":"Anses (one board for raw meat, another for cooked foods and clean vegetables)","mistakes.0":"Putting the lentils in with the raw lamb: the meat must first cook on its own until it starts to soften, as it takes much longer to cook than the lentils (My Moroccan Food: about 1 hour, then 40 minutes for the lentils; Choumicha and Bladi also cook the meat first).","mistakes.1":"Adding the potatoes at the same time as the lentils, or cutting them too small: they fall apart (Cooking With Alia; Choumicha and My Moroccan Food add these vegetables at the end of cooking).","mistakes.2":"Using red lentils (MarocMama).","mistakes.3":"Letting the sauce dry out and catch on the bottom (Cooking With Alia; Choumicha).","mistakes.4":"Topping up with cold water: Choumicha adds boiling water, the Mamatkamal blog hot water.","mistakes.5":"Serving a sauce that is too runny (Mamatkamal blog; Choumicha reduces it).","mistakes.6":"Cutting the raw lamb and the herbs on the same board (Anses).","mistakes.7":"Leaving leftovers at room temperature for more than 2 hours, or keeping them in the fridge for more than 3 days (Anses).","variants.0":"With beef: same method with pieces of halal beef (Mamatkamal blog: 200 g beef for 350 g lentils; Bladi uses 300 g “red meat” for 300 g lentils).","variants.1":"Without meat (Morocco World News 2019: only some people add small pieces of meat): onion, tomato, garlic, paprika, cumin, herbs and olive oil; 35 to 40 minutes in a saucepan (Cooking With Alia no. 094 and no. 462; My Moroccan Food), or 25 to 35 minutes in a pressure cooker (Choumicha; Morocco World News 2019 and 2020).","variants.2":"With dried meat (gueddid) and carrots: Choumicha soaks the gueddid in water for 15 minutes to remove the salt, cooks it for 30 to 40 minutes, then adds the lentils and carrots. With khlii (preserved dried meat), she adds it only at the end of cooking.","variants.3":"With gueddid, sweet potato and squash: Choumicha adds these vegetables after the lentils have cooked for 30 minutes, then cooks for another 20 to 25 minutes.","variants.4":"With smen (aged salted butter): Bladi flavours the meat with smen and cinnamon, then drizzles the dish with a hot mixture of parsley, lemon juice and smen.","variants.5":"With squash and shallots: My Moroccan Food replaces the onion with shallots and adds squash at the end of cooking.","variants.6":"With a little rice at the end of cooking, for a version closer to a soup (Bladi).","variants.7":"With white vinegar when serving (Choumicha), or grilled sardines on the side (Morocco World News 2020).","variants.8":"More spiced: some families add ginger (Morocco World News 2019 and 2020) or turmeric (Cooking With Alia no. 094; Morocco World News 2020)."},"loubia":{"title":"Loubia, Moroccan white beans","darija":"Loubia","tagline":"Meltingly tender white beans, simmered in a generous tomato sauce with garlic, cumin and paprika: the comforting everyday dish, to mop up with bread.","region":"All over Morocco (a very common dish according to Taste of Maroc and MarocMama)","occasion":"An everyday dish, as a main course or a side dish (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2023); it is also eaten at breakfast or lunch, in small cheap eateries (MarocMama)","img_alt":"White beans in tomato sauce with parsley, here with pieces of meat (photo taken in Tunisia)","serving":"Hot, as a main course or a side dish (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2023), with khobz to mop up the sauce, by hand or with a spoon (Taste of Maroc; MarocMama). In Morocco, it is also eaten at breakfast or lunch, often in small cheap eateries (MarocMama). As a side dish, it goes with grilled chicken or beef (MarocMama), fish such as sardines, or meat (Morocco World News 2023).","ing.0.group":"Beans","ing.haricots.name":"dried white beans","ing.haricots.note":"small white beans or cannellini","ing.1.group":"Sauce","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe tomato","ing.tomates.pl":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"flesh grated, skin discarded","ing.oignon.name":"medium onion","ing.oignon.pl":"medium onions","ing.oignon.note":"to be grated","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"to be finely chopped or crushed","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.concentre.name":"tomato purée","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end of cooking","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.gingembre.note":"optional","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.curcuma.note":"optional, for colour","ing.piment.name":"ground hot chilli (cayenne)","ing.piment.note":"optional","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau-plus.name":"a little water","ing.eau-plus.note":"only if the liquid reduces too soon","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"to mop up the sauce","equipment.0":"Large bowl, for soaking","equipment.1":"Colander","equipment.2":"Large heavy-based casserole of about 6 l, with a lid (or a pressure cooker); a casserole twice as large if you double the recipe","equipment.3":"Grater","equipment.4":"Knife or garlic press","equipment.5":"Wooden spoon","steps.0.title":"The day before: soak the beans","steps.0.text":"Put {{haricots}} dried white beans in a large bowl. Cover them with plenty of water, 2.5 to 5 cm above them, as they will swell. Leave them to soak overnight.","steps.0.tip":"No time? Soak them for at least 5 hours (the minimum recommended by the FDA; Choumicha goes down to 2 or 3 hours in some recipes), throw away the soaking water and allow a longer cooking time (MarocMama).","steps.1.title":"Drain the beans","steps.1.text":"The next day, drain the beans and throw away the soaking water.","steps.2.title":"Prepare the tomatoes, onion and garlic","steps.2.text":"Cut the tomatoes in half ({{tomates:full}}), grate the flesh and discard the skin. Grate the onion ({{oignon:full}}). Finely chop the garlic ({{ail:full}}), or put it through a garlic press.","steps.2.img.alt":"Tomato grated on a grater","steps.2.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.3.title":"Chop the herbs","steps.3.text":"Wash the parsley and coriander, dry them and chop them finely: you need {{persil}} parsley and {{coriandre}} coriander.","steps.3.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.3.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.4.title":"Put everything in the casserole","steps.4.text":"In a large heavy-based casserole, put the drained beans, tomatoes, onion, garlic and herbs. Add {{concentre}} tomato purée, {{paprika}} paprika, {{cumin}} cumin, {{poivre}} pepper, {{sel}} salt and {{huile}} olive oil. If you like, add {{gingembre}} ginger, {{curcuma}} turmeric and {{piment}} hot chilli. Mix well.","steps.5.title":"Pour in the water","steps.5.text":"Pour in {{eau}} of water and bring to a full boil over high heat.","steps.5.tip":"If white foam forms at the start, skim it off with a spoon (Kookmutsjes).","steps.6.title":"Cook at a gentle boil","steps.6.text":"Cover and cook over medium heat for about 1 h 30 min, until the beans are meltingly tender. The liquid must boil gently, not just simmer, for at least the first 30 minutes. Stir from time to time. If the liquid reduces before the beans are cooked, add {{eau-plus}}.","steps.6.timer.label":"Loubia","steps.6.timer.done":"Crush a bean against the side of the pot: it should crush easily. If not, cook it longer.","steps.7.title":"Check the beans are cooked","steps.7.text":"Crush a bean against the side of the pot with a spoon: if it crushes effortlessly, it is cooked. If not, keep cooking, covered.","steps.8.title":"Adjust the sauce","steps.8.text":"The sauce should be thick, but still plentiful: the beans will keep soaking it up. If they are still swimming in liquid, let it reduce without the lid, stirring.","steps.8.tip":"To thicken the sauce, crush a few beans with a fork (MarocMama).","steps.9.title":"Taste and adjust","steps.9.text":"Taste and adjust the salt and spices.","steps.10.title":"Serve hot with bread","steps.10.text":"Serve the loubia hot, as a main course or a side dish, with {{khobz}} to mop up the sauce. Eat it with a spoon, or the Moroccan way, scooping up the beans with a piece of bread.","steps.11.title":"Put away the leftovers","steps.11.text":"Do not leave the loubia at room temperature for more than 2 hours: put it in the fridge in a closed container and eat it within 3 days. If there is more than a litre, divide it into small portions so that it cools quickly. Reheat it gently, covered, with a little water, until steam escapes.","secrets.0.text":"Soak the beans overnight in plenty of water: they swell and absorb some of it.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha (“soaked since the day before”); Bladi; Morocco World News 2023; MarocMama (water 2.5 to 5 cm above the beans)","secrets.1.text":"Did not soak them the day before? Allow at least 5 hours of soaking, and a longer cooking time.","secrets.1.source":"FDA (at least 5 hours of soaking); MarocMama (at least a few hours, and longer cooking); Choumicha (2 to 3 hours for her beans with fried peppers, 2 hours for her beans with meatballs)","secrets.2.text":"Grate the tomatoes and the onion.","secrets.2.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha (grated tomatoes)","secrets.3.text":"Garlic, cumin and paprika give loubia its flavour: do not be stingy with them.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc (“lots of garlic”, paprika, cumin); MarocMama (5 garlic cloves, cumin and paprika); Choumicha (cumin and sweet paprika in all her versions)","secrets.4.text":"Turmeric is not traditional in loubia: Taste of Maroc adds it for colour. Use it or not, as you like.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc; it does, however, appear in Morocco World News 2023 and in two of Choumicha's recipes (fried peppers, douara)","secrets.5.text":"Keep plenty of sauce at serving time: the beans go on soaking up the liquid while they wait.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.6.text":"If the sauce is still too runny when the beans are cooked, reduce it uncovered.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2023; MarocMama","secrets.7.text":"To thicken the sauce, crush a few beans with a fork.","secrets.7.source":"MarocMama","secrets.8.text":"A cooked bean crushes effortlessly: loubia should be meltingly tender.","secrets.8.source":"Morocco World News 2023; Choumicha (“the white beans should be meltingly tender”)","secrets.9.text":"If using meat, brown it first in the olive oil, then cook everything together. Dried meat does not need browning.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.10.text":"With a pressure cooker, do not open it straight away: let the pressure drop, so that you do not burn yourself.","secrets.10.source":"Morocco World News 2023","secrets.11.text":"Najat prefers small white beans, which she finds the best for loubia.","secrets.11.source":"Kookmutsjes (Najat, “Marokkaanse bonensoep/Loubia/Joubiath”)","secrets.12.text":"If white foam forms at the start of cooking, skim it off with a spoon.","secrets.12.source":"Kookmutsjes (Najat)","secrets.13.text":"In Morocco, loubia is eaten with bread, by hand or with a spoon.","secrets.13.source":"Taste of Maroc; MarocMama; Morocco World News 2023","mistakes.0":"Using tinned beans in this recipe: already cooked, they may not take on the flavour of the sauce well (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.1":"Cooking dried beans without soaking them: soak them for at least 5 hours (FDA) and allow longer cooking (MarocMama). Or cooking them in an electric slow cooker: it does not get hot enough to destroy the lectin in the beans (FDA).","mistakes.2":"Letting the casserole dry out: add a little water if the liquid reduces before the beans are cooked (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.3":"Serving a watery sauce: it should be thick, but still plentiful (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.4":"Opening the pressure cooker as soon as cooking ends (Morocco World News 2023).","mistakes.5":"Leaving leftovers at room temperature for more than 2 hours, or keeping them in the fridge for more than 3 days (Anses).","variants.0":"In a pressure cooker: same ingredients, 40 minutes under pressure over medium heat (Taste of Maroc; MarocMama; Morocco World News 2023 allows 40 to 45 minutes). Taste of Maroc keeps the 2 litres of water and then reduces the sauce uncovered; Morocco World News 2023 and MarocMama use just enough water to cover the beans generously. Let the pressure drop before opening (Morocco World News 2023).","variants.1":"With halal meat: 1 pound (about 450 g) of bone-in beef or lamb for 1 pound of beans, browned first in the olive oil (Taste of Maroc); Bladi cooks 500 g veal with 250 g beans, cumin, tomato coulis and a pinch of saffron.","variants.2":"With dried meat (gueddid): Choumicha first cooks the beans for 30 minutes with unpeeled garlic cloves and a bunch of coriander and parsley, then adds the dried meat, grated tomatoes, tomato purée, spices and olive oil; she removes the bunch of herbs when serving.","variants.3":"With khlii (preserved dried meat): Nargisse Benkabbou's mother added it while the loubia was simmering (My Moroccan Food); Taste of Maroc and MarocMama also mention it.","variants.4":"With douara (sheep's tripe): Choumicha cooks the tripe for 30 minutes with the spices, then adds the soaked beans, tied up in a muslin bag, and cooks for about 1 h 30 min. According to MarocMama, when meat is added to loubia, the most common choice in Morocco is pieces of sheep's stomach.","variants.5":"With fried red peppers, or with meatballs added 5 minutes before the end (Choumicha).","variants.6":"As a thinner soup, with carrots, celery and ras el hanout (Kookmutsjes, “Marokkaanse bonensoep”).","variants.7":"Express version with tinned beans, 20 to 35 minutes (Cooking With Alia; My Moroccan Food): quicker, but Taste of Maroc advises against it.","variants.8":"Cooked for hours in a clay pot over charcoal: the tastiest version according to MarocMama.","variants.9":"Some of the olive oil added only at the end of cooking (Morocco World News 2023); in her version with peppers, Choumicha even adds all the oil and spices once the beans are cooked."},"malwi":{"title":"Malwi (meloui), spiral layered flatbreads","darija":"malwi or meloui (plural: malawi, mlawi)","tagline":"A dough stretched very thin, folded into a strip, then rolled up into a snail: a well-made malwi unrolls layer by layer, piping hot, with butter and honey.","region":"All over Morocco (sold in the street and at bakers'); a great iftar classic in Rabat","occasion":"Breakfast, afternoon snack with tea, Ramadan iftar","img_alt":"Malwi (meloui) served on a Moroccan plate, in a riad in Marrakesh","serving":"Piping hot, with butter and honey (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News 2017 and 2019), jam or fresh cheese (Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia: honey or cheese); for the cheese, choose one made with microbial coagulant, without animal rennet, if you want to be strict. The traditional way, according to Taste of Maroc: dip them in a hot syrup of melted butter and honey. At breakfast and teatime (Taste of Maroc), and for the Ramadan iftar: Choumicha files her m'laoui under “Ramadan”, and Femmes du Maroc lists it among the great iftar classics in Rabat. They do not keep for more than a day at room temperature; once cooled, they can be frozen for up to 2 months and reheated in a frying pan or in the oven at 180 °C (Taste of Maroc).","ing.0.group":"Dough","ing.farine.name":"plain white flour (T45 or T55)","ing.farine.note":"soft wheat flour","ing.semoule.name":"extra-fine semolina (fino) or fine semolina","ing.semoule.note":"this is what gives malwi its flavour and texture","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.levure.name":"yeast","ing.levure.note":"no more: a very small amount is enough, with no rising time","ing.sucre.name":"sugar","ing.sucre.note":"optional: Choumicha and Cooking with Alia do not use any","ing.eau.name":"lukewarm water","ing.eau.note":"approximately: pour it in gradually, you need 200 to 225 ml depending on your flour","ing.1.group":"For layering","ing.huile.name":"neutral oil (sunflower), for the work surface, your hands and the dough balls","ing.huile.note":"approximately","ing.beurre.name":"butter, melted","ing.semoule-feuilletage.name":"fine semolina, to sprinkle between the layers","ing.semoule-feuilletage.note":"approximately","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.miel.name":"honey","ing.beurre-service.name":"butter","equipment.0":"A large mixing bowl (or a gsaa)","equipment.1":"Kitchen scales","equipment.2":"A large smooth work surface, at least 30 cm on each side (or a large tray)","equipment.3":"A large oiled tray, for the dough balls and the spirals","equipment.4":"Three bowls: oil, melted butter, semolina","equipment.5":"Cling film or a clean tea towel","equipment.6":"A large heavy-based frying pan (or two pans, or a double griddle)","equipment.7":"A wire rack","steps.0.title":"Mix the dough","steps.0.text":"In a large mixing bowl, mix {{farine}} flour, {{semoule}} semolina, {{sel}} salt, {{levure}} of yeast and, if using, {{sucre}} sugar. Pour in {{eau}} of lukewarm water gradually, mixing by hand, until you have a soft dough that does not stick. If it sticks, add a little flour; if it is dry, a little water.","steps.1.title":"Knead for 10 minutes","steps.1.text":"Knead for 10 minutes, with your fists and palms, until the dough is smooth, supple and elastic.","steps.1.timer.label":"Kneading","steps.1.timer.done":"Touch the dough: if it is not yet smooth and elastic, knead a little longer.","steps.1.img.alt":"Hands kneading dough","steps.1.img.caption":"Kneading the dough","steps.2.title":"Shape the dough balls","steps.2.text":"Oil your hands and a large tray. Divide the dough into egg-sized balls, about 60 g each, one per malwi. Roll them in a little oil, place them on the tray without letting them touch, cover and leave to rest for 15 minutes.","steps.2.timer.label":"Resting the dough balls","steps.2.timer.done":"The dough balls have rested: you can start stretching.","steps.3.title":"Set up your workstation","steps.3.text":"While the dough rests, melt {{beurre}} butter. Keep three bowls within reach: the melted butter, {{huile}} oil and {{semoule-feuilletage}} semolina. Oil your work surface generously.","steps.4.title":"Stretch very thin","steps.4.text":"Place a dough ball in the centre of the oiled surface. With oiled hands, flatten it, then stretch it with your fingertips, from the centre out to the edges, into a very thin, almost transparent sheet. Add more oil if the dough sticks, and try not to tear it.","steps.4.img.alt":"Oiled hands spreading out a very thin dough, in Morocco (here for msemen)","steps.4.img.caption":"Stretching with the fingertips","steps.5.title":"Butter and sprinkle","steps.5.text":"Spread a little melted butter over the whole sheet with your fingers, then sprinkle it lightly with semolina.","steps.6.title":"Fold into a long strip","steps.6.text":"Fold the top edge down to the centre, butter it and sprinkle it with a little semolina. Fold the bottom edge up over it: you now have a long, narrow strip. Butter and sprinkle it in turn.","steps.6.tip":"For even more layers, fold the sheet in four lengthways, buttering and sprinkling at each fold.","steps.7.title":"Roll into a spiral","steps.7.text":"Flatten the strip slightly to push out the air. Roll it up on itself, nice and tight, like a rug: you get a snail. Tuck the end under the spiral and pinch it so that it holds.","steps.8.title":"Leave the spirals to rest","steps.8.text":"Stand the spiral on the oiled tray, coiled side up. Stretch, butter, fold and roll the other dough balls in the same way, one by one, without letting the spirals touch. Cover and leave to rest for 15 minutes.","steps.8.timer.label":"Resting the spirals","steps.8.timer.done":"The spirals have rested: heat the frying pan.","steps.9.title":"Heat the frying pan","steps.9.text":"Heat a large heavy-based frying pan over medium heat.","steps.10.title":"Flatten the spiral","steps.10.text":"Oil your work surface and your hands. Take the first spiral, coiled side up, and flatten it firmly with your fingertips, from the centre out to the edges, into a disc about 3 mm thick, or about 15 cm across.","steps.11.title":"Cook, turning several times","steps.11.text":"Place the malwi in the hot pan and turn it several times, until it is nicely golden and crisp on both sides and cooked in the centre: allow a few minutes. If it browns too quickly, turn the heat down a little, otherwise the centre stays raw. If your pan is large, cook two at a time.","steps.11.img.alt":"Malwi cooking on a domed clay griddle over a wood fire, in Morocco","steps.11.img.caption":"The old-fashioned way, over a wood fire","steps.12.title":"Transfer to a wire rack","steps.12.text":"Put the cooked malwi on a wire rack. Carry on with the other spirals, flattening them as you go.","steps.13.title":"Serve hot","steps.13.text":"Serve the malwi hot, with {{beurre-service}} and {{miel}}.","steps.13.tip":"The traditional way: heat equal parts butter and honey in a frying pan, turn off the heat as soon as it starts to bubble, and dip each hot malwi in it on both sides.","secrets.0.text":"Malwi means “rolled” in Darija (Moroccan Arabic). When well made, it keeps the trace of its spiral: you can unroll it from the edge towards the centre as you eat it.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.1.text":"Semolina (or durum wheat flour) is the key to the traditional flavour and texture. Here, it makes up a third of the flour mixture.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha's proportion (250 g flour to 125 g durum wheat), the same as Lalla Moulati's (500 g to 250 g)","secrets.2.text":"The yeast is there only in a very small amount, and there is no rising stage.","secrets.2.source":"Taste of Maroc (“a very tiny amount of yeast”); Choumicha (2 pinches, no rising)","secrets.3.text":"Knead well, then let it rest: kneading gives the elasticity needed for layering, and resting makes the dough more pliable.","secrets.3.source":"Choumicha, tips for successful r'ghaifs","secrets.4.text":"Oil the dough balls and cover them: otherwise, they form a crust.","secrets.4.source":"Choumicha, tips for r'ghaifs; Taste of Maroc and Cooking with Alia (dough balls oiled and covered)","secrets.5.text":"Before rolling up the strip, flatten it to push out the air bubbles.","secrets.5.source":"Cooking with Alia","secrets.6.text":"Roll it up tightly: if the layers are too loose, they separate when you flatten the spiral.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.7.text":"Secure the end of the strip in the spiral, otherwise it unrolls.","secrets.7.source":"Choumicha (ends tucked into the coil); Cooking with Alia (end pinched)","secrets.8.text":"For sprinkling between the layers, fine semolina works better than durum wheat flour.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.9.text":"Cook the spirals in the order in which you rolled them.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.10.text":"Do not use too high a heat: malwi needs time to cook through to the centre.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking with Alia (brown until the centre is cooked)","secrets.11.text":"As soon as it comes out of the pan, push the edges towards the centre to fluff it up.","secrets.11.source":"Cooking with Alia","secrets.12.text":"Completely cooled malwi can be frozen for up to 2 months. Reheat them, thawed or still frozen, in a frying pan, or for a few minutes directly on the oven rack at 180 °C.","secrets.12.source":"Taste of Maroc; freezing also in Lalla Moulati (“طريقة الاحتفاظ به فالمجمد”)","mistakes.0":"Pouring in all the water at once: the dough becomes sticky; add it gradually (Cooking with Alia; My Moroccan Food, for msemen dough).","mistakes.1":"Cutting short the kneading or the resting: the dough is less supple and does not layer well (Choumicha, tips for r'ghaifs).","mistakes.2":"Leaving the dough balls exposed to the air without oil: they form a crust (Choumicha).","mistakes.3":"Tearing the sheet while stretching it: add more oil and stretch more gently (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.4":"Rolling the strip too loosely: the layers separate when you flatten it (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.5":"Cooking over too high a heat: golden outside, raw in the centre (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.6":"Keeping malwi for more than a day at room temperature: freeze them instead (Taste of Maroc).","variants.0":"Malwi with khlii (preserved dried meat): scatter crumbled khlii over the sheet before folding and rolling it; Choumicha uses 100 g for her dough made with 250 g flour and 125 g durum wheat (Choumicha, “M'laoui / M'laoui au Khliï”). Khlii made from halal meat, and with halal fat too: it is preserved in beef suet and olive oil, or kept in olive oil alone (Taste of Maroc, “Moroccan Preserved Meat – Khlii or Khlea”).","variants.1":"Half semolina, half flour: a more rustic texture, Taste of Maroc's favourite mix for malwi; Morocco World News (2019) also describes it as “half semolina, half flour” (Taste of Maroc; Morocco World News).","variants.2":"A little wholemeal flour in the dough, for flavour (Taste of Maroc: 60 g wholemeal flour out of 630 g).","variants.3":"Taste of Maroc's quick method: stretch a dough ball into a square, butter it, sprinkle it and roll it up like a cinnamon roll; wrap this roll in a second square, then a third, until you have used half the dough balls; cut the big roll into 8 slices, lay them spiral side up and leave to rest for 15 to 20 minutes before flattening (Taste of Maroc).","variants.4":"Baking powder in the dough: recipes published by Lalla Moulati use a sachet (Lalla Moulati, 2 August 2022 and 2 August 2023; the list from 1 October 2023 also mentions it, without saying whether it is for the dough or for the layering)."},"maqarouna":{"title":"Moroccan-style macaroni","darija":"Maqarouna (ma9arouna)","tagline":"Macaroni mixed with a tomato and kefta sauce with Moroccan spices, covered with béchamel and cheese, then browned in the oven. A family pasta bake found in several Moroccan recipes written in Darija (Moroccan Arabic).","region":"Moroccan home cooking (recipes in Darija published on Lalla Moulati; Cooking With Alia)","occasion":"A family dish for lunch or dinner","img_alt":"Macaroni and minced meat bake under a golden béchamel (photo of a Greek pastitsio, a very similar dish)","serving":"Serve hot, straight from the oven (Lalla Moulati, 2014 and 2019), after a few minutes' rest (Lalla Moulati, 2023), with a green salad or other salads (Lalla Moulati, 2019 and 2023).","ing.0.group":"Pasta","ing.pates.name":"macaroni","ing.pates.note":"or other short pasta","ing.eau-pates.name":"water","ing.sel-pates.name":"salt","ing.sel-pates.note":"for the pasta water","ing.huile-pates.name":"oil","ing.huile-pates.note":"a drizzle, optional","ing.1.group":"Kefta sauce","ing.huile.name":"oil","ing.huile.note":"neutral oil (sunflower) or olive oil","ing.oignon.name":"onion","ing.oignon.pl":"onions","ing.oignon.note":"to be grated","ing.kefta.name":"kefta (minced beef)","ing.kefta.note":"halal","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"to be chopped","ing.carotte.name":"carrot","ing.carotte.pl":"carrots","ing.carotte.note":"to be grated","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"to be grated","ing.concentre.name":"tomato purée","ing.persil.name":"chopped parsley","ing.persil.note":"or parsley and coriander","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust to taste","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.poivre.note":"to taste","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.gingembre.note":"to taste","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.curcuma.note":"to taste","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.paprika.note":"to taste","ing.cumin.name":"cumin","ing.cumin.note":"a little","ing.thym.name":"dried thyme","ing.thym.note":"no more","ing.2.group":"Béchamel","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.farine.name":"plain flour","ing.lait.name":"milk","ing.sel-bech.name":"salt","ing.poivre-bech.name":"black pepper","ing.muscade.name":"grated nutmeg","ing.muscade.note":"a hint","ing.3.group":"Gratin","ing.beurre-plat.name":"butter","ing.beurre-plat.note":"to grease the dish","ing.fromage.name":"grated cheese","ing.fromage.note":"if you want to be strict about halal: certified halal cheese; failing that, cheese made with microbial coagulant, with no rennet or other animal enzymes","equipment.0":"Large stockpot for cooking the pasta","equipment.1":"Colander","equipment.2":"Large deep frying pan or sauté pan","equipment.3":"Small saucepan for the béchamel","equipment.4":"Wooden spoon","equipment.5":"Grater","equipment.6":"Gratin dish","equipment.7":"Oven","steps.0.title":"Grate and chop the vegetables","steps.0.text":"Grate {{oignon:full}} and {{carotte:full}}. Cut {{tomates}} of tomatoes in half and grate the flesh; discard the skin. Chop {{ail:full}} and {{persil}} parsley.","steps.0.img.alt":"Tomato grated on a grater","steps.0.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.1.title":"Soften the onion","steps.1.text":"In a large deep frying pan, heat {{huile}} oil over low heat. Add the grated onion and stir until it is soft.","steps.2.title":"Add the kefta","steps.2.text":"Add {{kefta}} kefta. Break it up with the wooden spoon and fry it over low heat, stirring, until it is well browned.","steps.2.tip":"Minced meat must reach 71 °C in the centre: check with a thermometer if you have one (FDA).","steps.3.title":"Add the tomatoes and spices","steps.3.text":"Add the garlic, carrot, grated tomatoes and {{concentre}} tomato purée. Season with {{sel}} salt, then {{poivre}}, {{gingembre}}, {{curcuma}}, {{paprika}}, a little {{cumin}} and {{thym}} of thyme. Add the parsley and mix.","steps.4.title":"Reduce the sauce","steps.4.text":"Cook over low heat, stirring from time to time, until the sauce is thick and almost no water is left. Taste and adjust the salt.","steps.5.title":"Cook the macaroni","steps.5.text":"Meanwhile, bring {{eau-pates}} of water to the boil with some {{sel-pates}} and, if you like, a drizzle of {{huile-pates}}. Tip in {{pates}} macaroni and cook for the time shown on the packet, stirring from time to time.","steps.6.title":"Drain the pasta","steps.6.text":"Drain the macaroni in a colander and set aside.","steps.7.title":"Preheat the oven","steps.7.text":"Preheat the oven to 180 °C (moderate oven). Butter a gratin dish with a little {{beurre-plat}}.","steps.8.title":"Make the béchamel","steps.8.text":"In a small saucepan over low heat, melt {{beurre}} butter. Add {{farine}} flour and stir. Pour in {{lait}} of milk little by little, stirring constantly, until the sauce thickens. Add some {{sel-bech}}, {{poivre-bech}} and a hint of {{muscade}}, then remove from the heat.","steps.9.title":"Mix the pasta and sauce","steps.9.text":"Tip the macaroni into the kefta sauce and mix well.","steps.10.title":"Assemble the gratin","steps.10.text":"Spread half the pasta in the dish. Cover it with some of the béchamel and sprinkle over half of the {{fromage}} of grated cheese. Add the rest of the pasta, then the rest of the béchamel and the rest of the cheese.","steps.11.title":"Bake until golden","steps.11.text":"Bake until the top is nicely golden and the centre is piping hot: 74 °C (165 °F) on a thermometer if you have one (FDA). Check after 10 minutes and cook longer if needed, keeping an eye on it.","steps.11.timer.label":"Gratin in the oven","steps.11.timer.done":"Is the top nicely golden? If not, give it a few more minutes, keeping an eye on it.","steps.12.title":"Serve hot","steps.12.text":"Leave to rest for a few minutes, then serve hot, with a green salad.","secrets.0.text":"Grate the onion, carrot and tomatoes: several Moroccan recipes for maqarouna with kefta do so (the onion is sometimes just chopped).","secrets.0.source":"Lalla Moulati: grated onion (30 September 2015, in the meatballs; 23 July 2019; simply chopped on 10 November 2014), grated carrot (10 November 2014; 27 December 2014; 30 September 2015), grated tomatoes (2015; 2019)","secrets.1.text":"Let the sauce cook until almost no water is left before mixing it with the pasta.","secrets.1.source":"Lalla Moulati (27 December 2014: “يطيب وينشف من الماء”)","secrets.2.text":"The Moroccan spices for the kefta: salt, pepper (ibzar), ginger (skinjbir), turmeric (kharqoum), paprika (tahmira) and a little cumin.","secrets.2.source":"Lalla Moulati (recipes from 2014, 2015 and 2019)","secrets.3.text":"A tiny pinch of dried thyme: that was the secret of Alia's grandmother. Any more, and the sauce turns bitter.","secrets.3.source":"Cooking With Alia (her grandmother's recipe); thyme is also in two Lalla Moulati recipes (2014, 2019)","secrets.4.text":"Plenty of grated cheese on top: the other secret of Alia's grandmother.","secrets.4.source":"Cooking With Alia (her grandmother's recipe)","secrets.5.text":"Allow 1 litre of water per 100 g of pasta, well salted.","secrets.5.source":"Cooking With Alia (3 litres for 300 g); chef Andrea Tranchero, quoted by Lalla Moulati (2021)","secrets.6.text":"Many Moroccan recipes add a drizzle of oil to the pasta water; the Italian chef quoted by Lalla Moulati considers it unnecessary. It is up to you.","secrets.6.source":"Lalla Moulati (2014, 2015) and Cooking With Alia for the oil; Lalla Moulati (2021, chef Andrea Tranchero) against","secrets.7.text":"Pour the milk for the béchamel in little by little, stirring all the time: it stays smooth, with no lumps.","secrets.7.source":"Lalla Moulati (2014: stir until it thickens; 2023: milk added gradually, smooth sauce with no lumps)","secrets.8.text":"A hint of grated nutmeg flavours the béchamel.","secrets.8.source":"Lalla Moulati (three recipes in Darija from 2014 and 2015)","secrets.9.text":"Butter the dish before putting in the pasta.","secrets.9.source":"Lalla Moulati (2014; 2023)","secrets.10.text":"Build the gratin in two layers of pasta, with béchamel and cheese in the middle and on top.","secrets.10.source":"Lalla Moulati (27 December 2014; béchamel without cheese on 10 November 2014 and 30 September 2015)","secrets.11.text":"Let the gratin rest for a few minutes once it is out of the oven, before serving it.","secrets.11.source":"Lalla Moulati (2023)","secrets.12.text":"Safety: minced meat must reach 71 °C (160 °F) in the centre. Put leftovers in the fridge within 2 hours, in shallow containers, eat them within 3 days (3 to 4 days according to the USDA) and reheat them to 74 °C (165 °F).","secrets.12.source":"FDA, “Safe Food Handling”; Anses (8 April 2020); USDA FSIS, “Leftovers and Food Safety”","mistakes.0":"Putting a sauce that is still runny into the dish: let it reduce until almost no water is left (Lalla Moulati, 2014).","mistakes.1":"Overdoing the thyme: a small pinch is enough, otherwise it is bitter (Cooking With Alia).","mistakes.2":"Pouring all the milk into the béchamel at once: add it gradually while stirring, to avoid lumps (Lalla Moulati, 2023).","mistakes.3":"Overcooking the pasta before it goes in the oven: one Lalla Moulati recipe (2019) stops cooking it halfway, since it then goes into the oven.","mistakes.4":"Leaving leftovers out of the fridge for more than 2 hours (FDA).","variants.0":"With meatballs: mix the kefta with a small grated onion, a grated carrot, parsley, 2 tbsp breadcrumbs, salt, pepper, a little cumin, ginger and a pinch of cinnamon. Shape into small meatballs and cook them in a sauce of grated tomatoes with garlic (Lalla Moulati, 30 September 2015).","variants.1":"More vegetables: 2 peppers, finely diced and softened with the onion (Lalla Moulati, 10 November 2014); a grated courgette (Lalla Moulati, 27 December 2014).","variants.2":"With chicken or turkey: replace the kefta with turkey (“bibi”) or chicken breast in small pieces (Lalla Moulati, 10 November 2014), cooked to 74 °C (165 °F) in the centre (FDA). There is also a version with chicken breast, peppers, peas and sweetcorn (Amina Marrakchia on Lalla Moulati, 2023; ingredients only).","variants.3":"Without béchamel: kefta, mushrooms and green olives mixed with the pasta and thyme, covered with grated cheese, then baked (Lalla Moulati, 23 July 2019).","variants.4":"Without meat: tomato sauce with onion and garlic and a pinch of thyme, pasta covered with plenty of grated cheese, 10 minutes in the oven (Cooking With Alia, her grandmother's recipe).","variants.5":"With eggs: 2 beaten eggs in the béchamel, and 2 spoonfuls of béchamel in the bottom of the dish; the same recipe suggests the eggs instead of the 50 g of cheese (Lalla Moulati, 10 November 2014). In that case, leave it in the oven until the egg béchamel has set and reaches 71 °C (160 °F) in the centre (FDA).","variants.6":"On the hob, without an oven: cooked pasta mixed for 5 minutes into a tomato sauce with minced meat, with mozzarella (Lalla Moulati, 22 November 2015; a Levantine-style recipe).","variants.7":"Tuna and cream bake: 400 g pasta, 250 ml cream, 100 g grated cheese and 1 tin of tuna (Lalla Moulati, 3 November 2021)."},"msemen":{"title":"Msemen, square layered flatbreads","darija":"msemen (also rghayef)","tagline":"Hand-layered squares, slightly crisp on the outside and soft inside, to dip piping hot in honey and melted butter.","region":"Morocco (also Algeria and Tunisia)","occasion":"Breakfast, afternoon snack with tea, Ramadan iftar","serving":"Piping hot, dipped on both sides in a hot syrup of equal parts honey and butter: the most traditional way, according to Taste of Maroc. Or simply drizzled with honey and melted butter, with mint tea (Rachida, Pâtisserie GATÔ). At breakfast, as an afternoon snack and on the iftar table during Ramadan. It is also eaten plain or with jam. Once cooled, msemen freeze well: reheat them from frozen, in a frying pan over medium-low heat, or on a rack in the oven at 180 °C (Taste of Maroc).","ing.0.group":"Dough","ing.farine.name":"plain white flour (T45 or T55)","ing.semoule.name":"fine durum wheat semolina","ing.semoule.note":"or extra-fine semolina, or durum wheat flour","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.sel.note":"1 to 2 tsp depending on the source","ing.eau.name":"lukewarm water","ing.eau.note":"pour it in gradually, adjusting to suit your flour","ing.1.group":"For layering","ing.huile-faconnage.name":"neutral oil (sunflower or rapeseed), for the work surface, your hands and the dough balls","ing.huile-faconnage.note":"approximately; the dough does not absorb it, there will be some left over","ing.beurre.name":"butter, melted","ing.beurre.note":"unsalted or lightly salted","ing.huile-melange.name":"neutral oil, to mix with the melted butter","ing.semoule-pliage.name":"fine semolina, to sprinkle between the layers","ing.semoule-pliage.note":"about 1 tsp per msemen","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.miel.name":"Honey, as much as the butter","ing.miel.note":"in equal parts with the butter","ing.beurre-service.name":"Unsalted butter, as much as the honey","equipment.0":"A large mixing bowl","equipment.1":"Kitchen scales","equipment.2":"A large smooth work surface (granite, stainless steel) or a large tray","equipment.3":"An oiled tray or baking sheet, for the dough balls and the squares","equipment.4":"Three bowls: oil, butter-oil mixture, semolina","equipment.5":"A pastry brush","equipment.6":"Cling film","equipment.7":"A large heavy-based (cast-iron) or non-stick frying pan, or a griddle","equipment.8":"A wire rack","steps.0.title":"Mix the dough","steps.0.text":"In a large mixing bowl, mix {{farine}} flour, {{semoule}} fine semolina and {{sel}} salt. Pour in {{eau}} of lukewarm water gradually, mixing by hand, until it forms a ball. If the dough sticks, add a little flour; if it is dry, a little water.","steps.1.title":"Knead for a long time","steps.1.text":"Knead for 15 minutes: press the dough down with your palm or fist, fold it over, and repeat. It is ready when it is smooth, supple and elastic, and no longer sticks to your fingers. Do not cut it short: this kneading is what lets you stretch the dough very thin.","steps.1.timer.label":"Kneading","steps.1.timer.done":"Touch the dough: if it is not yet smooth, supple and elastic, knead for a few more minutes.","steps.1.img.alt":"Hands kneading dough","steps.1.img.caption":"Kneading the dough","steps.2.title":"Shape the dough balls","steps.2.text":"Oil your hands and a large tray. Divide the dough into equal balls of about 85 g, one per msemen, nice and smooth on top. Roll them in the oil, place them on the tray without letting them touch, cover with cling film and leave to rest for 15 minutes.","steps.2.timer.label":"Resting the dough balls","steps.2.timer.done":"The dough balls have relaxed: you can start stretching.","steps.3.title":"Set up your workstation","steps.3.text":"While the dough rests, mix {{beurre}} melted butter with {{huile-melange}} oil. Keep within reach this mixture, a bowl with {{huile-faconnage}} oil and a bowl with {{semoule-pliage}} semolina. Oil your work surface and your hands generously.","steps.4.title":"Stretch very thin","steps.4.text":"Dip a dough ball in the oil and place it in the centre of the work surface. Stretch it from the centre out to the edges with your fingertips, in small quick movements, into a disc about 25 cm across, so thin that you can almost see the work surface through it. A few small holes do not matter. Work quickly, otherwise the dough dries out.","steps.4.img.alt":"Hands stretching msemen dough on an oiled surface","steps.5.title":"Make the first fold","steps.5.text":"Brush the whole disc with the butter-oil mixture and sprinkle it lightly with semolina. Fold one edge to the centre, then the opposite edge over it: you now have a long strip.","steps.6.title":"Fold into a square","steps.6.text":"Brush the strip with butter-oil and sprinkle it with semolina. Fold one end to the centre, then the other end over it: you now have a square of about 8 cm. Place it on the oiled tray and repeat with the other dough balls, remembering the order.","steps.7.title":"Leave the squares to rest","steps.7.text":"Cover the squares with cling film and leave them to relax for at least 15 minutes. If they resist when you flatten them, let them rest a little longer instead of forcing them.","steps.7.timer.label":"Resting the squares","steps.7.timer.done":"The squares have rested: heat the frying pan.","steps.8.title":"Heat the frying pan","steps.8.text":"Heat a large heavy-based frying pan over medium heat. It is ready when a drop of water sizzles and evaporates almost immediately. Oil it lightly, with a pastry brush or kitchen paper.","steps.9.title":"Flatten to double the size","steps.9.text":"Take the first folded square and place it on the oiled work surface. With oiled hands, pat it out firmly to about 18 cm on each side, a little more than double the folded square, keeping the square shape. Do not overwork it, otherwise the layers fuse together.","steps.10.title":"Cook, turning several times","steps.10.text":"Place it in the hot pan and turn it several times, about 5 minutes in total. It is ready when it is golden and crisp outside, and cooked through in the centre. If it browns too quickly, turn the heat down a little: otherwise the centre stays raw. If your pan is large, cook two at a time.","steps.10.timer.label":"Cooking one msemen","steps.10.timer.done":"Golden and crisp on both sides? Take it out onto the wire rack.","steps.10.img.alt":"Msemen cooking on a large griddle","steps.11.title":"Separate the layers","steps.11.text":"Put the msemen on a wire rack. After 1 to 2 minutes, hold it by two opposite edges and flex it back and forth for a few seconds, like a swing: the layers come apart. Carry on with the other squares, in the order in which you folded them.","steps.12.title":"Dip in honey and butter, serve with tea","steps.12.text":"In a frying pan, heat equal parts honey and butter. As soon as the mixture starts to simmer, turn off the heat. Dip each hot msemen in it on both sides, then serve straight away with mint tea.","secrets.0.text":"The real secret is the kneading: at least 15 minutes, until you can stretch the dough so thin that you can see the work surface through it.","secrets.0.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida","secrets.1.text":"Add fine semolina to the flour: without it, the msemen stays rubbery to eat after cooking.","secrets.1.source":"Gâteau et cuisine Rachida","secrets.2.text":"The semolina sprinkled between the layers keeps them separate during cooking.","secrets.2.source":"Morocco World News (Layla Dahamou); MarocMama","secrets.3.text":"Be generous with the oil for stretching: it is not absorbed by the dough, and a well-made msemen is not greasy.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.4.text":"Let the dough balls rest, then the squares: if the dough resists stretching, wait a little longer instead of forcing it.","secrets.4.source":"The Hot Bread Kitchen Cookbook (via The Splendid Table)","secrets.5.text":"Stretch quickly: otherwise the dough dries out and may tear.","secrets.5.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou)","secrets.6.text":"Medium heat, no higher: too hot, and the msemen browns too quickly, the centre stays raw and the layers do not separate well.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.7.text":"One to two minutes after cooking, “swing” the msemen, holding it by two opposite edges: the layers come apart.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.8.text":"Cook the squares in the order in which you folded them.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc; The Hot Bread Kitchen Cookbook","mistakes.0":"Pouring in all the water at once: the dough becomes very sticky and can no longer be kneaded (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.1":"Cutting the kneading short: it is what lets you stretch the dough very thin (Rachida).","mistakes.2":"Being stingy with butter and semolina when folding: the layers do not separate (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.3":"Cooking over too high a heat: too brown outside, raw in the centre (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.4":"Forcing a dough that resists instead of letting it rest a little longer (Hot Bread Kitchen).","mistakes.5":"Stacking the msemen while you are stretching them: they stick together (Hot Bread Kitchen).","mistakes.6":"Overworking the square when flattening it: the layers fuse together (MarocMama).","mistakes.7":"Letting the dough rise if you have added yeast (Rachida).","variants.0":"Stuffed msemen (rghayef): the filling goes on the stretched disc before folding. Classic filling: onion, parsley, khlii (preserved dried meat) and spices (Bladi.net).","variants.1":"Rghayef with onions: lots of very thinly sliced onions, a large bunch of parsley, salt, cumin, red chilli and a pinch of pepper, fried in oil with beef fat or khlii; about 2 tbsp of filling in the centre of the disc, then the dough is closed up around it (Bladi.net).","variants.2":"Minced meat filling: minced meat, onion, peppers, parsley, cumin and paprika, cooked then cooled before filling (Morocco World News; My Moroccan Food, her mother's version). Traditionally cooked one by one in a frying pan, they can also be baked in the oven (My Moroccan Food).","variants.3":"Sweet filling: crushed almonds and sugar (Bladi.net).","variants.4":"With a pinch of yeast added at the end of kneading, without letting the dough rise: softer msemen that still stay crisp (Rachida). Taste of Maroc and Morocco World News use only 1/4 tsp for all the dough.","variants.5":"A little baking powder mixed into the semolina that is sprinkled between the layers (Rachida; Pâtisserie GATÔ).","variants.6":"More semolina in the dough: half flour, half semolina (My Moroccan Food, Pâtisserie GATÔ, Kookmutsjes), or even more semolina than flour (Paula Wolfert); Taste of Maroc finds the texture coarser with half and half.","variants.7":"Even flakier: wrap a folded square in a second disc of very thin dough, then fold it into a square again (Rachida; Pâtisserie GATÔ).","variants.8":"Rfissa: cooked msemen are torn up while hot, reheated over steam, then topped with chicken, lentils and broth; a dish for special occasions (Paula Wolfert).","variants.9":"Malwi (meloui): the round cousin of msemen, rolled into a spiral (Taste of Maroc)."},"omelette":{"title":"Rolled omelette with fines herbes","tagline":"Three eggs beaten with parsley, chives and other fines herbes, cooked in an instant in butter and then rolled: smooth outside, soft inside, the French way.","region":"France: Escoffier's classic cuisine (Le Guide culinaire, 1903)","occasion":"A quick meal, ready in a few minutes, whenever there are eggs and a few fresh herbs","img_alt":"Rolled omelette, yellow and not browned, sprinkled with chives","serving":"Straight away, rolled and glazed with butter (Escoffier, Chef Simon), with a green salad if you like, as the custom cited by Wikipedia allows. If there is any left, put it in the fridge within 2 hours (1 hour if it is above 32 °C), eat it within 3 to 4 days and reheat it right through, to 74 °C (FDA, the US health agency).","ing.0.group":"Omelette","ing.oeufs.name":"fresh egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"fresh eggs","ing.oeufs.note":"3 per omelette; throw away any that are cracked or broken, and do not wash them (Anses)","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.poivre.name":"pepper","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.beurre.note":"15 g per omelette, for cooking","ing.1.group":"Fines herbes","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.ciboulette.name":"chives","ing.cerfeuil.name":"chervil","ing.cerfeuil.note":"optional, if you can find it","ing.estragon.name":"tarragon","ing.estragon.note":"optional, if you can find it","ing.2.group":"To finish","ing.beurre-lustrer.name":"butter","ing.beurre-lustrer.note":"a small knob per omelette, to make it shine","equipment.0":"One bowl per omelette and a fork","equipment.1":"A small non-stick frying pan","equipment.2":"A flexible spatula (a metal fork damages the non-stick coating)","equipment.3":"A chopping board and a very sharp knife, for slicing the herbs finely","equipment.4":"One plate per omelette","steps.0.title":"Slice the herbs finely","steps.0.text":"Finely slice the {{persil}}, the {{ciboulette}} and, if you have them, the {{cerfeuil}} and {{estragon}}, with a very sharp knife.","steps.0.tip":"Slice finely, do not chop: chopped herbs give the omelette a “grassy taste” (Chef Simon).","steps.1.title":"Crack the eggs","steps.1.text":"Crack the eggs: 3 per omelette, in a separate bowl for each ({{oeufs:full}} in total).","steps.2.title":"Season and beat","steps.2.text":"In each bowl, add {{sel}}, {{poivre}} and that omelette's share of the herbs. Beat with a fork, not too much, just enough to mix the whites and yolks well.","steps.3.title":"Heat the butter","steps.3.text":"Put 15 g butter in the small frying pan (the amount for one omelette; {{beurre}} in total) and heat over medium heat. Wait until it melts and foams, without letting it brown.","steps.3.tip":"Chef Simon prefers clarified butter, or a mixture of butter and oil.","steps.4.title":"Pour in and stir","steps.4.text":"Pour the beaten eggs from one bowl into the hot pan. Stir immediately with the spatula, drawing the edges that are starting to set towards the centre, so that the egg that is still liquid runs out and cooks in turn.","steps.5.title":"Stop while it is still runny","steps.5.text":"Stop stirring when the omelette has almost set and there is still a thin layer of runny egg on top. The underside should stay pale, without browning.","steps.5.tip":"The FDA (the US health agency) advises cooking eggs until both the white and the yolk are firm, and points out that children, elderly people, pregnant women and vulnerable people are at greater risk of serious salmonella infection: for them, cook the omelette until it is fully set.","steps.6.title":"Roll the omelette","steps.6.text":"Turn down the heat. Loosen the edge with the spatula, then tilt the pan over the plate: the omelette rolls over on itself. Help it along with the spatula.","steps.7.title":"Glaze it and serve","steps.7.text":"Rub a small knob of {{beurre-lustrer}} over the top to make it shine, and serve immediately. If there are more omelettes to make, go back to the step “Heat the butter” with the next bowl.","secrets.0.text":"An omelette, according to Escoffier, is “scrambled eggs of a special kind enclosed in a coating of coagulated egg”: soft inside, just set enough outside to be rolled.","secrets.0.source":"Escoffier, Le Guide culinaire (1903); Cuisine et Vins de France, cited by Wikipedia","secrets.1.text":"True fines herbes are parsley, chives, chervil and tarragon. For Escoffier, an omelette with parsley alone is not a fines herbes omelette.","secrets.1.source":"Escoffier, Le Guide culinaire (1903); Wikipedia (definition taken up by the 1938 Larousse gastronomique)","secrets.2.text":"Escoffier's measurements for a 3-egg omelette: half a pinch of fine salt, a dash of pepper, 15 g butter and a spoonful of fines herbes.","secrets.2.source":"Escoffier, Le Guide culinaire (1903)","secrets.3.text":"Slice the herbs finely with a knife instead of chopping them, otherwise the omelette takes on a “grassy taste”.","secrets.3.source":"Chef Simon","secrets.4.text":"Do not beat too much: a few strokes of the fork are enough to mix the whites and yolks.","secrets.4.source":"Chef Simon (“not too much”); Jules Gouffé and A. Bautte, cited by Wikipedia","secrets.5.text":"The butter must never brown. Chef Simon prefers clarified butter, or a mixture of butter and oil.","secrets.5.source":"Wikipedia (all the authorities call for very good butter, without burning it); Chef Simon","secrets.6.text":"No browning: the rolled omelette stays pale yellow. Chef Simon considers this point “very important”.","secrets.6.source":"Chef Simon; the English Wikipedia, citing Julia Child (a French omelette is not browned, or only very slightly)","secrets.7.text":"Cooking is quick: Larousse defines an omelette as beaten eggs “cooked quickly in a frying pan”.","secrets.7.source":"Larousse; Wikipedia (“cooking must stay quick”)","secrets.8.text":"“Baveuse” means cooked little enough for the inside to stay soft and moist. But, as Escoffier points out, some people like it well cooked, others just done: ask everyone.","secrets.8.source":"Larousse (“baveux”); Escoffier; Chef Simon (runny, soft or well done)","secrets.9.text":"A pan of the right size: too many eggs for the pan, and the omelette becomes thick, badly cooked and tough.","secrets.9.source":"Wikipedia, citing Cora Millet-Robinet (1884); Il Giornale del Cibo recommends an 18 to 20 cm pan","secrets.10.text":"The chefs' finishing touch: a little butter on the rolled omelette to make it shine.","secrets.10.source":"Escoffier; Chef Simon; Wikipedia (“glaze”, citing Michel Maincent-Morel)","secrets.11.text":"Several people to feed? Make several omelettes rather than one that is too big.","secrets.11.source":"Jules Gouffé (1873), cited by Wikipedia; Escoffier takes the 3-egg omelette as his model","mistakes.0":"Beating the eggs for too long (Chef Simon; Jules Gouffé, cited by Wikipedia).","mistakes.1":"Letting the butter brown or the omelette colour (Chef Simon; Wikipedia).","mistakes.2":"Putting too many eggs in a small pan: the omelette becomes thick and tough (Wikipedia, citing Cora Millet-Robinet).","mistakes.3":"Overcooking it: it becomes dry (Wikipedia, citing Louis Liger).","mistakes.4":"Chopping the herbs instead of slicing them finely (Chef Simon).","mistakes.5":"Using only parsley and calling it “fines herbes” (Escoffier).","variants.0":"With mushrooms: Escoffier adds 40 g of sliced mushrooms, sautéed in butter, to the eggs for a 3-egg omelette.","variants.1":"With tarragon only, “à la Chartres”: a pinch of chopped tarragon in the eggs (Escoffier).","variants.2":"With coriander: Chef Simon leaves the choice of herbs open (chives, chervil or coriander).","variants.3":"Softer and cheaper with milk: for 4 people, Chef Simon replaces his usual 9 eggs with 6 eggs and 1.5 dl (150 ml) milk.","variants.4":"Folded in three, the classic way (Wikipedia), or in half, the English way (Great British Chefs), instead of rolled.","variants.5":"With cheese, a classic filling according to Great British Chefs. If you want to be strict: cheese made with microbial coagulant, without animal rennet."},"pastilla-poulet":{"title":"Chicken and almond pastilla (Fez style)","darija":"bastila bel djaj","tagline":"Crackling pastry, melt-in-the-mouth chicken with saffron onions and a layer of almonds with cinnamon and orange blossom: the great sweet-and-savoury dish of Fez cooking.","region":"Fez (the Fez version)","occasion":"Celebrations, weddings and grand dinner parties","serving":"Serve it piping hot on a large round dish. At a celebration meal, it is served as a starter, before the chicken tagine or the méchoui. You can add a few whole fried almonds to the decoration.","ing.0.group":"Chicken and onion sauce","ing.poulet.name":"halal chicken, cut into pieces","ing.poulet.note":"or whole legs and thighs; beldi if possible","ing.oignons.name":"onions","ing.oignons.note":"thinly sliced or grated","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"chopped","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.coriandre.note":"chopped","ing.huile.name":"olive or neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.smen.name":"smen (aged salted butter) or butter","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.cannelle-sauce.name":"ground cinnamon","ing.safran.name":"saffron threads","ing.sel.name":"salt, to taste","ing.sel.note":"as a guide, about 1.5 to 2 tsp; adjust the seasoning once the sauce has reduced","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper, to taste","ing.poivre.note":"as a guide, about 1 tsp","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.sucre.name":"caster sugar","ing.sucre.note":"or honey","ing.oeufs.name":"egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"eggs","ing.1.group":"Almond layer","ing.amandes.name":"blanched almonds (skins removed)","ing.huile-friture.name":"neutral oil","ing.huile-friture.note":"for frying the almonds","ing.sucre-glace.name":"icing sugar","ing.sucre-glace.note":"adjust to taste","ing.cannelle-amandes.name":"ground cinnamon","ing.fleur-oranger.name":"orange blossom water","ing.2.group":"Assembly","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.beurre.note":"to be melted; or smen","ing.feuilles-tour.name":"warqa or brick pastry sheet (for the sides)","ing.feuilles-tour.pl":"warqa or brick pastry sheets (for the sides)","ing.feuilles-tour.note":"allow 12 to 15 sheets in all, to be on the safe side","ing.feuilles-fond.name":"warqa or brick pastry sheet (for the base)","ing.feuilles-fond.pl":"warqa or brick pastry sheets (for the base)","ing.feuilles-dessus.name":"warqa or brick pastry sheet (for the top)","ing.feuilles-dessus.pl":"warqa or brick pastry sheets (for the top)","ing.jaune.name":"egg yolk","ing.jaune.pl":"egg yolks","ing.jaune.note":"to seal the folds","ing.3.group":"Decoration","ing.sucre-glace-decor.name":"icing sugar","ing.cannelle-decor.name":"ground cinnamon","equipment.0":"Large pot or casserole dish with a lid","equipment.1":"Frying pan","equipment.2":"Colander and kitchen paper","equipment.3":"Food processor (or pestle and mortar)","equipment.4":"30 cm round, low-sided tin (like a tart dish)","equipment.5":"Pastry brush","equipment.6":"Cling film and a tea towel","equipment.7":"Fine sieve for the icing sugar","equipment.8":"Large round serving dish","steps.0.title":"Prepare the chicken and herbs","steps.0.text":"Cut {{poulet}} of chicken into pieces. Thinly slice {{oignons}} of onions. Chop {{persil}} of parsley and {{coriandre}} of coriander.","steps.0.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.0.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.1.title":"Sauté the chicken with the spices","steps.1.text":"Put the chicken, onions, herbs, {{huile}} of oil and {{smen}} of smen (or butter) in a large pot. Add {{gingembre}} of ginger, {{curcuma}} of turmeric, {{cannelle-sauce}} of cinnamon, {{safran}} of saffron, and some salt and pepper. Mix, cover and leave to cook over medium heat, stirring from time to time.","steps.1.timer.label":"Chicken with spices","steps.1.timer.done":"Move on to the next step: pour in the water.","steps.2.title":"Simmer","steps.2.text":"Pour in {{eau}} of water and bring to the boil. Turn down to medium-low heat, cover and simmer, turning the pieces from time to time. It is ready when the meat comes away easily from the bone. Meanwhile, prepare the almonds.","steps.2.timer.label":"Simmering the chicken","steps.2.timer.done":"Does the meat come away from the bone? If not, cook it a little longer.","steps.3.title":"Fry the almonds","steps.3.text":"Put {{amandes}} of almonds in a frying pan with {{huile-friture}} of oil that is still cold. Heat over medium heat, stirring often, until they are evenly golden. Drain them on kitchen paper and leave them to cool.","steps.4.title":"Make the almond mixture","steps.4.text":"Coarsely crush the almonds in the food processor with short pulses: there should still be small pieces, not a powder. Mix them with {{sucre-glace}} of icing sugar, {{cannelle-amandes}} of cinnamon and {{fleur-oranger}} of orange blossom water. Set aside.","steps.5.title":"Reduce the onion sauce","steps.5.text":"Take out the chicken and put it on a plate to cool slightly. Add {{sucre}} of sugar to the sauce and leave it to reduce over medium-low heat, uncovered, stirring often. It is ready when there is no liquid left, only the soft onions in their fat.","steps.5.timer.label":"Reducing the sauce","steps.5.timer.done":"No liquid left, just the fat? If not, keep going for a few more minutes, stirring.","steps.6.title":"Shred the chicken","steps.6.text":"While the sauce is reducing, remove the skin and bones from the chicken. Shred the meat into small pieces and set it aside.","steps.7.title":"Cook the eggs in the sauce","steps.7.text":"Beat {{oeufs:full}} as for an omelette. Pour gradually into the reduced sauce over low heat, stirring constantly. Stop when the mixture has set into a thick cream, like very dry scrambled eggs, with no liquid.","steps.8.title":"Drain and leave to cool","steps.8.text":"Pour the eggs into a colander set over a bowl and keep the fat that drips through. Leave the eggs and the chicken to cool completely, separately. You can prepare the fillings the day before and keep them in the fridge.","steps.9.title":"Prepare the tin","steps.9.text":"Preheat the oven to 180 °C. Melt {{beurre}} of butter and mix it with the reserved fat. Generously butter a 30 cm round tin. Keep the pastry sheets under cling film and a tea towel so that they don't dry out.","steps.10.title":"Build the base","steps.10.text":"Brush {{feuilles-tour:full}} with butter and arrange in a rosette in the tin, overlapping each other and hanging well over the edge. Lay {{feuilles-fond:full}}, brushed with butter, in the centre to strengthen the base.","steps.11.title":"Layer the fillings","steps.11.text":"Spread the shredded chicken in an even layer. Add the eggs, then finish with the almond mixture, spread nice and flat.","steps.12.title":"Close the pastilla","steps.12.text":"Fold the overhanging sheets over the almonds, brushing them with butter as you go. Beat {{jaune:full}} and brush over the folds to stick them down well.","steps.13.title":"Cover the top","steps.13.text":"Brush {{feuilles-dessus:full}} with butter and lay on top, overlapping. Tuck the edges down between the pastilla and the tin to get a neat round edge. Brush the whole top with the rest of the butter.","steps.14.title":"Bake","steps.14.text":"Put it in the oven on the middle shelf. Bake until the pastilla is golden brown and crisp.","steps.14.timer.label":"Baking","steps.14.timer.done":"Golden brown and crisp? Take it out. If not, give it a few more minutes, keeping an eye on it.","steps.15.title":"Rest, decorate and serve","steps.15.text":"Leave the pastilla to rest before cutting it. Carefully slide it onto a large round dish. Sift {{sucre-glace-decor}} of icing sugar over the top through a fine sieve, then draw criss-cross lines with {{cannelle-decor}} of cinnamon. Serve piping hot.","steps.15.timer.label":"Resting before cutting","steps.15.timer.done":"Decorate and serve piping hot!","secrets.0.text":"Reduce the onion sauce until it “sits in its own fat”: not a drop of liquid left. A wet filling makes the pastilla soggy.","secrets.0.source":"Barlamane (Mariam Bensellham, Moroccan cooking expert); My Moroccan Food; Amour de cuisine","secrets.1.text":"Cook the eggs in the reduced sauce, stirring constantly, until they have the texture of very dry scrambled eggs.","secrets.1.source":"Cooking with Alia; Ma fleur d'oranger; Paula Wolfert (The Food of Morocco)","secrets.2.text":"Drain the egg mixture and keep the fat: mixed with the melted butter, it is used to brush the pastry sheets.","secrets.2.source":"Femmes du Maroc (the cooks of Maymana); La Cuisine Marocaine; Paula Wolfert","secrets.3.text":"Put the almonds into the oil while it is still cold, before heating: they will brown evenly.","secrets.3.source":"Barlamane (Mariam Bensellham)","secrets.4.text":"Crush the almonds coarsely, without grinding them to a powder.","secrets.4.source":"Barlamane; Amour de cuisine; Paula Wolfert","secrets.5.text":"Assemble the pastilla with thoroughly cold fillings, otherwise the sheets will tear.","secrets.5.source":"Ma fleur d'oranger","secrets.6.text":"Butter each sheet generously: that is what makes it crisp.","secrets.6.source":"Barlamane; Amour de cuisine","secrets.7.text":"Keep the sheets under cling film and a tea towel during assembly so that they don't dry out.","secrets.7.source":"Amour de cuisine","secrets.8.text":"A little sugar (or honey) added to the onion sauce while it reduces strengthens the sweet-and-savoury balance.","secrets.8.source":"My Moroccan Food; Ma fleur d'oranger; Femmes du Maroc","secrets.9.text":"Make the fillings in advance (the day before, up to 48 h) and keep them separately in the fridge: on the day, all that is left is the assembly.","secrets.9.source":"Amour de cuisine; Paula Wolfert; Ma fleur d'oranger","mistakes.0":"Assembling with a sauce that is still runny: the pastilla turns soggy.","mistakes.1":"Leaving the eggs runny: their liquid soaks the pastry sheets.","mistakes.2":"Assembling with a lukewarm filling: the sheets tear.","mistakes.3":"Grinding the almonds to a fine powder instead of crushing them coarsely.","mistakes.4":"Leaving the sheets uncovered during assembly: they dry out.","mistakes.5":"Being stingy with the butter: the pastilla won't be crisp.","mistakes.6":"Cutting the pastilla straight out of the oven: leave it to rest for 10 min.","mistakes.7":"Reheating leftovers in the microwave: the pastry goes soft. Reheat them in the oven instead.","variants.0":"With pigeon, the traditional Fez version: replace the chicken with halal pigeons, cooked the same way with onions, spices and herbs, then boned and skinned; everything else stays the same. The blog Épices et douceurs (recipe attributed to Fatéma Hal) simmers them for 30 to 35 min, covered. At weddings, pigeon pastilla is served just before the chicken m'hammar (Taste of Maroc).","variants.1":"Almonds toasted in the oven instead of fried, with a drizzle of oil, until golden (My Moroccan Food: about 18 min at 160 °C; Amour de cuisine: about 15 min at 160 °C).","variants.2":"Eggs scrambled separately in a little butter, instead of being cooked in the sauce (My Moroccan Food).","variants.3":"Honey instead of sugar in the sauce (Femmes du Maroc; My Moroccan Food).","variants.4":"Individual pastillas: the same fillings in 8 to 12 cm rings, 20 to 30 min in the oven (Femmes du Maroc: 180 °C, 20 to 25 min; Amour de cuisine: 180 °C, about 30 min; My Moroccan Food: 200 °C, about 25 min).","variants.5":"Filo pastry instead of warqa or brick: 2 to 3 buttered filo sheets per layer and about 5 min extra baking time (Amour de cuisine).","variants.6":"Paula Wolfert's way (The Food of Morocco): oven at 220 °C for 20 min, then the pastilla is turned out onto a buttered baking tray, brushed with butter and put back in the oven for 10 min; she also adds lemon juice to the sauce before the eggs, and a pinch of nutmeg.","variants.7":"Other touches, depending on the family: a little crushed gum arabic in the sauce (Cooking with Alia, recipe 695; Épices et douceurs) or some ras el hanout (Femmes du Maroc)."},"pates-thon-tomate":{"title":"Tuna and tomato pasta","tagline":"A softened onion, tomatoes and tuna in oil: a sauce that simmers while the spaghetti cooks, the Italian classic for evenings when the cupboard is almost bare.","region":"All over Italy: Bologna recipe registered in 2018 by the Accademia Italiana della Cucina (Dissapore; Il Giornale del Cibo), Rome and Palermo versions (Wikipedia, Dissapore)","occasion":"Quick dinner using tins from the cupboard. In Italy, it is the students' dish, and it used to be the dish for meatless days (Dissapore, Il Giornale del Cibo)","img_alt":"Spaghetti coated in tomato and tuna sauce","serving":"Straight away, with a little raw parsley on the plate if you like (the Bologna recipe, according to Dissapore and Il Giornale del Cibo). Leftovers: in an airtight container in the fridge, for 1 day at most (Cookist); refrigerate them within 2 hours of cooking (1 hour if it is above 32 °C) and reheat them until the centre reaches 74 °C (FDA, the US health agency; Anses for the 2 hours).","ing.0.group":"Sauce","ing.huile.name":"extra virgin olive oil","ing.oignon.name":"medium onion","ing.oignon.pl":"medium onions","ing.tomates.name":"tinned peeled tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"or chopped tomatoes","ing.thon.name":"tuna in oil, drained","ing.thon.note":"preferably in olive oil","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"for the sauce and the pasta water","ing.1.group":"Pasta","ing.spaghetti.name":"spaghetti","ing.spaghetti.note":"or short pasta","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"a large panful, for cooking the pasta","ing.eau-cuisson.name":"pasta cooking water","ing.eau-cuisson.note":"a few spoonfuls","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"optional","equipment.0":"A large saucepan with its lid","equipment.1":"A sauté pan or large frying pan","equipment.2":"A colander","equipment.3":"A chopping board and a knife","equipment.4":"A wooden spoon","equipment.5":"A tin opener and a bowl","steps.0.title":"Put the water on to heat","steps.0.text":"Fill a large saucepan with {{eau}}, cover it and bring it to the boil.","steps.1.title":"Slice the onion","steps.1.text":"Peel and very thinly slice {{oignon:full}}.","steps.2.title":"Prepare the tuna and tomatoes","steps.2.text":"Drain {{thon}} of tuna and flake it. Cut {{tomates}} of peeled tomatoes into small pieces.","steps.3.title":"Soften the onion","steps.3.text":"In the sauté pan, heat {{huile}} of olive oil over low heat and add the onion. Let it soften, stirring from time to time, until tender and translucent.","steps.4.title":"Add the tomatoes","steps.4.text":"Add the tomatoes, season lightly with {{sel}} and leave to simmer over low heat, stirring from time to time.","steps.5.title":"Start the pasta","steps.5.text":"When the water boils, salt it, then add {{spaghetti}} of spaghetti. Cook until al dente, following the time on the packet.","steps.6.title":"Add the tuna","steps.6.text":"As soon as the pasta is in the water, add the tuna to the sauce and stir gently. Leave to simmer over low heat until the sauce is smooth and velvety: the tuna should heat through without cooking for too long, or it will dry out.","steps.7.title":"Save some water","steps.7.text":"Just before draining, scoop a little {{eau-cuisson}} into a bowl.","steps.8.title":"Mix the pasta and sauce","steps.8.text":"Drain the al dente pasta and tip it into the sauté pan. Toss briefly over the heat, adding a little cooking water to bind the sauce.","steps.9.title":"Serve at once","steps.9.text":"Serve straight away. If you like, sprinkle a little {{persil}} over each plate.","secrets.0.text":"Add the tuna towards the end: if it cooks for too long, it dries out.","secrets.0.source":"Cookist (chef Ruben Bondì); the Bologna recipe according to Dissapore, 2018 (tuna added 10 minutes before the end); Il Giornale del Cibo (the tuna must not cook for long in the sauce and goes in at the end); Cristina Bragaglia, quoted by Dissapore (“in Bologna, the tuna goes in afterwards”)","secrets.1.text":"Use tuna in oil, preferably olive oil: its flesh is softer and more flavourful.","secrets.1.source":"Dissapore (cherry tomato version: “softer texture, richer flavour”)","secrets.2.text":"Let the onion soften gently until tender and translucent, without letting it brown.","secrets.2.source":"Dissapore (2018 and 2022)","secrets.3.text":"Drain the tuna well, then flake it.","secrets.3.source":"Dissapore (drained tuna); the Bologna recipe according to Dissapore, 2018, and Il Giornale del Cibo (flaked tuna)","secrets.4.text":"A little pasta cooking water helps the sauce coat the pasta well.","secrets.4.source":"Dissapore (half a ladleful; “a few spoonfuls” in the cherry tomato version)","secrets.5.text":"Spaghetti or short pasta? Spaghetti is the tradition; Dissapore finds that short pasta goes better with tuna, and Cookist uses rigatoni.","secrets.5.source":"Wikipedia (it); Dissapore; Cookist","secrets.6.text":"For a more refined version, use ventresca, the fattiest and finest part of the tuna.","secrets.6.source":"Dissapore","secrets.7.text":"The parsley goes on raw, on the plate once it has been served.","secrets.7.source":"The Bologna recipe, according to Dissapore (2018) and Il Giornale del Cibo (2019): an accepted variation","secrets.8.text":"It was a dish for meatless days: in Bologna, people ate it on Fridays and on Christmas Eve.","secrets.8.source":"Cristina Bragaglia, quoted by Il Giornale del Cibo and by Dissapore; Wikipedia (it)","secrets.9.text":"Tinned tuna arrived in Italy in 1868, in Genoa; the recipe spread after that.","secrets.9.source":"Il Giornale del Cibo; Dissapore (Cristina Bragaglia); Wikipedia (it)","secrets.10.text":"The Bologna recipe was registered with the Bologna Chamber of Commerce by the Accademia Italiana della Cucina, after historical research.","secrets.10.source":"Dissapore (Anna Silveri, 2018); Il Giornale del Cibo; Wikipedia (it)","mistakes.0":"Letting the tuna cook for too long: it becomes dry (Cookist).","mistakes.1":"Browning the onion instead of letting it soften (Dissapore).","mistakes.2":"Forgetting to drain the tuna (Dissapore).","mistakes.3":"Overcooking the pasta: drain it al dente (Dissapore, Cookist).","mistakes.4":"Keeping the dish for more than a day: 1 day at most in the fridge, in an airtight container (Cookist).","variants.0":"The Bologna recipe, registered by the Accademia Italiana della Cucina (reproduced by Dissapore in 2018 and by Il Giornale del Cibo in 2019): for 4, 320 g of spaghetti, 180 g of tuna in oil, one onion and 700 g of fresh tomatoes (or a 400 g tin of peeled tomatoes), cooked for half an hour over low heat; the tuna goes in 10 minutes before the end. It takes longer than the quick version. Accepted variations: anchovies or parsley.","variants.1":"Roman style: garlic and chilli instead of the onion (Wikipedia; Cristina Bragaglia, quoted by Dissapore).","variants.2":"With olives, capers or peas (Dissapore); or olives, capers and anchovies, with cherry tomatoes and passata (Cookist, chef Ruben Bondì).","variants.3":"With cherry tomatoes cooked over high heat with a garlic clove (Dissapore). That recipe adds stock powder, which is left out here.","variants.4":"In Palermo: no tomato, but raisins, pine nuts and wild fennel (Wikipedia)."},"poisson-frit":{"title":"Moroccan fried fish","darija":"Hout mqli","tagline":"Sardines sandwiched together in pairs with a garlic, cumin and paprika chermoula, and whiting fillets marinated, floured, then fried until golden brown. With lemon, this is home-style fried fish.","region":"All of Morocco, especially the coastal cities. The sardine is the most abundant fish off the Moroccan coast (Choumicha) and whiting is a very common choice (Taste of Maroc). Fried stuffed sardines are also sold in the street far from the sea, sometimes only on certain days, for example in Jemaa el-Fna in Marrakesh and in the medina of Fez (Taste of Maroc).","occasion":"As a starter, or as a main course with pulses and salads for a full lunch (Taste of Maroc); at the Ramadan iftar in El Jadida, where fried fish is on the menu (Hespress)","img_alt":"Floured fried sardines arranged in a rosette with lemon","serving":"Serve the fish hot, as soon as it has drained: sardines are best eaten hot, with lemon slices (Bladi); Cooking With Alia serves them straight away, with a squeeze of lemon juice. Taste of Maroc also serves them warm or at room temperature, on a bed of parsley or lettuce, with lemon wedges and harissa, alongside white beans in tomato sauce or a cucumber, tomato and onion salad. Choumicha serves her fried fish with a green salad, lemon slices and m'chermlines olives. Do not leave the fish out of the fridge for more than 2 hours (FDA).","pairs_label":"In a sandwich","ing.0.group":"Fish","ing.sardines.name":"fresh sardines","ing.sardines.note":"preferably small; to be scaled, gutted and opened out flat (step 1)","ing.merlans.name":"whiting","ing.merlans.note":"to be scaled, gutted and filleted (step 2), or bought already filleted from the fishmonger","ing.1.group":"Chermoula","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"finely chopped","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.coriandre.note":"finely chopped","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"to be peeled","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika (sweet pepper powder)","ing.citron-jus.name":"lemon","ing.citron-jus.pl":"lemons","ing.citron-jus.note":"for the juice; or vinegar, as Choumicha does","ing.piment-fort.name":"hot chilli powder","ing.piment-fort.note":"optional; Choumicha uses 1 tsp per 1 kg of fish","ing.2.group":"For frying","ing.farine.name":"plain flour","ing.farine.note":"for coating; Choumicha allows 100 g per 1 kg of fish","ing.semoule.name":"fine semolina","ing.semoule.note":"optional, to mix with the flour","ing.huile.name":"frying oil","ing.huile.note":"enough to cover at least half the thickness of the fish","ing.3.group":"To serve","ing.citron-service.name":"lemon","ing.citron-service.pl":"lemons","ing.citron-service.note":"cut into wedges or slices","equipment.0":"Chopping board and small knife","equipment.1":"Colander and kitchen paper","equipment.2":"Pestle and mortar (or food processor)","equipment.3":"Bowl and deep dish for the marinade, with cling film or a lid","equipment.4":"Deep plate for the flour","equipment.5":"Electric deep-fat fryer with a thermostat, or a large, deep, high-sided frying pan","equipment.6":"Lid that fits the pan, always within reach","equipment.7":"Slotted spoon or tongs","equipment.8":"Cooking thermometer (optional)","steps.0.title":"Scale and gut the sardines","steps.0.text":"Under a trickle of cold water, scale {{sardines}} of sardines. Cut off the head, clean out the belly, then remove the backbone: each sardine opens out flat, into two fillets still joined along the back. You can also ask the fishmonger to do this.","steps.0.tip":"Choose shiny, firm sardines with their scales still on and bright red gills, with a fresh, mild smell, never strong or of ammonia.","steps.1.title":"Prepare the whiting","steps.1.text":"Scale and gut {{merlans}} of whiting. Cut off the heads and fillet the fish, or ask the fishmonger to do it.","steps.2.title":"Rinse and dry the fish","steps.2.text":"Quickly rinse all the fish in cold water and leave it to drain in a colander. Pat it thoroughly dry with kitchen paper. Then wash your hands with soap for at least 20 seconds, and wash the board and knife in hot soapy water.","steps.2.tip":"Well-dried fish is safer: water makes hot oil bubble up, and the oil can overflow.","steps.3.title":"Pound the garlic and herbs","steps.3.text":"Very finely chop {{persil}} of parsley and {{coriandre}} of coriander. In the mortar, crush {{ail:full}} with {{sel}} of salt. Add the herbs and pound a little more.","steps.3.tip":"Chop the herbs well before pounding them, otherwise the chermoula turns stringy. No mortar? A food processor will do.","steps.3.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.3.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.4.title":"Finish the chermoula","steps.4.text":"Put the paste in a bowl. Add {{cumin}} of cumin, {{paprika}} of paprika and the juice of {{citron-jus:full}}. Mix well. If you like it spicy, add a little {{piment-fort}}.","steps.4.tip":"Before touching the raw fish, set a little chermoula aside in a small bowl: it will serve as a sauce at the table or in a sandwich.","steps.5.title":"Sandwich the sardines in pairs","steps.5.text":"Lay an opened sardine skin side down. Spread a good layer of chermoula over the flesh, then cover with a second sardine, skin side up. Press gently. Carry on with the rest.","steps.5.tip":"One sardine left on its own? Fill it with chermoula and fold it over on itself.","steps.6.title":"Coat the whiting","steps.6.text":"Spread chermoula over the whiting fillets and fold them over on themselves. Also rub the outside of the sardines with the rest of the chermoula.","steps.7.title":"Marinate in the fridge","steps.7.text":"Cover and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes. Taste of Maroc marinates for at least 2 hours.","steps.7.timer.label":"Marinating","steps.7.timer.done":"The marinating is done. Move on to the frying.","steps.8.title":"Prepare the pan","steps.8.text":"Use a deep-fat fryer with a thermostat or a large, deep, high-sided frying pan. Pour in enough {{huile}} to cover at least half the thickness of the fish, but never fill it more than a third of the way up. Keep a lid of the right size within reach.","steps.9.title":"Heat the oil","steps.9.text":"Heat the oil gradually over medium heat. It should be very hot but never smoking: if it smokes, turn the heat down and wait for it to cool a little. With a fryer or a thermometer, do not go above 180 °C. From now on, do not take your eyes off the pan.","steps.9.tip":"If the oil catches fire: never throw water on it. Slide the lid over the pan, turn off the heat if you can do so safely, do not move the pan and leave the lid on until it has cooled down. Call the fire brigade straight away, even if the fire seems to be out. If the fire does not go out or spreads, get everyone out, close the kitchen door and do not go back in.","steps.10.title":"Flour the fish","steps.10.text":"Take the fish out of the marinade and let it drain. Just before frying, dip each piece in the {{farine}} on both sides. You can mix a little {{semoule}} into the flour.","steps.11.title":"Fry the first side","steps.11.text":"Carefully lower a few pieces into the oil, without crowding them. Fry for about 3 minutes, until the underside is golden brown.","steps.11.timer.label":"First side","steps.11.timer.done":"Turn the pieces over with tongs or a slotted spoon.","steps.12.title":"Turn and finish cooking","steps.12.text":"Turn the pieces over and fry for about 3 more minutes. The fish is cooked when it is golden brown and the flesh flakes easily with a fork.","steps.12.tip":"With a probe thermometer, the fish is cooked when the centre reaches 63 °C.","steps.12.timer.label":"Second side","steps.12.timer.done":"Golden brown on both sides? Take the fish out of the oil.","steps.13.title":"Drain and carry on","steps.13.text":"Lift the fish out with the slotted spoon and put it on kitchen paper. Remove any bits floating in the oil, then fry the next batch in the same way. When you have finished, turn off the heat.","steps.13.tip":"Oil that has turned brown or black, thick, foamy or smoking is spent: do not reuse it, and never pour it down the sink.","steps.13.img.alt":"Golden fried sardines on kitchen paper","steps.13.img.caption":"Draining on kitchen paper","steps.14.title":"Serve hot","steps.14.text":"Serve straight away, with {{citron-service:full}} cut into wedges, a green salad and olives.","steps.14.tip":"Do not leave the fried fish out of the fridge for more than 2 hours, or more than 1 hour if it is above 32 °C.","secrets.0.text":"Choose sardines under 15 cm long: the smaller the sardine, the tastier its flesh. They should be shiny and firm, with their scales still on and moist, carmine-red gills.","secrets.0.source":"Choumicha, “La sardine, un trésor de bienfaits”; Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa): small fresh sardines are ideal","secrets.1.text":"Fresh fish smells fresh and mild, never strong, sour or of ammonia. Its eyes are clear and bright, and its firm flesh springs back when you press it.","secrets.1.source":"FDA, “Selecting and Serving Fresh and Frozen Seafood Safely”; Choumicha (shiny, firm sardines, red gills)","secrets.2.text":"Sardines are very perishable: cook them on the day you buy them. If necessary, keep them in the fridge for 24 hours at most, scaled, gutted, rinsed, patted dry and covered with cling film, in a fridge at 4 °C or below.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha (24 h maximum, preferably the same day); FDA (seafood kept at 40 °F, i.e. 4 °C, or below)","secrets.3.text":"The two sardines stuck together with chermoula are called “mzeouej” (married), “machrouk” (partnered) or “ʿachiq w maʿchouq” (the lovers).","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa, a Casablanca native); SBS (Ellie Bouhadana): “mzeouejim”, which means “married”","secrets.4.text":"Real chermoula is made with fresh herbs and garlic, not with a shop-bought dry mix.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); all the Moroccan recipes consulted (Choumicha, Bladi, Cooking With Alia) use fresh herbs","secrets.5.text":"For fish, a lemony chermoula is the favourite.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa and Christine Benlafquih); Bladi (lemon juice in “Sardines farcies”, “Poisson frits” and “Chermoula pour le poisson”; its version of Choumicha's recipe uses vinegar); Lalla Moulati (lemon juice, with a little vinegar, in her chermoula for fish)","secrets.6.text":"First pound the garlic with the salt, then add the finely chopped herbs: if they are roughly chopped, the chermoula turns stringy.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); Bladi, “Chermoula pour le poisson” (coriander pounded in a mortar with coarse salt and garlic); Choumicha (finely cut herbs)","secrets.7.text":"Keep a little clean chermoula, set aside before touching the raw fish, to serve as a sauce: never the chermoula that has touched the raw fish.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa likes to keep some aside to serve with grilled food, fish included, especially in summer); FDA (prevent bacteria from raw fish passing to ready-to-eat foods)","secrets.8.text":"Drain and dry the fish well before frying: water makes the oil bubble up and overflow.","secrets.8.source":"SFDA (Saudi Food and Drug Authority); Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service; Taste of Maroc and Choumicha (fish wiped and dried)","secrets.9.text":"Flour the fish just before lowering it into the oil.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha (“dip the fish in the flour and plunge them into a bath of hot oil”); Taste of Maroc (take out of the marinade, coat, fry)","secrets.10.text":"Fry in small batches, without crowding the pieces.","secrets.10.source":"SBS (Ellie Bouhadana): in batches, without overloading the pan; Bladi, “Beignets de sardines”: four at a time","secrets.11.text":"Heat the oil gradually over medium heat, then keep it very hot but not smoking.","secrets.11.source":"Bladi, “Merlans frits” (oil bath heated over medium heat); SFDA (heat slowly, without reaching the smoke point); Cooking With Alia (heat turned down once the oil is hot)","secrets.12.text":"Change the oil as soon as it turns brown or black. Oil that is dark, thick, foamy, smoking or smells rancid is spent. Do not pour it down the sink.","secrets.12.source":"SFDA; SCAV (Geneva), “Information sur les huiles de friture”","secrets.13.text":"In Morocco, fried fish is often served with white beans in tomato sauce or fresh salads (cucumber, tomato, onion), or with a green salad, lemon and m'chermlines olives.","secrets.13.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); Choumicha, “Friture de poissons m'chermel”","mistakes.0":"Throwing water on burning oil: it causes an explosion of flames. Slide a lid over the pan, turn off the heat if you can do so safely, do not move the pan and leave the lid on until it has cooled down; call the fire brigade straight away, even if the fire seems to be out, and if the fire keeps burning, get everyone out and close the door (USFA; Québec government; Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service; Warwickshire County Council).","mistakes.1":"Carrying on heating oil that is smoking: turn the heat down and let it cool before adding the fish (Québec government; Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service; SFDA).","mistakes.2":"Filling the pan more than a third full of oil (Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service; Warwickshire County Council).","mistakes.3":"Taking your eyes off the pan while frying, even for a few seconds (Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service; Warwickshire County Council; Québec government).","mistakes.4":"Putting wet fish, or fish dripping with liquid marinade, into the oil: the oil bubbles up and can overflow (SFDA; Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service).","mistakes.5":"Crowding too many pieces into the pan (SBS; Bladi).","mistakes.6":"Chopping the herbs roughly: the chermoula turns stringy (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha uses finely cut herbs).","mistakes.7":"Serving chermoula that has touched the raw fish as a sauce (FDA; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.8":"Buying fish that smells strong, sour or of ammonia, or dull, soft sardines (FDA; Choumicha).","mistakes.9":"Leaving the fried fish out of the fridge for more than 2 hours, or more than 1 hour if it is above 32 °C (FDA).","variants.0":"Stuffed sardines only: the great street-food classic (Taste of Maroc; Bladi, “Sardines farcies”).","variants.1":"A single sardine filled with chermoula and folded over on itself, instead of two sardines sandwiched together (Taste of Maroc).","variants.2":"Whole whiting: scaled, gutted, stuffed with chermoula, dipped in sifted flour and fried for a few minutes, turning them over, served with lemon wedges (Bladi, “Merlans frits”).","variants.3":"Whole horse mackerel coated with marinade (parsley, garlic, cumin, paprika, lemon) on the outside and a little inside, then floured and fried (Bladi, “Poisson frits”).","variants.4":"Chermoula made with vinegar instead of lemon (Choumicha), or spicier with hot chilli (Choumicha) or harissa (Taste of Maroc).","variants.5":"Chermoula with olive oil (Taste of Maroc; Cooking With Alia; Bladi, “Chermoula pour le poisson”).","variants.6":"Northern chermoula: with thyme, black pepper and plenty of flat-leaf parsley (Taste of Maroc).","variants.7":"Chermoula with preserved lemon (SBS, Ellie Bouhadana; Taste of Maroc, as an option).","variants.8":"A flour and semolina coating (SBS, Ellie Bouhadana) or flour and durum wheat flour (Taste of Maroc).","variants.9":"Sardine fritters: cleaned sardines, heads and backbones removed, dipped in a batter (egg, flour, oil, iced water) and fried for 5 minutes, four at a time (Bladi).","variants.10":"In El Jadida, fried fish is part of the Ramadan iftar (Hespress, 2021)."},"poulet-creme-champignons":{"title":"Chicken strips with cream and mushrooms","tagline":"Seared chicken strips, nicely browned mushrooms and a creamy sauce without wine: a mild, comforting evening dish, with rice or pasta.","region":"France and Switzerland. Chicken sautéed with cream from classic French cooking (Escoffier, Chef Simon) and sliced chicken in cream from Swiss cooking (Migusto, Betty Bossi), a cousin of Zurich-style sliced veal (Wikipedia).","occasion":"Quick weeknight dinner. A creamy sauce that “always brings the whole family together around the table”, according to Migusto.","img_alt":"Sliced chicken with mushrooms in a cream sauce, on tagliatelle","serving":"Piping hot, coated in sauce, with rice (Chef Simon serves it with Creole-style rice) or pasta, tagliatelle for example (Betty Bossi; Chef Simon recommends fresh pasta). In Switzerland, it is also served with rösti or spätzli (Migusto, Wikipedia). Leftovers: put the chicken in sauce in the fridge within 2 hours (Anses, FSA) and eat it within 2 days (FSA; Anses says less than 3 days for a cooked dish). The rice should be cooled as quickly as possible, ideally within the hour, and kept in the fridge for 24 hours at most (FSA). Reheat everything only once, covered, until it is piping hot and steaming (Anses, FSA). Opened cream: follow the “once opened” time on the label, otherwise 3 days at most (Anses, FSA).","ing.0.group":"Chicken","ing.poulet.name":"halal chicken breasts","ing.poulet.note":"or ready-cut chicken strips","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.huile.name":"neutral oil","ing.huile.note":"sunflower, rapeseed or groundnut","ing.1.group":"Mushroom sauce","ing.oignon.name":"onion","ing.oignon.pl":"onions","ing.champignons.name":"button mushrooms","ing.champignons.note":"preferably white","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.beurre.note":"a small knob; otherwise a spoonful of oil","ing.bouillon.name":"halal chicken stock","ing.bouillon.note":"a halal stock cube dissolved in hot water, or homemade stock; failing that, water (the sauce will have less flavour)","ing.creme.name":"thick crème fraîche","ing.creme.note":"at least 30% fat, or full-fat liquid cream; not reduced-fat cream","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"a few sprigs, optional","ing.2.group":"To serve (your choice)","ing.riz.name":"rice","ing.riz.note":"your choice: the rice OR the pasta","ing.pates.name":"pasta (tagliatelle or spaghetti)","ing.pates.note":"your choice: the pasta OR the rice","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"a large panful, salted","equipment.0":"A large frying pan or sauté pan (cast iron or stainless steel if you have one: it leaves more browned bits on the bottom)","equipment.1":"A large saucepan and a colander, for the rice or pasta","equipment.2":"A chopping board and a sharp knife","equipment.3":"A clean plate, for the seared chicken","equipment.4":"A wooden spoon or a spatula","equipment.5":"Kitchen paper or a small brush, for the mushrooms","equipment.6":"A cooking thermometer (optional)","steps.0.title":"Put the water on to heat","steps.0.text":"Fill a large saucepan with {{eau}}, salt it and bring it to the boil, for the rice or pasta.","steps.1.title":"Chop the onion","steps.1.text":"Peel and finely chop {{oignon:full}}.","steps.2.title":"Clean the mushrooms","steps.2.text":"Take {{champignons:full}}: cut off the earthy end of the stalks, then wipe them with damp kitchen paper or a small brush. Never soak them: if they are very dirty, rinse them very quickly under the tap and dry them straight away.","steps.3.title":"Slice the mushrooms","steps.3.text":"Cut the mushrooms into thin slices.","steps.4.title":"Slice the chicken","steps.4.text":"Cut {{poulet}} of chicken breasts into strips about 1 cm wide. Season them with salt and pepper. Then wash your hands, the board and the knife with soap.","steps.4.tip":"Only one board? Always cut the vegetables first and the raw chicken last (FSA, the UK health agency).","steps.5.title":"Start the rice or pasta","steps.5.text":"When the water boils, add {{riz}} of rice or {{pates}} of pasta. Cook for the time given on the packet, then drain.","steps.6.title":"Sear half the chicken","steps.6.text":"In the large frying pan, heat half the oil ({{huile}} in total) over high heat, until very hot but not smoking. Add half the chicken, or about 300 g if you are cooking for more than 4, and let it brown for about 2 minutes: first leave it untouched, then turn it to brown all sides.","steps.6.tip":"If you stir too soon or all the time, the pan cools down and the chicken steams instead of browning (Betty Bossi).","steps.6.timer.label":"First half","steps.6.timer.done":"Take the chicken out onto a clean plate.","steps.7.title":"Sear the other half","steps.7.text":"Take the chicken out onto a clean plate, not the one the raw chicken was on. Pour in the rest of the oil and sear the rest of the chicken in the same way, about 2 minutes per batch, then take it out too.","steps.7.tip":"The chicken will finish cooking in the sauce: by the end, it must be thoroughly cooked (Migusto).","steps.7.timer.label":"Second half","steps.7.timer.done":"Take the chicken out and turn down the heat.","steps.8.title":"Soften the onion","steps.8.text":"Turn down to medium heat. Put the {{beurre}} in the pan, then the onion. Fry it, stirring, until it turns translucent.","steps.9.title":"Brown the mushrooms","steps.9.text":"Add the mushrooms. Let them cook, stirring from time to time, until their liquid has evaporated and they start to brown.","steps.9.tip":"Don't salt them now: salted too early, they release their liquid and brown less (Betty Bossi). You will season the sauce at the end.","steps.10.title":"Deglaze with the stock","steps.10.text":"Pour in {{bouillon:full}}. Scrape the bottom of the pan with the wooden spoon to loosen the browned bits: that is where the flavour of the sauce lies. Let it bubble for a moment.","steps.11.title":"Add the cream","steps.11.text":"Add {{creme:full}} and stir. Let it simmer gently, stirring from time to time, until the sauce thickens slightly.","steps.11.tip":"Reduced-fat cream can split if it boils; thick crème fraîche and full-fat cream can take the heat (Betty Bossi, Migusto, Wikipedia).","steps.12.title":"Finish cooking the chicken","steps.12.text":"Put the chicken back in the sauce, with the juices it has released on the plate. Let it simmer gently for a few minutes, without boiling hard, until it is cooked through: cut the biggest piece open; it should be white in the centre, with no pink, and the juices should run clear. With a thermometer, the centre must reach at least 75 °C.","steps.12.tip":"A rolling boil toughens chicken breast: a gentle simmer is enough (Escoffier, Chef Simon).","steps.13.title":"Taste and serve","steps.13.text":"Taste, then adjust the seasoning with {{sel}} and {{poivre}}. Sprinkle with chopped {{persil}} and serve straight away, with the rice or pasta.","secrets.0.text":"Cut and prepare everything before you turn on the heat under the pan: once it starts, the cooking goes fast.","secrets.0.source":"Betty Bossi (“Faire sauter”: mise en place)","secrets.1.text":"Sear the chicken in two batches, in a very hot pan: if it is crowded, it stews in its own juices instead of browning.","secrets.1.source":"Betty Bossi (“Faire sauter”: in 2 or 3 batches; sliced chicken in cream: in batches); Migusto (Zurich-style sliced veal: batch by batch)","secrets.2.text":"Leave the chicken alone for one to two minutes before turning it: a golden crust forms and adds flavour.","secrets.2.source":"Betty Bossi (“Faire sauter”)","secrets.3.text":"A cast-iron or stainless-steel pan leaves more browned bits than a non-stick pan: the sauce is tastier.","secrets.3.source":"Betty Bossi (“La crème des crèmes”)","secrets.4.text":"Deglazing means dissolving the caramelised browned bits on the bottom of the pan: they are the basis of the sauce's flavour.","secrets.4.source":"Escoffier, Le Guide culinaire (the series of chicken sautés); Betty Bossi (“La crème des crèmes”: more browned bits, tastier sauce)","secrets.5.text":"No need for wine: Escoffier also deglazes with chicken stock or with the mushroom cooking liquid, and both Migusto (sliced chicken with cream and herbs) and Chef Simon (chicken fricassée) make their sauce without wine, with stock.","secrets.5.source":"Escoffier; Migusto; Chef Simon","secrets.6.text":"Chicken breast must not boil hard in the sauce: it toughens. A gentle simmer is enough.","secrets.6.source":"Escoffier (chicken sautés: boiling toughens the white pieces); Chef Simon (fricassée: “always cook over low heat (gentle simmer)”)","secrets.7.text":"Mushrooms should never be soaked: they absorb the water, lose their flavour and turn soft.","secrets.7.source":"Julien Sebbag (Académie du Goût), Migusto, Betty Bossi","secrets.8.text":"Cook the mushrooms well, until their liquid evaporates and they brown: that is when they develop their flavour.","secrets.8.source":"Julien Sebbag (Académie du Goût); Cuisine AZ","secrets.9.text":"For a pale sauce with poultry, choose white button mushrooms.","secrets.9.source":"Betty Bossi (“Champignons d'élevage”)","secrets.10.text":"Thick crème fraîche or full-fat cream: they can take the heat; lighter creams may split if they boil.","secrets.10.source":"Wikipedia (thick crème fraîche: “stands up well to cooking”); Betty Bossi and Migusto (demi-crème, a lighter cream, must not be boiled)","secrets.11.text":"Put the chicken back in the sauce with all the juices it has released on the plate.","secrets.11.source":"Betty Bossi (sliced chicken in cream)","mistakes.0":"Washing raw chicken: it splashes bacteria everywhere; only cooking destroys them (FSA).","mistakes.1":"Cutting the raw chicken, then the vegetables, on the same board without washing it (FSA, Anses).","mistakes.2":"Adding all the chicken at once or stirring it constantly: it boils in its juices instead of browning (Betty Bossi).","mistakes.3":"Soaking the mushrooms (Julien Sebbag, Migusto, Betty Bossi).","mistakes.4":"Boiling reduced-fat cream hard: it splits (Betty Bossi, Migusto).","mistakes.5":"Letting the chicken boil for a long time in the sauce: it toughens (Escoffier).","mistakes.6":"Serving without checking that the chicken is cooked through (FSA, Anses).","mistakes.7":"Putting the seared chicken back on the raw chicken's plate (Anses).","mistakes.8":"Using a stock cube without checking that it is halal, or keeping the wine from the Swiss recipes (Migusto, Betty Bossi): replace it with stock.","variants.0":"With herbs: snipped chives, and butter mixed with chopped herbs and lemon zest, added just before serving (Migusto).","variants.1":"With paprika: spice up the pan with paprika at the same time as the salt and pepper (Migusto).","variants.2":"With garlic: 2 chopped cloves, fried with the onion (Cuisine AZ; a reader's recipe on Journal des Femmes).","variants.3":"With shallots: replace the onion with finely chopped shallots (Chef Simon, fricassée; a reader's recipe on Journal des Femmes).","variants.4":"With other mushrooms, shiitake for example (Betty Bossi).","variants.5":"Zurich-style émincé: the classic Swiss version, with veal, served with rösti (Migusto, Wikipedia; Betty Bossi also gives a version); make it without wine and with halal veal.","variants.6":"Old-fashioned fricassée, for the weekend: a chicken cut into pieces and cooked for 40 minutes in chicken stock, then the cream and mushroom sauce (Chef Simon)."},"rfissa-poulet":{"title":"Chicken rfissa with lentils and fenugreek","darija":"rfissa bel djaj","tagline":"Steaming strips of msemen soaked in a fenugreek and ras el hanout broth, under tender chicken, lentils and meltingly soft onions: the celebration dish offered to a new mother.","region":"Morocco (many family versions; bormache in Meknes)","occasion":"Births (especially the third day after the delivery), naming ceremonies, family meals and Ramadan evenings","serving":"Serve the rfissa piping hot, in a large round dish set in the middle of the table: everyone eats from their own side, with their hands or a spoon (Taste of Maroc). Keep one or two bowls of broth on the side, and the fenugreek seeds in a small bowl for those who like them. It is the dish offered to a new mother, especially on the third day after the birth, and shared with the family (Le360, Taste of Maroc).","ing.0.group":"Chicken and onion sauce","ing.poulet.name":"whole halal chicken, beldi (free-range) if possible","ing.poulet.note":"whole or cut into quarters; a beldi chicken needs about 1 h more cooking","ing.oignons.name":"large onion","ing.oignons.pl":"large onions","ing.oignons.note":"to be thinly sliced","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.smen.name":"smen (aged salted butter)","ing.smen.note":"or butter","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.ras-el-hanout.name":"ras el hanout","ing.ras-el-hanout.note":"fresh, bought from a grocer you trust","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.safran.name":"saffron threads","ing.safran.note":"about 15 threads","ing.sel.name":"Salt, to taste","ing.sel.note":"as a guide, about 1½ tsp in all; adjust at the end","ing.1.group":"Broth, fenugreek and lentils","ing.fenugrec.name":"fenugreek seeds (helba)","ing.fenugrec.note":"to be soaked the day before","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"plus a little hot water if the broth level drops","ing.lentilles.name":"green or brown lentils","ing.lentilles.note":"not red lentils: they fall apart","ing.2.group":"To finish and serve","ing.smen-fin.name":"smen, to finish","ing.smen-fin.note":"or butter","ing.msemen.name":"msemen","ing.msemen.pl":"msemen","ing.msemen.note":"one batch of the app's msemen recipe, i.e. 2 per person","equipment.0":"A bowl for soaking the fenugreek","equipment.1":"A large heavy-based pot with a lid","equipment.2":"A couscoussier (couscous steamer), to reheat the msemen by steaming","equipment.3":"A square of muslin (or cheesecloth) and some string, if you want to wrap up the fenugreek","equipment.4":"A ladle and a slotted spoon","equipment.5":"A large round serving dish","equipment.6":"One or two bowls for the broth served on the side","steps.0.title":"The day before: soak the fenugreek","steps.0.text":"Put {{fenugrec}} of fenugreek seeds in a bowl and cover them generously with cold water. Leave to soak overnight, or for at least 6 h: the seeds soften and become milder. Forgot? Pour boiling water over them and wait 1 to 2 h.","steps.1.title":"Make the msemen in advance","steps.1.text":"Make {{msemen:full}} using the app's msemen recipe, without the final honey and butter step, the day before or while the chicken simmers. As soon as they come out of the pan, while still hot, tear them into bite-sized strips. Once cool, keep the strips in a bag; if you make them more than a day ahead, freeze them.","steps.2.title":"Slice, chop, rinse","steps.2.text":"Thinly slice {{oignons:full}}. Finely chop {{persil}} of parsley and {{coriandre}} of coriander. Rinse {{lentilles}} of lentils and drain them. Drain the fenugreek; if you like, tie it up in a square of muslin so that you can take it out when serving.","steps.2.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.2.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.3.title":"Put everything in the pot","steps.3.text":"Put {{poulet}} of chicken, whole or cut into quarters, in a large pot with the onions, {{huile}} of olive oil and {{smen}} of smen. Add {{gingembre}} of ginger, {{curcuma}} of turmeric, {{ras-el-hanout}} of ras el hanout, {{poivre}} of pepper, {{safran}} of crumbled saffron and a little salt. Stir to coat the chicken well with the spices.","steps.4.title":"Let the sauce form","steps.4.text":"Cover and cook over medium heat for 20 min, stirring from time to time. It is ready when the onions have softened and a thick sauce has formed.","steps.4.timer.label":"Onion sauce","steps.4.timer.done":"Has a thick sauce formed? Add the water and fenugreek.","steps.5.title":"Add the water and fenugreek","steps.5.text":"Add the drained fenugreek, the chopped herbs and {{eau}} of water. Bring to the boil, then cover and turn down to medium-low heat. Beldi chicken: simmer for 1 h before adding the lentils. Standard chicken: add the lentils straight away and go on to the next step.","steps.5.timer.label":"Beldi chicken, 1st hour","steps.5.timer.done":"Add the lentils: that is the next step.","steps.6.title":"Add the lentils and simmer","steps.6.text":"Add the lentils, cover and simmer over medium-low heat for about 1 h, until the lentils and chicken are very tender. Halfway through, taste the broth and add more hot water if it has reduced too much: there should be plenty left. If the chicken is cooked before the lentils, take it out and set it aside, covered.","steps.6.timer.label":"Lentils","steps.6.timer.done":"Taste a lentil: it should be tender. If not, cook a little longer.","steps.7.title":"Adjust the seasoning and finish with smen","steps.7.text":"Taste the broth: it should be very fragrant. Adjust the salt, then add {{smen-fin}} of smen and swirl the pot to melt it into the broth. If you took the chicken out, put it back for a few minutes to heat through.","steps.8.title":"Steam the msemen to reheat them","steps.8.text":"Bring some water to the boil in the bottom of the couscoussier. Put the msemen strips in the top and steam them for 10 min, until they are piping hot and supple.","steps.8.timer.label":"Steaming the msemen","steps.8.timer.done":"The strips are piping hot and supple: assemble the dish straight away.","steps.9.title":"Assemble the rfissa","steps.9.text":"Spread the hot strips in a thick layer in a large round dish: you should no longer see the bottom. Pour a few ladlefuls of broth over them, then place the chicken in the centre, whole or in pieces. Spoon the lentils and onions over the top with the slotted spoon, then pour over almost all the broth, keeping one or two bowls aside.","steps.10.title":"Serve piping hot","steps.10.text":"If you tied the fenugreek in muslin, tip the seeds into a small bowl for those who like them. Set the dish in the middle of the table and serve at once, piping hot, with the bowls of broth: everyone eats from their own side of the dish, with their hands or a spoon.","secrets.0.text":"Soak the fenugreek overnight, or for at least 6 h: the seeds soften and lose some of their strength. Forgot? Cover them with boiling water and wait 1 to 2 h.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc; Paula Wolfert (at least 4 h in lukewarm water)","secrets.1.text":"Tie the fenugreek up in a small cloth bag (the “kemmoussa”): it flavours the broth without anyone biting into the seeds, and you serve them separately for those who like them.","secrets.1.source":"Barlamane (Hajja Fatima, a celebration cook in Casablanca); Choumicha; Taste of Maroc","secrets.2.text":"The thick, fragrant broth (“mdaghmar”) comes from the onions: use plenty and let them cook for a long time over low heat, until they melt into a cream.","secrets.2.source":"Barlamane (Hajja Fatima)","secrets.3.text":"Use fresh ras el hanout, bought from a grocer you trust: it is the “soul” of rfissa. For Aunt Aïcha, from Beni Mellal, it “takes the dish up ten notches”.","secrets.3.source":"Barlamane (Hajja Fatima); Paula Wolfert (Aunt Aïcha's recipe)","secrets.4.text":"Make plenty of broth: the msemen soak up a lot of it. Add hot water during cooking if it reduces, taste it (it should be very fragrant) and keep one or two bowls of it to serve on the side.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc; MarocMama; Paula Wolfert; Lalla Moulati","secrets.5.text":"Tear the msemen while they are still hot, then reheat the strips for 10 min over the steam of the couscoussier just before serving: they become piping hot and supple, ready to soak up the broth.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc; Paula Wolfert; Choumicha","secrets.6.text":"A beldi chicken has more flavour and stands up better to the long cooking. With a standard chicken, take it out of the broth as soon as it is cooked so that it doesn't fall apart.","secrets.6.source":"Barlamane (Hajja Fatima); Taste of Maroc","secrets.7.text":"Finish with a spoonful of smen, swirling the pot: without it, those who know the dish can tell the difference.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha","mistakes.0":"Adding the fenugreek without soaking it if you want it mild: the seeds stay harder and stronger (Taste of Maroc). Soaking is not compulsory, though: Choumicha, Lalla Moulati and Bladi.net skip it.","mistakes.1":"Using red lentils: they fall apart and disappear into the sauce. Use green or brown ones.","mistakes.2":"Being stingy with the broth: the rfissa must stay well moistened, because the msemen absorb a lot of it.","mistakes.3":"Leaving a standard chicken in the pot once it is cooked, if the lentils are not done yet: it ends up falling apart.","mistakes.4":"Waiting for the msemen to cool before tearing them: do it as soon as they come out of the pan.","mistakes.5":"Using stale ras el hanout: it is what gives the broth its fragrance.","mistakes.6":"Being stingy with the msemen: you need enough to cover the whole bottom of the dish.","mistakes.7":"Serving it lukewarm: rfissa is eaten piping hot, msemen included.","variants.0":"With trid instead of msemen: very thin sheets of dough, cooked without browning, then cut into strips (Choumicha, “pâte à rfissa”; Bladi.net; Lalla Moulati). According to Hajja Fatima (Barlamane), trid should be as thin as paper and well pulled apart, so that it soaks up the broth without turning pasty.","variants.1":"Rfissa medhoussa on crumbled harcha, or treda on diced stale bread (Taste of Maroc); MarocMama also confirms torn bread, in reply to a reader who had used a baguette.","variants.2":"Msakhen instead of ras el hanout, especially in cold weather, as it is spicier: Taste of Maroc uses 1½ tbsp of msakhen for 1 tbsp of ras el hanout; Lalla Moulati also leaves the choice open.","variants.3":"Bormache, the Meknes version: lots of garlic, dried herbs (zaatar, fliou, merseta), cayenne pepper and tomatoes; the lentils are optional (Taste of Maroc, Jameela Khiyati's recipe).","variants.4":"With garlic (4 to 6 cloves in the marinade and a whole head in the broth) and, in winter, a cup of dried fava beans (Lalla Moulati).","variants.5":"Marinating the day before: chicken, onions, oil and spices mixed in the pot and kept chilled overnight (at least 6 h), then cooked straight away (Taste of Maroc; Lalla Moulati).","variants.6":"Onions in two stages: one grated onion at the start and the rest sliced, added with the lentils (Paula Wolfert); Lalla Moulati adds 2 chopped onions at the start and 4 sliced ones once the chicken has taken on colour.","variants.7":"Lentils cooked separately: covered with water and cooked until it has evaporated, then simmered for 10 min with a ladleful of sauce (Choumicha).","variants.8":"Chicken browned for a few minutes in the oven or under the grill before serving (Taste of Maroc; Bladi.net); for a chicken served whole, Taste of Maroc finishes it in the oven at 230 °C.","variants.9":"Without a couscoussier: sprinkle the strips with a little water, cover them and reheat them in the microwave (Taste of Maroc)."},"rghayef":{"title":"Rghayef stuffed with onion and khlii","darija":"rghayef m'ammrin (singular: rghifa)","tagline":"Flaky flatbreads filled with meltingly soft onions, parsley and khlii, browned in the pan and served piping hot with tea.","region":"All of Morocco; in Fez, msemmen with khlii is one of the iftar treats","occasion":"Breakfast, afternoon snack, Ramadan iftar","img_alt":"An assortment of square rghayef, browned in the pan (the filling cannot be seen in the photo)","serving":"Piping hot, at breakfast or as an afternoon snack, with tea (Morocco World News 2013: “Serve hot with tea”; Guinaudeau: eaten hot with breakfast tea; Choumicha: “Serve the r'ghaifs hot”). They are also served at the Ramadan iftar: Choumicha files half of her r'ghaifs (5 recipes out of 10, including the one with minced meat and vegetables) under “Ramadan”, and Femmes du Maroc lists msemmen with khlii among the iftar treats in Fez. Leftovers go in the fridge within 2 hours, should be eaten within 3 days and are reheated until piping hot right through, in a frying pan or in the oven (Anses).","ing.0.group":"Filling","ing.huile-farce.name":"neutral oil (sunflower) or olive oil","ing.oignons.name":"large onion","ing.oignons.pl":"large onions","ing.oignons.note":"to be very thinly sliced","ing.khlii.name":"khlii (preserved dried meat)","ing.khlii.note":"made with halal meat (most often beef or camel, sometimes lamb or mutton) and halal fat, or preserved in olive oil only; crumbled or finely chopped","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"finely chopped","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika (sweet red pepper powder)","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.piment.name":"ground hot chilli","ing.piment.note":"optional, to taste","ing.sel-farce.name":"salt","ing.sel-farce.note":"to taste; taste first, the khlii is already seasoned","ing.1.group":"Dough","ing.farine.name":"plain white flour (T45 or T55)","ing.semoule.name":"fine semolina","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.eau.name":"lukewarm water","ing.eau.note":"approximately: pour it in gradually, you will need 280 to 360 ml depending on your flour","ing.2.group":"For shaping","ing.huile-faconnage.name":"neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.huile-faconnage.note":"for the work surface, your hands and the dough balls","ing.beurre.name":"butter, melted","ing.huile-beurre.name":"neutral oil, to mix with the melted butter","ing.semoule-faconnage.name":"fine semolina, for sprinkling","equipment.0":"A sauté pan with a lid, for the filling","equipment.1":"A wooden spoon","equipment.2":"A large plate, for cooling the filling","equipment.3":"A large mixing bowl (or a gsaa)","equipment.4":"Kitchen scales","equipment.5":"A large smooth work surface (or a large tray)","equipment.6":"Two oiled trays: one for the dough balls, one for the filled flatbreads","equipment.7":"Three bowls: oil, butter and oil, semolina","equipment.8":"A pastry brush","equipment.9":"Cling film or a clean tea towel","equipment.10":"A large heavy-based frying pan","equipment.11":"A spatula","steps.0.title":"Soften the onions","steps.0.text":"In a sauté pan, heat {{huile-farce}} of oil over medium heat. Add the sliced onion ({{oignons:full}}), cover and cook for 10 minutes, stirring from time to time, until it is tender and translucent.","steps.0.timer.label":"Onions","steps.0.timer.done":"Is the onion tender and translucent? If not, give it a few more minutes.","steps.1.title":"Add the khlii, parsley and spices","steps.1.text":"Add {{khlii}} of crumbled khlii, {{persil}} of chopped parsley, {{paprika}} of paprika, {{cumin}} of cumin, {{poivre}} of pepper and, if you like, a little {{piment}}. Cook for 5 minutes uncovered, stirring, until there is no liquid left.","steps.1.timer.label":"Filling","steps.1.timer.done":"If there is still some liquid, let it evaporate, then turn off the heat.","steps.2.title":"Taste and cool","steps.2.text":"Taste the filling and add {{sel-farce}} only if needed: the khlii is already seasoned. Spread it out on a large plate and leave it to cool completely: if it is hot, it damages the dough. If you make it in advance, put it in the fridge within 2 hours.","steps.3.title":"Mix the dough","steps.3.text":"In a large mixing bowl, mix {{farine}} of flour, {{semoule}} of fine semolina and {{sel}} of salt. Gradually pour in {{eau}} of lukewarm water, mixing by hand, until it forms a soft ball. If it is sticky, add a little flour; if it is dry, a little water.","steps.4.title":"Knead for 10 minutes","steps.4.text":"Knead for 10 minutes, until the dough is smooth, soft and elastic, and no longer sticky.","steps.4.timer.label":"Kneading","steps.4.timer.done":"Touch the dough: if it is not smooth and elastic, knead a little longer.","steps.4.img.alt":"Hands kneading dough","steps.4.img.caption":"Kneading the dough","steps.5.title":"Shape the dough balls","steps.5.text":"Oil your hands and a tray. Divide the dough into balls of about 75 g, one per flatbread. Roll them in oil, place them on the tray without letting them touch, cover and leave to rest for 15 minutes.","steps.5.timer.label":"Resting the dough balls","steps.5.timer.done":"The dough balls have rested: you can stretch and fill them.","steps.6.title":"Set up your work station","steps.6.text":"While the dough rests, mix {{beurre}} of melted butter with {{huile-beurre}} of oil. Put this mixture within reach, along with a bowl of {{huile-faconnage}}, {{semoule-faconnage}} of fine semolina and the cold filling. Oil your work surface generously.","steps.7.title":"Stretch very thin","steps.7.text":"Place a dough ball on the oiled surface. With oiled hands, stretch it with your fingertips, from the centre outwards, into a very thin disc about 28 cm across. Add more oil if the dough sticks.","steps.7.img.alt":"Oiled hands spreading very thin dough, in Morocco (here for msemen)","steps.7.img.caption":"Stretching with the fingertips","steps.8.title":"Brush and fill","steps.8.text":"Brush the disc with the butter and oil mixture and sprinkle it with a little semolina. Place 1 to 2 tablespoons of filling in the centre, leaving the edges clear: the filling must last for all the flatbreads.","steps.9.title":"Fold into a square","steps.9.text":"Fold three sides of the dough over the filling, then the fourth over the top, stretching it a little: this last fold, with no filling, strengthens the flatbread and stops it from opening. Put the closed square on the second oiled tray and repeat with the other dough balls.","steps.10.title":"Heat the pan","steps.10.text":"Heat a large heavy-based frying pan over medium-high heat, then oil it lightly.","steps.11.title":"Flatten gently","steps.11.text":"With oiled hands, flatten the first square with your fingertips, without piercing the dough, until each side is about 12 to 15 cm. The filling must stay well sealed inside.","steps.12.title":"Cook, turning several times","steps.12.text":"Put the flatbread in the pan and turn it several times, until it is golden brown and crisp on both sides. If it browns too quickly, turn the heat down a little: the dough must cook right through to the centre.","steps.12.img.alt":"Square flaky flatbreads browning on a large griddle","steps.12.img.caption":"Cooking until golden brown","steps.13.title":"Carry on with the rest","steps.13.text":"Put the cooked flatbread on a board or a rack, and carry on with the other squares, flattening them as you go.","steps.14.title":"Serve piping hot","steps.14.text":"Serve the rghayef piping hot, with tea.","secrets.0.text":"In Morocco, filled flaky flatbreads are called rghayef m'ammrin (“stuffed”): with khlii, minced meat, vegetables or spiced fat.","secrets.0.source":"Wikipedia (fr), “Msemen”; Lalla Moulati (“رغايف معمرين”); Morocco World News 2013 (“Msemen Maamer”)","secrets.1.text":"A thoroughly cold filling: if it is hot, it damages the dough and makes folding difficult.","secrets.1.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); Choumicha, Morocco World News and Cooking with Alia (filling cooled before use)","secrets.2.text":"A very dry filling: let all the liquid evaporate before turning off the heat.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha (“until the vegetables' water has evaporated”); My Moroccan Food (“until all liquids have evaporated”)","secrets.3.text":"Don't use too much filling and keep the edges clear, so that you can close the flatbread properly.","secrets.3.source":"My Moroccan Food; Choumicha (1 tbsp of filling in the middle of each r'ghifa)","secrets.4.text":"The last fold, with no filling, strengthens the flatbread and stops it from opening and losing its filling during cooking.","secrets.4.source":"Choumicha, tips for successful r'ghaifs; Bladi.net (“pull the fourth edge to strengthen the dough where it is fragile over the filling”)","secrets.5.text":"Knead the dough well and let it rest for the full time: it becomes elastic and pliable, so it is easier to layer.","secrets.5.source":"Choumicha, tips for successful r'ghaifs","secrets.6.text":"Oil the dough balls so that they don't form a crust.","secrets.6.source":"Choumicha, tips for flaky r'ghaifs","secrets.7.text":"Khlii is gueddid (sun-dried meat, most often beef or camel, sometimes lamb or mutton) slowly cooked and preserved in beef suet and olive oil; at the market, it is sold in a mixture of fat and oil, or in olive oil alone. Once the jar is open, keep it in the fridge or freezer.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc, “Moroccan Preserved Meat – Khlii or Khlea”","secrets.8.text":"The filling can be made up to 2 days ahead and kept in the fridge.","secrets.8.source":"My Moroccan Food; Anses recommends eating home-cooked dishes within 3 days","secrets.9.text":"During cooking, press the edges with a spatula so that they cook through.","secrets.9.source":"Cooking with Alia","secrets.10.text":"To freeze the flatbreads: half-cook them, leave them to cool and wrap them individually. On the day, put them in the fridge for 15 minutes, then finish cooking them in the oven until golden and very hot.","secrets.10.source":"Choumicha, tips for successful r'ghaifs; Anses accepts thawing directly by cooking or reheating","secrets.11.text":"In Fez, Guinaudeau saw rghaif being taken to the neighbourhood oven, like bread: about an inch thick, glistening with fat and reddened with chilli, eaten hot with morning tea.","secrets.11.source":"Z. Guinaudeau-Franc, Traditional Moroccan Cooking: Recipes from Fez","mistakes.0":"Using the filling while it is still hot: the dough gets damaged (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.1":"Leaving liquid in the filling (Choumicha; My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.2":"Salting the filling without tasting it: the khlii is already seasoned (Taste of Maroc, khlii recipe: salt, garlic, cumin and coriander in the fat).","mistakes.3":"Using too much filling, or spreading it right to the edges: it must stay sealed inside the dough (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.4":"Piercing the dough while flattening the square: the filling escapes (My Moroccan Food: flatten “without breaking the dough”).","mistakes.5":"Skimping on the kneading or the resting: the dough lacks elasticity and does not layer well (Choumicha).","mistakes.6":"Leaving the filling or the flatbreads at room temperature for more than 2 hours before refrigerating them (Anses).","mistakes.7":"Cooking over too high a heat: the flatbread browns before the dough is cooked in the centre (Taste of Maroc, for flaky flatbreads; MarocMama; Cooking with Alia: cook until “the centre is cooked”).","variants.0":"With minced meat (kefta rghayef): fry the onions, then add the halal minced meat, herbs and spices, and cook the meat completely before filling (Choumicha, “R'ghaifs à la viande hachée et aux oignons”: 300 g of meat, 4 onions, parsley, coriander, paprika, salt and pepper for 500 g of dough; My Moroccan Food: 200 g of minced beef, 4 large onions and 4 peppers for 12; Morocco World News 2013; Cooking with Alia).","variants.1":"With halal beef fat (chehma) instead of khlii: Bladi.net uses “1 glass of beef fat or khlii”, added to the softened onion and cooked for 5 minutes, stirring, like the khlii here; in Fez, Guinaudeau describes a filling of chopped mutton or beef fat, seasoned with sweet and hot red pepper, cooked just long enough for the fat to start melting, but those rghaif were then baked in the neighbourhood oven.","variants.2":"Khlii spread over the whole sheet, which is then folded into a square like a msemen: 1 tbsp of khlii per flatbread and semolina mixed with a little baking powder (Cooking with Alia, “Msemmen with Khlii”).","variants.3":"With onions and eggs: 6 onions, parsley, cinnamon, ginger, pepper, turmeric and 4 scrambled eggs in the filling; served dusted with icing sugar and cinnamon (Choumicha).","variants.4":"With vegetables and halal minced lamb: peppers, grated carrots and courgettes, tomato, coriander and spices, cooked until the water has evaporated and the meat is thoroughly cooked (Choumicha; Anses). Choumicha also suggests r'ghaifs with spinach and prawns, or with spinach and cheeses (cheese made with microbial coagulant, without animal rennet, if you want to be strict).","variants.5":"In the oven: on a baking tray lined with baking paper, glazed with beaten egg, at 180 °C until golden (Choumicha), or 18 to 20 minutes at 200 °C (My Moroccan Food); brush them with oil during baking for colour and crispness (Choumicha).","variants.6":"Sweet: r'ghaifs filled with almonds, sugar and cinnamon, fried, then dipped in honey and sprinkled with sesame seeds, for Ramadan and Eid al-Fitr (Choumicha); Lalla Moulati also publishes “رغايف معسلة” (honey rghayef) for Ramadan, and Meknes-style almond rghayef among the Eid pastries."},"riz-saute-oeufs":{"title":"Egg fried rice","darija":"Rouz mqli b l-bid","tagline":"Yesterday's rice, eggs, spring onions and salt: China's simplest fried rice, ready in a quarter of an hour.","region":"China: 蛋炒饭 (dàn chǎofàn), the simplest Chinese fried rice. Maggie Zhu ate it as a child in Beijing, as an afternoon snack; Yangzhou developed a rich version of it, Yangzhou fried rice (Omnivore's Cookbook; 中国新闻网).","occasion":"A tired evening, when there is cooked rice left in the fridge: a small one-dish meal or a side dish. In China, it is a home dish, not a restaurant one (Omnivore's Cookbook); in old Yangzhou, families made it in the evening with the rice left over from lunch (中国新闻网, citing the Ta Kung Pao).","serving":"Serve at once, piping hot, as a light meal or a side dish (Omnivore's Cookbook). Maggie Zhu serves it with a cucumber salad when she makes a meal of it, or with a dish in sauce and a soup for a full Chinese dinner (Omnivore's Cookbook); The Woks of Life serve it with lots of hot sauce; at Made With Lau, the rice is packed into a bowl that is turned out onto the plate, for a neat dome.","ing.0.group":"Fried rice","ing.riz.name":"white rice cooked the day before, well chilled","ing.riz.note":"long-grain; cooled within an hour of cooking and kept in the fridge for one day at most","ing.oeufs.name":"egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"eggs","ing.oignons-verts.name":"spring onion","ing.oignons-verts.pl":"spring onions","ing.oignons-verts.note":"white and green parts","ing.huile.name":"neutral oil","ing.huile.note":"groundnut or sunflower","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust to taste","equipment.0":"Wok or large frying pan, about 30 cm","equipment.1":"Wide spatula","equipment.2":"Bowl and fork (or chopsticks) for beating the eggs","equipment.3":"Board and knife","equipment.4":"Spoon for breaking up the rice","steps.0.title":"Break up the cold rice","steps.0.text":"Take {{riz}} of rice out of the fridge just before cooking. Break it up with a spoon or your fingertips to loosen the clumps and separate the grains.","steps.1.title":"Beat the eggs","steps.1.text":"Crack {{oeufs:full}} into a bowl. Beat just enough to mix the yolk and white.","steps.2.title":"Slice the spring onions","steps.2.text":"Wash {{oignons-verts:full}} and cut into thin rounds, both the white and the green parts.","steps.3.title":"Heat the oil","steps.3.text":"Put the wok or large frying pan over medium-high heat with {{huile}} of oil. Wait until the oil is very hot and starts to smoke slightly. With a non-stick pan, do not let it reach smoking point: the oil is ready when its surface starts to shimmer.","steps.4.title":"Fry the eggs","steps.4.text":"Pour the contents of the bowl into the hot oil. Let the underside set for about fifteen seconds, then scramble roughly. Stop when it is almost set but still a little runny on top.","steps.5.title":"Add the rice","steps.5.text":"Immediately tip the rice on top, while the top of the eggs is still runny. Holding the spatula upright, chop everything into small pieces, so that some of the grains get coated in egg.","steps.6.title":"Stir-fry the rice","steps.6.text":"Stir, scraping the bottom all the way round, along the edges, not just in the centre. Keep chopping and stirring for about 2 minutes, until the grains are well separated and hot. If there are dry clumps left, a few drops of water on them help to break them up.","steps.7.title":"Season and brown","steps.7.text":"Sprinkle over {{sel}} of salt and add the sliced spring onions. Spread the rice in a layer against the pan and leave it untouched for 30 to 45 seconds, then stir and repeat. Carry on like this for about 3 minutes, until the rice is lightly browned.","steps.7.timer.label":"Browning the rice","steps.7.timer.done":"The rice should be lightly browned and steaming all over. Taste and adjust the salt.","steps.8.title":"Check it is piping hot","steps.8.text":"The rice must be steaming hot all over, right to the centre: steam should rise from it when you stir. Taste and add a little salt if needed.","steps.9.title":"Let it crisp and serve","steps.9.text":"Turn off the heat and leave the rice in the hot pan for a minute to crisp up a little more. Serve straight away, piping hot.","secrets.0.text":"Use rice cooked the day before: its grains stay firm and separate well instead of sticking.","secrets.0.source":"下厨房 (Xiachufang), recipe by 张恩勾: “身骨要挺，隔夜为佳”; 美食天下: “隔夜米饭是蛋炒饭的最佳选择”; Omnivore's Cookbook; The Woks of Life","secrets.1.text":"The rice should be neither too moist nor too dry: too moist and it turns mushy; too dry and it stays hard to the bite.","secrets.1.source":"下厨房 (Xiachufang), recipe by 张恩勾, “小贴士”","secrets.2.text":"For rice that is nice and dry on the surface, Maggie Zhu puts it in the fridge uncovered for one to two hours, then covers it.","secrets.2.source":"Omnivore's Cookbook (Maggie Zhu)","secrets.3.text":"Break up the clumps of cold rice before cooking, with a spoon, a spatula or your fingers.","secrets.3.source":"美食天下 (official account and 双鱼座的美食); Omnivore's Cookbook; The Woks of Life","secrets.4.text":"Hold the spatula upright and “chop” the rice: the grains separate better and stick less.","secrets.4.source":"美食天下, official account: “锅铲直立的炒制方法，可让饭粒更松散不易粘在一起”; Omnivore's Cookbook","secrets.5.text":"Tip the rice onto eggs that are still runny: some of the grains get coated in egg, and the slightly browned underside of the eggs breaks into small pieces.","secrets.5.source":"Omnivore's Cookbook; Made With Lau (eggs set but still soft before adding the rice)","secrets.6.text":"Home-style 蛋炒饭 has nothing but salt: the rice and egg remain the stars.","secrets.6.source":"Omnivore's Cookbook (“seasoned with nothing but salt”); 下厨房; 美食天下 (two recipes); 香港01","secrets.7.text":"Don't skimp on the oil: without enough oil, the rice steams instead of frying.","secrets.7.source":"Omnivore's Cookbook (at least 2 tbsp for 3 cups of rice)","secrets.8.text":"Use plenty of spring onion, both white and green parts: it is almost the only flavouring in the dish.","secrets.8.source":"Omnivore's Cookbook (4 spring onions for 3 cups of rice); 美食天下, official account (2 stalks for 2 eggs)","secrets.9.text":"For more flavour: first fry a little spring onion in the hot oil, then add the rice.","secrets.9.source":"下厨房, 张恩勾: “用底油，把切好的少许葱花呛锅”; 美食天下, 双鱼座的美食: “热油下葱花，爆香，下米饭”","secrets.10.text":"Use a wok or a large frying pan: the rice spreads out in a thin layer. In a small pan, it piles up and steams.","secrets.10.source":"Omnivore's Cookbook","secrets.11.text":"Scrape the bottom all the way round, along the edges, not just in the centre: otherwise everything sticks together.","secrets.11.source":"Made With Lau","secrets.12.text":"Its legendary ancestor is the “broken gold rice” (碎金饭) of the Sui dynasty: the egg scrambled into small golden flakes, “碎金”.","secrets.12.source":"下厨房 (description of 张恩勾's recipe); 中国新闻网, “扬州炒饭”, 2013","mistakes.0":"Using freshly cooked rice, still hot and moist: it sticks and turns mushy (下厨房; Made With Lau notes that fresh rice sticks more).","mistakes.1":"Leaving cooked rice lying around at room temperature, or keeping it in the fridge for more than a day before reheating it: it can cause food poisoning (Bacillus cereus). Cool it quickly, ideally within an hour (Food Standards Agency, UK).","mistakes.2":"Reheating leftover fried rice a second time: rice should only be reheated once. Make just the amount you will eat (Food Standards Agency).","mistakes.3":"Overcooking the eggs before adding the rice: they should still be a little runny (Omnivore's Cookbook; Made With Lau).","mistakes.4":"Too small a pan or too little oil: the rice steams instead of frying (Omnivore's Cookbook).","mistakes.5":"Piling in too much rice at once: Omnivore's Cookbook allows a 30 cm wok or frying pan for 3 cups of rice (about 475 g). If you are making much more, for 8 people for example, do it in several batches, as Made With Lau also advises.","mistakes.6":"Adding watery vegetables such as cabbage: fried rice must stay dry, with no liquid at the bottom (Made With Lau).","mistakes.7":"Adding Shaoxing wine, lard or ordinary soy sauce to “make it Chinese”: the classic version has nothing but salt, and brewed soy sauce contains alcohol from fermentation (Kikkoman: more than 2% by volume).","variants.0":"“Gold and silver” rice (金包银), a chef's method published by the Hong Kong media outlet 香港01: mix the egg yolks into the still-hot rice to coat each grain, with about 3 g of salt; half-cook the whites in a tablespoon of oil over medium heat, then add the rice and stir-fry. The chef advises against coating the rice with whole egg, because the white makes the rice wet (香港01, chef 尤瑞昌, 2025).","variants.1":"Cantonese restaurant version: finely diced carrot, sweetcorn and peas, then light and dark soy sauce, sugar and chicken stock powder (Made With Lau). For halal: certified halal soy sauce and certified halal stock, or just salt, as Made With Lau allows (“skip, use more salt”).","variants.2":"Omnivore's Cookbook's soy sauce version: instead of salt, 2 tbsp of soy sauce, 1 tsp of dark soy sauce and ½ tsp of sugar for 3 cups of rice. Same caveat: certified halal soy sauce only.","variants.3":"The Woks of Life version: some of the eggs beaten with a little water, turmeric and paprika for a bright yellow rice, plus onion, red pepper and peas.","variants.4":"HowToCook version (a Chinese collaborative project): ham, carrot, cucumber and a little light soy sauce (生抽). Use only poultry ham and soy sauce that are certified halal.","variants.5":"A drizzle of toasted sesame oil, off the heat, if you cooked with neutral oil (Omnivore's Cookbook; 美食天下, 双鱼座的美食, who finishes with 香油).","variants.6":"A pinch of chicken stock powder for more flavour (Omnivore's Cookbook): only if it is certified halal.","variants.7":"Without day-old rice: spread freshly cooked rice on a tray, let it cool, then put it in the fridge for at least 30 minutes (Omnivore's Cookbook), or leave it uncovered until it stops steaming (The Woks of Life), or fluff it with chopsticks for 1 to 2 minutes to let the steam escape (Made With Lau). The dish then takes more than 30 minutes.","variants.8":"Yangzhou fried rice (扬州炒饭), the rich version: the Yangzhou culinary association's standard adds sea cucumber, chicken, dried scallops, prawns, mushrooms, bamboo shoots and peas, but also ham and Shaoxing wine (绍酒): not halal as it stands (中国新闻网, citing the Ta Kung Pao, 2013)."},"salade-marocaine":{"title":"Moroccan salad","darija":"Chlada","tagline":"Tomatoes, cucumber and onion cut very small, parsley, lemon, olive oil and a hint of cumin: the fresh, no-cook salad that goes with many dishes in Morocco.","region":"All of Morocco: according to My Moroccan Food, it is the most common and simplest salad in Moroccan cooking.","occasion":"Every day, as a starter (Wikipedia FR, Cuisine marocaine) or alongside a tagine, grilled meat or fish (My Moroccan Food; MarocMama). Ready in 20 minutes, with no cooking (Cooking With Alia; Choumicha).","img_alt":"Tomatoes, cucumber and parsley cut into small dice in a salad bowl (photo of an Arab salad, very similar)","serving":"Well chilled, as a starter (Wikipedia FR, Cuisine marocaine) or alongside the main dish: tagine, grilled meat or fish (My Moroccan Food; MarocMama), with bread (Cooking With Alia). In Morocco, several salads are often put on the table with the main course (Wikipedia FR, Cuisine marocaine).","ing.0.group":"Vegetables and herbs","ing.tomates.name":"medium tomato","ing.tomates.pl":"medium tomatoes","ing.concombre.name":"cucumber","ing.concombre.pl":"cucumbers","ing.oignon.name":"small onion","ing.oignon.pl":"small onions","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"or fresh coriander","ing.1.group":"Dressing","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.citron.name":"lemon juice","ing.citron.note":"one lemon gives about 3 tbsp of juice","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust to taste","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper, to taste","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"to go with it","equipment.0":"Board and a sharp knife","equipment.1":"Clean vegetable brush, for the cucumber","equipment.2":"Clean tea towel or kitchen paper","equipment.3":"Salad bowl","equipment.4":"Small bowl, for the dressing","equipment.5":"Lemon squeezer (optional)","equipment.6":"Large pastry cutter (optional, for presentation)","steps.0.title":"Wash the vegetables","steps.0.text":"Wash your hands with soap. Rinse {{tomates:full}}, {{concombre:full}} and the parsley under running water, without soap. Scrub the cucumber with a clean brush, then dry everything with a clean tea towel.","steps.0.tip":"Vegetables and herbs eaten raw must be washed carefully (Anses). No soap or washing-up liquid: vegetables can absorb it (FDA).","steps.1.title":"Cut the tomatoes","steps.1.text":"Cut each tomato in half, remove the seeds, then cut the flesh into small dice of about ½ cm.","steps.1.tip":"Keep the skin on, as Cooking With Alia does. Choumicha, on the other hand, peels her tomatoes (salads 627 and 629).","steps.2.title":"Cut the cucumber","steps.2.text":"Cut each cucumber in half lengthways, remove the seeds, then cut it into small dice of the same size.","steps.3.title":"Chop the onion","steps.3.text":"Peel and very finely chop {{oignon:full}}.","steps.4.title":"Chop the parsley","steps.4.text":"Pick the parsley leaves and chop them finely: you need {{persil}} for the salad.","steps.4.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.4.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.5.title":"Combine the vegetables","steps.5.text":"Put the tomatoes, cucumber, onion and parsley in a salad bowl. Keep it in the fridge until it is time to serve.","steps.6.title":"Make the dressing","steps.6.text":"In a small bowl, mix {{huile}} of olive oil, {{citron}} of lemon juice, {{sel}} of salt, {{cumin}} of cumin and some {{poivre}}.","steps.6.tip":"Lalla Moulati keeps her dressing separately in the fridge until serving time.","steps.7.title":"Dress at the last moment","steps.7.text":"Just before serving, pour the dressing over the vegetables and mix well.","steps.7.tip":"If it is salted too early, the salad releases its water (Choumicha).","steps.8.title":"Taste and adjust","steps.8.text":"Taste and adjust the salt, lemon or cumin.","steps.9.title":"Serve well chilled","steps.9.text":"Serve straight away, well chilled, with {{khobz}}, alongside a tagine, grilled meat or fish.","steps.9.tip":"For a pretty plate, pack the salad into a large pastry cutter set on the plate, then gently lift it off (Cooking With Alia).","secrets.0.text":"The secret is the size: cut everything very small, so that you get a little of each vegetable in every mouthful.","secrets.0.source":"Cooking With Alia (recipes 208 and 543)","secrets.1.text":"Aim for small, even dice of about ½ cm, and onion chopped even more finely.","secrets.1.source":"My Moroccan Food (½ cm dice); Cooking With Alia, Lalla Moulati (2019) and MarocMama (very finely chopped onion)","secrets.2.text":"Keep only the flesh: remove the seeds from the tomatoes, and from the cucumber.","secrets.2.source":"My Moroccan Food (tomatoes and cucumber); MarocMama and Choumicha (tomatoes)","secrets.3.text":"Salt and lemon at the very last moment: otherwise the tomatoes release their water.","secrets.3.source":"Choumicha, tomato and pepper salad","secrets.4.text":"Make the dressing separately and keep it chilled until serving. If it is served without dressing, leftover salad is easy to reuse, in an omelette for example.","secrets.4.source":"Lalla Moulati (2019); Choumicha, tip “حول السلطة”","secrets.5.text":"Lemon juice rather than vinegar, if you have some.","secrets.5.source":"Lalla Moulati (2019); lemon in My Moroccan Food and Cooking With Alia","secrets.6.text":"A hint of cumin in the dressing, as Cooking With Alia, Choumicha and Lalla Moulati do.","secrets.6.source":"Cooking With Alia (recipe 208); Choumicha, tomato and pepper salad; Lalla Moulati (2019)","secrets.7.text":"Good tomatoes are the key: the salad is only as good as its vegetables.","secrets.7.source":"MarocMama","secrets.8.text":"Tomatoes not sweet enough? Nargisse Benkabbou adds a teaspoon of honey to balance the acidity.","secrets.8.source":"My Moroccan Food","secrets.9.text":"Parsley or coriander: both are used in Morocco.","secrets.9.source":"Parsley: My Moroccan Food, Choumicha; coriander: Cooking With Alia","secrets.10.text":"A little chopped preserved lemon peel (without the pulp) adds fragrance to the salad.","secrets.10.source":"Cooking With Alia (recipe 208, optional); Choumicha (tomato salads with preserved lemon)","secrets.11.text":"In Morocco, it mainly accompanies tagines, grilled meat and fish.","secrets.11.source":"My Moroccan Food; MarocMama","mistakes.0":"Salting the salad long in advance: the tomatoes release their water (Choumicha).","mistakes.1":"Cutting the vegetables too large (Cooking With Alia).","mistakes.2":"Washing the vegetables with soap or washing-up liquid (FDA).","mistakes.3":"Cutting the vegetables on the raw meat board without washing it first (Anses).","mistakes.4":"Leaving the salad at room temperature for more than 2 hours (Anses).","variants.0":"Choumicha's Moroccan salad: 4 tomatoes, 1 onion, 1 green pepper cut into small pieces, parsley, olive oil, vinegar or lemon juice, salt; no cucumber, served with kefta skewers.","variants.1":"Lalla Moulati's chlada with grilled peppers (2019): red, green and yellow peppers, grilled and peeled, 4 tomatoes, a small onion, green and black olives, sweetcorn if you like; a dressing of lemon (or vinegar), olive oil, salt, cumin and a little pepper.","variants.2":"Choumicha's tomato salad with preserved lemon: tomatoes peeled, deseeded and chopped, preserved lemon peel, parsley, a garlic clove, olive oil and salt.","variants.3":"Tomato and onion only, with vinegar and olive oil: the salad MarocMama serves with fish.","variants.4":"Coriander instead of parsley, and a little preserved lemon peel (Cooking With Alia, recipe 208)."},"salade-pois-chiches-thon":{"title":"Chickpea and tuna salad","darija":"Chlada d l-hommos b t-ton","tagline":"Chickpeas, tuna, cherry tomatoes and red onion, with lemon and olive oil: the no-cook Italian salad, ready in a quarter of an hour.","region":"Italy: “insalata di ceci e tonno”, a family salad served mostly in summer. Italian recipes often include red Tropea onion (Cotto in un click; La cucina di Loredana; Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè).","occasion":"A quick cold meal, at lunch or dinner, when you don't feel like cooking or turning on the hob; it is easy to take to work or on an outing (Il Cucchiaio d'Argento; Donna Moderna; Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè).","img_alt":"Chickpea and tuna salad (photo of a version with carrot and cheese)","serving":"Serve it cold, as a one-dish meal or as a starter (Cookist; Donna Moderna). Donna Moderna serves it in small bowls, with basil leaves and pan-fried bread croutons; Misya serves her bean and tuna salad with a lemon wedge.","ing.0.group":"Salad","ing.pois-chiches.name":"cooked chickpeas","ing.pois-chiches.note":"tinned; drained weight","ing.thon.name":"tinned tuna, in olive oil or in brine","ing.thon.note":"drained weight","ing.tomates-cerises.name":"cherry tomatoes","ing.oignon-rouge.name":"small red onion","ing.oignon-rouge.pl":"small red onions","ing.eau.name":"cold water","ing.eau.note":"for soaking the onion","ing.basilic.name":"fresh basil leaves","ing.basilic.note":"optional; or flat-leaf parsley","ing.1.group":"Seasoning","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.pl":"lemons","ing.citron.note":"for the juice","ing.huile-olive.name":"extra virgin olive oil","ing.origan.name":"dried oregano","ing.origan.note":"to taste","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"to taste","ing.poivre.name":"black pepper","ing.poivre.note":"to taste","equipment.0":"Tin opener","equipment.1":"Colander","equipment.2":"Large salad bowl","equipment.3":"Two small bowls","equipment.4":"Board and a sharp knife","equipment.5":"Lemon squeezer (optional)","equipment.6":"Fork","steps.0.title":"Squeeze the lemon","steps.0.text":"Squeeze {{citron:full}} and pour the juice into a small bowl.","steps.1.title":"Slice the onion","steps.1.text":"Peel {{oignon-rouge:full}} and cut into very thin slices.","steps.2.title":"Mellow the onion","steps.2.text":"Put the onion slices in another small bowl, cover with {{eau}} and add a little of the lemon juice. Leave to soak for 5 to 10 minutes while you prepare the rest: the onion becomes milder and stays crunchy.","steps.3.title":"Rinse the chickpeas","steps.3.text":"Tip the tinned chickpeas into a colander. Rinse them under the cold tap and leave them to drain well: you need {{pois-chiches}}.","steps.4.title":"Cut the tomatoes","steps.4.text":"Wash {{tomates-cerises}} of cherry tomatoes. Cut them in half, or into quarters if they are large.","steps.5.title":"Flake the tuna","steps.5.text":"Drain the tuna: you need {{thon}}. Flake it roughly with a fork, keeping some chunks.","steps.6.title":"Make the dressing","steps.6.text":"To the rest of the lemon juice, add {{huile-olive}} of olive oil, {{sel}} and {{poivre}}. Whisk with a fork.","steps.7.title":"Assemble the salad","steps.7.text":"Drain the onion. Put the chickpeas, tomatoes, onion and tuna in the salad bowl.","steps.8.title":"Dress the salad","steps.8.text":"Pour the dressing over the salad, sprinkle with {{origan}} and mix gently.","steps.9.title":"Taste and serve","steps.9.text":"Taste, then add a little salt, pepper or lemon if needed. Scatter over torn {{basilic}} and serve straight away, or leave in the fridge for 30 minutes so that the flavours blend.","secrets.0.text":"Rinse tinned chickpeas well, to remove the liquid and salt from the tin.","secrets.0.source":"Cookist (Silvia Ferrau); Chez Bibia; Donna Moderna; Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè","secrets.1.text":"Slice the red onion very thinly, then soak it for a few minutes in water with lemon juice: it loses its sharpness and stays crunchy.","secrets.1.source":"Il Cucchiaio d'Argento (insalata di fagioli e tonno: onion “in acqua acidulata per una decina di minuti”); Cotto in un click (onion marinated for 5 minutes, turning mild, pink and crunchy)","secrets.2.text":"Tuna in oil: mix it with the rest before dressing. Tuna in brine: dress the chickpeas and tomatoes first, and add the tuna afterwards.","secrets.2.source":"Il Cucchiaio d'Argento","secrets.3.text":"Choose good-quality tuna fillets; Cookist recommends tuna in oil in a glass jar.","secrets.3.source":"Il Cucchiaio d'Argento; Cookist (Elpidio)","secrets.4.text":"Flake the tuna roughly with a fork: it should stay in chunks.","secrets.4.source":"Chez Bibia; Cotto in un click","secrets.5.text":"La cucina di Loredana removes the juice and seeds from the tomatoes before adding them to the salad.","secrets.5.source":"La cucina di Loredana","secrets.6.text":"It is even better after 30 minutes in the fridge, once the flavours have blended; but it can also be eaten straight away.","secrets.6.source":"Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè; Cotto in un click; Misya (insalata di fagioli e tonno)","secrets.7.text":"If it has been in the fridge for a while, take it out 5 minutes before serving.","secrets.7.source":"Donna Moderna","secrets.8.text":"For crunch, add sliced celery or diced fennel; mamma Nenè also suggests cucumber.","secrets.8.source":"Donna Moderna; Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè","secrets.9.text":"In Italy, it is oregano and basil that flavour this salad.","secrets.9.source":"Il Cucchiaio d'Argento (fresh oregano); Cookist (dried oregano and basil); Donna Moderna (oregano and basil); Chez Bibia, La cucina di Loredana, Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè (basil)","secrets.10.text":"Don't be stingy with the extra virgin olive oil: Donna Moderna talks of “un generoso filo”.","secrets.10.source":"Donna Moderna","secrets.11.text":"No red onion? Use a spring onion, a shallot or some chives.","secrets.11.source":"Le dolcezze di mamma Nenè","mistakes.0":"Keeping leftover tuna in the opened tin: empty it into a bowl or another container and put it in the fridge, because once a can is open, the tin from the can may pass into the food more quickly (Food Standards Agency, UK).","mistakes.1":"Using a tin that is visibly swollen, deeply dented, or rusty because of a dent: do not eat its contents; a small, shallow dent with no other damage is not a problem (Food Standards Agency).","mistakes.2":"Adding the raw onion without soaking it: it is sharper and heavier on the stomach (Cotto in un click).","mistakes.3":"Not draining the chickpeas properly: they must lose all the liquid from the tin (Donna Moderna; Cookist).","mistakes.4":"Keeping the salad beyond the next day: in the fridge, in a closed container, for one day at most (Cookist; La cucina di Loredana).","mistakes.5":"For pregnant women, eating more than 4 medium-size tins of tuna a week (about 140 g drained weight each), because of pollutants such as mercury (NHS, UK).","variants.0":"Il Cucchiaio d'Argento's version: chickpeas, cherry tomatoes, tuna and fresh oregano, with just olive oil, salt and pepper, no onion or lemon.","variants.1":"Cookist's version: chickpeas, cherry tomatoes, tuna in oil, dried oregano and basil, no onion or lemon.","variants.2":"Donna Moderna's version: no tomatoes, with lemon juice, oregano and basil, and a little celery if you like; 2 hours in the fridge, then pan-fried bread croutons when serving.","variants.3":"Agrodolce's rich version: sweetcorn, Gaeta olives, walnut halves, sun-dried tomatoes and chives.","variants.4":"La cucina di Loredana's version: with thinly sliced cucumber, black olives and basil.","variants.5":"With hard-boiled eggs and rocket (Cucina facile con Elena; Rita amor di cucina): allow about 10 minutes to cook the eggs (Cucina facile con Elena).","variants.6":"With cider vinegar instead of lemon (Chez Bibia; Cotto in un click).","variants.7":"With white beans instead of chickpeas: “insalata di fagioli e tonno”, with spring onion, lemon juice and parsley (Il Cucchiaio d'Argento; Misya).","variants.8":"With parsley instead of basil, as in Misya's insalata di ceci or the insalata di fagioli e tonno (Il Cucchiaio d'Argento; Misya).","variants.9":"Another fish: mackerel in brine or smoked salmon instead of tuna (Donna Moderna; Cookist for the smoked salmon). If you are pregnant, the NHS advises cooking smoked salmon until it is steaming hot, because of listeria, and not eating more than 2 portions a week of oily fish such as mackerel."},"sandwich-poisson":{"title":"Sardine sandwich with chermoula","darija":"Sardine mzeouej","tagline":"One of Morocco's most popular street snacks: sardines stuffed with chermoula, fried and slipped, still hot, into half a roll with tomato, onion and harissa.","region":"All of Morocco, especially the coastal cities; fried stuffed sardines are also sold, sometimes only on certain days, in Jemaa el-Fna in Marrakesh and in the medina of Fez (Taste of Maroc). In Marrakesh, there is also a sardine kefta sandwich, hout quari (MarocMama, Halal Xplorer); in the north, fish bocadillos (Morocco World News).","occasion":"Street snack, to eat on the go (Morocco World News; Taste of Maroc)","img_alt":"Sardine sandwich in a roll, with salad","serving":"Eat the sandwich straight away, while the sardines are still hot (Bladi: stuffed sardines are best eaten hot). Morocco World News suggests slipping the stuffed sardines into half a small roll and having them with a drink, or serving them with Moroccan tomato and onion salad. Taste of Maroc adds lemon and harissa. Do not keep it out of the fridge for more than 2 hours (FDA).","pairs_label":"The recipes used","ing.0.group":"Sardines","ing.sardines.name":"sardines stuffed with chermoula","ing.sardines.note":"to be prepared and fried using the app's fried fish recipe (sardine part)","ing.1.group":"Bread","ing.pain.name":"small round roll","ing.pain.pl":"small round rolls","ing.pain.note":"khobz (Moroccan bread); half a roll per sandwich","ing.2.group":"Filling","ing.tomate.name":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.tomate.note":"grated","ing.oignon.name":"onion","ing.oignon.note":"finely chopped","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.harissa.name":"harissa","ing.harissa.note":"to taste","ing.olives.name":"green olives","ing.olives.note":"pitted, sliced; optional","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.note":"cut into wedges","equipment.0":"The equipment from the app's fried fish recipe (deep-fat fryer or deep frying pan, lid, slotted spoon, kitchen paper)","equipment.1":"Grater (for the tomatoes)","equipment.2":"Bread knife","equipment.3":"Chopping board","equipment.4":"Small bowls for the filling","steps.0.title":"Prepare the stuffed sardines","steps.0.text":"Prepare the {{sardines}} using the app's fried fish recipe, with sardines only: scale, gut and open them, make the chermoula and sandwich the sardines together in pairs. Before touching the raw fish, set a little chermoula aside for the sauce.","steps.0.tip":"Use small, very fresh sardines, bought the same day.","steps.1.title":"Marinate in the fridge","steps.1.text":"Cover the stuffed sardines and refrigerate them for at least 30 minutes. Meanwhile, prepare the filling.","steps.1.timer.label":"Marinating","steps.1.timer.done":"The marinating is done. Get ready to fry.","steps.2.title":"Grate the tomatoes","steps.2.text":"Cut the {{tomate}} in half and remove the seeds. Grate the flesh to a pulp and throw away the skin. Season with {{sel}} and {{poivre}}.","steps.2.tip":"Grating works best with very ripe tomatoes.","steps.2.img.alt":"Tomato grated on a grater","steps.2.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.3.title":"Chop the onion","steps.3.text":"Peel the {{oignon}} and chop it finely.","steps.4.title":"Fry the sardines","steps.4.text":"Fry the sardines as in the app's fried fish recipe: floured just beforehand, in very hot oil that is not smoking, in small batches, about 3 minutes per side, until golden brown.","steps.4.tip":"Do not take your eyes off the pan. If the oil catches fire: never use water; slide the lid over the pan, turn off the heat if it is safe to do so, do not move the pan and call the fire brigade straight away, even if the fire seems to be out.","steps.4.timer.label":"One side","steps.4.timer.done":"Turn the sardines over, then restart the timer for 3 minutes for the other side.","steps.5.title":"Drain the sardines","steps.5.text":"Put the sardines on kitchen paper to remove the excess oil.","steps.6.title":"Open the rolls","steps.6.text":"You need half a roll per sandwich, i.e. {{pain:full}} in all. Cut each roll in half, then open each half into a pocket, without tearing it.","steps.7.title":"Fill the sandwiches","steps.7.text":"In each pocket, put a little grated tomato and chopped onion, {{harissa}} to taste and a few {{olives}}. Slip in the sardines while still hot. Add a squeeze of {{citron}} juice and, if you kept some aside, a little chermoula.","steps.8.title":"Eat at once","steps.8.text":"Eat straight away, while the sardines are hot. Do not keep a sandwich out of the fridge for more than 2 hours, or more than 1 hour if it is above 32 °C.","secrets.0.text":"Fried stuffed sardines are one of Morocco's most classic street foods, even far from the coast, sometimes only on certain days: you can find them in Jemaa el-Fna in Marrakesh and in the old medina of Fez.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa, a Casablanca native); Morocco World News (2013, citing CNN: sardines, a basic street food, are most often fried; stuffed with a spicy chermoula, they make “a tasty sandwich”)","secrets.1.text":"In Morocco, sardines are prepared in many ways, but frying remains the most popular.","secrets.1.source":"Morocco World News, 27 May 2013 (citing CNN); Taste of Maroc (fried stuffed sardines, an iconic street food)","secrets.2.text":"Half a small roll is enough: slip the stuffed sardines into it.","secrets.2.source":"Morocco World News, 30 July 2021 (“in half a small roll”); MarocMama and Halal Xplorer (sardine sandwich in fresh bread)","secrets.3.text":"They are eaten with a tomato and onion salad: in the sandwich, the grated tomato and chopped onion play that role.","secrets.3.source":"Morocco World News, 2021 (Moroccan tomato and onion salad); Taste of Maroc (cucumber, tomato and onion salad); MarocMama and Halal Xplorer (tomato and raw onion in the Marrakesh sandwich)","secrets.4.text":"Harissa goes with fried sardines and with the Marrakesh sardine sandwich; add as much as you like.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc (fried sardines served with harissa); MarocMama and Halal Xplorer (harissa in the Marrakesh sardine sandwich)","secrets.5.text":"Olives go well with fried fish, and green olives in the sandwich.","secrets.5.source":"Choumicha (fried fish served with m'chermlines olives); MarocMama (sliced green olives); Halal Xplorer (chopped olives)","secrets.6.text":"Set aside a little clean chermoula, before touching the raw fish, to put in the bread: never the chermoula used for marinating.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa keeps some aside to serve with the fish); FDA (prevent bacteria from raw fish passing to ready-to-eat foods)","secrets.7.text":"Small fresh sardines are the best: under 15 cm, shiny and firm.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc; Choumicha, “La sardine, un trésor de bienfaits”","secrets.8.text":"Sardines stuck together in pairs are called “mzeouej”, the “married ones”.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); SBS (Ellie Bouhadana: “mzeouejim”, married)","secrets.9.text":"Stuffed sardines are best eaten hot, but they are also eaten warm or cold.","secrets.9.source":"Bladi, “Sardines farcies” (preferably hot); Taste of Maroc (hot, warm or at room temperature); Morocco World News, 2021 (cold or hot)","mistakes.0":"Putting chermoula that has touched the raw fish into the bread (FDA; Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.1":"Leaving a sandwich out of the fridge for more than 2 hours, or more than 1 hour if it is above 32 °C (FDA).","mistakes.2":"Using sardines that were not bought that day: sardines are very perishable (Choumicha; FDA for the signs of freshness).","mistakes.3":"Grating firm tomatoes: grating works best with very ripe tomatoes (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.4":"Forgetting the rules of frying: smoking oil, a pan more than a third full, wet fish, water on burning oil. It is all in the app's fried fish recipe (Québec government; Cambridgeshire Fire & Rescue Service; USFA; SFDA).","variants.0":"Marrakesh hout quari: sardines minced with ras el hanout, smen (aged salted butter) and coriander, shaped into balls, grilled or pan-fried, then served in bread with a raw tomato sauce, raw onion, harissa and green olives; the bread is sometimes spread with clarified butter and everyone builds their own sandwich (MarocMama; Halal Xplorer). Choumicha also makes a sardine kefta, with kidney fat (halal), onion, herbs and spices, as skewers grilled over charcoal.","variants.1":"Sardine kefta in sauce: sardine kefta is sold ready-made at the fish market (Taste of Maroc); Choumicha cooks it as balls with green olives, and in Marrakesh sardine balls are made as a tagine (Le360).","variants.2":"Grilled sardines from Essaouira: marinated in chermoula, grilled over the fire, served with bread and olives (Morocco World News Lifestyle, 2024).","variants.3":"Fish bocadillo in the north: with sardines or another white fish caught that day (Morocco World News, 2021).","variants.4":"Without bread: the stuffed sardines with tomato and onion salad (Morocco World News, 2021), or with white beans in tomato sauce and salads (Taste of Maroc)."},"soupe-lentilles-corail":{"title":"Turkish red lentil soup","darija":"Chorba d l-âdes l-hmer","tagline":"Mercimek çorbası, one of the most widely eaten soups in Turkey: red lentils, onion, carrot and potato blended into a smooth, velvety soup, with a drizzle of paprika butter and a lemon wedge. Ready in 30 minutes in a pressure cooker; allow 45 to 55 minutes in a saucepan.","region":"Turkey, throughout the country. In Istanbul, the most common version is made with lentils alone; Ozlem Warren, like her mother, adds carrot and potato (Ozlem's Turkish Table), as do most of the Turkish recipes consulted (Nefis Yemek Tarifleri; Yemek.com).","occasion":"An everyday soup, as a starter or a light meal; it is the queen of the menu in lokantas, small Turkish restaurants (Türk Mutfağı Haftası). In Anatolia, it is even eaten for breakfast (Ozlem Warren).","serving":"Serve piping hot, with a drizzle of paprika butter, a lemon wedge to squeeze into the bowl and toasted bread (Türk Mutfağı Haftası); Ozlem Warren also suggests croutons. The soup freezes well: Ozlem Warren often makes a double batch so she can freeze some of it (reheat only once, according to the Food Standards Agency).","ing.0.group":"Soup","ing.lentilles.name":"red lentils","ing.oignon.name":"medium onion","ing.oignon.pl":"medium onions","ing.carotte.name":"carrot","ing.carotte.pl":"carrots","ing.pomme-de-terre.name":"medium potato","ing.pomme-de-terre.pl":"medium potatoes","ing.huile.name":"sunflower oil","ing.huile.note":"or olive oil","ing.eau.name":"very hot water","ing.eau.note":"heated in the kettle","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"to taste","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.poivre.note":"to taste","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.cumin.note":"optional","ing.1.group":"Paprika butter and to serve","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.paprika.name":"paprika","ing.paprika.note":"sweet, or partly hot if you like","ing.citron.name":"lemon","ing.citron.pl":"lemons","ing.citron.note":"cut into wedges","ing.pain.name":"toasted bread","ing.pain.note":"optional","equipment.0":"Pressure cooker","equipment.1":"Hand blender, or a fine sieve and a ladle","equipment.2":"Kettle","equipment.3":"Sieve for rinsing the lentils","equipment.4":"Coarse grater","equipment.5":"Chopping board and knife","equipment.6":"Small frying pan","equipment.7":"Ladle","steps.0.title":"Heat the water","steps.0.text":"Heat {{eau}} of water in the kettle: with water that is already hot, the soup comes to the boil faster.","steps.1.title":"Rinse the lentils","steps.1.text":"Put {{lentilles}} of red lentils in a sieve. Rinse them under running water, stirring, until the water runs clear, then leave to drain.","steps.2.title":"Prepare the vegetables","steps.2.text":"Peel and chop {{oignon:full}}. Peel and coarsely grate {{carotte:full}}. Peel and finely dice {{pomme-de-terre:full}}.","steps.3.title":"Sauté the onion","steps.3.text":"In the open pressure cooker, heat {{huile}} of oil over medium heat. Sauté the onion for 1 to 2 minutes, stirring.","steps.4.title":"Add the vegetables","steps.4.text":"Add the grated carrot and diced potato. Sauté them for a few minutes, stirring, until they begin to soften: their colour passes into the oil and makes the soup a lovely golden colour.","steps.5.title":"Add the lentils","steps.5.text":"Add the drained lentils and stir them in the oil for 1 minute.","steps.6.title":"Pour in the water and skim","steps.6.text":"Pour in the hot water, add some {{sel}} and stir. Bring to the boil and use a spoon to skim off the foam that rises to the surface.","steps.7.title":"Cook under pressure","steps.7.text":"Close the pressure cooker. As soon as it starts to whistle, lower the heat and cook for 10 minutes.","steps.7.timer.label":"Pressure cooking","steps.7.timer.done":"Turn off the heat. Before opening, bring the pressure down as described in your pressure cooker's instructions.","steps.8.title":"Open the pressure cooker","steps.8.text":"Turn off the heat. Bring the pressure down by following your pressure cooker's instructions, then open it carefully.","steps.9.title":"Blend the soup","steps.9.text":"Wait 1 to 2 minutes, then blend with a hand blender until very smooth and velvety. Without a blender, push the soup through a fine sieve, pressing with the back of the ladle.","steps.10.title":"Season and adjust","steps.10.text":"Add {{cumin}} of cumin and some {{poivre}}, taste and adjust the salt. If the soup is too thick, add a little hot water. Leave to simmer for 2 to 3 minutes, stirring.","steps.11.title":"Make the paprika butter","steps.11.text":"In a small frying pan, melt {{beurre}} of butter over low heat. Add {{paprika}} of paprika, let it sizzle for a few seconds and take it off the heat straight away.","steps.12.title":"Serve piping hot","steps.12.text":"Cut {{citron:full}} into wedges. Pour the soup into the bowls, drizzle with a little paprika butter and put a lemon wedge alongside. Serve with {{pain}}.","secrets.0.text":"Rinse the lentils in plenty of water, until it runs clear.","secrets.0.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar); Yemek.com (Türk Mutfağı Haftası; Ev usulü mercimek çorbası)","secrets.1.text":"Pour in water that is already hot: the soup comes to the boil sooner and cooks faster.","secrets.1.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Nesli'nin Mutfağı, Altın mercimek çorbası); hot water also used by Elif Atalar and on Yemek.com (Düdüklüde mercimek çorbası)","secrets.2.text":"Sauté the carrot and potato in the oil before adding the water: their colour passes into the oil and makes the soup glossy and a lovely golden yellow.","secrets.2.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Nesli'nin Mutfağı)","secrets.3.text":"Stir the lentils in the fat for about a minute before adding the liquid.","secrets.3.source":"Yemek.com (Türk Mutfağı Haftası: “1 dakika kadar karıştırarak kavurun”); Nesli'nin Mutfağı (2 or 3 turns of the spoon)","secrets.4.text":"A pressure cooker greatly shortens the cooking time: about 40 minutes of cooking in a saucepan, compared with 15 to 20 minutes in all in a pressure cooker, 10 to 15 of them under pressure.","secrets.4.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar: “yaklaşık olarak 40 dakika” in a saucepan; 10 minutes under pressure); Yemek.com (Gökçen Mutaf: 10 to 15 minutes); Nesli'nin Mutfağı (10 to 15 minutes)","secrets.5.text":"For a silky, golden-yellow soup, push it through a sieve while it is still hot instead of blending it: blending makes it duller. Yemek.com does both, blender then fine sieve.","secrets.5.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Nesli'nin Mutfağı); Yemek.com (Süzme mercimek çorbası, Çiğdem Esastürk)","secrets.6.text":"“Süzme” (strained) refers to the smooth, velvety texture of this soup, the queen of lokanta menus.","secrets.6.source":"Yemek.com (Türk Mutfağı Haftası, with the contribution of the Turkish Ministry of Culture and Tourism)","secrets.7.text":"The soup thickens as it stands: thin it with a little hot water.","secrets.7.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar; Nesli'nin Mutfağı); Ozlem's Turkish Table","secrets.8.text":"A chicken or meat stock gives more flavour: one or two glasses are enough. To keep it halal, use homemade stock or a certified halal stock cube.","secrets.8.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar); Yemek.com (Türk Mutfağı Haftası, with meat stock)","secrets.9.text":"The paprika butter heats for only a few seconds, then comes off the heat.","secrets.9.source":"Yemek.com (Türk Mutfağı Haftası: “birkaç saniye kızdırın ve ocaktan alın”); Ozlem Warren (chilli infused for 35 seconds over low heat)","secrets.10.text":"Most often, it is served with a lemon wedge and sizzling chilli butter.","secrets.10.source":"Yemek.com (Türk Mutfağı Haftası); Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar); Ozlem's Turkish Table","secrets.11.text":"The cumin goes in at the end, with the pepper.","secrets.11.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar: after blending); Ozlem's Turkish Table (once the soup is cooked)","secrets.12.text":"Like it spicy? Add more chilli (pul biber).","secrets.12.source":"Nefis Yemek Tarifleri (Elif Atalar: “Acı seviyorsanız pul biber miktarını artırabilirsiniz”)","mistakes.0":"Opening the pressure cooker before bringing the pressure down: always follow the instructions (Yemek.com, Düdüklüde mercimek çorbası).","mistakes.1":"Blending the soup as soon as you open the pressure cooker: Yemek.com waits 1 to 2 minutes before blending it.","mistakes.2":"Not rinsing the lentils properly (Nefis Yemek Tarifleri, Elif Atalar).","mistakes.3":"Letting the flour burn, if you use any: don't fry it over too high a heat (Nefis Yemek Tarifleri, Elif Atalar, lokanta usulü recipe).","mistakes.4":"Serving the soup too thick: it thickens further as it cools, so thin it with hot water (Elif Atalar; Nesli'nin Mutfağı).","mistakes.5":"In a saucepan, forgetting to stir: the lentils stick to the bottom (Ozlem Warren).","mistakes.6":"Leaving leftovers at room temperature: put them in the fridge within 1 to 2 hours, eat them within 48 hours and reheat them only once, until they are steaming hot all the way through (Food Standards Agency, UK).","variants.0":"Without a pressure cooker: the same recipe in a large saucepan, at a gentle simmer with the lid ajar, for 20 to 25 minutes (Ozlem Warren, 2026) or 25 to 30 minutes (Türk Mutfağı Haftası), stirring from time to time. Then allow 45 to 55 minutes in all, including preparation: 15 + 30 minutes for Elif Atalar (Nefis Yemek Tarifleri), 15 + 35 minutes for the Türk Mutfağı Haftası, 15 + 40 minutes for Ozlem Warren in 2010; Nefis gives about 40 minutes of cooking in a saucepan.","variants.1":"The Turkish Cuisine Week version (Türk Mutfağı Haftası): just lentils, onion fried in butter until golden and halal meat stock, homemade or certified (or vegetable stock, or hot water), blended, then paprika butter; in Istanbul, the most common version is also made with lentils alone (Ozlem Warren).","variants.2":"With a little long-grain rice, cooked with the lentils (Ozlem Warren: 25 g for 4).","variants.3":"Thicker: a roux of butter and plain flour (Türk Mutfağı Haftası: 20 g of butter and 1 tbsp of flour); Elif Atalar fries 2 tbsp of flour with the onion.","variants.4":"With tomato purée or pepper paste fried with the onion (Elif Atalar; Yemek.com, Düdüklüde mercimek çorbası).","variants.5":"With turmeric and cumin in the soup, without carrot or potato (Elif Atalar, lokanta usulü recipe).","variants.6":"Chilli olive oil (pul biber) instead of the paprika butter, with the juice of one lemon in the soup (Ozlem Warren).","variants.7":"With a glass of chicken stock in place of some of the water (Elif Atalar, pressure-cooker recipe): homemade or certified halal stock."},"spaghetti-aglio-olio":{"title":"Spaghetti aglio, olio e peperoncino","tagline":"Spaghetti coated in olive oil flavoured with barely golden garlic and chilli: the great Neapolitan classic, ready while the pasta cooks.","region":"Italy. Traditionally attributed to Lazio, it was born in Naples and Campania according to Turismo Roma and Reporter Gourmet; its origin is still debated between Tuscany, Naples and Rome (Dissapore)","occasion":"A quick supper for tired evenings, made with ingredients you always have at home (Turismo Roma); Antonio Carluccio even makes it a midnight dish","img_alt":"Spaghetti with garlic, olive oil and red chilli, with parsley","serving":"Straight away, piping hot, with a little raw parsley on top (Turismo Roma, Dissapore). Giorgio Locatelli recommends a green salad on the side (Reporter Gourmet). If there is any left over, put the leftovers in the fridge within 2 hours (1 hour if it is above 32 °C) and reheat them right through, to 74 °C (FDA, the US health agency; Anses for the 2 hours).","ing.0.group":"Pasta","ing.spaghetti.name":"spaghetti","ing.spaghetti.note":"or vermicelli or linguine","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"a large panful, for cooking the pasta","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"for the pasta water: it is the only salt in the recipe","ing.1.group":"Sauce","ing.huile.name":"extra virgin olive oil","ing.huile.note":"good quality: the oil is the sauce","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.piment.name":"hot red chilli","ing.piment.pl":"hot red chillies","ing.piment.note":"preferably fresh, otherwise dried","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"to taste, optional","ing.eau-cuisson.name":"pasta cooking water","ing.eau-cuisson.note":"a few spoonfuls, taken from the saucepan","equipment.0":"A large saucepan with a lid, to hold plenty of water","equipment.1":"A large frying pan or sauté pan, wide enough to toss the pasta in","equipment.2":"A colander, or pasta tongs to lift the spaghetti out of the water","equipment.3":"A bowl, to save a little cooking water","equipment.4":"A chopping board and a small, very sharp knife","steps.0.title":"Put the water on to heat","steps.0.text":"Fill a large saucepan with {{eau}}, cover it and bring it to the boil.","steps.0.tip":"The lid saves energy while the water heats up (a study by the Italian pasta makers of Unione Italiana Food, cited by Il Giornale del Cibo).","steps.1.title":"Slice the garlic","steps.1.text":"Peel {{ail:full}}. Cut each clove in half lengthways, remove the germ from the centre, then cut into thin slices.","steps.1.tip":"Don't like biting into garlic? Keep the cloves halved and take them out of the pan before adding the pasta, as Dissapore does.","steps.2.title":"Prepare the chilli","steps.2.text":"Split {{piment:full}} lengthways, remove the seeds and cut the flesh into thin strips.","steps.3.title":"Chop the parsley","steps.3.text":"If using, finely chop the {{persil}} and set a little aside for serving.","steps.3.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.3.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.4.title":"Put the pasta on","steps.4.text":"When the water boils, add plenty of {{sel}}, then add {{spaghetti}} of spaghetti. Check the cooking time on the packet: you will drain it a little before that, nicely al dente.","steps.5.title":"Cook the garlic gently until pale golden","steps.5.text":"While the pasta is cooking, pour {{huile}} of olive oil into the large frying pan. Add the garlic and chilli, then warm over low heat. Let the garlic cook very gently until it is barely golden: it must never turn brown.","steps.5.tip":"Another way (Turismo Roma, Dissapore, Giorgio Locatelli): add the chilli only when the garlic starts to turn golden.","steps.6.title":"Stop the garlic cooking","steps.6.text":"As soon as the garlic is pale golden, take the frying pan off the heat and pour in a few spoonfuls of {{eau-cuisson}}, taken from the saucepan. This stops the garlic cooking and loosens the oil into a sauce.","steps.7.title":"Save a little water","steps.7.text":"Just before draining, scoop a little more cooking water into a bowl and keep it to hand.","steps.8.title":"Drain the pasta al dente","steps.8.text":"Drain the spaghetti while it is still nicely al dente, or lift it out of the water with tongs.","steps.9.title":"Toss the pasta in the sauce","steps.9.text":"Put the frying pan back on the heat and tip the spaghetti into it. Toss briskly with the tongs, adding a little cooking water if it looks dry, until the pasta is well coated.","steps.10.title":"Add the parsley and serve","steps.10.text":"Off the heat, add almost all the parsley, if using, and mix. Serve immediately, piping hot, with the rest of the parsley on top.","secrets.0.text":"The garlic should cook very gently and only turn pale golden, never brown or burn.","secrets.0.source":"Turismo Roma, Dissapore, Giorgio Locatelli; Antonio Carluccio even wants it with no colour at all","secrets.1.text":"As soon as the garlic is pale golden, a few spoonfuls of pasta water poured into the oil stop it cooking and loosen the oil into a sauce.","secrets.1.source":"Turismo Roma, Giorgio Locatelli","secrets.2.text":"Take this water at the last moment: at the end of cooking, it is richer in starch. It lightens the oil while giving it the consistency of a sauce.","secrets.2.source":"Turismo Roma","secrets.3.text":"No salt in the frying pan: the well-salted pasta water seasons the whole sauce.","secrets.3.source":"Turismo Roma (no other salt is called for); Dissapore (salt only for the spaghetti water)","secrets.4.text":"Drain the pasta a little before the end of the cooking time on the packet: it finishes cooking in the frying pan, with the sauce.","secrets.4.source":"Dissapore (one minute before the end), Turismo Roma","secrets.5.text":"The sauce is made while the pasta cooks: it only takes a few minutes.","secrets.5.source":"Antonio Carluccio (no more than 5 minutes), Turismo Roma","secrets.6.text":"The olive oil is the sauce: use a good-quality extra virgin oil with a pronounced flavour.","secrets.6.source":"Dissapore (advice from Piero Palanti, of the Extravoglio guide), Giorgio Locatelli, Turismo Roma","secrets.7.text":"No cheese: Antonio Carluccio doesn't use any, and neither Turismo Roma nor the two Dissapore recipes include it.","secrets.7.source":"Antonio Carluccio (Great Italian Chefs), Turismo Roma, Dissapore","secrets.8.text":"Remove the germ from the garlic. For a milder flavour, leave the cloves whole or halved and take them out before adding the pasta.","secrets.8.source":"Dissapore (both of its recipes)","secrets.9.text":"Very finely chopped parsley stalks, added with the chilli, flavour the oil; the leaves go in at the end, off the heat.","secrets.9.source":"Turismo Roma","secrets.10.text":"Spaghetti, vermicelli or linguine: you need a long pasta, neither too thick nor too wide.","secrets.10.source":"Turismo Roma; Dissapore (according to the gastronome Carnacina)","secrets.11.text":"It is a poor man's dish from Naples: according to Turismo Roma, it was made when people could not afford clams.","secrets.11.source":"Turismo Roma; Reporter Gourmet (article on Giorgio Locatelli) also links it to vermicelli with clams","mistakes.0":"Letting the garlic brown or burn (Turismo Roma, Dissapore, Giorgio Locatelli).","mistakes.1":"Using too high a heat: the garlic cooks over low heat (Turismo Roma, Antonio Carluccio).","mistakes.2":"Overcooking the pasta: drain it al dente, it finishes cooking in the frying pan (Dissapore, Turismo Roma).","mistakes.3":"Throwing away all the cooking water: keep a little to bind the sauce (Dissapore, Giorgio Locatelli).","mistakes.4":"Salting the sauce when the pasta water already seasons it (Turismo Roma).","mistakes.5":"Letting the dish sit: it is eaten as soon as it is ready (Turismo Roma, Giorgio Locatelli).","variants.0":"With anchovy: melt chopped anchovy fillets in the oil with the garlic; Antonio Carluccio uses 4 for 4 people (Great Italian Chefs). Dissapore also mentions olives.","variants.1":"With breadcrumbs (pangrattato), a “not uncommon” addition according to Dissapore.","variants.2":"Without parsley: just the garlic, oil and chilli, like Antonio Carluccio and Giorgio Locatelli; Dissapore (2022) considers it “not necessary”.","variants.3":"With red prawns and parsley (a suggestion from Great Italian Chefs alongside Antonio Carluccio's recipe).","variants.4":"With grated cheese, like Giorgio Locatelli (Parmesan or Grana Padano). Be aware: Parmesan (Parmigiano Reggiano) and Grana Padano are always made with calf rennet, which their production specifications require; if you want to be strict, choose another hard cheese made with a microbial coagulant, without animal rennet."},"taboule":{"title":"Lebanese tabbouleh","tagline":"The real Lebanese salad: a mountain of finely chopped parsley, a little bulgur, tomatoes, mint and plenty of lemon. No cooking, ready in 30 minutes.","region":"Lebanon and Syria: tabbouleh was born in the mountains of Lebanon and Syria, and it is a dish of the Levantine mezze (Wikipedia). It is not a Moroccan dish.","occasion":"As a mezze starter, or alongside grilled meat (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine). In Lebanon, it is a must at celebrations and family meals (Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; recettelibanaise.com). A National Tabbouleh Day has been celebrated there on the first Saturday of July since 2001 (English and Arabic Wikipedia, citing a guide to Lebanon published by Souk el Tayeb).","img_alt":"Parsley tabbouleh garnished with lemon slices and lettuce leaves (photo taken in Jordan)","serving":"Serve it straight away (Taste of Beirut; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine), as a mezze starter (Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine) or alongside grilled meat (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine: Hadia serves her kafta with tabbouleh, for example). It is eaten with the hands, using romaine lettuce leaves (Taste of Beirut; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Wikipedia), white cabbage leaves or vine leaves (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Wikipedia); Lebanese bread can also go with it (recettelibanaise.com; Libnanews). It is best straight away: if it is dressed too early, the herbs go limp and the salad turns soggy (Taste of Beirut; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen). Leftovers: into the fridge within 2 hours (Anses; FSA), in the coldest part of the fridge at 4 °C (Anses), and eat within 2 days (FSA).","ing.0.group":"Herbs and vegetables","ing.persil.name":"flat-leaf parsley","ing.persil.note":"very fresh; only the leaves are used","ing.menthe.name":"fresh mint","ing.menthe.note":"leaves only","ing.tomates.name":"medium tomato","ing.tomates.pl":"medium tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"deep red, ripe but firm, chosen one by one","ing.oignon.name":"white onion","ing.oignon.pl":"white onions","ing.oignon.note":"or spring onions","ing.1.group":"Bulgur and dressing","ing.boulgour.name":"fine bulgur (no. 1)","ing.boulgour.note":"definitely not couscous","ing.citrons.name":"lemon","ing.citrons.pl":"lemons","ing.citrons.note":"fresh, squeezed yourself (not bottled juice)","ing.huile.name":"extra virgin olive oil","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"to taste","ing.poivre.name":"black pepper","ing.poivre.note":"to taste","ing.piment-jamaique.name":"allspice","ing.piment-jamaique.note":"optional; or Lebanese seven-spice","ing.2.group":"To serve","ing.laitue.name":"romaine lettuce leaves","ing.laitue.note":"or white cabbage leaves, or young vine leaves","equipment.0":"A large, very sharp knife (no food processor or blender)","equipment.1":"A large, clean chopping board that has not been used for raw meat","equipment.2":"A large bowl to wash the herbs in, and another for serving","equipment.3":"A salad spinner, or a clean tea towel and kitchen paper","equipment.4":"A sieve","equipment.5":"A small bowl","equipment.6":"A lemon squeezer","steps.0.title":"Wash the herbs","steps.0.text":"Wash your hands. Plunge {{persil}} of parsley and {{menthe}} of mint into a large bowl of cold water, and swish them around gently. Then rinse them under the tap.","steps.0.tip":"Parsley and mint are eaten raw: wash them carefully (Anses). Hadia adds a pinch of salt to the water and leaves them to soak for 5 minutes before rinsing them.","steps.1.title":"Dry them well","steps.1.text":"Spin the herbs dry, then spread them out on a clean tea towel or kitchen paper. They must be completely dry before chopping, otherwise the tabbouleh will be soggy.","steps.2.title":"Soak the bulgur","steps.2.text":"Squeeze {{citrons:full}}. Put {{boulgour}} of fine bulgur, half the lemon juice and a little salt in a small bowl. Leave the bulgur to swell while you prepare the rest: it does not need cooking.","steps.2.tip":"Fine bulgur, not couscous: bulgur is wheat that has been precooked, dried and then cracked, so it only needs to soak up the juice. With couscous, you get the French-style “taboulé à l'orientale”, where the couscous takes the place of the parsley (Wikipedia).","steps.3.title":"Keep the leaves","steps.3.text":"Cut off the thick stalks of the parsley and mint. Keep only the leaves.","steps.4.title":"Chop the parsley","steps.4.text":"Gather the parsley into small, tight bunches and slice it very finely, into thin shreds, with a sharp knife. Do it by hand: a food processor crushes the leaves and turns them to mush.","steps.4.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.4.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.5.title":"Chop the mint","steps.5.text":"Finely chop the mint leaves in the same way.","steps.6.title":"Dice the tomatoes","steps.6.text":"Cut {{tomates:full}} into very small dice. Put them in a sieve so they lose their juice.","steps.7.title":"Chop the onion","steps.7.text":"Very finely chop {{oignon:full}}.","steps.8.title":"Mix at the last moment","steps.8.text":"Just before serving, put the parsley, mint, drained tomatoes, onion and bulgur in a large bowl.","steps.9.title":"Dress the salad","steps.9.text":"Pour over the rest of the lemon juice and {{huile}} of olive oil. Add some {{sel}}, some {{poivre}} and {{piment-jamaique}} of allspice if you have it. Mix well.","steps.10.title":"Taste","steps.10.text":"Taste: the tabbouleh should be distinctly lemony. Adjust the salt if needed.","steps.11.title":"Serve straight away","steps.11.text":"Serve immediately, with {{laitue}}: everyone picks up the tabbouleh by hand, pinching it in a leaf.","steps.11.tip":"You can also serve it with white cabbage leaves or young vine leaves (Hadia; Wikipedia).","secrets.0.text":"Lebanese tabbouleh is first and foremost a parsley salad: lots of herbs, very little bulgur.","secrets.0.source":"Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Taste of Beirut (Joumana Accad); Wikipedia (articles “Taboulé” and “Tabbouleh”)","secrets.1.text":"Use fine bulgur, no. 1: it is the finest, the one for tabbouleh.","secrets.1.source":"Mama's Lebanese Kitchen (“fine cracked wheat #1”); Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; recettelibanaise.com; Libnanews","secrets.2.text":"Why not couscous: bulgur is wheat that has been precooked, dried and then cracked; fine bulgur only needs to soak up the juice of the lemons and tomatoes, without cooking, and it stays in the background among the parsley. Couscous is a different product: grains of semolina that are rolled and then steamed. With it, you get the “taboulé à l'orientale” served in France, where the couscous becomes the main ingredient and the parsley takes a back seat: it is no longer Lebanese tabbouleh.","secrets.2.source":"Wikipedia, “Boulgour” and “Bulgur” (the English article: soaking alone is enough); Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine (fine bulgur soaks up the juice of the tomatoes and lemon); recettelibanaise.com (fine bulgur that stays in the background); Wikipedia, “Couscous” and “Taboulé”","secrets.3.text":"Dry the herbs thoroughly before chopping them: if they are wet, they turn to paste and make the salad soggy.","secrets.3.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen","secrets.4.text":"Chop by hand, with a sharp knife, into thin shreds; never in a food processor.","secrets.4.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Taste of Beirut; recettelibanaise.com; Anissa Helou (blog, “chopping parsley for a living”)","secrets.5.text":"Choose firm tomatoes and drain them: too much juice makes the salad soggy.","secrets.5.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; recettelibanaise.com","secrets.6.text":"Mix and dress at the last moment: otherwise the herbs go limp and the tabbouleh turns soggy.","secrets.6.source":"Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Taste of Beirut; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine","secrets.7.text":"Freshly squeezed lemon, never bottled. Hadia squeezes it just before mixing: squeezed in advance and kept, it can turn bitter.","secrets.7.source":"Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine","secrets.8.text":"You can tell a good tabbouleh by its distinctly lemony taste.","secrets.8.source":"Mama's Lebanese Kitchen","secrets.9.text":"A pinch of allspice or Lebanese seven-spice is the spice of Lebanese tabbouleh; Hadia highly recommends it.","secrets.9.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Taste of Beirut (optional); Mama's Lebanese Kitchen (seven-spice)","secrets.10.text":"Flat-leaf parsley if possible; curly parsley will do at a pinch, as long as it is chopped very finely.","secrets.10.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Taste of Beirut (Joumana Accad's reply to a reader)","secrets.11.text":"To save time, chop the parsley, mint and onion a few hours ahead, cover and keep in the fridge; keep the tomatoes separate and assemble at the last moment.","secrets.11.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Taste of Beirut (dress at the last moment if preparing ahead)","secrets.12.text":"In Lebanon, it is eaten with the hands, pinched in a romaine, cabbage or vine leaf.","secrets.12.source":"Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Taste of Beirut; Wikipedia (“Taboulé”); Libnanews (François El Bacha)","mistakes.0":"Using couscous, or far too much bulgur: it is no longer Lebanese tabbouleh (Wikipedia; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen).","mistakes.1":"Using coarse bulgur: you need fine no. 1 bulgur (Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; recettelibanaise.com; Libnanews).","mistakes.2":"Chopping the parsley while it is still wet, or in a food processor: it turns to mush (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; Mama's Lebanese Kitchen).","mistakes.3":"Leaving in the thick stalks (Taste of Beirut; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine).","mistakes.4":"Dressing it long in advance: the tabbouleh turns soggy (Mama's Lebanese Kitchen; Taste of Beirut; Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine).","mistakes.5":"Using bottled lemon juice (Mama's Lebanese Kitchen).","mistakes.6":"Using tomatoes that are too soft: they release too much water (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine; recettelibanaise.com).","mistakes.7":"Forgetting to wash the parsley and mint, which are eaten raw (Anses; FSA).","mistakes.8":"Chopping the herbs on the board used for raw meat (Anses).","mistakes.9":"Leaving the tabbouleh at room temperature for more than 2 hours before putting it in the fridge (Anses).","variants.0":"With spring onions instead of the white onion (Taste of Beirut; Arabic Wikipedia).","variants.1":"With a very finely chopped cucumber: this is the Palestinian way (Wikipedia; Libnanews); Mama's Lebanese Kitchen also adds one.","variants.2":"With chopped lettuce mixed in: the Armenian version (Wikipedia; Libnanews).","variants.3":"Milder: with sumac and pomegranate molasses instead of lemon (Wikipedia); Hadia just adds a drizzle of pomegranate molasses on top.","variants.4":"The old way, as before the tomato reached the Levant: diced tomatoes served separately or placed on top (Wikipedia).","variants.5":"Bulgur added dry to the salad: it soaks up the juice of the tomatoes and lemon (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine).","variants.6":"With a pinch of cinnamon, a little paprika and some lemon zest (recettelibanaise.com).","variants.7":"Gluten-free: quinoa instead of bulgur, which Mama's Lebanese Kitchen suggests on the advice of a few people, without having tested it.","variants.8":"Tabbouleh kazzabé, the “liar's tabbouleh” of South Lebanon: here the bulgur is the star, soaked in the cabbage cooking water, with red onion and tomato purée (Hadia's Lebanese Cuisine, a recipe from her friend Rima Hamad).","variants.9":"Turkish kısır and Armenian eetch: far more bulgur than parsley (Wikipedia)."},"tajine-agneau-pruneaux":{"title":"Lamb tagine with prunes and almonds","darija":"Lham bel barkouk","tagline":"Melt-in-the-mouth lamb with saffron and ginger in its onion sauce, crowned with cinnamon-caramelised prunes, fried almonds and toasted sesame.","region":"Morocco, a flagship dish of Moroccan cuisine","occasion":"Celebrations and weddings, Eid al-Adha, receptions, iftar during Ramadan","img_alt":"Tagine with prunes and almonds, in its earthenware dish","serving":"Piping hot, in a warmed serving tagine or a deep dish placed in the centre of the table to be shared: the meat coated in its sauce mixed with a little syrup, the caramelised prunes on top, then the fried almonds and toasted sesame. With bread to mop up the sauce; couscous is a separate dish.","ing.0.group":"Meat and sauce","ing.viande.name":"halal lamb (shoulder or neck, on the bone)","ing.viande.note":"in large pieces; Choumicha uses shoulder or leg, My Moroccan Food and Grantourismo shoulder, neck or shanks","ing.oignons.name":"medium onion","ing.oignons.pl":"medium onions","ing.oignons.note":"finely chopped or grated","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.huile.name":"olive oil or neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.smen.name":"smen (aged salted butter)","ing.smen.note":"optional; this is Choumicha's touch","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end of cooking","ing.safran.name":"saffron threads","ing.safran.note":"Choumicha uses 8 threads per 1 kg of meat","ing.eau-safran.name":"lukewarm water, for the saffron","ing.cannelle-baton.name":"cinnamon stick","ing.cannelle-baton.pl":"cinnamon sticks","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.persil.name":"fresh parsley","ing.eau.name":"hot water","ing.eau.note":"never cold; add a little more during cooking if the meat is no longer half covered","ing.1.group":"Caramelised prunes","ing.pruneaux.name":"dried prunes","ing.pruneaux.note":"Choumicha removes the stones","ing.beurre.name":"butter","ing.sucre.name":"caster sugar (or honey)","ing.sucre.note":"a little less if you want it less sweet","ing.cannelle-moulue.name":"ground cinnamon","ing.2.group":"Garnish","ing.amandes.name":"blanched almonds (skins removed)","ing.huile-friture.name":"oil for frying the almonds","ing.huile-friture.pl":"oil for frying the almonds","ing.sesame.name":"sesame seeds","equipment.0":"Large heavy-based pot with a lid, or a glazed earthenware tagine on a heat diffuser","equipment.1":"Serving tagine or deep dish","equipment.2":"Small saucepan with a lid (prunes)","equipment.3":"Small frying pan (almonds, then sesame)","equipment.4":"Small bowl and large bowl","equipment.5":"Kitchen string","equipment.6":"Slotted spoon and kitchen paper","steps.0.title":"Infuse the saffron","steps.0.text":"Put {{safran}} of saffron threads in a small bowl with {{eau-safran}} of lukewarm water. Leave to infuse for 10 min while you prepare the rest.","steps.0.timer.label":"Infusing the saffron","steps.0.timer.done":"The saffron has infused: keep the bowl within reach.","steps.1.title":"Prepare the onions, garlic and herb bundle","steps.1.text":"Finely chop or grate {{oignons:full}}, and crush {{ail:full}}. Tie {{coriandre}} of coriander and {{persil}} of parsley together with string into a small bundle. If the butcher hasn't done it, cut the meat into large pieces, keeping the bones.","steps.2.title":"Sauté the meat in the spices","steps.2.text":"In a large heavy-based pot over medium-low heat, put {{huile}} of oil, {{smen}} of smen, the onions, the garlic, {{gingembre}} of ginger, {{poivre}} of pepper, {{sel}} of salt, {{cannelle-baton:full}} and the saffron water. Stir, add {{viande}} of lamb and sauté everything for 5 to 10 min, stirring often: the meat should take on colour on all sides without burning.","steps.2.timer.label":"Sautéing the meat","steps.2.timer.done":"The meat has coloured on all sides: pour in the hot water.","steps.3.title":"Add the water and simmer for 1 h","steps.3.text":"Pour in {{eau}} of hot water, never cold: the meat should be roughly half covered; top up a little if needed. Lay the herb bundle on top and bring to the boil. Cover, turn the heat down to low and leave to simmer for 1 h, stirring from time to time.","steps.3.timer.label":"Simmering: 1st hour","steps.3.timer.done":"1 h of simmering: take out some sauce for the prunes.","steps.4.title":"Meanwhile, plump up the prunes","steps.4.text":"Rinse {{pruneaux}} of prunes, cover them with boiling water and leave them to plump up for 15 min. Drain them.","steps.4.timer.label":"Soaking the prunes","steps.4.timer.done":"Drain the prunes and set them aside.","steps.5.title":"Fry the almonds","steps.5.text":"Heat a thin layer of oil in a small frying pan over medium heat. Fry {{amandes}} of blanched almonds in it, stirring constantly, until they are golden brown. Drain them on kitchen paper.","steps.6.title":"Toast the sesame seeds","steps.6.text":"Wipe out the frying pan and dry-toast {{sesame}} of sesame seeds in it over medium-low heat, stirring, just until they turn pale golden. Tip them straight into a bowl.","steps.7.title":"Take out some sauce for the prunes","steps.7.text":"After an hour of simmering, pour about a quarter of the sauce from the pot into a small saucepan. If there is hardly any sauce left, add a little hot water to the pot. Cover again and leave the meat to simmer for another 30 min over low heat.","steps.7.timer.label":"Final simmer","steps.7.timer.done":"Check: the meat should come away from the bone; if not, cook for another 15 min.","steps.8.title":"Cook the prunes in the sauce","steps.8.text":"Add {{beurre}} of butter to the small saucepan and let it melt over medium heat. Add the drained prunes, cover and cook for 10 min, until they are tender.","steps.8.timer.label":"Prunes in the sauce","steps.8.timer.done":"The prunes are tender: add the sugar and cinnamon.","steps.9.title":"Caramelise the prunes","steps.9.text":"Add {{sucre}} of sugar (or honey) and {{cannelle-moulue}} of ground cinnamon, then stir gently so as not to crush the prunes. Cover and leave to candy gently for 10 min over low heat: they should be glossy, in a thick syrup. If the syrup is still runny, uncover and let it reduce for a few minutes. Keep warm.","steps.9.timer.label":"Caramelised prunes","steps.9.timer.done":"Thick syrup, glossy prunes: keep them warm.","steps.10.title":"Reduce the meat sauce","steps.10.text":"When the meat comes away from the bone, remove the herb bundle. If the sauce is still runny, reduce it uncovered for 10 to 15 min over medium-low heat, stirring from time to time: it should be thick, with the onions cooked down until very soft. Taste and adjust the salt. Meanwhile, warm the serving dish in a hot oven that has been switched off, or fill it with hot water and then empty it.","steps.10.timer.label":"Reducing the sauce","steps.10.timer.done":"The sauce is thick: time to serve.","steps.11.title":"Arrange and serve","steps.11.text":"Arrange the meat in the warm dish and top it with the prunes. Mix the meat sauce with a little of the prune syrup and spoon it over the meat. Scatter over the fried almonds and toasted sesame seeds. Serve immediately, in the centre of the table, with bread.","secrets.0.text":"Cook the prunes separately, not in the pot: they caramelise better and the dish doesn't turn blackish-brown.","secrets.0.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); also cooked separately by Choumicha, Bladi, Cooking with Alia, Morocco World News and Femmes du Maroc","secrets.1.text":"Candy the prunes in a little sauce taken from the meat, with butter, then the sugar or honey and the cinnamon, until you have a thick syrup.","secrets.1.source":"Cooking with Alia; Morocco World News; Bladi (“two ladlefuls of sauce”); My Moroccan Food (sauce taken out after 1 h)","secrets.2.text":"First plump up the prunes in boiling water to soften them.","secrets.2.source":"Cooking with Alia (15 min); My Moroccan Food (5 min); Bladi (prunes “plumped up in water”)","secrets.3.text":"Infuse the saffron threads for 10 min in a little lukewarm water before pouring them into the pot.","secrets.3.source":"Cooking with Alia","secrets.4.text":"Add a spoonful of smen with the spices for the meat.","secrets.4.source":"Choumicha","secrets.5.text":"Lay a tied bundle of coriander and parsley on the meat, and remove it before serving.","secrets.5.source":"Choumicha (parsley and coriander bundle); Grantourismo, a recipe by Terence Carter learned from Jamila and then adapted (bundle to be removed); My Moroccan Food (tied bunch of coriander)","secrets.6.text":"During cooking, always top up with hot water, never cold, and keep the meat half covered: add a little hot water if the sauce starts to stick.","secrets.6.source":"Choumicha (advice given for the earthenware tagine); Cooking with Alia (meat half covered); My Moroccan Food (water added if it sticks)","secrets.7.text":"The sauce is ready when it has reduced to melted onions and oil, thick and syrupy.","secrets.7.source":"Grantourismo (Terence Carter's recipe card, adapted from Jamila's recipe); Choumicha (“reduced sauce”); Cooking with Alia (“thick sauce”)","secrets.8.text":"For a thicker, more generous sauce, use more onions: My Moroccan Food uses 800 g for 1.2 kg of meat.","secrets.8.source":"My Moroccan Food","secrets.9.text":"Before spooning it over, mix the meat sauce with a little of the prune syrup.","secrets.9.source":"Choumicha","secrets.10.text":"Blanched almonds fried in oil and then drained; sesame lightly toasted in a dry pan.","secrets.10.source":"Bladi; Femmes du Maroc (almonds “blanched and fried”); Cooking with Alia (fried almonds)","secrets.11.text":"Choose a cut on the bone that stands up to long cooking: lamb shoulder, neck or shank.","secrets.11.source":"My Moroccan Food; Grantourismo; Choumicha (shoulder or leg); Bladi (shoulder)","secrets.12.text":"Today in Morocco, the tagine is often cooked in a large pot, then transferred to the earthenware tagine to be served in the centre of the table.","secrets.12.source":"Grantourismo, according to Jamila, a cook at a riad in Marrakesh","secrets.13.text":"The glazed, decorated serving tagine never goes on the heat: warm it before plating, in a hot oven that has been switched off or with hot water.","secrets.13.source":"Choumicha (advice on the tagine)","mistakes.0":"Cooking the prunes in the pot with the meat if you want them well caramelised: they caramelise less well and the dish turns brown (My Moroccan Food). It is not a mistake as such, though: My Moroccan Food says so, and Grantourismo, like another Bladi.net recipe, cooks them in the pot.","mistakes.1":"Topping up with cold water during cooking: always use hot water (Choumicha, advice given for the earthenware tagine).","mistakes.2":"Letting the sauce dry out: the meat sticks. Add a little hot water as soon as there is hardly any left (My Moroccan Food, Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.3":"Stopping the cooking too soon: the meat should come away easily from the bone (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.4":"Handling the prunes roughly at the end of cooking: stir them gently (Cooking with Alia).","mistakes.5":"Over-toasting the sesame: it should just turn pale golden (Bladi: “toast lightly”).","mistakes.6":"Putting an earthenware tagine over high heat: it can crack. Always use low heat, with a diffuser on a gas hob (Choumicha).","mistakes.7":"Putting the glazed, decorated tagine on the heat: it is only for serving (Choumicha).","mistakes.8":"Thinking that in Morocco tagine comes with couscous: there, couscous is usually a separate dish, and tagine is eaten with bread to mop up the sauce (Grantourismo; Morocco World News). Nothing stops you serving some: My Moroccan Food suggests couscous or bread.","variants.0":"Beef: beef shin or neck, same method; simmer for longer, until the meat is meltingly tender (Cooking with Alia: 1 to 2 h depending on the cut; My Moroccan Food, Femmes du Maroc, Morocco World News).","variants.1":"Pressure cooker: Bladi cooks the shoulder for 20 min under pressure, then finishes it for about 15 min, stirring; Morocco World News allows 40 min for beef, then reduces the sauce uncovered.","variants.2":"With honey: My Moroccan Food and Cooking with Alia caramelise the prunes with honey rather than sugar; Morocco World News mixes sugar and honey.","variants.3":"Prunes and apricots: Femmes du Maroc (dried apricots) and chef Asmaa Alami (Le360) pair caramelised prunes and apricots; Femmes du Maroc also adds caramelised quinces or apples.","variants.4":"Extra spices: a pinch of ras el hanout (Choumicha, Cooking with Alia) or a little turmeric (My Moroccan Food, Morocco World News, Cooking with Alia).","variants.5":"Toasted almonds rather than fried (Choumicha, My Moroccan Food), or flaked almonds (Le360, Grantourismo).","variants.6":"Cooking it all in a glazed earthenware tagine: on the gas with a diffuser, always over low heat; line the base with parsley and coriander stalks or onion rings so that nothing sticks (Choumicha)."},"tajine-kefta-oeufs":{"title":"Kefta tagine with eggs","darija":"Tajine kefta mkaouara bel bid","tagline":"Small meatballs with herbs and cumin simmering in a spicy tomato sauce, eggs with runny yolks, and khobz (Moroccan bread) to mop it all up.","region":"Morocco (no specific region of origin in the sources consulted)","occasion":"A simple, quick family meal, shared around the tagine","serving":"Serve immediately, in the tagine or sauté pan it was cooked in, placed in the middle of the table. Traditionally, everyone eats together straight from the dish, by hand, with pieces of khobz to scoop up the meatballs, the sauce and the runny yolk (Taste of Maroc, Amour de cuisine, My Moroccan Food). Mint tea goes well with it (Taste of Maroc, Cooking With Alia).","ing.0.group":"Kefta (meatballs)","ing.viande.name":"halal minced meat (beef, lamb or a mix of both)","ing.viande.note":"not too lean: 15 to 20% fat","ing.oignon-kefta.name":"small onion, grated","ing.oignon-kefta.pl":"small onions, grated","ing.persil-kefta.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre-kefta.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.paprika-kefta.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin-kefta.name":"ground cumin","ing.sel-kefta.name":"fine salt","ing.poivre-kefta.name":"ground black pepper","ing.piment-kefta.name":"cayenne pepper","ing.1.group":"Tomato sauce","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe fresh tomatoes","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.oignon-sauce.name":"medium onion, finely chopped","ing.oignon-sauce.pl":"medium onions, finely chopped","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove, crushed","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves, crushed","ing.paprika-sauce.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin-sauce.name":"ground cumin","ing.sel-sauce.name":"fine salt","ing.sel-sauce.note":"adjust after tasting","ing.poivre-sauce.name":"ground black pepper","ing.piment-sauce.name":"cayenne pepper","ing.piment-sauce.note":"or 2 small whole hot chillies","ing.persil-sauce.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.coriandre-sauce.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"only if the sauce sticks","ing.2.group":"To finish and serve","ing.oeufs.name":"egg","ing.oeufs.pl":"eggs","ing.oeufs.note":"free-range (beldi) if possible","ing.sel-oeufs.name":"fine salt","ing.sel-oeufs.note":"a small pinch on each egg","ing.coriandre-deco.name":"finely chopped fresh coriander","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"as much as you like, for mopping up the sauce","equipment.0":"Earthenware or ceramic tagine, medium to large","equipment.1":"Heat diffuser if you are not cooking on gas","equipment.2":"Or a deep sauté pan with a lid","equipment.3":"Coarse grater","equipment.4":"Large bowl","equipment.5":"Wooden spoon","steps.0.title":"Knead the kefta","steps.0.text":"Put {{viande:full}}, {{oignon-kefta:full}}, {{persil-kefta:full}} and {{coriandre-kefta:full}} in a large bowl. Add {{paprika-kefta:full}}, {{cumin-kefta:full}}, {{sel-kefta:full}}, {{poivre-kefta:full}} and {{piment-kefta:full}}. Knead by hand for 1 to 2 minutes, just enough to spread the spices evenly, without packing the meat down. Don't add egg or breadcrumbs.","steps.1.title":"Roll small meatballs","steps.1.text":"Shape cherry-sized meatballs, about 2.5 cm across. Put them on a plate, cover and keep them chilled while you make the sauce.","steps.2.title":"Grate the tomatoes","steps.2.text":"Cut {{tomates:full}} in half. Grate the cut sides on the coarse holes of the grater, over a bowl. Throw away the skin left in your hand.","steps.2.img.alt":"Grated tomato on a grater","steps.2.img.caption":"Grating the tomato","steps.3.title":"Sauté the onion and garlic","steps.3.text":"Put the tagine on a diffuser (or use a sauté pan) over medium-low heat and heat {{huile:full}}. Add {{oignon-sauce:full}}, and cook for a few minutes, stirring, until softened. Add {{ail:full}}, and stir for 1 to 2 minutes, until fragrant, without letting the garlic burn.","steps.4.title":"Simmer the sauce","steps.4.text":"Pour in the grated tomatoes with their juice. Add {{paprika-sauce:full}}, {{cumin-sauce:full}}, {{sel-sauce:full}}, {{poivre-sauce:full}}, {{piment-sauce:full}}, {{persil-sauce:full}} and {{coriandre-sauce:full}}. Bring to a simmer, then cover and leave to simmer gently over low heat, stirring from time to time.","steps.4.timer.label":"Tomato sauce","steps.4.timer.done":"Check the sauce: it should be thick and fragrant.","steps.5.title":"Check the sauce","steps.5.text":"Crush the pieces of tomato with the back of a spoon. The sauce is ready when it is thick and fragrant. If it sticks, add a little {{eau:full}}; if it is still runny, cook it for longer, as it all depends on how ripe the tomatoes are. Taste and adjust the salt.","steps.6.title":"Poach the meatballs","steps.6.text":"Place the raw meatballs in the sauce one by one, leaving a little space between them. Cover and leave to simmer gently over low heat. Cut one meatball open to check that it is cooked through, but don't cook them any longer than that: overcooked, they turn hard.","steps.6.timer.label":"Meatballs","steps.6.timer.done":"Cut a meatball open: if it is cooked through, move straight on to the eggs.","steps.7.title":"Crack in the eggs","steps.7.text":"Make a small well in the sauce, between the meatballs, for each egg. Crack {{oeufs:full}} one at a time into the wells, without breaking the yolks. Lightly salt each egg ({{sel-oeufs:full}}).","steps.8.title":"Let the eggs set","steps.8.text":"Put the lid back on and cook over low heat. It is ready when the whites are set and the yolks are still runny. Take off the heat immediately. For a pregnant woman, a young child or an elderly or vulnerable person, cook for a few more minutes, until the yolk is set.","steps.8.timer.label":"Eggs","steps.8.timer.done":"Whites set, yolks still runny: turn off the heat straight away.","steps.9.title":"Serve in the tagine","steps.9.text":"Scatter over {{coriandre-deco:full}}. Put the tagine in the middle of the table and serve straight away, in the cooking dish. Everyone helps themselves by hand with pieces of {{khobz:full}}, dipping the bread into the sauce and the yolk.","secrets.0.text":"The classic seasoning for kefta is based, at the very least, on paprika, cumin, onion and fresh herbs.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih), kefta recipe","secrets.1.text":"No egg or breadcrumbs in the kefta: that is a departure from Moroccan tradition.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.2.text":"Use meat that is not too lean (15 to 20% fat): a little fat is needed for the classic texture and flavour of kefta.","secrets.2.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); Taste of Maroc, kefta recipe","secrets.3.text":"For Fatéma Hal, this dish has the scent of Morocco: parsley, coriander, mint and cumin. Cumin, which she sometimes finds overpowering, finds favour with her only in this tagine.","secrets.3.source":"TelQuel, “À la recherche du goût perdu”, Fatéma Hal","secrets.4.text":"The meatballs are traditionally made very small (the size of a cherry, about 2.5 cm); the smaller they are, the faster they cook.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food; MarocMama","secrets.5.text":"Cut the tomatoes in half, grate them and throw away the skin, rather than peeling and chopping them: the cooking time is a little shorter.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking With Alia; MarocMama","secrets.6.text":"Go for very ripe fresh tomatoes: tinned ones taste slightly metallic. The reducing time depends a great deal on how ripe and juicy they are.","secrets.6.source":"MarocMama (Amanda Mouttaki); Taste of Maroc","secrets.7.text":"Reduce the sauce first, then poach the raw meatballs in it just long enough to cook them: overcooked, they become hard.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food","secrets.8.text":"Leave a little space between the meatballs so they soak up the sauce.","secrets.8.source":"MarocMama","secrets.9.text":"The eggs go in at the very end, in small wells, with a pinch of salt on each, lid on: 4 to 5 minutes are enough for set whites and runny yolks.","secrets.9.source":"Amour de cuisine; My Moroccan Food","secrets.10.text":"Choose free-range (beldi) eggs if you can.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.11.text":"Earthenware tagine: always use a diffuser on any heat source other than gas; on gas without a diffuser, the flame must not touch the base.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc; MarocMama","secrets.12.text":"If the sauce is too acidic, a pinch of sugar will correct it.","secrets.12.source":"Amour de cuisine","mistakes.0":"Putting egg or breadcrumbs in the kefta: it is not the Moroccan tradition.","mistakes.1":"Using meat that is too lean: you need a little fat for texture and flavour.","mistakes.2":"Over-kneading or packing the meat: work it just enough to mix.","mistakes.3":"Making big meatballs: traditionally they are small and cook faster.","mistakes.4":"Letting the garlic burn at the start of the sauce.","mistakes.5":"Adding the meatballs before the sauce has thickened.","mistakes.6":"Cooking the meatballs for too long: they turn hard.","mistakes.7":"Packing the meatballs tightly together: they take up less of the sauce.","mistakes.8":"Breaking the yolks when cracking the eggs.","mistakes.9":"Forgetting the lid or leaving the eggs too long: the yolks are no longer runny.","mistakes.10":"Putting an earthenware tagine on an electric or ceramic hob without a diffuser.","variants.0":"The Fez version without tomato (Zette Guinaudeau-Franc, Traditional Moroccan Cooking: Recipes from Fez): kefta meatballs that have already been poached, cooked in butter and water with salt and soudania chilli until the water has almost evaporated, then eggs cracked over them just before serving. No tomato in the visible part of the recipe (ckbk preview).","variants.1":"Pitted green olives added at the end of cooking, just to warm them through; in that case, use less salt in the sauce (Taste of Maroc).","variants.2":"Chopped green pepper with the onion and a bay leaf in the sauce (Taste of Maroc).","variants.3":"Chopped fresh mint in the kefta (My Moroccan Food; optional for Taste of Maroc, which recommends it with lamb).","variants.4":"A hint of turmeric or cinnamon in the kefta (both optional for Taste of Maroc; cinnamon for Cooking With Alia, episode 372).","variants.5":"Garlic in the kefta (Cooking With Alia, MarocMama, Amour de cuisine).","variants.6":"Seasoned kefta left to rest, covered, for 30 minutes before shaping (Cooking With Alia, episode 372).","variants.7":"Sauce with turmeric and hot “soudaniya” paprika (MarocMama).","variants.8":"Tomato purée diluted in water added to the sauce, meatballs simmered for 40 minutes (Cooking With Alia, episode 023).","variants.9":"Meatballs browned in a frying pan before going into the sauce, for more flavour and so they hold together better (MarocMama; Amour de cuisine when she has time).","variants.10":"Well-cooked yolks (MarocMama), beaten eggs poured over the sauce, or no eggs at all: they remain optional (MarocMama, Taste of Maroc).","variants.11":"With vegetables: peas (MarocMama, Amour de cuisine), diced aubergine and harissa (My Moroccan Food), courgette or pepper (Amour de cuisine).","variants.12":"More sauce: increase the tomato sauce by half (Taste of Maroc).","variants.13":"Other halal meats: minced chicken or turkey (Taste of Maroc, MarocMama), with ginger for chicken (Cooking With Alia).","variants.14":"Tinned tomatoes at a pinch (whole peeled for My Moroccan Food, chopped for Amour de cuisine)."},"tajine-poisson":{"title":"Fish tagine with chermoula","darija":"Tajine l'hout bel chermoula","tagline":"Fish marinated in a lemony chermoula, cooked gently on a bed of carrots, potatoes and tomatoes, with peppers, olives and preserved lemon.","region":"All over Morocco; in the north (Tangier, Tetouan), fish is also cooked in a tagra","occasion":"A dish for sharing, on its own or with salads","img_alt":"Fish tagine with potatoes and green olives, in tomato sauce","serving":"Piping hot or warm (Bladi.net), straight from the tagine placed in the centre of the table: everyone eats from their own side of the dish with Moroccan bread, as a one-dish meal or with salads (Taste of Maroc). Cooking with Alia serves it with crusty bread; My Moroccan Food serves its sardine tagine with bread or couscous.","ing.0.group":"Fish","ing.poisson.name":"firm-fleshed fish (sea bass, sea bream, pandora, meagre or conger eel)","ing.poisson.note":"scaled, gutted and cut into thick steaks; or a whole fish","ing.1.group":"Chermoula","ing.coriandre.name":"fresh coriander","ing.coriandre.note":"the leaves for the chermoula, the stalks for the base of the tagine","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.ail.note":"peeled","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika (ground sweet pepper)","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.piment.name":"ground hot chilli (cayenne), optional","ing.jus-citron.name":"fresh lemon juice","ing.huile-chermoula.name":"olive oil","ing.2.group":"Vegetable bed and toppings","ing.huile-fond.name":"olive oil, for the base","ing.celeri.name":"celery sticks or small wooden skewers","ing.celeri.note":"optional: only if the coriander stalks are not enough to cover the base","ing.carottes.name":"carrot","ing.carottes.pl":"carrots","ing.carottes.note":"peeled and cut into 0.5 cm sticks","ing.pommes-de-terre.name":"large potato","ing.pommes-de-terre.pl":"large potatoes","ing.pommes-de-terre.note":"about 200 g each; peeled and cut into 0.5 cm slices","ing.tomates.name":"large ripe tomato","ing.tomates.pl":"large ripe tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"sliced into rounds","ing.poivrons.name":"pepper (green, red or yellow)","ing.poivrons.pl":"peppers (green, red or yellow)","ing.poivrons.note":"deseeded and cut into thin strips; a red pepper makes the dish milder","ing.poivre.name":"ground black pepper, to taste","ing.piment-vert.name":"hot green chilli","ing.piment-vert.pl":"hot green chillies","ing.piment-vert.note":"optional; left whole","ing.citron-rondelles.name":"a few slices of fresh lemon","ing.olives.name":"green or red olives","ing.citron-confit.name":"preserved lemon","ing.citron-confit.pl":"preserved lemons","ing.citron-confit.note":"cut into quarters, seeds removed","ing.eau.name":"water","equipment.0":"Large earthenware tagine with its lid, or a deep sauté pan with a lid","equipment.1":"Heat diffuser (for an earthenware tagine, except on gas)","equipment.2":"Pestle and mortar, or a chopper","equipment.3":"Deep dish with a lid or cling film, for marinating","equipment.4":"Small bowl","equipment.5":"Chopping board and knife kept for raw fish","equipment.6":"Kitchen paper","equipment.7":"Small saucepan (to reduce the sauce if needed)","steps.0.title":"Choose very fresh fish","steps.0.text":"Get {{poisson:full}}. It should smell fresh and mild, never strong, sour or of ammonia. When whole, it has clear, shiny eyes, red gills and firm flesh that springs back when you press it. Ask the fishmonger to scale it, gut it and cut it into thick steaks.","steps.0.tip":"Keep it in the coldest part of the fridge (4 °C) and cook it within 2 days; otherwise, freeze it. If it is frozen, thaw it overnight in the fridge (FDA; ANSES).","steps.1.title":"Rinse and dry the fish","steps.1.text":"Rinse the steaks in cold water, drain them, then pat them dry with kitchen paper. Then wash your hands with soap for 20 seconds, and wash the board, knife and worktop in hot soapy water.","steps.2.title":"Pound the chermoula","steps.2.text":"Wash {{coriandre:full}} and pick off the leaves: finely chop the leaves and keep the stalks. In the mortar, pound {{ail:full}} with {{sel}} of salt, then add the chopped coriander and pound again. Mix in {{paprika}} of sweet paprika, {{cumin}} of cumin, {{piment}} of hot chilli if you like it, {{jus-citron}} of lemon juice and {{huile-chermoula}} of olive oil. If the chermoula is too thick, loosen it with a little water.","steps.2.tip":"Chop the coriander well before pounding it, otherwise the chermoula turns stringy. Without a mortar, a chopper or blender will do.","steps.3.title":"Set half the chermoula aside","steps.3.text":"Before touching the fish, put half the chermoula in a small bowl: it is for the vegetables. Cover it and keep it in the fridge.","steps.4.title":"Marinate the fish","steps.4.text":"Coat each steak well with the other half of the chermoula. Cover and leave to marinate in the fridge for at least 30 min, and up to 2 h if you have time.","steps.4.tip":"Don't leave raw fish to marinate outside the fridge.","steps.4.timer.label":"Marinating the fish","steps.4.timer.done":"The fish has marinated for 30 min: you can assemble the tagine, or leave the fish in the fridge for up to 2 h.","steps.5.title":"Cut the vegetables","steps.5.text":"While the fish marinates, wash the vegetables and the fresh lemon in drinking water. Peel {{carottes:full}}, then cut into 0.5 cm sticks. Peel {{pommes-de-terre:full}}, then cut into 0.5 cm slices, no thicker. Slice {{tomates:full}} into rounds and cut {{poivrons:full}} into thin strips, without the seeds. Cut {{citron-confit:full}} into quarters, removing the seeds.","steps.5.tip":"Slices that are too thick cook too slowly: the fish would dry out before they were tender.","steps.6.title":"Line the base of the tagine","steps.6.text":"Pour {{huile-fond}} of olive oil into the base of the tagine. Lay the coriander stalks on top in a criss-cross pattern; if there aren't enough, add a few {{celeri}}. This bed stops everything else from touching the base, sticking and burning.","steps.7.title":"Build the bed of carrots and potatoes","steps.7.text":"Spread the carrot sticks over the stalks, then cover them with the potato slices. Sprinkle with {{poivre}}, but don't add salt: the preserved lemon and olives will make the dish salty enough.","steps.8.title":"Add the tomatoes and the reserved chermoula","steps.8.text":"Cover the potatoes with the tomato slices, then spread the chermoula you set aside over them.","steps.9.title":"Add the fish and the toppings","steps.9.text":"Arrange the steaks and their marinade on the tomatoes. Cover them with the pepper strips, then add {{piment-vert:full}} if you like and {{citron-rondelles}}. Scatter {{olives}} of olives and the preserved lemon quarters all around.","steps.10.title":"Add the water and put it on the heat","steps.10.text":"Pour {{eau}} of water into the tagine and cover. Put it over medium-low heat and let it slowly come to a simmer. If you are not cooking on gas, always put a diffuser under an earthenware tagine.","steps.11.title":"Cook covered","steps.11.text":"Cook covered for 30 min, then check: the potatoes should be tender and the fish should flake easily with a fork. If not, carry on over low heat and check often. In an earthenware tagine, it takes longer: Taste of Maroc allows about 1 h of simmering.","steps.11.tip":"With a thermometer, the centre of the fish should reach 70 °C (Health Canada).","steps.11.timer.label":"Cooking the tagine","steps.11.timer.done":"Potatoes tender and fish flaking easily with a fork? If not, cook a little longer over low heat and check often.","steps.12.title":"Adjust the sauce","steps.12.text":"The sauce should be reduced and thick. If there is too much, carefully pour it into a small saucepan, reduce it, then pour it back over the fish. Taste and add salt only if needed.","steps.13.title":"Serve in the tagine","steps.13.text":"Serve piping hot or warm, straight from the tagine placed in the centre of the table. Everyone eats from their own side, with Moroccan bread.","steps.14.title":"Put leftovers away quickly","steps.14.text":"Don't leave the tagine at room temperature for more than 2 h, or more than 1 h if it is above 32 °C. Put the leftovers in a closed container in the fridge and eat them within 3 days. Reheat them covered until steam escapes and they are piping hot right through (74 °C).","secrets.0.text":"Protect the base: Choumicha lines the tagine with coriander stalks or wooden skewers, Bladi lays coriander sprigs in a grid, Lalla Moulati a little parsley and some vegetables, and Taste of Maroc celery sticks or small skewers. Nothing touches the base, nothing sticks.","secrets.0.source":"Choumicha; Bladi.net; Lalla Moulati; Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.1.text":"The carrots form the first layer of the bed, under the potatoes; Cooking with Alia and Lalla Moulati put them straight on the base of the tagine.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih); Cooking with Alia; Lalla Moulati","secrets.2.text":"Half and half: one half of the chermoula marinates the fish, the other flavours the vegetables. Set it aside before touching the raw fish.","secrets.2.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih); Choumicha; My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); Lalla Moulati","secrets.3.text":"The best chermoula is made in a mortar: first pound the garlic with the salt, then add the finely chopped herbs and the spices.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); Bladi.net","secrets.4.text":"For fish, a lemony chermoula is the favourite. Choumicha, for her part, uses vinegar.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); Choumicha","secrets.5.text":"Slice the potatoes thinly (0.5 cm): if they are too thick, they need longer cooking and the fish dries out before they are tender.","secrets.5.source":"My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou); Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.6.text":"In Morocco, people use a firm, thick fish: meagre, sea bass, sea bream or pandora, conger eel. Whole sardines or whiting also work. Conger eel is oily but has few bones, and it releases water as it cooks: with it, you can leave out the water.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih); Bladi.net; Choumicha","secrets.7.text":"With thin fillets or small fish, first half-cook the vegetables, then put the fish on top to finish cooking.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih, reply to a reader); My Moroccan Food (quick version)","secrets.8.text":"For a short cooking time, Choumicha uses small potatoes that have already been boiled; Akhbarona blanches them lightly before putting them under the fish.","secrets.8.source":"Choumicha; Akhbarona","secrets.9.text":"Preserved lemon is very salty: if it cooks from the start, don't salt the vegetables. Taste of Maroc often prefers to add it at the end, for a few minutes, just long enough to heat it through.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.10.text":"Too much sauce at the end? Pour it into a small saucepan, reduce it, then put it back: the sauce should be thick.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih); Bladi.net","secrets.11.text":"With an earthenware tagine on anything other than gas, a diffuser is essential, and cooking takes longer than in a sauté pan.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.12.text":"Don't overcook the fish: its flesh should stay juicy. Adjust the time to its weight.","secrets.12.source":"Choumicha","secrets.13.text":"With a whole fish, also spread chermoula inside it and score the back so that it soaks in.","secrets.13.source":"Bladi.net; Choumicha; Akhbarona","mistakes.0":"Cooking fish that smells strong, sour or of ammonia: it is no longer fresh, so don't eat it (FDA).","mistakes.1":"Marinating raw fish outside the fridge (Taste of Maroc; Bladi.net; My Moroccan Food; MarocMama).","mistakes.2":"Touching the raw fish before setting aside the chermoula for the vegetables (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.3":"Cutting the potatoes too thick: the fish dries out before they are cooked (My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.4":"Forgetting the bed of stalks or carrots on the base: the vegetables stick and burn (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha; Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.5":"Salting the vegetables when the preserved lemon is cooking with them (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.6":"Putting an earthenware tagine on an electric or ceramic hob without a diffuser (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.7":"Overcooking the fish: it becomes dry (Choumicha).","mistakes.8":"Leaving the dish at room temperature for more than 2 h before chilling it, or for more than 1 h if it is above 32 °C (FDA; ANSES).","variants.0":"Northern Morocco: in Tangier and Tetouan, many fish dishes are cooked in a tagra, an oval dish of porous, unglazed earthenware made by potters near the oued Laou, over embers or in the baker's oven; it lets moisture evaporate slowly and gives the fish an earthy flavour; Paula Wolfert layers potatoes, carrots, tomatoes, red pepper and sardines in it, puts the tagra in the oven, then turns the oven on to 180 °C and cooks it for 1 h; when it comes out, she sets it on wood or a folded tea towel to stop it cracking, and leaves it to rest for 30 min. Most tagras have no lid: cover it loosely with foil (Paula Wolfert; Taste of Maroc).","variants.1":"In the oven: a whole fish on a bed of thinly sliced potatoes, tomatoes, roasted peppers and chillies, about 40 min in a moderately hot oven, basting halfway through (Bladi.net); Choumicha bakes a 700 g fish for 25 min at 170 °C, then leaves it to rest for 15 to 20 min in the switched-off oven; My Moroccan Food bakes its sardines for 30 to 35 min at 200 °C under foil.","variants.2":"A whole fish stuffed with chermoula, laid on sliced potatoes, carrots and tomatoes, and covered with peppers: the traditional version described by Nargisse Benkabbou (My Moroccan Food).","variants.3":"Saffron fish tagine: sea bass steaks marinated for 1 h in a chermoula with saffron, smen (aged salted butter) and white pepper, laid on coriander sprigs with green olives and preserved lemon, without vegetables, 20 to 25 min over charcoal (Bladi.net).","variants.4":"Pandora with m'slalla olives: onions and tomatoes on the base, bay leaf, green m'slalla olives and preserved lemon peel, chermoula made with vinegar, 45 min in the oven at 180 °C or over charcoal (Choumicha).","variants.5":"Swordfish tagine: cubes of swordfish, carrots and cabbage cut into julienne strips, small potatoes already cooked, cherry tomatoes and olives, 20 to 25 min over medium heat (Choumicha).","variants.6":"Conger eel steaks dusted with flour and browned, then simmered with onions, red pepper, tomatoes, thyme and bay leaf (Choumicha).","variants.7":"With onions and raisins: pieces of conger eel with plenty of sliced onions, raisins, saffron, ginger, turmeric and cinnamon, 40 min over medium heat (Cooking with Alia).","variants.8":"Sardine meatball (kefta) tagine in tomato sauce, finished with preserved lemon and green olives (Cooking with Alia); Taste of Maroc says it is popular all over Morocco, especially on the coast, where fish is plentiful.","variants.9":"A bed of sliced onions on the base, which caramelise during cooking (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha for the pandora).","variants.10":"Extra spices: ginger and turmeric on the vegetables, saffron in the chermoula (Taste of Maroc); harissa instead of chilli (Choumicha, Lalla Moulati, My Moroccan Food); a bay leaf (Cooking with Alia; Choumicha); a little tomato purée (Cooking with Alia) or tomato sauce (Lalla Moulati) in the chermoula.","variants.11":"Preserved lemon chermoula, cooked for 5 min over low heat with herbs, garlic, sweet paprika, cumin, vinegar and oil; it keeps for a week, chilled, in a sealed jar (Choumicha)."},"tajine-poulet-citron-olives":{"title":"Chicken tagine with preserved lemons and olives","darija":"Djaj mqualli bel hamd marqad w zitoun","tagline":"Melt-in-the-mouth chicken with saffron and ginger, coated in a sauce of meltingly soft onions and lifted with preserved lemon and olives.","region":"All over Morocco (a national dish), m'qalli style of cooking","occasion":"Family meals, guests and celebrations","serving":"Piping hot, on a platter or straight from the tagine placed in the centre of the table: the sauce underneath, the chicken on top, coated with onion sauce and garnished with olives and strips of preserved lemon. In Morocco, tagines are traditionally eaten by hand, with pieces of Moroccan bread to scoop up the chicken and sauce; it can also be served with rice or couscous, and some people serve it with fries on top or around it.","ing.0.group":"Chicken and marinade","ing.poulet.name":"halal chicken, free-range (beldi) if possible","ing.poulet.pl":"halal chickens, free-range (beldi) if possible","ing.poulet.note":"about 1.5 kg each, to be cut into 4 to 6 pieces","ing.safran.name":"saffron threads","ing.safran.note":"a generous pinch; Choumicha uses 8 threads","ing.eau-safran.name":"hot water, for infusing the saffron","ing.sel.name":"fine salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust at the end: the preserved lemon and olives are already salty","ing.gingembre.name":"ground ginger","ing.gingembre.note":"heaped","ing.curcuma.name":"turmeric","ing.poivre.name":"ground black or white pepper, to taste","ing.smen.name":"smen (aged salted butter)","ing.smen.note":"or butter; optional","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove, crushed or grated","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves, crushed or grated","ing.jus-citron.name":"fresh lemon juice","ing.herbes.name":"chopped fresh coriander and parsley","ing.1.group":"Cooking","ing.oignons.name":"medium onion (brown, white or red)","ing.oignons.pl":"medium onions (brown, white or red)","ing.oignons.note":"finely chopped","ing.eau-depart.name":"water, for the start of cooking","ing.huile.name":"oil: half olive oil, half neutral oil (sunflower)","ing.eau-chaude.name":"hot water","ing.eau-chaude.note":"the liquid should come about two thirds of the way up the chicken","ing.2.group":"Preserved lemon and olives","ing.citron-confit.name":"preserved lemon","ing.citron-confit.pl":"preserved lemons","ing.citron-confit.note":"peel only, in thin strips","ing.olives.name":"good-quality green or purple olives","ing.olives.note":"pitted or not","equipment.0":"Heavy-based pot with a lid, or an earthenware tagine","equipment.1":"Heat diffuser (for an earthenware tagine, except on gas)","equipment.2":"Large bowl","equipment.3":"Small bowl","equipment.4":"Small saucepan","equipment.5":"Wooden spatula","equipment.6":"Aluminium foil","steps.0.title":"Infuse the saffron","steps.0.text":"Put {{safran}} of saffron in a small bowl with {{eau-safran}} of hot water. Leave to infuse for 10 min.","steps.0.timer.label":"Infusing the saffron","steps.0.timer.done":"The saffron has infused: make the marinade.","steps.1.title":"Make the marinade","steps.1.text":"Pour the saffron water into a large bowl and add {{sel}} of salt, {{gingembre}} of ginger, {{curcuma}} of turmeric, the pepper and {{smen}} of smen. Add {{ail:full}}, {{jus-citron}} of lemon juice and {{herbes}} of chopped coriander and parsley, then mix well.","steps.2.title":"Marinate the chicken","steps.2.text":"Cut the chicken into 4 to 6 pieces. Coat each piece with the marinade, rubbing it in well, right under the skin. Cover and leave in the fridge for at least 1 h, ideally 2 h or overnight.","steps.2.timer.label":"Marinating the chicken","steps.2.timer.done":"The chicken has marinated: move on to cooking.","steps.3.title":"Prepare the onions, preserved lemon and olives","steps.3.text":"While the chicken marinates, finely chop {{oignons:full}}. Remove the pulp from {{citron-confit:full}} and cut the peel into thin strips. Rinse {{olives}} of olives; if they come from a tin, they are saltier: blanch them for 10 min in boiling water, then drain them.","steps.3.timer.label":"Blanching tinned olives","steps.3.timer.done":"Drain the olives and set them aside.","steps.4.title":"Start from raw, without oil","steps.4.text":"Put the chicken and all its marinade in a heavy-based pot with the onions and {{eau-depart}} of water. Leave to simmer for 10 to 15 min over medium heat, turning the pieces from time to time. It is ready when the water has almost evaporated and the chicken has changed colour on all sides. With an earthenware tagine, put it on a diffuser if you don't have gas, and don't go above medium heat.","steps.4.timer.label":"Raw start","steps.4.timer.done":"Has the water almost gone and has the chicken changed colour all over? Move on to the next step.","steps.5.title":"Add the oil and hot water","steps.5.text":"Add {{huile}} of oil and {{eau-chaude}} of hot water: the liquid should come about two thirds of the way up the chicken; top up a little if needed. In an earthenware tagine, use less water than in a pot.","steps.6.title":"Simmer covered","steps.6.text":"Cover and leave to simmer over low to medium-low heat for about 50 min, checking often that nothing is sticking to the bottom. The chicken is ready when it feels tender and the liquid has reduced well. A beldi chicken, like cooking in an earthenware tagine, takes considerably longer.","steps.6.timer.label":"Simmering the chicken","steps.6.timer.done":"Does the chicken feel tender? If not, keep cooking and check regularly.","steps.7.title":"Take out the chicken","steps.7.text":"Carefully lift out the chicken pieces and keep them warm under a sheet of foil, without throwing away the juices that run out. With an earthenware tagine, leave the chicken where it is instead, and pour the sauce into a small saucepan to reduce it separately.","steps.8.title":"Reduce the onion sauce","steps.8.text":"Reduce the sauce over medium heat for about 20 min, stirring often and crushing the onions with the spatula. It is ready when you can no longer make out the onions: the sauce is thick and silky and is starting to separate from the oil. Keep a little liquid and, if it sticks, add a little water.","steps.8.timer.label":"Reducing the sauce","steps.8.timer.done":"Have the onions melted and has the sauce separated from the oil? If not, carry on for a few minutes, stirring.","steps.9.title":"Add the olives and preserved lemon","steps.9.text":"Add the olives and preserved lemon strips to the sauce and cook for 10 min over medium-low heat. Only now, taste and adjust the salt. If the sauce becomes too lemony, take out the strips and keep them for garnishing.","steps.9.timer.label":"Olives and preserved lemon","steps.9.timer.done":"Taste the sauce and adjust the salt.","steps.10.title":"Brown the chicken in the sauce","steps.10.text":"Put the chicken pieces back in the pot over medium heat. Turn them several times in the oil that has separated from the sauce, until they are piping hot and golden. With a tagine, simply pour the reduced sauce back around the chicken.","steps.11.title":"Arrange and serve piping hot","steps.11.text":"Pour the sauce into the serving dish, place the chicken on top and coat it with the onion sauce. Garnish with the olives and preserved lemon strips, then serve piping hot with bread. With a tagine, put it straight in the centre of the table.","secrets.0.text":"M'qalli is built on two key spices, saffron and ginger, and is recognisable by its yellow sauce.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa), Cooking Styles and Sauces in Moroccan Cuisine","secrets.1.text":"First infuse the saffron for about ten minutes in a little hot water, then mix in salt, turmeric, ginger, pepper and smen: this is the base of the marinade.","secrets.1.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa)","secrets.2.text":"Leave to marinate for at least 1 h, ideally 2 h or overnight in the fridge, working the marinade right under the skin.","secrets.2.source":"Choumicha; Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa)","secrets.3.text":"A “raw” start: the chicken first cooks for 10 to 15 min in its marinade with the onions and a little water, over medium heat. The oil and hot water only come afterwards, once the water has almost evaporated and the meat has changed colour.","secrets.3.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa)","secrets.4.text":"Don't cut down on the oil: with less oil, the onion sauce may not reach its thick texture. Use a mixture of olive oil and neutral oil.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); Choumicha","secrets.5.text":"For the onion sauce (daghmira), reduce it patiently, stirring and crushing the onions with the spatula, until you can no longer make them out and the silky sauce separates from the oil.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa; Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.6.text":"Take the chicken out and keep it covered while the sauce reduces, and only put it back at the end. Still keep a little liquid: the sauce must not end up dry.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa); My Moroccan Food (Nargisse Benkabbou)","secrets.7.text":"The final step gives the dish its name: the cooked chicken goes back into the oil that has separated from the sauce and is browned over medium heat; it stays moist inside.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa)","secrets.8.text":"Use preserved lemon, never fresh lemon in its place: it is the only way to get the traditional flavour. If the sauce becomes too lemony, take out the pieces of preserved lemon as soon as you like the taste and keep them for garnishing.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","secrets.9.text":"The olives go in at the very end, or only as a garnish. If they come from a tin, blanch them for 10 min in boiling water, as they are saltier.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa)","secrets.10.text":"Go easy on the salt before adding the preserved lemon and olives, which make the sauce much saltier, then adjust at the end.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih; Nada Kiffa)","secrets.11.text":"The garlic should stay in the background: 1 to 2 cloves per chicken are enough.","secrets.11.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa, reply to a comment)","secrets.12.text":"In Moroccan families, the chicken is cleaned (the clumps of fat under the skin are removed), then left for at least 30 min in a brine of cold water, salt, lemon and a little white vinegar, before being rinsed and marinated. Bladi simply salts it and leaves it for 2 h to draw out its juices. Afterwards, carefully clean everything that has touched the raw chicken. Health authorities, however, advise against rinsing raw poultry, because splashes spread bacteria: if you keep this habit, do it without splashing and clean the sink and worktop straight afterwards.","secrets.12.source":"Taste of Maroc (Nada Kiffa), Cleaning and Brining Chicken the Moroccan Way; Bladi.net","secrets.13.text":"A free-range (beldi) chicken needs longer cooking than a farmed chicken: go by how tender it feels, not just by the timer.","secrets.13.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih; Nada Kiffa)","secrets.14.text":"In an earthenware tagine: a diffuser is essential if you are not cooking on gas; medium heat until it simmers (this can take 15 min or more), then medium-low heat. It needs less water than a pot and considerably more time.","secrets.14.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih; Nada Kiffa)","secrets.15.text":"With a tagine, if the sauce is still too runny at the end of cooking, pour it into a small saucepan to reduce it separately, then put it back in the tagine.","secrets.15.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih)","mistakes.0":"Salting as usual from the start: the preserved lemon and olives already make the sauce very salty.","mistakes.1":"Cutting down the oil to make it lighter: the onion sauce does not thicken or turn silky.","mistakes.2":"Letting the bottom stick: keep an eye on the pot while it simmers and reduces, stir and add a little water if it catches.","mistakes.3":"Reducing the sauce until it is completely dry: a little liquid should remain.","mistakes.4":"Replacing the preserved lemon with fresh lemon: you lose the traditional flavour.","mistakes.5":"Using tinned olives without blanching them: the sauce becomes too salty.","mistakes.6":"Overdoing the garlic: it should stay in the background.","mistakes.7":"Putting an earthenware tagine directly on an electric or ceramic hob, without a diffuser.","mistakes.8":"Taking a beldi chicken off the heat too soon: it needs longer cooking than a farmed chicken.","variants.0":"Thicker daghmira (the festive version): cook the chicken with just one onion, take it out once cooked, then melt the other two chopped onions in the sauce until you get a silky paste that separates from the oil (Taste of Maroc).","variants.1":"With chicken livers: blanch the livers for 10 to 15 min, then cook them in the sauce while it reduces; leave them whole or mash them into the sauce for a richer texture (Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food).","variants.2":"More lemony: add the finely chopped pulp of half a preserved lemon with the onions (Taste of Maroc); Choumicha puts half a preserved lemon, finely diced, in the marinade. Nargisse Benkabbou's mother finds that the pulp makes the dish too sour.","variants.3":"Whole chicken (Choumicha, “Poulet M'Qalli aux olives rouges”): a whole free-range chicken rubbed, right under the skin, with a marinade of onion, garlic, coriander, parsley, preserved lemon, lemon juice, smen, spices and oil, then trussed and cooked covered over low heat; the red olives go into the sauce while it reduces.","variants.4":"Choumicha's “Poulet au safran, coriandre et citron confit” version: the sliced onion is sautéed for 5 min in the oil over low heat before the chicken goes in, the marinade contains ground coriander, and pitted cracked green olives (m'slalla) cook with the chicken until the end.","variants.5":"Browned in the oven: brush the cooked chicken with a little oil from the sauce and brown it for about 20 min at 200 °C, turning it; a whole chicken may need up to 30 min (Taste of Maroc).","variants.6":"Roast (Christine Benlafquih, Taste of Maroc): a whole chicken marinated overnight, then roasted at 160 °C for 2 h 30 to 3 h; the sliced-onion sauce cooks separately for 1 to 2 h, then the cooking juices, preserved lemon and olives are added to it for 5 to 10 min.","variants.7":"Version with two whole beldi chickens (Bladi.net): sliced onions, garlic, saffron, ginger, lemon juice and a glass of water, covered, over medium heat; the preserved lemon peel and green olives finish with 5 min over low heat.","variants.8":"English-language adaptations of Paula Wolfert's recipe (Sunset, Zarela): grated onion, paprika and cumin in the marinade, chicken stock, shorter cooking or oven cooking; not used here because they are absent from the Moroccan sources consulted."},"the-menthe":{"title":"Moroccan mint tea","darija":"Atay b nanaa","tagline":"The green mint tea of Moroccan families: you keep the first infusion, rinse the tea, then pour it from a height for the foam.","region":"All over Morocco (the national drink); the herbs change with the seasons.","occasion":"Every day, at any time: it is the drink of hospitality, the first thing you make to welcome a guest.","img_alt":"Moroccan teapots and a glass of mint tea on a tray","serving":"Serve very hot, in small glasses rather than cups, set on a tray (sinia) around the teapot (berrad); have more glasses than guests. Traditionally, it is the master of the house who makes the tea in front of his guests. The leaves stay in the teapot: at least three glasses are served, each stronger than the last; a saying goes that the first is gentle as life, the second strong as love, the last bitter as death; there is another version, in which the first is bitter as life and the last soothing as death. For more aroma, slip a little fresh mint into each glass. With gazelle horns, ghriba or almond briouats, or simply bread and olives.","pairs_label":"With the tea","ing.0.group":"Mint tea","ing.the.name":"Chinese gunpowder green tea","ing.the.note":"heaped; tea rolled into small pellets. If you can't find it, chunmee","ing.menthe.name":"fresh mint (nanaa)","ing.menthe.note":"a large bunch of spearmint, stalks included","ing.sucre.name":"sugar","ing.sucre.note":"ideally pieces broken off a sugar loaf; adjust after tasting","ing.1.group":"Boiling water","ing.eau-premier-jet.name":"boiling water, for the first infusion","ing.eau-rincage.name":"boiling water, for rinsing the tea","ing.eau.name":"boiling water, for filling the teapot","ing.eau.note":"depending on your teapot: about three quarters full, never to the brim","equipment.0":"Moroccan teapot (berrad) that can go on the hob, or a stovetop kettle","equipment.1":"Kettle for boiling the water","equipment.2":"Small tea glasses, plus one glass to hold the first infusion","equipment.3":"Tray (sinia)","equipment.4":"Cotton tea towel to hold the hot handle","equipment.5":"Small strainer, if you make the tea in a kettle","equipment.6":"Small hammer to break the sugar loaf (optional)","steps.0.title":"Boil the water and wash the mint","steps.0.text":"Boil all the water in the list in a kettle. Meanwhile, gently wash {{menthe}} of mint and drain it.","steps.1.title":"The first infusion: keep the spirit of the tea","steps.1.text":"Put {{the}} of green tea in the teapot and pour {{eau-premier-jet}} of boiling water over it. Leave to infuse for 1 minute, then pour this first infusion into a glass and set it aside: it is the spirit of the tea, so don't throw it away.","steps.1.timer.label":"First infusion","steps.1.timer.done":"Pour the first infusion into a glass and keep it.","steps.2.title":"Rinse the tea and throw away this water","steps.2.text":"Pour {{eau-rincage}} of boiling water over the leaves and swirl the teapot two or three times. Pour this darker water away through the spout: it takes away the bitterness and the dust, and the leaves stay in the teapot.","steps.3.title":"Add the mint, sugar and first infusion","steps.3.text":"Pack the mint into the teapot, stalks included, and add {{sucre}} of sugar. Pour the first infusion back in, then top up with {{eau}} of boiling water, without filling to the brim. The mint must be completely under the water.","steps.4.title":"Bring gently to the boil","steps.4.text":"Put the teapot over low to medium heat and let it slowly come to the boil: steam escapes from the teapot. Take it off the heat straight away, holding the handle with a tea towel, as it is scalding hot.","steps.5.title":"Mix by pouring out and pouring back","steps.5.text":"Fill a glass with tea and pour it back into the teapot. Repeat 2 or 3 times: the sugar dissolves and the flavours blend.","steps.6.title":"Taste and adjust the sugar","steps.6.text":"Pour a little into the bottom of a glass and taste. If it needs more sugar, add some, then pour a glass and tip it back into the teapot once more.","steps.7.title":"Pour from a height for the foam","steps.7.text":"Pour the tea into the small glasses, raising the teapot high in a steady movement: the higher you pour from, the more it foams. This foam should take up about a quarter of the glass. Serve very hot.","steps.7.img.alt":"Man pouring tea into glasses from a height","steps.7.img.caption":"Pouring from a height","secrets.0.text":"Keep the first infusion: this first water, poured over the tea and left for 1 minute, is “the spirit of the tea”, the most fragrant part. Set it aside in a glass; it goes back into the teapot later.","secrets.0.source":"Cooking with Alia (“spirit of the tea”, the name used in Morocco according to her); My Moroccan Food (“essence”, “don't throw it away”); Royal Mansour (“premier essentiel retenu”: the first essential, kept back)","secrets.1.text":"It is the second water, the rinsing water, that you throw away: it washes the leaves, removes some of the bitterness and the dust, and comes out darker.","secrets.1.source":"Cooking with Alia, My Moroccan Food and Royal Mansour (second water thrown away, darker according to Alia); Al Jazeera (Hend Aklaw) and Moroccan Zest (rinsing removes bitterness, strong flavours and dust)","secrets.2.text":"Let the tea come slowly to the boil over low to medium heat: this is what gives the tea and mint time to infuse.","secrets.2.source":"My Moroccan Food; Cooking with Alia; Morocco World News","secrets.3.text":"Be generous with the mint and pack it in, folding the stalks if necessary, but keep it under the water: exposed to the air, it can make the tea bitter.","secrets.3.source":"Moroccan Zest","secrets.4.text":"Mix by pouring a glass of tea and tipping it back into the teapot several times, then taste and adjust the sugar before serving.","secrets.4.source":"Cooking with Alia (2 to 3 times); Al Jazeera (3 or 4 glasses; you taste, then add sugar)","secrets.5.text":"Pour from a height: the higher the teapot, the more foam there is. This foam takes up about a quarter of the glass; the Moroccan columnist Najib Refaif jokingly compares it to a “rozza” (a turban).","secrets.5.source":"La Vie Éco (Najib Refaif, for the image of the “rozza”); Moroccan Zest; Al Jazeera; Cooking with Alia","secrets.6.text":"The sugar comes from a sugar loaf, broken with a small hammer or with the bottom of a glass; connoisseurs prefer it to boxed sugar cubes.","secrets.6.source":"Maroc.ma (MAP news agency report); Morocco World News","secrets.7.text":"The tea is made in front of the guests, traditionally by the master of the house, with more glasses than guests on the tray.","secrets.7.source":"Royal Mansour; Al Jazeera; Moroccan Zest","secrets.8.text":"The base is a Chinese green tea rolled into small pellets: gunpowder.","secrets.8.source":"My Moroccan Food; Moroccan Zest; Morocco World News; Maroc.ma (“often of the Gunpowder type”)","mistakes.0":"Throwing away the first infusion: it is the spirit of the tea and must go back into the teapot.","mistakes.1":"Skipping the rinse: the tea stays bitter and keeps its dust.","mistakes.2":"Leaving mint stalks sticking out of the water: the tea can turn bitter.","mistakes.3":"Filling the teapot to the brim: leave about 2.5 cm of space.","mistakes.4":"Heating over high heat: the tea should come slowly to the boil.","mistakes.5":"Letting it boil for a long time: the longer the tea boils, the stronger and more caffeine-laden it becomes.","mistakes.6":"Serving in cups: Moroccan tea is drunk from small glasses.","mistakes.7":"Pouring from too low: it is the height that makes the foam.","variants.0":"Chiba (wormwood, the plant) in winter: from October to April, it replaces mint when it is cold and the mint is not as good; in cafés, it is served separately and everyone adds it to their own glass. A distinctly more bitter taste. Use only a little: wormwood contains thujone, which is considered neurotoxic; the European Medicines Agency restricts it to adults and advises against it during pregnancy and breastfeeding, and in cases of liver or bile duct disease or allergy to plants of the Asteraceae family — Hespress (Anadolu report); European Medicines Agency; also Al Jazeera, Moroccan Zest, Morocco World News, Wikipedia.","variants.1":"Louiza (lemon verbena), instead of the mint or with it — Al Jazeera; Morocco World News; verbena also in My Moroccan Food and Moroccan Zest.","variants.2":"Zaatar in winter, instead of the mint — Hespress (Anadolu report); Asharq also mentions “أتاي بالزعتر”.","variants.3":"Pennyroyal and wormwood when it is cold, said to be warming; for wormwood, the same precautions as for chiba — Moroccan Zest; European Medicines Agency. For pennyroyal during pregnancy, ask a health professional for advice: its oil was once used to induce abortion (European Medicines Agency, statement on pulegone).","variants.4":"Orange blossom: orange blossom water added off the heat — Moroccan Zest; Al Jazeera also mentions citrus blossoms (“زهر الليمون”) instead of the mint.","variants.5":"Geranium, wild thyme or sage instead of the mint — Morocco World News; geranium also in My Moroccan Food.","variants.6":"For celebrations (engagements, weddings): rosebuds, verbena and cinnamon — Moroccan Zest."},"zaalouk":{"title":"Aubergine zaalouk","darija":"Zaâlouk","tagline":"Smoky grilled aubergines, mashed into a tomato sauce with garlic, cumin and paprika, then cooked until everything thickens. Warm or cold, with bread.","region":"All over Morocco; the recipe changes from one region and one family to another (My Moroccan Food)","occasion":"An everyday cooked salad, served as a starter or alongside main dishes, with bread","serving":"Serve the zaalouk warm, cold or at room temperature (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha: warm or cold; Cooking With Alia: hot or cold, with bread). It is eaten as a starter (Morocco World News; El Fenn) or alongside main dishes, scooped up with khobz (Moroccan bread), which serves as cutlery (Taste of Maroc). Drizzle it with olive oil and scatter over coriander or parsley (Taste of Maroc, My Moroccan Food). Fatéma Hal serves it with lemon wedges and black olives.","ing.0.group":"Aubergines","ing.aubergines.name":"aubergines","ing.aubergines.note":"whole, with skin and stalk","ing.1.group":"Tomato sauce","ing.tomates.name":"very ripe tomatoes","ing.tomates.note":"soft and deep red","ing.huile.name":"olive oil","ing.ail.name":"garlic clove","ing.ail.pl":"garlic cloves","ing.paprika.name":"sweet paprika","ing.cumin.name":"ground cumin","ing.sel.name":"salt","ing.sel.note":"adjust after tasting","ing.coriandre.name":"chopped fresh coriander","ing.persil.name":"chopped flat-leaf parsley","ing.eau.name":"water","ing.eau.note":"only if the sauce sticks","ing.2.group":"To finish and serve","ing.citron-jus.name":"lemon","ing.citron-jus.pl":"lemons","ing.citron-jus.note":"for the juice","ing.citron-service.name":"lemon","ing.citron-service.pl":"lemons","ing.citron-service.note":"cut into wedges, to serve","ing.huile-service.name":"olive oil","ing.huile-service.note":"a drizzle, to serve","ing.herbes-service.name":"chopped coriander or parsley","ing.herbes-service.note":"to garnish","ing.khobz.name":"khobz (Moroccan bread)","ing.khobz.note":"for scooping up the zaalouk","equipment.0":"Gas hob or grill (otherwise, an oven with a grill function and a baking tray lined with foil)","equipment.1":"Clean plastic bag","equipment.2":"Large deep sauté pan","equipment.3":"Wooden spoon","equipment.4":"Large bowl and fork","equipment.5":"Serrated peeler or grater, for the tomatoes","equipment.6":"Garlic press, or pestle and mortar","steps.0.title":"Prepare the tomatoes, garlic and herbs","steps.0.text":"Peel {{tomates}} of tomatoes (a serrated peeler helps), deseed them and cut them into very small dice. Simpler: cut them in half, grate the flesh and throw away the skin. Peel and crush {{ail:full}} with a garlic press or a pestle. Wash and finely chop the coriander and parsley, and keep a little for serving.","steps.0.img.alt":"Parsley chopped with a knife on a board","steps.0.img.caption":"Chopping the herbs","steps.1.title":"Grill the aubergines","steps.1.text":"Put {{aubergines}} of whole aubergines, with skin and stalk, directly on the gas flame over medium heat, or on a grill. Turn them from time to time, until the skin is blackened all over and the flesh is very soft. Without gas: cut them in half lengthways, place them skin side up on a foil-lined baking tray and put them under the oven grill for 20 to 25 minutes, as close as possible to the heating element.","steps.1.timer.label":"Aubergines on the flame","steps.1.timer.done":"Skin blackened all over and flesh very soft? If not, leave them a few more minutes, turning them. Under the oven grill, allow 20 to 25 minutes in all.","steps.2.title":"Seal them in a bag","steps.2.text":"Slip the still-hot aubergines into a plastic bag, close it and leave them to cool slightly while you make the sauce. The charred skin will then come off very easily. Don't run them under water: they would lose their flavour and the skin would be harder to remove.","steps.3.title":"Cook the tomatoes down into a sauce","steps.3.text":"Put the tomatoes, garlic, {{huile}} of olive oil, {{paprika}} of paprika, {{cumin}} of cumin and {{sel}} of salt in a large deep sauté pan over medium heat. Add {{coriandre}} of chopped coriander and {{persil}} of chopped parsley. Cook, stirring often, until the tomatoes break down into a thick sauce. If it sticks, add a little {{eau}}.","steps.3.timer.label":"Tomato sauce","steps.3.timer.done":"The tomatoes should have broken down into a thick sauce. If not, cook for a few more minutes.","steps.4.title":"Peel and mash the aubergines","steps.4.text":"Take the aubergines out of the bag. Gently scrape off the charred skin and cut off the stalk; for halves done in the oven, simply scoop out the flesh with a spoon. Put the flesh in a large bowl and mash it with a fork until you have a paste.","steps.5.title":"Reduce the zaalouk","steps.5.text":"Tip the mashed aubergine into the sauce and mix well. Cook over medium heat, stirring regularly and scraping the bottom, and crush any pieces that are still firm with the back of the spoon. It is ready when the mixture is thick and there is no liquid left.","steps.5.timer.label":"Reducing","steps.5.timer.done":"The zaalouk should be thick, with no liquid at the bottom. If not, keep cooking and stirring; turn the heat down if it sticks.","steps.6.title":"Add the lemon","steps.6.text":"Squeeze {{citron-jus:full}} and pour the juice into the zaalouk. Mix and cook for another 2 minutes. Taste, adjust the salt, then take off the heat.","steps.6.timer.label":"Lemon","steps.6.timer.done":"Taste, adjust the salt and take off the heat.","steps.7.title":"Serve warm or cold","steps.7.text":"Spread the zaalouk in a shallow dish and serve it warm or cold. Drizzle with {{huile-service}} and scatter over {{herbes-service}}. Arrange the wedges of {{citron-service:full}} around it. Everyone scoops it up with pieces of {{khobz}}, which serve as cutlery.","secrets.0.text":"Zaalouk tastes of chermoula: cumin, paprika, garlic, coriander, parsley and olive oil. The more tomatoes you use, the more seasoning it needs.","secrets.0.source":"Taste of Maroc (Christine Benlafquih, who got this comparison from the Moroccan food writer Nada Kiffa); Choumicha seasons her zaalouk with a chermoula spice mix","secrets.1.text":"Grill the aubergines whole until the skin is blackened all over: this is what gives zaalouk its smoky flavour.","secrets.1.source":"Cooking With Alia (zaalouk no. 689); Taste of Maroc","secrets.2.text":"Seal the grilled aubergines in a plastic bag for a few moments: the charred skin then comes off very easily.","secrets.2.source":"Lalla Moulati, “الزعلوك بطريقة جديدة” (10 November 2014)","secrets.3.text":"Never run the grilled aubergines under water: they lose their flavour, and the skin is harder to remove.","secrets.3.source":"Lalla Moulati: “زعلوك بالدنجال مشوي” (13 August 2016) for the flavour, “الزعلوك بطريقة جديدة” (2014) for the skin","secrets.4.text":"Choose ripe, soft, deep red tomatoes: firm tomatoes don't break down as well into a sauce and can be more acidic.","secrets.4.source":"Taste of Maroc; Cooking With Alia, Ch'hiwatine's recipe (“super red” tomatoes)","secrets.5.text":"First cook the tomatoes down into a thick sauce with the oil, garlic, spices and herbs, and only then add the aubergine.","secrets.5.source":"Taste of Maroc; Lalla Moulati (2014); Cooking With Alia (no. 689); Choumicha","secrets.6.text":"Use a large deep sauté pan rather than a small saucepan: with more surface area, the liquid evaporates faster.","secrets.6.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.7.text":"Texture is a matter of taste, from chunky zaalouk to a purée: mash well with a fork for a paste, less to keep some chunks.","secrets.7.source":"Taste of Maroc; Lalla Moulati (2014, mashed with a fork)","secrets.8.text":"A little lemon juice at the end of cooking brightens the flavour. Fatéma Hal puts the juice of half a lemon in the pan and serves the other half in wedges.","secrets.8.source":"Taste of Maroc; Fatéma Hal (Gaggenau recipe card)","secrets.9.text":"Too acidic? A tiny touch of sugar or honey will correct it, but go easy: a slightly acidic zaalouk is better than one that is too sweet.","secrets.9.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.10.text":"The khobz serves as cutlery: like many Moroccan salads, zaalouk is eaten by scooping it up with pieces of bread.","secrets.10.source":"Taste of Maroc","secrets.11.text":"Put it in the fridge within 2 hours of cooking, in a closed container: it will then keep for 3 days. In the freezer, it keeps for 2 to 3 months. Thaw it in the fridge, reheat it in a frying pan to reduce any excess liquid, adjust the seasoning and don't refreeze it.","secrets.11.source":"Anses (home-cooked dishes: in the fridge within 2 hours, eat within 3 days, thaw in the fridge, do not refreeze); My Moroccan Food (up to 4 days); Taste of Maroc (2 to 3 months in the freezer, reheating in a frying pan)","mistakes.0":"Rinsing the grilled aubergines under water: they lose their flavour and the skin is harder to remove (Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.1":"Using firm or underripe tomatoes: they don't break down as well and can make the zaalouk acidic (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.2":"Stopping the cooking too soon: the zaalouk should be thick, with no liquid (Cooking With Alia, My Moroccan Food, Lalla Moulati).","mistakes.3":"Letting it stick: stir often, scrape the bottom and turn the heat down if needed (Taste of Maroc, My Moroccan Food).","mistakes.4":"Overdoing the lemon: along with underripe tomatoes, this is what makes a zaalouk too acidic (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.5":"Correcting the acidity with too much sugar: slightly acidic is better than too sweet (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.6":"Thinking that smoked paprika or roasted garlic are part of the traditional recipe: neither is traditional, even though Christine Benlafquih finds that smoked paprika can work very well in zaalouk and sometimes adds roasted garlic to it (Taste of Maroc).","mistakes.7":"Forgetting to taste at the end to adjust the salt (Taste of Maroc, My Moroccan Food).","variants.0":"The family all-in-the-pot method (Christine Benlafquih's mother-in-law, Nargisse Benkabbou's mother): aubergines peeled, keeping a few strips of skin for colour if you like, cut up and cooked with the tomatoes, oil, garlic, spices and herbs, plus a splash of water (¼ to ⅓ cup, or about 60 to 80 ml, for Taste of Maroc; no water for My Moroccan Food), covered, over medium or medium-low heat for 25 to 35 minutes, then uncovered, mashing until the liquid has evaporated (Taste of Maroc; My Moroccan Food).","variants.1":"Boiled aubergines (Cooking With Alia, episode 47): fully or half peeled, diced, cooked for 20 to 30 minutes in salted water, drained and pressed with a spoon to squeeze out all the water, then cooked for 30 minutes over medium heat with the tomatoes, oil, garlic and spices, mashing with a wooden spoon; 1 tsp of vinegar in the last 5 minutes.","variants.2":"Steamed aubergines: mentioned by Taste of Maroc among the possible methods, and by Fiona Dunlop (The North African Kitchen), who presents the steamed version of a cook, Aicha, as lighter. Detailed method not read.","variants.3":"Fried aubergines (Choumicha): 1 cm slices salted and left to drain, rinsed, squeezed and patted dry, fried, then roughly chopped; a sauce of peeled, deseeded tomatoes with 1 tsp of tomato purée, 10 cl of water, garlic and chermoula spices, 8 to 10 minutes covered over low heat; aubergines added for 3 minutes, then coriander. Fiona Dunlop presents the fried version as the traditional way.","variants.4":"Ch'hiwatine's version (on Cooking With Alia): aubergines partly peeled, sliced, salted and fried; tomatoes reduced on their own to a thick paste; half a grilled red pepper, cumin, salt and garlic; 10 to 15 minutes, stirring constantly.","variants.5":"In the oven (Fatéma Hal): aubergines roasted for about forty minutes on a tray in the oven, peeled, diced and mashed, then cooked for 30 minutes in a frying pan with all the other ingredients and the juice of half a lemon.","variants.6":"Vinegar instead of lemon: 1 tsp in the last 5 minutes (Cooking With Alia, episode 47), 1 tbsp of vinegar or lemon juice for 2 minutes (no. 689), or half a glass added off the heat (Lalla Moulati, 2016).","variants.7":"A little heat: cayenne or whole chillies to taste (Taste of Maroc), 1 tsp of ground chilli (Cooking With Alia, no. 689), ½ tsp of hot chilli (Lalla Moulati, 2014).","variants.8":"Black pepper: ¼ tsp (Cooking With Alia, episode 47) or ½ tsp (Fatéma Hal).","variants.9":"Redder and saucier: 2 to 3 tomatoes per aubergine, like Christine Benlafquih's mother-in-law, or a spoonful of tomato purée (Taste of Maroc; Choumicha).","variants.10":"With one or two grilled peppers, halfway to taktouka (Taste of Maroc), or with courgettes and red peppers (My Moroccan Food).","variants.11":"Zaalouk “the new way”: 100 g of pitted olives and half a preserved lemon, added after the aubergine while you keep stirring, then cooked until the zaalouk is well reduced (Lalla Moulati, 2014).","variants.12":"Grated tomatoes instead of peeled and chopped ones (Cooking With Alia, no. 689; Fiona Dunlop calls grated tomato a classic Moroccan technique)."}},"ingredients":{"agneau":"Lamb","ail":"Garlic","amandes":"Almonds","anis":"Aniseed","artichaut":"Globe artichoke","aubergine":"Aubergine","badiane":"Star anise","basilic":"Basil","beurre":"Butter","bicarbonate":"Bicarbonate of soda","blanc-poulet":"Chicken breasts","boeuf":"Beef","boeuf-hache":"Minced meat","bouillon":"Broth from the pot","bouillon-volaille":"Halal chicken stock","boulgour":"Bulgur wheat","cannelle":"Cinnamon","cardamome":"Cardamom","carotte":"Carrot","carvi":"Caraway seeds","celeri":"Celery","cerfeuil":"Chervil","champignons":"Button mushrooms","chapelure":"Breadcrumbs","cheddar":"Cheddar","chou":"Cabbage","ciboulette":"Chives","citron":"Lemon","citron-confit":"Preserved lemon","clou-girofle":"Cloves","concentre-tomate":"Tomato purée","concombre":"Cucumber","confiture":"Jam","coriandre":"Coriander","coriandre-moulue":"Ground coriander","cornichons":"Gherkins","courge":"Squash","courgette":"Courgette","couscous":"Couscous","creme-fraiche":"Crème fraîche","cumin":"Cumin","curcuma":"Turmeric","eau":"Water","estragon":"Tarragon","farine":"Flour","fenugrec":"Fenugreek","feves-seches":"Dried fava beans","fleur-oranger":"Orange blossom water","fromage-rape":"Grated cheese","garam-masala":"Garam masala","ghee":"Ghee (clarified butter)","gingembre":"Ground ginger","haricots-blancs":"White beans","harissa":"Harissa","huile":"Oil","huile-olive":"Olive oil","jben":"Jben (fresh cheese)","kasuri-methi":"Dried fenugreek leaves","ketchup":"Ketchup","khlii":"Khlii","khobz":"Khobz","lait":"Milk","laitue":"Lettuce","laurier":"Bay leaves","lentilles":"Lentils","lentilles-corail":"Red lentils","levure-boulangere":"Baker's yeast","levure-chimique":"Baking powder","macaronis":"Macaroni","mastic":"Mastic","mayonnaise":"Mayonnaise","menthe":"Mint","merlan":"Whiting","miel":"Honey","moutarde":"Mustard","msemen":"Msemen","navet":"Turnip","nigelle":"Nigella seeds","noix-cajou":"Cashew nuts","noix-de-muscade":"Nutmeg","oeuf":"Eggs","oignon":"Onion","oignon-rouge":"Red onion","oignon-vert":"Spring onion","olives":"Olives","orge-concassee":"Cracked barley","origan":"Oregano","pain-burger":"Burger bun","pain-pita":"Pitta bread","paprika":"Paprika","pate-ail-gingembre":"Fresh ginger and garlic","persil":"Flat-leaf parsley","petits-pois":"Peas","piment-cachemire":"Kashmiri chilli","piment-cayenne":"Hot chilli","piment-flocons":"Chilli flakes","piment-jamaique":"Allspice","piment-vert":"Green chilli","pois-chiches":"Chickpeas","poisson":"Fish","poivre":"Black pepper","poivron":"Pepper","poivron-vert":"Green pepper","pomme-de-terre":"Potato","poulet":"Chicken","pruneaux":"Prunes","raisins-secs":"Raisins","ras-el-hanout":"Ras el hanout","riz":"Rice","safran":"Saffron","sardine":"Sardines","sel":"Salt","semoule":"Semolina","sesame":"Sesame seeds","sirop-erable":"Maple syrup","smen":"Smen","spaghetti":"Spaghetti","sucre":"Sugar","sucre-glace":"Icing sugar","sucre-vanille":"Vanilla sugar","tahini":"Tahini","the-vert":"Green tea","thon":"Tinned tuna","thym":"Thyme","tomate":"Tomato","tomates-pelees":"Tinned peeled tomatoes","vermicelles":"Vermicelli","vinaigre":"White vinegar","warqa":"Brick pastry sheets","yaourt":"Plain yoghurt"}};
